-- ── v2 Stage 3: the `IO` widening union alias (design §3.2) ─────────────────
-- `IO` is the coarse security alias: in a BOUND/annotation row it stands for
-- the join of the narrow security labels the selected profile grants, every one
-- at ⊤ when none is selected.  `hostLabels` lists them (every minted security
-- label EXCEPT IO itself); internal labels (Mut/Panic) are NOT members, so they
-- are tracked independently of IO.
-- NOTE: `FFI` is deliberately NOT a member. #2071/#2070 (epic, Layer 1, R1):
-- an `<IO>` bound must NOT subsume an `<FFI>`-performing body — FFI crosses a
-- trust boundary the IO alias is not meant to paper over. Do not "complete"
-- this list by adding FFI.

-- What `IO` joins in this drive: the selected profile's grants of the alias
-- labels, each at the parameter granted, in `hostLabels` order.  With no
-- profile that is all eleven at their tops.  Every module of the program is
-- judged against the one join, the shipped stdlib included.
export
ioJoinAtoms : List (String, Param) -> List (String, EffParamTy) -> List Atom
ioJoinAtoms domains grants =
  filterMap
    (l =>
      map
        (granted =>
          atomWith
            (builtinLabel l)
            (writtenParam (dtopIn domains (builtinLabel l)) granted))
        (lookupAssoc l grants))
    hostLabels

-- ── global inference state ────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- sorted + deduplicated string set (canonical effect-label order, matching the
-- OCaml `List.sort_uniq String.compare` so multi-label rows render identically)
-- sortUniqS / sortUniqSGo / sortInsertS moved to support/util.mdk (imported above).

-- ── effect propagation (Phase 79) ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- the latent effect of the function body currently being inferred: application
-- arms widen it; entering a lambda saves+reopens it, and the accumulated row
-- becomes the lambda's arrow effect. The accumulator begins at the empty row;
-- a closed row is an effect value, never a switch for discarding effects.

-- Keyed indices — so the hot per-field-access read paths never walk a linear registry
-- (issue #149).  #415 item 3: `registerRecordInfoKeyed` is the sole REGISTRATION writer of
-- both; they are cleared together by `freshModuleCtx`; and the cross-module record floor is
-- loaded by `loadDataUniverse` from `universeRecordPop`.
--
-- 🚨 THE LOCKSTEP WAS "STRUCTURAL, NOT AN OBLIGATION" UNTIL #1111 A-2.12 (#1319 unit 2),
-- AND IT IS NOW AN OBLIGATION.  `applyRecordScopeOverrides` re-points `recordByNameRef`
-- for a collided key and does NOT touch `fieldOwnersRef`, so the two are no longer written
-- only in pairs and nothing enforces that they agree.  Re-pointing key `K` cannot change
-- which KEYS own a field name, but it does change which FIELDS `K` declares — so with two
-- same-named records having DISJOINT field sets, `fieldOwnersRef` can name `K` as the owner
-- of a field the newly-selected `K` does not declare.
-- ⚠️ THAT IS NOT HYPOTHETICAL AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS NOTE SAID IT WAS.  It called the
-- divergence "an obligation with no observed consequence" on the strength of one probe that
-- kept every projection inside the module which NAMED the record — where the overlay and the
-- floor agree by construction, so the probe could not have shown anything.  **#1377 was the
-- consequence**: a module naming one `Cfg` and holding a value of the other was refused.
-- 🚨 #1319 UNIT 3 DRAINED #1377, AND THE OBLIGATION SURVIVES IT.  Unit 3 did not reconcile
-- the two tables; it stopped ASKING the overlay a question the overlay could not answer —
-- `resolveFieldRecord`'s arm (a) now selects by the receiver's identity (see
-- `lookupRecordForReceiver`).  So the one observed consequence is gone and the write
-- asymmetry is not.  ⚠️ Do NOT restate this as "no observed consequence today": that exact
-- sentence has now been wrong twice here, and what is true is narrower — the consequence
-- that was found has been removed, and nobody has established that it was the only one.
-- Anything added here must state which of the two it writes and why the other is safe.
--   recordByNameRef : registry KEY → RecordInfo.  omInsert is last-write-wins and
--     the register site inserts in registration order, so it reproduces the old
--     prepend-list first-match (latest-registered wins) for lookupRecordByName.
--   fieldOwnersRef  : field name → the registry keys whose RecordInfo declares it
--     (a multimap).  fieldOwnerNames still `sortUniqS`es this so the owner-list
--     order that feeds ambiguousFieldMsg/head stays byte-identical.

-- Transparent type aliases (`type Foo = Int`, `type Pair a = (a, a)`).  Keyed by
-- alias name → (params, RHS surface type).  Populated in a pre-pass by
-- `registerData`'s DTypeAlias arm (mirrors `dataParamKindsRef`); consulted
-- on-demand at the `Ty→Mono` seam (`fromAstTypeE`) to expand an alias to its RHS.
-- The param list is stored even though stage 2 only expands the zero-param case,
-- so parameterized expansion (stage 3) can extend this without reshaping the table.

-- Names of record/named-field types exported ABSTRACTLY (`export data`/`export
-- record` without `public`).  Seeded ONCE (over EVERY module's decls, prelude
-- included) by the multi-module check drivers, BEFORE per-module checking, and
-- deliberately NOT cleared by `freshModuleCtx` — it is whole-program metadata, not a
-- per-module accumulator.  An abstract export brings the type NAME into scope but
-- never registers its fields (publicDataDecls drops VisAbstract decls, so they never
-- reach the record registry in an importer — its PARAM KINDS do travel, since #804/#1028, via
-- kindPublicDataDecl's VisAbstract arm, but a kind is not a field).  A dot-access on
-- such a type therefore lands at resolveFieldRecord's `head known but no record
-- info` branch with the type name still recoverable as the receiver's head tycon;
-- membership here distinguishes that case (clear abstract-export diagnostic) from a
-- genuinely-unknown field on an unbound receiver (the `<unknown>` message).  This
-- mirrors resolve's destructure-side AbstractFieldAccess signal (env.types ∋ owner
-- ∧ owner owns no field) without threading anything new through the import seed.

-- collect the names of all VisAbstract record-shaped (a DData whose variants
-- carry named fields) decls across the given decl list.  Only
-- record-shaped abstract types can be a dot-access receiver, but harvesting any
-- abstract DData name too is harmless (a positional ADT can't reach the abstract
-- branch with a field access — its fields never exist).
collectAbstractRecordTypes : List Decl -> List String
collectAbstractRecordTypes [] = []
collectAbstractRecordTypes ((DData { dataVis = VisAbstract, dataName = n }) :: rest) =
  n :: collectAbstractRecordTypes rest
collectAbstractRecordTypes (_ :: rest) = collectAbstractRecordTypes rest

-- seed abstractRecordTypesRef from every module's decls (the per-module progs plus
-- the prelude/core decls), once, before checking begins.  Keyed by the type
-- word (`typeTagOf`), so a program's own type that shares an abstract export's
-- name is not mistaken for it.
seedAbstractRecordTypes : GraphCtx ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  Unit
seedAbstractRecordTypes gctx coreDecls modules =
  let abstracts =
    collectAbstractRecordTypes coreDecls
      ++ flatMap (m => abstractTypeWords (fst m) (snd m)) modules
  gctx.abstractRecordTypesRef := namesToSet abstracts omEmpty

abstractTypeWords : String -> List Decl -> List String
abstractTypeWords mid decls =
  map (n => typeTagOf (OriginModule mid) n) (collectAbstractRecordTypes decls)

-- return-position method sites discovered during inference: (method name, the
-- EMethodAt route ref to fill, the EMethodAt impl-dicts ref to fill, the method's
-- result mono).  After the whole program is inferred, each result mono's head
-- tycon is the concrete impl tag the site dispatches to (RKey) — or, when it is a
-- constrained function's constraint variable, that function's dict parameter
-- (RDict).  The impl-dicts ref is filled (single-module elaborate path only) when
-- the route is a parametric impl with a `requires` — one route per requires.
-- #156 S3a: entries are `PendingEntry`s of kind `SKReturn`.

-- Phase 151 / Gap G: comparison/equality OPERATOR sites discovered during
-- inference: (method name, the EBinOp route ref to fill, the operand mono).
-- After inference resolveBinopSites stamps each ref with the resolved Eq/Ord impl
-- route (RKey) — but ONLY when the operand grounds to a NON-primitive type; the
-- structural-builtin EBinOp eval path handles primitive / ungroundable operands
-- (so `eq a b = a == b` on Int never recurses).  dictPass then rewrites the
-- stamped node into the method application.
-- (method, route ref, operand mono, inImpl-flag, enclosing-fn name).  The 5th
-- field is currentFn at record time: for a TOP-LEVEL constrained fn an operator on
-- its own constraint var routes RDict $dict_<encl>_<slot> (via enclDictVarOf), the
-- operator analogue of the direct `eq x y` arg-stamp path.
-- #156 S3a: entries are `PendingEntry`s of kind `SKOp`.

-- Roadmap #18c: the EUnOp analogue of pendingBinopSites — `-` (Num's `negate`)
-- sites discovered during inference.  Same tuple shape/fields, same resolve/
-- rewrite seam (resolveUnopSites / rewriteUnopExpr), one operand instead of two.

-- Arithmetic EBinOp sites whose operand type may ground to a concrete primitive.
-- Recorded during inference (operand Mono + the node's route ref);
-- resolveArithSites reads each solved Mono after the whole module is inferred and
-- stamps its supported scalar tag so lowering carries the result type into
-- CBinPrim and the native emitter need not recover it from operand structure. A still-
-- polymorphic (Num a) operand never grounds → stays RNone → today's dict path
-- (mirrors resolveBinopSite's grounded-only guard, so dict-routed arithmetic is
-- untouched).

-- Error-path BLOCKER B4 (audit finding D3): missing-impl dispatch obligations.
-- `methodEntryHere` maps each interface method NAME → (iface, the iface's typarams,
-- the method's declared AST type), as the reading module's scope resolves it
-- (`methodScopeAt`).  At each EVar/EMethodAt occurrence of a
-- registered method, recordImplObligation captures the OCCURRENCE mono so that,
-- after inference, checkImplObligations can read what the interface params
-- resolved to (parallel-walking the declared type against the normalized
-- occurrence mono) and reject the call when those concrete head types have NO
-- matching impl in the program (the `No impl of … for …` reject).  Skips while a param is still a
-- polymorphic tyvar (deferred to the caller).
-- A bare-name collision between two unrelated interfaces is decided by the reading
-- module's scope, never by registration order (#1092) — see `MethodEnv`.

-- C5 (TYPECHECK-AUDIT / Phase 112): names that are BOTH an interface method AND a
-- cross-module-imported standalone top-level function (e.g. box's `toList`/`isEmpty`
-- shadowing Foldable's).  Populated per module by elabModuleStamp from the full graph
-- (a top-level funDef name that is also a method name and is NOT defined in the
-- current module).  resolveSite consults it: a method site at a concrete receiver
-- with NO impl, whose name is here, routes RLocal (eval reads the standalone) instead
-- of RKey<head> (which would panic with no impl).
-- `freshModuleCtx` starts it empty; `checkBodyImpl` rebuilds it per module.

-- DEFINER-shadow names (facet 2): the names THIS module declares as a top-level
-- funDef that ALSO name an interface method (buildDefinerShadows) — e.g. a user
-- `eq : List Int -> List Int -> Bool`.  A DIRECT call `eq [1] [2]` in user code
-- must resolve to the user's OWN definition (RLocal), NOT to a method impl, even
-- when the receiver's head HAS an impl of the interface (`List` has `Eq`).  The
-- arg-stamp pass would otherwise stamp `RKey:List` (the impl), making the eval path
-- dispatch to the prelude `Eq (List a)` impl (`[1] ≠ [2]` → False) instead of the
-- user's fn (True) — a run≠build divergence (the EMIT path already routes to the
-- user's def).  resolveRLocalSite stamps these RLocal unconditionally.  Populated
-- per-module in checkModuleFullImpl from buildDefinerShadows; reset by `freshModuleCtx`.

-- P0-19: the declared top-level SIGNATURES of definer-shadow standalones
-- (`size : Int -> Int`), keyed by name.  When a definer-shadow occurrence resolves
-- to the STANDALONE (no visible impl for the grounded receiver, or an ungrounded
-- receiver), inferDefinerShadowApp enforces this declared domain against the actual
-- argument — closing the S2/S5 "must then type against the standalone's domain" hole
-- that let `size "hi"` / a poly wrapper `useIt x = size x; useIt (Box 3)` type against
-- the permissive method scheme and reach codegen as garbage.  Set per-path from
-- `sigsOf prog`; empty for an unsignatured standalone (no domain to enforce → the old
-- permissive behavior, unchanged).

-- P0-19 d4b (S4): True while inferring the HEAD of a definer-shadow APPLICATION
-- (`size (Box 3)`) — set around the head-infer call in inferDefinerShadowVarApp /
-- inferDefinerShadowApp.  A shadow occurrence reaches inferVar / inferMethodAt in
-- two shapes: as an application head (dispatch-vs-standalone decided per-receiver by
-- the surrounding shadow-app machinery, so the head must keep the permissive METHOD
-- scheme `a -> Int`) OR in bare VALUE position (`map size xs`, `let f = size`), which
-- S4 says ALWAYS denotes the standalone.  This flag distinguishes the two so only the
-- value-position occurrence is pinned to the standalone scheme.  Default/reset False.

-- P0-18 (EMIT path only): the mangler (`mangleUnits`) renames a definer-shadow
-- standalone def + its call sites to `<mid>__name` BEFORE marking, but (correctly)
-- leaves the interface method name bare — so the mark pass's NAME-based shadow
-- detector (buildStandaloneShadowsGraph / buildDefinerShadows, keyed on
-- funDef-name ∩ iface-method-name) no longer sees a shadow and the occurrence never
-- becomes an `EMethodAt` dispatch site (→ a silent direct call to the standalone =
-- the build miscompile).  elaborateModules recovers the shadow post-mangle by
-- forward-constructing `mangledName mid method` for every (module, iface-method)
-- pair and checking it against the module's actual funDef names; each hit is a
-- `(mangledSymbol ↦ bareMethod)` entry recorded here.  The mark pass then (a) marks
-- the mangled occurrence `EMethodAt` with the BARE dispatch name (so `implFor` finds
-- the impl for a receiver that HAS one) and (b) carries the mangled symbol in the
-- route's `RLocal <symbol>` fallback for a receiver that does NOT.
-- #2809 RETIRED THE "EMPTY OFF THE EMIT PATH" INVARIANT.  Mangling now runs AFTER
-- elaboration, so NO path reaches this map with mangled funDefs and the rows every path
-- gets are the BARE ones `unitMangledShadows` adds: `(name ↦ name)` for a definer
-- shadow, in every module of the graph.  Both spellings are still recorded there, so
-- (a) and (b) above read the same way; what changed is that the SYMBOL half is now the
-- bare standalone name and the rename that moves it runs later.  A consumer must no
-- longer treat a non-empty map as "this is the emit path".
-- Set once at the top of elaborateModules;
-- deliberately NOT cleared by `freshModuleCtx` (checkModuleFullImpl's per-module `freshModuleCtx`
-- must not wipe it mid-graph) — the forward-construction membership check makes a
-- stale value inert (no funDef matches → no entry).

-- one entry per occurrence of a registered interface method: (iface, typarams,
-- declared method Ty, the occurrence's instantiated mono).  Resolved post-inference.
-- B.10.2c: the 5th element is the enclosing construct's span (`mcLoc`) snapshotted
-- at the RECORD site (during inference, when the construct is live), so the post-HM
-- obligation check (checkImplObligations) can pair a `No impl of …` reject with
-- the offending expression's range — captured at record time
-- rather than reading the stale global at the post-HM raise.

-- PERF: the obligation list's length, in O(1).  That list grows with every
-- constrained occurrence in the program and is NEVER trimmed, yet each top-level
-- group brackets its inference with a length-before / length-after pair to recover
-- the obligations IT added (they are consed on the front, so `wWindow` recovers
-- them).  Measuring that length by walking the list was O(N) over an O(N) list once
-- per group — O(N²), and the single hottest symbol in the typecheck stage on a large
-- file.  #841 ports this channel onto `Windowed` (see `implObls` below): `wPush`,
-- the per-module `ModuleCtx` mint, and `wRestore` (checkMethodBodyAgainst's error
-- rollback) are its only mutators, so the list and its length can no longer drift.

-- PLAN.md #11: one entry per source integer literal (ENumLit):
-- (litVar, fref, n, dref).  [litVar] is the literal's fresh Num-obligated type var;
-- [fref] is the node's own Option Float cell; [n] the int value; [dref] is the
-- node's `fromInt`-route cell (filled by resolveSites — the literal is modelled as a
-- `fromInt n` method occurrence).  After defaulting + grounding, the post-HM pass
-- setNumlitFloats decides the runtime rep: litVar ground Float ⇒ fref Some, dref cleared ⇒ ELit (LFloat f);
-- litVar ground Int ⇒ both cleared ⇒ ELit (LInt n); litVar still polymorphic `Num a`
-- (the soundness case — `1` in `inc x = x + 1`) ⇒ dref KEPT (RDictFwd onto the
-- enclosing Num dict) ⇒ dictPass emits `EApp (EMethodAt "fromInt" …) (ELit (LInt n))`.

-- Chunk A (error framing): the union-find root ids of every type var that took
-- part in a unification failure (`typeMismatch`).  Consulted ONLY by
-- `undeterminedGoalExempt` to SUPPRESS a secondary `ambiguous instance`
-- cascade on a var the primary mismatch already explained — never read on the
-- inference path, so a well-typed program (no mismatch) never poisons anything
-- and behaves identically.

-- Chunk D (Num mis-framing): (litVar, loc) for every numeric literal whose var
-- carried a Num obligation — captured at inferNumLit/inferNumLitBare record time
-- while the var is still unbound.  Lets checkOneImplObligation reframe a purely
-- literal Num obligation forced to a concrete non-Num type (`Type mismatch: Int vs
-- T`) while an OPERATOR-sourced one (`x + 1`) keeps `No impl of Num for T`.  Storing
-- the VAR (not just the loc) lets the operator-taint pass below share a root through
-- `normalize`, so `1 + x` and `x + 1` are both recognised as operator-sourced
-- regardless of unify direction.  Error-path only; reset per run.

-- PERF: the literals that are NOT YET operator-tainted.  `markNumlitOpTaint` scans
-- THIS instead of the full `numlitVarLocs` (which is never trimmed).  Sound because
-- tainting is idempotent: it appends a loc to `numlitOpLocs`, whose only reader
-- (`numlitReframeLoc`) does a membership test — so re-tainting an already-tainted
-- literal cannot change any outcome, and a tainted literal may leave the worklist.
-- Scanning EVERY literal ever recorded on EVERY arithmetic operand was O(N²), and
-- after the allocation fix (see `rootIdOf`) it was still the typecheck stage's
-- dominant remaining cost on a large file.
--
-- `numlitVarLocs` itself keeps every literal — `numlitReframeLoc` and
-- `tagLitLocsWithRoot` both still need the complete list.

-- Chunk D: locs of literals whose var was ALSO an operand of an arithmetic op
-- (recordIfaceObligation).  Such a literal keeps the Num message — the operator is the
-- real culprit — so the reframe skips it (it reframes `litLocs \ opLocs`).

-- Chunk D: (literalLoc, contextKind) recorded when inferIf / inferListLit / consOp
-- is about to force a still-unbound literal var against a concrete sibling type —
-- lets the reframe pick site-specific wording (if-branches / list elements / cons
-- head-vs-element) over the bare `Type mismatch: Int vs T`.

-- Every local binding (let-bound name, lambda param, function-clause param,
-- match-arm binder) flows through inferPat's PVar/PAs arms, which capture
-- (name, mono var) here.  The mono var is union-find-solved by the time inference
-- ends; the module checker then generalizes each into `localSchemesOut`, and
-- `captureEntryTables` freezes them into the drive's `PartialAnalysis`.  The seed
-- (runtime.mdk externs: putStrLn, readLine, …) stays in scope for inference but
-- is excluded from the returned env, so `seedSchemesOut` is frozen the same way.
-- Neither enters the returned env.

-- #1114 (DICT-SEMANTICS §4.2 D1): would `checkOneCallObligation` reach a VERDICT on this
-- recorded predicate right now?  That — not groundness — is the question the D1-preserving
-- window in `inferUserImplBodies` has to ask, and getting it wrong is silent by
-- construction (see the warning at the end of this comment).
--
-- The gate decides a goal now exactly when every argument has a constructed head
-- (`allConcreteHeads`), and that verdict cannot change later: grounding is monotone, so
-- a constructed head (a type constructor, a rigid, or an arrow) never becomes a variable.
-- A predicate this filter drops while the gate would decide it vanishes with no verdict
-- from any channel (D4 exempts the impl channel from D3), which is #792's three-verdict
-- signature (`check` 0 / `run` `intToString: not an Int` / `build` `arg-tag dispatch on
-- impl type that owns no constructors`).
-- `numUnimplementableHead`'s `TApp` arm (a spine head that is an unbound tyvar) is NOT
-- kept: that head can still bind, so its verdict is not stable and the predicate belongs
-- to D2's deferred population.  An EMPTY argument vector is inert at the gate
-- (`if isEmptyL args then ()`), so dropping it loses no verdict.
--
-- The set is closed, enumerated from the type rather than sampled.  `Mono` has six
-- constructors and `headMonoNode` peels `TApp`, so a head is one of `TVar`, `TCon`,
-- `TRigid`, `TFun`, `TEff`.  `TCon`/`TRigid`/`TFun` are the constructed heads
-- `headTyconMono` answers, so `allConcreteHeads` keeps them; `TVar` can still bind, so
-- gate and filter agree to defer it; and `TEff` is decided by neither (the gate reaches
-- its own `allConcreteHeads` arm and defers).  A vector mixing a constructed head with a
-- variable defers in both, whatever the constructed head is.
--
-- ⚠️ NO GATE CAN CATCH A REGRESSION HERE.  A predicate this filter drops is one no channel
-- ever reports, so it produces silence, and silence is what every golden in the corpus
-- already records for a program the compiler accepts.  If you narrow this predicate, the
-- suite stays green.  The obligation is on the reader: any arm added to
-- `checkOneCallObligation` ABOVE its `allConcreteHeads` test must be mirrored here, or it
-- becomes unreachable for every parametric impl body in the language.
uOblIsDecidableNow : GraphCtx -> UObligation -> Bool
uOblIsDecidableNow gctx o =
  let args = map normalize (uOblArgs gctx o)
  isNonEmptyL args && allConcreteHeads args

-- the top-level fn whose body is currently being inferred ("" = none); captured
-- into a deferred RecDictApp so its recursive calls forward the right dict param.

-- G4: True while inferring an impl method BODY (set around inferImplMethod).  A
-- comparison/equality OPERATOR site records this flag so resolveBinopSite consults
-- `activeDictVarOf` (→ route the impl's threaded element dict, RDict) ONLY for sites
-- genuinely inside an impl body.  A TOP-LEVEL `<=`/`==` must NOT — its operand could
-- normalize to an unsolved tyvar whose id collides with a stale activeDictVars entry
-- left by a previously-inferred impl, spuriously routing it RDict (which flipped the
-- repl's String-keyed `:browse` sort).  activeDictVars is global + uncleared, so the
-- flag is the scope gate the id-lookup alone cannot provide.

-- INFERRED (unsignatured) `=>`-constraint promotion (the reference's two-pass
-- Elaborate / Phase 83/84/115).  An unsignatured fn whose body uses a
-- return-position method — or calls a constrained fn — at one of its OWN
-- generalized tyvars carries a constraint only AFTER inference.  We discover this
-- by recording, per occurrence, the enclosing fn (currentFn) and the live mono,
-- then (post-generalization) intersecting with the fn's quantified ids.
--   methodSiteFns : (enclosing fn, a return-position method site's result mono)
--   dictAppFns    : (enclosing fn, a constrained-fn call's instantiated constraint
--                   monos) — propagates a constraint to an unsignatured CALLER
--   promotedRef   : fns discovered THIS pass to carry an inferred constraint; read
--                   by the retired promotion fixpoint to grow the dict-passed set
--   dictEligibleRef: the USER fn names eligible for promotion (the prelude's
--                   constrained fns stay arg-tag/RKey — out of scope).  Set by
--                   the old elaborator; NOT cleared by `freshModuleCtx` (constant per run).
--
-- PERF: both are INDEXED BY ENCLOSING FN rather than kept as flat (fn, mono) assoc
-- lists.  Their sole reader, `inferredConstraintIds`, selects the entries of ONE fn,
-- and it runs once per member of every top-level group — so scanning a flat list that
-- accumulates a site for every constrained occurrence in the whole file was O(N²).
-- Each key's list is built by consing, exactly like the flat lists were, so a fn's
-- sites keep the same most-recent-first order the old scan produced.

-- The graph's promoted names so far, in load order.  `freshModuleCtx` clears
-- `promotedRef` per module, so `graphModuleWorker`'s `GOutTrees` arm unions each
-- module's `promotedRef.value` into here right after that module's check (before
-- the next module's `freshModuleCtx` wipes it).  Its reader is `beginModuleMarking`: a
-- module's dict-name set is the bare set plus THIS list, which is what lets one
-- sweep mark a promoted callee's cross-module call sites (ARCH §E).  Cleared at
-- every Module-arm driver entry (`elaborateModules`, `checkModulesPreambleK`) so an
-- earlier elaboration in the process cannot leak promoted names into a later mark
-- set; NOT touched by `freshModuleCtx`.  `elaborateModules` reads it once more at the
-- end, for the set `dictPass` prepends dict params over (`markDictNames`).

-- O(log n) membership index of dictEligibleRef.  #415 item 3: DERIVED from the list by
-- `setDictEligible`, the single writer of both — not mirrored at each set site, so the
-- two cannot drift.  maybeInferConstraint tests it per SCC member per discovery pass —
-- an O(members × eligible) scan over the whole program when it was a list.

-- Whether the retired single-program checker also inferred each parametric impl's
-- method bodies (instance-`requires` dict-passing, Phase 83/84 single-level).  ON
-- only for the old elaborate/dict path; off for the
-- `=== TYPES ===` golden path (checkProgram) and the multi-module path
-- (elaborateModules) so their scheme output stays byte-identical.  Deliberately
-- NOT reset by `freshModuleCtx` — the entrypoint that owns the path sets it.

-- ── D0.5 arg-position dispatch MEASUREMENT (probe-only) ─────────────────────
-- Classify every ARGUMENT-position interface-method occurrence (the bare-EVar
-- arg-tag population D0 counted, upper bound 1065) by the inferred type of its
-- DISCRIMINATING argument: concrete-primitive / concrete-ADT / type-variable.
-- Mirrors recordSite/resolveSites (which stamp RETURN-position sites) but for
-- arg-position sites it MEASURES, never stamps.  An arg-position method is exactly
-- one that reached inferVar (return-position + method-constrained methods were
-- already rewritten to EMethodAt/EDictAt by prePassDict, so they never arrive there).

-- method name → 0-based index of its first argument mentioning the interface
-- typaram (its dispatch position).  Set by the probe before elaboration; absence
-- of a name means "not an arg-position interface method" → not measured.

-- ── D3a arg-position dispatch STAMPING (Native backend, ALL elaboration paths) ──
-- The action analog of the D0.5 measurement: instead of classifying each
-- ARGUMENT-position interface-method occurrence, STAMP a static `RKey <head>` for
-- every one whose discriminating-argument type resolves to a concrete head (the
-- 903 concrete sites = 25 primitive + 878 ADT).  Type-variable occurrences (the
-- 110 D3b sites) stay RNone → the emitter falls back to runtime arg-tag dispatch.
-- Stamping is UNCONDITIONAL: emit, eval, and the `=== TYPES ===` / Core-IR golden +
-- ceval-oracle drivers all elaborate identically — one single elaboration mode
-- (#157).  Measured byte-identical across the 204/204 fixtures, so the unification
-- is representation-neutral, not a behaviour change.

-- the route refs awaiting an arg-position stamp + their discriminating-argument
-- mono (captured during inference, resolved by resolveArgStamps while the tyvar
-- cells are still bound).  Recorded as `GKArgStamp` goals on `GraphRun.goals`,
-- drained per module like the return-position sites.
-- D3b-1: also carries the method name + the EMethodAt impl-dicts ref, so a
-- concrete site whose head names a parametric `requires` impl can thread the
-- element dict (resolveArgStamp → argImplDictRoutesForEncl), exactly as the
-- return-position sites carry implRef for resolveSite.
-- #156 S3a: entries are `PendingEntry`s of kind `SKArg`.

-- Native Gap C (parametric default-method dict-synthesis, 2026-06-12): a BY-NAME
-- relational method (`lt`/`gt`/…) at a parametric Ord head (tuple/`List a`/`Box a`)
-- has NO own impl, so its element `requires` dicts must be keyed off the inner
-- `compare` (whose impl carries them).  emitDefaultDefine eta-prepends matching
-- dict params; emitDefaultRKey forwards these words.  Concrete head / a method
-- with its own impl ⇒ same key as before (empty for non-parametric), unchanged.

-- ── type-error accumulator ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Unification failures record diagnostics here instead of panicking, so
-- ill-typed programs emit matching `TYPE ERROR: …` lines.  occursCheckFailed
-- is a per-bindVar flag: occursAdjustUnbound sets it so bindVar can skip the
-- actual link (preventing a cyclic union-find entry).
-- Each accumulated error carries the span of the construct around its push site
-- (the record's `mcLoc`, see `mctxAt`) unless the push names one.  severity = 1.
--
-- issue 1146 — THE CHANNEL IS SPLIT BY ROLE.  "Which diagnostics are REPORTED"
-- is OUTPUT and lives in `ModuleCells.mcCells.typeErrors`, a `Windowed TcDiag` like its
-- obligation-channel siblings (see `Windowed` above — `wPush`/`wRestore` are
-- its sole WRITERS, so its list and its count cannot drift apart).  "An error
-- OCCURRED" is CONTROL and lives in `ModuleCells.mcCells.errorsDetected`, a monotone-per-module
-- occurrence counter that is not a diagnostics channel at all.
--
-- The counter counts errors DETECTED, not errors STORED (PR2).  The three dedup
-- helpers below still read the list by CONTENT to decide whether to store, but a
-- short-circuit bumps the counter anyway (`noteTypeErrorDetected`), so "was an error
-- detected here" and "is this diagnostic worth printing" are now independent
-- judgments.  That is the property the whole split exists for: NO diagnostics
-- decision — dedup key, rendering, ordering, loc selection, code selection — can
-- reach a control decision, because the only thing control reads is a count of
-- detections and none of those choices changes whether a detection happened.
--
-- PR1 stopped short of this: it bumped only on a real store, so the counter's delta
-- equalled `listLen typeErrors`'s delta exactly (which is what let PR1 land without
-- moving a golden), and a deduped push therefore answered `erredDuring` False and let
-- a gated `unify` run that a non-deduped push would have suppressed.  PR2 gives that
-- equality up on purpose; it is no longer an invariant and must not be restored.
--
-- See `recordTypeError` / `noteTypeErrorDetected` (the counter's sole writers) and
-- `erredDuring` (its sole reader) below.

-- G1 (SOUNDNESS): `GraphCtx.typeErrorsSticky` is a sticky mirror of typeErrors that
-- `freshModuleCtx` does not clear.  The multi-module elaborate path
-- (elaborateModules → checkModuleFullImpl) calls `freshModuleCtx` at the start of each
-- module, wiping `typeErrors`, so after the whole graph only the last module's errors
-- survive there.  build/run must abort on a type error in any module, so
-- `pushTypeError` also records into this accumulator, which the drive's mint
-- starts `False`.  Read via `hadTypeErrors` to gate the CLI exit code before
-- emit/eval.
--
-- #2089 / #2544 (M4): `GraphCtx.typeErrorsStickyDiags` holds the diagnostics behind
-- the flag, newest first, across every module of the graph.  `ModuleCells.mcCells.typeErrors` is
-- wiped per module, so this is the only place a caller with no per-module hook can
-- read what armed the sticky flag rather than merely that it was armed — it is what
-- `elaborateModules` returns as its residual, each entry with its own `Loc`.  Written
-- only by `recordTypeError`, started empty by the drive's mint, and rolled back
-- wherever the flag is (`checkGraphFinish`) — the same pairing obligation the
-- detection counter states.  Both are the drive's own.

-- #2544 (M4): every diagnostic that armed the sticky gate in the drive, each tagged
-- with the module it was recorded in, in push order, deduplicated PER GOAL —
-- module, code, location and text together — never by text alone (a text key would
-- re-introduce the suppressed-check shape OD5 forbids: two goals at two sites with
-- the same rendering are two findings).
export
stickyTypeErrorDiags : DriveOutputs -> List (String, TcDiag)
stickyTypeErrorDiags o = stickyDiagsInPushOrder o.doStickyDiags

-- True iff the drive recorded a type error, as the drive left it.
export
hadTypeErrors : DriveOutputs -> Bool
hadTypeErrors o = o.doHadTypeErrors

-- Whether the `main` binding recorded obligations remains inference-private; the
-- publication module receives its Boolean answer rather than this side table.
mainHasObligations : ModuleCtx -> Bool
mainHasObligations mctx =
  let id = optionOr 0 (omLookup "main" mctx.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef.value)
  match lookupSchemeObls "main" id mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value
    Some (_ :: _) => True
    _ => False

-- ── non-exhaustive-match warning accumulator (the type-aware `check_match`) ──
-- `inferMatch` pushes a warning here for every `match` whose non-guarded arms
-- don't cover the scrutinee's type.  `matchOracle` is the ctor oracle the matrix
-- recursion consults; `checkOneMatchToLines` seeds it from the program's
-- data decls (so it sees the same user + builtin ctors check_match does) before
-- inference runs.  `freshModuleCtx` installs both from the `CheckRunSeed` and the driver
-- reads `matchWarnings` after the typecheck pass; runs that ignore match warnings
-- (the schemes/selfproc paths) simply never read it.
-- #159: element type is now `TcDiag` (severity 2), carrying the span (formerly the
-- parallel `matchWarningLocs`) and the actionable-fix help (formerly the message-
-- keyed `matchWarningHelp`) INLINE — no parallel lists, no lookup-by-message.  The
-- `TcDiag.msg` holds the FULL "Warning: …"-prefixed string exactly as the old
-- `List String` did, so the text-rendering paths (`checkOneMatchToLines`,
-- checkToLinesWithRuntime, checkModulesEntryReport) stay byte-identical; only the
-- prefix strip happens on the driver/diagnostics.mdk side.
--
-- ⚠️ THE `W-*` CODE IS AUTHORED AT THE PUSH SITE — write it into the `TcDiag`, never
-- leave it `""` for a downstream reader to guess.  `driver/diagnostics.mdk`'s
-- `diagOfTypeWarning` carries whatever code it finds here straight through to
-- `--json`; it used to re-derive one by prefix-matching this message text, which
-- silently relabelled any third warning `W-NONEXHAUSTIVE` (see that function's own
-- note, and DIAGNOSTIC-CODES-DESIGN.md — "*No chokepoint can infer these*").
-- Register the code in DIAGNOSTIC-CODES-DESIGN.md's warning table in the same change.
--
-- ⚠️ THE `W-` PREFIX IS LOAD-BEARING, not a naming convention.  The JSON `kind` is
-- DERIVED from the code's prefix (`codeKind`), so the code you write here decides
-- how every consumer classifies the diagnostic.  A demoted error that KEEPS its
-- `T-*` code is the trap: it reads as a type error to anything filtering the
-- per-stage taxonomy.  `diagKind` now stops that from rendering an incoherent
-- `{"kind":"type","severity":2}`, but it deliberately does NOT rewrite your code —
-- inventing `W-FOO` from `T-FOO` would be the same derive-identity-from-spelling
-- mistake in the other direction.  Author a `W-*` code; do not reuse the error's.
--
-- This is THE channel for a typecheck-stage warning — including one that used to be
-- an error.  Do NOT demote an error by pushing a severity-2 `TcDiag` through
-- `recordTypeError` instead: it arms `typeErrorsSticky` (aborts `build`/`run`) and
-- bumps `errorsDetected` (steers `erredDuring`-gated inference, issue 1146)
-- UNCONDITIONALLY, without reading severity at all — so the "warning" keeps both of
-- an error's side effects.  It does not even RENDER as a warning: `diagOfTypeError`
-- binds the severity field to `_` and prints severity 1.  (Removing that hardcode
-- would not rescue the idea — it would print severity 2 and still abort the build.
-- The side effects are the reason, not the rendering.)  `matchWarnings` has neither.

-- True iff the last typecheck pass pushed any match warning.  Read by the snapshot
-- runner (`tools/snapshot.mdk`) to decide whether a rendered `# TYPES` section carries
-- diagnostic prose — a `W-*` warning is graded by `compiler/ERROR-QUALITY.md` exactly
-- like an error is, so a `# TYPES` section holding one is NOT `--bless`-able.  Keyed on
-- the accumulator rather than sniffing the rendered text: the renderer must not have to
-- guess which of its own lines are diagnostics.
export
hadMatchWarnings : DriveOutputs -> Bool
hadMatchWarnings o = o.doHadMatchWarnings

-- The drive inputs of a driver that loads no stdlib ownership and selects no
-- profile: no module stdlib-owned, the flat entry not stdlib, every trace off, and
-- the `IO` join of no profile.  A pure value (it holds no `Ref`).
--
-- No ownership means every `extern` is stamped (#2072, `ffiStampModeOwned`), and that
-- direction is the safe one on purpose: a driver that passes no ownership suppresses
-- nothing, so the worst it can do is render an `FFI` atom on the stdlib catalog when
-- someone points it at stdlib source.  The inverse default would leave #2072's hole
-- (a user `extern f : Int -> <> Int` typing pure) open on every such path, which is
-- a soundness hole rather than a cosmetic one.
export
defaultDriveInputs : DriveInputs
defaultDriveInputs = DriveInputs {
  diExecutedMainDriver = None,
  diManifestRequests = [],
  diStdlibOwned = omEmpty,
  diFlatEntryIsStdlib = False,
  diTrace = None,
  diIoJoinGrants = ioJoinGrants None,
  diProfileName = "",
  diPreludeKey = None,
}

-- The drive inputs a driver builds from the profile it selected and the loader's
-- owning-root fact (`stdlibOwnership`, `driver/loader.mdk`): whether the flat entry
-- is a stdlib module, and the stdlib-owned module ids.  That fact is read by
-- `ffiStampMode`, which decides whether an `extern` DECLARATION in the program
-- under check is the stdlib's extern catalog (never stamped) or a user's FFI
-- declaration (always stamped).
--
-- Two ownership fields because graph checks carry different amounts of identity.
-- `Module mid _ _` has a module id, so the Module arm asks "is THIS module
-- stdlib-owned" of the set.  `Flat _` has no id at all (the flat driver has already
-- flattened the program into one anonymous decl list), so the entry's ownership
-- arrives as its own Bool.
--
-- NOT `projectTrustedMods` (`driver/loader.mdk`): that predicate answers a different
-- question (may module M *reference* an internal-only extern?) and is opt-in-able
-- from any `medaka.toml` via `allow-internal` (#1713).  Routing this through it would
-- let a user exempt their own externs from the stamp by writing one manifest line.
export
driveInputsFor : Option TargetProfile -> Bool -> List String -> DriveInputs
driveInputsFor profile flatEntry mods = { defaultDriveInputs |
  diStdlibOwned = namesToSet mods omEmpty,
  diFlatEntryIsStdlib = flatEntry,
  diIoJoinGrants = ioJoinGrants profile,
  diProfileName = match profile
    None => ""
    Some p => profileName p,
}

-- Every route a goal carries, in slot order — one for a site, one per slot for
-- a dictionary application.
obligationRoutes : Obligation -> List Route
obligationRoutes o = match o.oDest
  EvRoute r => [r.value]
  EvRoutes rs => rs.value

-- The predicate mono behind each slot, aligned by position with
-- `obligationRoutes`'s answer for the SAME obligation. `GKMethodDict`'s slots
-- carry an `IfaceRef`/`PredicateSlotArgs` pair rather than a `Mono` (#8251), so
-- it answers `None` per slot rather than reconstructing one.
obligationPredicateMonos : Obligation -> List (Option Mono)
obligationPredicateMonos o = match o.oPayload
  GPSite _ m _ => [Some m]
  GPArith m => [Some m]
  GPDictApp app => map Some app.pdaMonos
  GPMethodDict pmd => map (_ => None) pmd.pmdSlots
  GPRecDictApp (RecDictApp _ _ _ m _ _) => [Some m]

routeOutcomeKind : Route -> EvidenceOutcomeKind
routeOutcomeKind RNone = EOKNone
routeOutcomeKind (RKey _ _ _) = EOKInstance
routeOutcomeKind (RDict _) = EOKGiven
routeOutcomeKind (RDictFwd _) = EOKGiven
routeOutcomeKind (RLocal _ _) = EOKLocal
routeOutcomeKind (RScalar _) = EOKScalar
routeOutcomeKind (RProj r _) = routeOutcomeKind r

routeGivenBinder : Route -> Option String
routeGivenBinder (RDict n) = Some n
routeGivenBinder (RDictFwd n) = Some n
routeGivenBinder (RProj r _) = routeGivenBinder r
routeGivenBinder _ = None

routeInstanceKey : Route -> Option String
routeInstanceKey (RKey k _ _) = Some k
routeInstanceKey _ = None

-- The nested route list an `RKey`/`RLocal` packs at this slot: the packed
-- `requires`, exactly as `entailInst` produces them.
routePrerequisiteRoutes : Route -> List Route
routePrerequisiteRoutes (RKey _ rs _) = rs
routePrerequisiteRoutes (RLocal _ rs) = rs
routePrerequisiteRoutes _ = []

renderRouteIdentity : Route -> String
renderRouteIdentity RNone = "RNone"
renderRouteIdentity (RKey k _ _) = "RKey " ++ k
renderRouteIdentity (RDict n) = "RDict " ++ n
renderRouteIdentity (RDictFwd n) = "RDictFwd " ++ n
renderRouteIdentity (RLocal n _) = "RLocal " ++ n
renderRouteIdentity (RScalar n) = "RScalar " ++ n
renderRouteIdentity (RProj r path) =
  "RProj (\{renderRouteIdentity r}) \{joinWith "." (map intToString path)}"

noteEvidenceObservation : GraphCtx -> Obligation -> Unit
noteEvidenceObservation gctx o = match gctx.trace
  Some sink =>
    if sink.tsEvidenceObservationEnabled then
      let routes = obligationRoutes o
      let predicates = obligationPredicateMonos o
      let arity = listLen routes
      sink.tsEvidenceObservationEntries :=
        evidenceObservationEntriesGo o routes predicates arity 0
          ++ sink.tsEvidenceObservationEntries.value
  None => ()

evidenceObservationEntriesGo : Obligation ->
  List Route ->
  List (Option Mono) ->
  Int ->
  Int ->
  List EvidenceObservationEntry
evidenceObservationEntriesGo _ [] _ _ _ = []
evidenceObservationEntriesGo o (r :: rs) preds arity slot =
  let (predHere, predsRest) = match preds
    p :: ptail => (p, ptail)
    [] => (None, [])
  EvidenceObservationEntry {
      eoeEv = o.oEv,
      eoeGoalKind = goalKindName o.oKind,
      eoeSlot = slot,
      eoeArity = arity,
      eoePredicate = map ppMono predHere,
      eoeOutcome = routeOutcomeKind r,
      eoeGivenBinder = routeGivenBinder r,
      eoeInstanceKey = routeInstanceKey r,
      eoePrerequisites = map renderRouteIdentity (routePrerequisiteRoutes r),
      eoeScope = o.oScope,
    }
    :: evidenceObservationEntriesGo o rs predsRest arity (slot + 1)

-- Each channel's length as this module begins.
graphMarksNow : GraphCtx -> Unit -> GraphMarks
graphMarksNow gctx _ = GraphMarks {
  mGoals = gctx.graphRun.goalsN.value,
  mNumlit = gctx.graphRun.numlitN.value,
}

recordModuleStart : GraphCtx -> String -> Unit
recordModuleStart gctx mid =
  gctx.graphRun.moduleRanges :=
    (mid, graphMarksNow gctx ()) :: gctx.graphRun.moduleRanges.value

-- The summary's operations over the view of the entry the drive [o] checked.  The
-- field instantiation runs in a graph record of its own, whose effect-variable
-- counter continues from the drive's (`doEffvarNext`).
export
lastInvocationOps : DriveOutputs -> InvocationOps
lastInvocationOps o = match o.doInvocationView
  InvocationView ctors pols kinds =>
    let byHead = ctorsByHead invocationHeadKey ctors
    let gctx = initialGraphCtx builtinCtors (freshGraphRun ())
    gctx.graphRun.effvarCounter := o.doEffvarNext
    InvocationOps {
      headVariances =
        h => match headTyconMono h
          Some (HkDecl key) =>
            slotVariances (paramPolaritiesIn pols key) (lookupTab key kinds)
          _ => None,
      appliedFields =
        applied =>
          let (h, args) = spineParts applied
          flatMap
            (c => instantiateFields gctx args c)
            (optionOr [] (omLookup (invocationHeadKey h) byHead)),
      headKey = invocationHeadKey,
    }

-- Generalize each captured local binding's (now-solved) mono into a Scheme for
-- the LSP hover fallback (see localBindRefs).  Runs at the end of inference when
-- currentLevel is back at the top, so a still-free local var quantifies cleanly
-- to `a`; a ground var (e.g. a `let` bound to a String) renders concretely.
mapLocalSchemes : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, Option Loc, Mono) ->
  List (String, Option Loc, Scheme)
mapLocalSchemes mctx [] = []
mapLocalSchemes mctx ((n, l, m) :: rest) =
  (n, l, generalize mctx m) :: mapLocalSchemes mctx rest

-- The context the module last checked recorded for its binding [n].
ownContext : ModuleCtx -> String -> List VecObl
ownContext mctx n =
  -- #837: key the obligation lookup by (name, binding-id), exactly as the check-path
  -- reader does — recovering the id from schemeDefIdsRef (top-level binders of the
  -- module last checked).  With the leak-guard windows retired this is what stops a
  -- where-helper `g`'s obligation rendering on an unrelated top-level `g`; a binder
  -- absent from schemeDefIdsRef (id 0) falls to the bare-name scan (today's path).
  let id = optionOr 0 (omLookup n mctx.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef.value)
  optionOr [] (lookupSchemeObls n id mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value)

-- A module's own schemes, each with its context, taken when the module closes:
-- [mctx] still holds that module's tables, and the next module's check
-- mints its own record.
publishOwn : ModuleCtx -> List (String, Scheme) -> List (String, PubScheme)
publishOwn mctx = publishWithContexts (ownContext mctx)

-- Core's own obligations, keyed by bare name (#837: the binding-id half of each
-- key is dropped, as every cross-module reader looks a prelude name up by name).
-- Read right after the core pass, from the record [mctx] core was checked in.
coreOwnContexts : ModuleCtx -> OrdMap (List VecObl)
coreOwnContexts mctx =
  omFromPairs
    (map (kv => (fst (fst kv), snd kv)) mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value)
    omEmpty

-- The context a seed name renders with, by three steps in order.  A name the
-- module last checked BINDS takes that module's own context, or none, and never
-- reaches the prelude store, so a buffer's own `isEven x = "s"` cannot pick up the
-- prelude `isEven`'s context (#2054's shape).  The prelude's contexts are consulted
-- ONLY for a name this module does not bind and whose per-run table has no entry
-- at all: on the module arm each module gets its own `freshModuleCtx`, so the
-- prelude's obligations are not in [mctx], and a prelude scheme would otherwise
-- render without its `=>`.
-- The third step reads [coreContexts], the prelude's contexts keyed by bare name.
seedContext : ModuleCtx -> OrdMap (List VecObl) -> String -> List VecObl
seedContext mctx coreContexts n =
  match omLookup n mctx.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef.value
    Some _ => ownContext mctx n
    None => match lookupSchemeObls n 0 mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value
      Some obs => obs
      None => optionOr [] (omLookup n coreContexts)

-- The last module of [modules], checked in [mctx]: its locals and its import seed,
-- frozen with their contexts.  The seed's prelude contexts come from
-- [coreContexts], `graphPreamble`'s snapshot.
captureEntryTables : ModuleCtx ->
  OrdMap (List VecObl) ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  EntryTables
captureEntryTables mctx coreContexts modules = match last modules
  None => noEntryTables
  Some (mid, _) => EntryTables {
    etModule = mid,
    etLocals =
      map
        ((n, loc, s) => (n, freezePub s (ownContext mctx n), loc))
        mctx.mcCells.localSchemesOut.value,
    etSeed =
      map
        (b => (
          fst b,
          freezePub (snd b) (seedContext mctx coreContexts (fst b)),
        ))
        mctx.mcCells.seedSchemesOut.value,
  }

-- bind the field pattern vars with fresh types (no field-type info) so the
-- arm body still typechecks; the oracle fails fast on the unknown record, we
-- accumulate, so dropping the bindings would raise spurious unbound-var errors

-- bind the field's pattern vars against a fresh type so the arm body still
-- typechecks (the oracle fails fast on the unknown field; we accumulate, so
-- skipping the binding would raise spurious downstream unbound-variable errors)

-- still infer the sub-patterns so their bindings reach the arm body
-- (else the continued path raises spurious unbound-variable errors the
-- oracle never reaches — it fails fast on the unknown constructor)

-- (ESlice's `inferSlice`/`indexKind` were retired with #670: `a.[lo..hi]` now
-- desugars to a `slice` method call before typecheck, so slicing infers
-- through normal interface dispatch on `Slice`, not a bespoke arm here.)

-- C5: if [name] is a standalone-shadow method, record its discriminating-arg
-- mono cell UNGATED so resolveRLocalSites can route RLocal when the receiver
-- grounds to a no-impl head (the arg-stamp path would otherwise leave it RNone
-- → arg-tag panic on a receiver with no impl).

-- Cause-A prerequisite: ALSO record the discriminating-arg mono into
-- methodSiteFns keyed by currentFn (mirroring recordSite, which only fires
-- for return-position).  Without this, an unsignatured fn whose body
-- dispatches a method in ARGUMENT position (`f s = display s`) never
-- surfaces its inferred constraint to inferredConstraintIds → never gets
-- promoted → no leading dict param on the build path → dict word 0 (SIGSEGV/
-- silent).  Inert unless promotion (`registerInferredConstraints`, whose result the
-- schedule marks with) consumes it.

-- How many grants a top-level binding with this scheme takes ahead of its value
-- arguments.
export
bindingGrantArity : DriveOutputs -> String -> Scheme -> Int
bindingGrantArity o _ sch =
  if o.doGrantsEnabled then listLen (grantOrder sch) else 0

-- Phase 151 / Gap G: record a binop site for the comparison/equality operators
-- so resolveBinopSites can stamp [dref] once the operand type grounds.  The
-- operand mono is [lt] (unified with [rt] by eqCompareOp/ordCompareOp below).

-- Seed the table from the declaration list THIS pass treats as the prelude, so that a
-- synthetic occurrence's key and the prelude's own `impl Num Int` key agree.
--
-- The source list is chosen for symmetry with `insertUnivImpl`, which is what
-- correctness depends on — not for tidiness.  `insertUnivImpl` keys the impl side off
-- `DImpl.implOrigin`, so this side must read the origin of the interface declaration
-- that lives in the SAME universe:
--   * With a real prelude, the prelude pass installs all four identities from `core`.
--   * Once a non-empty prelude has been seeded, a later module contributes nothing: a
--     program interface spelled `Eq` is not the prelude's, whatever the prelude lacks.
--   * In a prelude-free graph, builtin declarations can be split across modules.
--     Each module contributes only identities not already present, so Eq→Num and
--     Num→Eq module orders retain both identities without allowing a later duplicate
--     declaration to replace an earlier one.
seedBuiltinClasses : GraphCtx -> String -> List Decl -> Unit
-- Both spellings of the prelude's module id are tested, for the same reason
-- `declEnvsOrdOf` indexes it twice: the export indexes (`meExports`,
-- `deCtorExports`) key the prelude under `"core"`, but the `mid` `graphPreamble`
-- actually PASSES for the core pass is `""` on both output selections
-- (`GOutDiags`: `checkModuleFull` → `checkModuleFullImpl ""`;
-- `GOutTrees`: `elabModuleStamp ""`).  Testing
-- only `"core"` would silently never fire on either selection — and the miss is silent
-- because an unseeded table yields `OriginUnresolved`, i.e. a `TkBare` goal key against
-- `TkIdent` impls: a false REJECT of the prelude, which is the #1112 prototype's
-- 634-diagnostic signature.
seedBuiltinClasses gctx mid prog =
  if isPreludeMid mid then
    let seeded = builtinClassesOf prog
    gctx.crossRun.builtinClassesRef :=
      BuiltinClasses { seeded | bcPreludeSeeded = isNonEmptyL prog }
  else if gctx.crossRun.builtinClassesRef.value.bcPreludeSeeded then
    ()
  else
    gctx.crossRun.builtinClassesRef :=
      builtinClassesGo prog gctx.crossRun.builtinClassesRef.value

-- @Impl: if [x] is an `@Name` disambiguation hint (a bare `EVar "@Name"`, possibly
-- under an ELoc wrapper), return the name with the leading `@` stripped.  Mirrors
-- the two oracle arms `EApp(f, EVar h)` and `EApp(f, ELoc(_, EVar h))` with
-- `h.[0]='@'`.  Anything else → None → the ordinary application path.

-- P0-16 beginner call-syntax hint.  A newcomer writes `add(1, 2)` (C/Python/JS
-- call syntax) meaning `add 1 2`; in Medaka `(1, 2)` is a TUPLE, so `add(1, 2)`
-- applies `add` to ONE tuple argument → a partial application / no-impl error
-- whose message ("No impl of Display for ((Int, Int) -> (Int, Int))") is
-- inscrutable.  We DETECT the shape at the application site — a curried arity-N
-- function applied to a single arity-N tuple literal whose first param is NOT
-- itself an N-tuple — and record the residual result type as a candidate; the
-- no-impl + type-mismatch surface sites then append the tailored hint when the
-- offending type IS that residual.  Recording is generous (a legitimate partial
-- application like `map (const (1, 2)) xs` also records) but the hint is only
-- ever ATTACHED to a genuine downstream error, so a legit partial application —
-- whose residual is consumed WITHOUT error — never shows it.  (residualMono,
-- help, optional (span, replacement) fix); reset by `freshModuleCtx`.

-- T2: a recursive inline function binding (`let go n = … in …`) must
-- pre-bind `go` with a monomorphic placeholder so the RHS can reference
-- itself, then generalize (a function is a value — oracle `:1668-1681`).

-- Type-aware exhaustiveness (`check_match`): run AFTER the arms have unified
-- the scrutinee type, so `scrutTy` is fully resolved when we read its head.

-- instantiate a record's quantified params with fresh vars.  Rebuilds ALL fields —
-- used by the record-CREATE and record-PATTERN paths, which must see every declared
-- field (missing-field check / binding each pattern field).  The single-field access
-- and record-update paths use `instantiateRecordShared` + the field index instead so
-- they never rebuild all N fields per access (issue #980).

-- Publish every top-level function clause and top-level let-group member under
-- the scheme its group generalized to, mirroring the decl shapes `funDefs`
-- flattens.  Each clause of a multi-clause function has its own id and records
-- the one scheme, and whether the module declares a signature for its name.
recordTopBinderTypes : GraphCtx -> OrdMap Scheme -> List Decl -> Unit
recordTopBinderTypes gctx schemes decls =
  let signed = signedTopNames decls omEmpty
  fold (_ d => recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx schemes signed d) () decls

-- The names a top-level type signature declares, an annotated one included.
signedTopNames : List Decl -> OrdMap Unit -> OrdMap Unit
signedTopNames [] acc = acc
signedTopNames ((DTypeSig _ n _ _) :: rest) acc =
  signedTopNames rest (omInsert n () acc)
signedTopNames ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) acc = signedTopNames (d :: rest) acc
signedTopNames (_ :: rest) acc = signedTopNames rest acc

recordTopDeclBinderTypes : GraphCtx ->
  OrdMap Scheme ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  Decl ->
  Unit
recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx schemes signed (DFunDef _ n ps _ _ bid) =
  recordTopFunBinderType gctx schemes signed n bid (listLen ps)
recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx schemes signed (DLetGroup _ binds) =
  fold
    (_ b => match b
      LetBind n clauses _ bid => match clauses
        (FunClause ps _) :: _ =>
          recordTopFunBinderType gctx schemes signed n bid (listLen ps)
        [] => recordTopFunBinderType gctx schemes signed n bid 0)
    ()
    binds
recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx schemes signed (DAttrib _ d) =
  recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx schemes signed d
recordTopDeclBinderTypes gctx _ _ _ = ()

recordTopFunBinderType : GraphCtx ->
  OrdMap Scheme ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  String ->
  BinderId ->
  Int ->
  Unit
recordTopFunBinderType gctx schemes signed n bid arity =
  match omLookup n schemes
    Some sch =>
      recordBinderType
        gctx
        bid
        n
        (schemeMono sch)
        (Some arity)
        (omHasKey n signed)
    None => ()

-- #799: the guard above (checkLetRecDecls/checkLetBind) only walks TOP-LEVEL
-- DLetGroup declarations. BLOCK-level (`let rec x = v` as a DoStmt) and
-- EXPRESSION-level (`let rec x = v in e2`, an ELetGroup) `let rec` reach
-- blockRecLet/inferRecLet/inferLetGroup directly, with no non-function check at
-- all — a non-function binding (e.g. `let rec loop = loop`) silently pre-binds
-- the name to a placeholder Ref and evaluates to it, never erroring.

-- ── elaboration: resolve return-position method dispatch (RKey) ────────────
-- Rewrite each return-position method occurrence to EMethodAt (with a fresh route
-- ref), type-check (recording the sites), then stamp each site's route from its
-- now-resolved result type.  Returns the SAME tree (refs filled) for eval.
-- There is no flat elaboration driver left: every caller goes through the
-- 1-module `elaborateOne` (the typed/dict tree of the degenerate 1-module multi
-- path).

-- ── 1-module wrapper over the multi-module path ────────────────────────────
-- elaborateOne runs the SINGLE program [prog] (the user's decls, the live prelude
-- ALREADY shadow-dropped by the caller) through elaborateModules as the degenerate
-- 1-module list [(rootId, prog)], returning the flat shape its callers consume:
-- core2 (dict-passed core) ++ the dict-passed module's decls.
--
-- The 1-module case automatically satisfies that shape's invariants — one
-- `freshModuleCtx` (one module ⇒ one checkModuleFullImpl), zero imports ⇒ the full
-- constraint set, and elaborateModules runs the eval-dict layer unconditionally
-- (ARGSTAMP-UNIFY Phase 4), so the return-position constrained-fn dict layer is
-- present here too.
--
-- The dict-name set is the module path's own: elaborateModules computes it via
-- `moduleDictNames` (the prelude's constrained names followed by the module graph's
-- FULL `constrainedSigNames`), rather than taking an externally built list.  This
-- wrapper therefore takes `coreDecls` SEPARATE (not pre-flattened into prog),
-- exactly as elaborateModules expects.  The grader for the Flat-vs-Module relation
-- is test/diff_compiler_flat_vs_onemodule.sh (ARCH B-2.1-a): it now pins the
-- own acceptance and diagnostics, and grades the relation only through a clause
-- true on both sides of the #1564 fix — every arm that ACCEPTS must compute the
-- same value.  Read its header before changing either arm.
export
elaborateOne : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  List Decl
elaborateOne inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls prog =
  fst (elaborateOneFacts inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls prog)

-- `elaborateOne` with the `ProgramFacts` value its elaboration built, for a caller that lowers
-- the flat decls.
export
elaborateOneFacts : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (List Decl, ProgramFacts)
elaborateOneFacts inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls (rootId, prog) =
  let elab = elaborateModules inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls [(rootId, prog)]
  let (coreD, modules, _, _, _, _, _) = elab
  (coreD ++ flatMap snd modules, factsOfElab elab)

-- #415 item 3: the ONLY writer of dictEligibleRef / dictEligibleSetRef.  The set is
-- DERIVED from the list here rather than mirrored at each set site, so the two cannot
-- drift: there is no longer a second side for a future edit to be absent from.  (Both
-- former sites hand-wrote `namesToSet <names> omEmpty` next to the list store; the
-- module site additionally recomputed `moduleUserFnNames modules` for the mirror.)
setDictEligible : GraphCtx -> List String -> Unit
setDictEligible gctx names =
  gctx.dictEligibleRef := names
  gctx.dictEligibleSetRef := namesToSet names omEmpty

-- the USER modules' top-level fn names (NOT core/prelude) — the promotion-eligible
-- set on the build path. Mirrors the retired elaborator's user-function eligibility.
moduleUserFnNames : List (String, List Decl) -> List String
moduleUserFnNames modules =
  dedup (flatMap (p => map fst (funDefs (snd p))) modules)

-- The PRELUDE's own `=>`-constrained fns that must ALSO be dict-passed: those
-- whose body uses a return-position method (`when`/`unless` use `pure`), so
-- arg-tag dispatch can't resolve it at a constraint variable's type.  Every OTHER
-- constrained prelude fn (sum/any/elem/clamp/…) uses only arg-position methods —
-- arg-tag already resolves those, and the goldens type-check with them on RKey, so
-- they stay OUT.  [allDecls] supplies the interface set for returnPosMethodNames;
-- bodies come from [preludeDecls] (the shadow-dropped prelude).
export
preludeReturnPosDictNames : List Decl -> List Decl -> List String
preludeReturnPosDictNames preludeDecls allDecls =
  let rpNames = returnPosMethodNames allDecls
  filterList
    (n => anyMember (declBodyVars n preludeDecls) rpNames)
    (constrainedSigNames preludeDecls)

-- D3b-2: the PRELUDE's `=>`-constrained fns that dispatch a method in ARGUMENT
-- position (the ~23 helpers: neq/elem/any/all/find/count/sum/product/maximum/
-- minimum/clamp/flatMap/flat/debugListItems/displayListItems/hashListItems/print/
-- println).  These stay OUT of the eval/golden dict set — arg-tag resolves them in
-- ceval (tagged values) and the goldens type-check with them on RKey — but the
-- NATIVE emit path MUST dict-pass them: arg-tag cannot dispatch on a PRIMITIVE
-- element (an `Int`/`String` immediate carries no cell tag).  The arg-position
-- analogue of preludeReturnPosDictNames: every constrained prelude fn that is NOT
-- already return-position.  Wired (alongside preludeReturnPosDictNames) into the
-- emit + dict_pp-oracle drivers ONLY (llvm_emit_typed_main / core_ir_dict_pp_main),
-- which no golden driver shares — so module/`=== TYPES ===`/eval goldens stay
-- byte-identical; the body sites route RDict $dict_<method>_<slot> through the same
-- activeDictVars layer registerConstraintRegs already builds for return-position.
export
preludeArgPosDictNames : List Decl -> List Decl -> List String
preludeArgPosDictNames preludeDecls allDecls =
  let rpSet = preludeReturnPosDictNames preludeDecls allDecls
  filterList (n => not (contains n rpSet)) (constrainedSigNames preludeDecls)

-- the EVars referenced anywhere in [n]'s definition body (multi-clause: union)
declBodyVars : String -> List Decl -> List String
declBodyVars n decls = flatMap (declBodyVarsOf n) decls

declBodyVarsOf : String -> Decl -> List String
declBodyVarsOf n (DFunDef _ m _ body _ _)
  | n == m = allEVars body
  | otherwise = []
declBodyVarsOf _ _ = []

resolveSites : SiteCtx -> OrdMap Unit -> List PendingEntry -> Unit
resolveSites sx _ [] = ()
resolveSites sx rpNames ((PendingEntry name tagRef resultMono encl kind loc scope _) :: rest) =
  let sx = { sx | sxSite = loc }
  match kind
    SKReturn implRef fullMono =>
      resolveSite
        sx
        rpNames
        name
        tagRef
        implRef
        resultMono
        fullMono
        encl
        loc
        scope
    SKAdmittedReturn ifaceRef implRef fullMono =>
      resolveAdmittedSite sx name ifaceRef tagRef implRef fullMono encl scope
    SKExactReturn implRef request =>
      resolveExactReturnSite
        sx
        rpNames
        name
        tagRef
        implRef
        resultMono
        request
        encl
        loc
        scope
    SKNumReturn predicate fullMono =>
      resolveNumLitSite
        sx
        rpNames
        name
        tagRef
        resultMono
        fullMono
        predicate
        encl
        loc
        scope
    _ => ()
  resolveSites sx rpNames rest

-- C5: stamp RLocal on each recorded standalone-shadow arg-position site whose
-- receiver mono now grounds to a concrete head tycon with NO impl of the method.
-- The receiver HAS an impl (the genuine-method case, e.g. `toList (Some 7)`) → leave
-- the route untouched (RNone → eval arg-tag-dispatches to the real impl).  An
-- ungrounded receiver (still a tyvar) → leave it (polymorphic, deferred).
-- RLocal only when no impl exists.
-- S-1: the stamp calls routesOfMonosTop — a PURE
-- function of the (union-find-solved) constraint monos — to fill the standalone's own
-- dict routes IN PLACE.  Deliberately NOT routed through pendingDictApps: that list is
-- resolved by resolveDictApps, which runs AFTER this in `moduleStampOrder`, so the stamp
-- would read `[]`.  Resolving inside the stamp is ordering-immune.  An unconstrained
-- standalone has no monos ⇒ routesOfMonosTop returns [] immediately (it cannot even
-- reach its AmbiguousImpl arm) ⇒ `RLocal sym []` ⇒ byte-identical to the pre-S1 stamp.
-- #156 S3d — resolveRLocalSites is DELIBERATELY NOT routed through `entail`, and DICT-
-- SEMANTICS §3 conformance REQUIRES that, rather than merely permitting it.  This pass
-- implements §4 `(var)`'s FIRST premise — binding selection: does this occurrence denote the
-- module's own standalone `sym` or a shadowed interface method?  (Keyed on the solved receiver
-- mono, which is why it runs post-inference.)  That decision reads shadow facts — isDefinerShadow,
-- definerShadowNamesRef — that are NOT components of entailment's domain `(IE, CE, P, π)` (§3
-- resolution-determinism); they are facts about Γ.  Folding them into the one entailment function
-- would make it depend on inputs outside its spec-given domain — violating the very law a merge
-- would invoke.  The tell: the `Some tag` arm treats "a live impl exists" as DO-NOTHING (the name
-- is a genuine method, dispatched elsewhere) and emits RLocal for the NO-impl case — the inverse of
-- `entailInst`'s success condition.  "No π to discharge; the name denotes another binding" is not an
-- evidence statement.  §4 `(var)`'s SECOND premise — the chosen standalone's actual entailment
-- obligations — IS routed through the one engine: every RLocal payload is filled by routesOfMonosTop
-- → entail (EKNestedTop).  So the single-entailment-function law holds at 4-of-5 BY CONSTRUCTION; what
-- stays out is exactly the binding-selection axis §3 never claimed (cf. §8 I1: identity keyed by
-- binding, not bare name).  "Does an impl exist" and "which impl entails" must agree on ONE
-- impl table — a drift between them would be a real dispatch bug.  #415 item 2: that
-- agreement is STRUCTURAL, not a standing obligation.
-- Arch B-2.1-g rebased both reads, together, onto `ModuleCells.mcCells.bodyImplEnvRef`, and the
-- sentence that stood here — "There is no second table in scope to drift TO" — WAS ALREADY
-- FALSE and is what licensed a bad scoping.  `bodyImplEnvRef` has been a second impl table,
-- in scope from anywhere, since B-2.1-a2, and B-2.1-b2 moved three SELECTION legs onto it
-- while this site's two reads stayed on the threaded prefix `[keyTable]`.  `B-2.1-c` then
-- moved the sibling SHADOW existence reads and deferred this one, which produced exactly the
-- drift this block claimed was unreachable: existence answered True off the whole graph while
-- `routesOfMonosTop … keyTable`, one line below, had no row to select — a located reject
-- became exit 0 printing a garbage pointer.  So the agreement is restored the only way it can
-- be, by moving BOTH: `ieImplExistsForHead …bodyImplEnvRef…` directly, and `keyForSite` (also
-- repointed onto `bodyImplEnvRef`) via routesOfMonosTop → routeOf → entail (EKNestedTop) →
-- entailInst.  The invariant is unchanged and is still the one that matters: these two
-- reads must name ONE population.  It is no longer enforced by a shared parameter, so it is
-- now enforced by a shared REF — and the check on any future edit here is "does the thing I
-- am reading answer over the same population `keyForSite` selects from?", not "am I using
-- [keyTable]?".  No impl list is threaded through this signature or through
-- `routesOfMonosTop`'s subtree any more.
-- (The inference-time existence reads in `inferShadowApp` / `definerReceiverDispatches` are
-- the same decision at an earlier PHASE and are now on the same ref for the same reason —
-- B-2.1-c's measurement is that a phase split of one decision is as fatal as a table split.
-- Do not re-derive the retired defence "GLOBAL by design — local ∪ imported ∪ prelude":
-- `SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md:242-245` retires that phrase as a false synonym for graph-global, and
-- `shadowKeyTableRef` was in fact the cumulative topological PREFIX on the Module arm.)
resolveRLocalSites : SiteCtx -> List PendingEntry -> Unit
resolveRLocalSites sx [] = ()
resolveRLocalSites sx ((PendingEntry name tagRef am encl kind loc scope _) :: rest) =
  let sx = { sx | sxSite = loc }
  match kind
    SKRLocal sym forceLocal monos ifaces argVecs =>
      if forceLocal then
        tagRef :=
          RLocal sym (routesOfMonosTopV sx encl scope monos ifaces argVecs)
      else
        resolveRLocalSite sx name tagRef am encl scope sym monos ifaces argVecs
    _ => ()
  resolveRLocalSites sx rest

-- After the module is inferred, classify each recorded arithmetic operand mono and
-- stamp the supported concrete scalar tag on the node's route. Grounded-only,
-- exactly like resolveBinopSite: a still-
-- polymorphic operand (headTyconMono None) is left untouched (RNone → dict path),
-- so polymorphic-Num arithmetic keeps routing through the runtime Num dict. Never
-- overwrite a route another pass already set (a comparison op has a different
-- node/ref; an arithmetic route starts RNone).
resolveArithSites : List (Ref Route, Mono) -> Unit
resolveArithSites [] = ()
resolveArithSites ((dref, m) :: rest) =
  resolveArithSite dref m
  resolveArithSites rest

-- EBinOp and EUnOp share ONE dict-route resolution family
-- (resolveOpSites/resolveOpSite/stampOpRouteVal/opDictVarOf), selected by an `isBinop`
-- flag (Roadmap #18c collapsed the binop/unop mirror pair — unopMethodApp already
-- reused binopRouteSplit, proving the shapes unify).  The two paths differ in exactly
-- two flag-keyed places: the dict-var-of policy (opDictVarOf) and the RKey stamp
-- (stampOpRouteVal — its builtin-head guard and Ord-style innerDefaultMethod reduction).
-- #156 S3c: resolveOpSite is now a thin adapter over `entail` (EKOp kind); the inst
-- route is stampOpRouteVal, a pure Route-returning extraction of the old stampOpRoute.
resolveOpSites : SiteCtx -> Bool -> List PendingEntry -> Unit
resolveOpSites sx _ [] = ()
resolveOpSites sx isBinop ((PendingEntry method tagRef operandMono encl kind loc scope _) :: rest) =
  let sx = { sx | sxSite = loc }
  match kind
    SKOp inImpl =>
      resolveOpSite sx isBinop method tagRef operandMono inImpl encl scope
    SKPredicateOp inImpl predicate =>
      resolvePredicateOpSite
        sx
        isBinop
        method
        tagRef
        operandMono
        inImpl
        predicate
        encl
        scope
    _ => ()
  resolveOpSites sx isBinop rest

-- Gap #21 (CLOSED): element reqs are now forwarded unconditionally on both eval and
-- emit paths.  The impl method `eq`/`compare` carries leading `$dict_*` params
-- (resolveArgStamps runs on both paths); emitter applies them; eval's methodAtNarrow
-- forwards dict reqs (like RDictFwd) truncated to the matched impl's reqCount.

-- ── coherence check (TYPECHECK-AUDIT S3) ───────────────────────────────────
-- Phase 68: reject incoherent USER
-- impl sets at declaration time.  Two impls of the SAME interface whose head
-- types can match the same concrete type (impls_overlap) are unresolvable unless
-- one strictly specializes the other (most-specific-wins).  Genuinely incoherent:
--   • two overlapping impls that are NOT a strict specialization → OverlappingImpls.
-- (Named impls + `@Name` selection + `default impl` were removed 2026-07-11; the
-- newtype-wrapper approach is the answer for a second instance.)
-- Prelude/seeded impls are excluded entirely (the driver passes USER decls only):
-- user impls are meant to override prelude impls (e.g. single-file `impl Ord Int`),
-- so they must not pair with a prelude `impl Ord Int`.  At most ONE error is
-- emitted (mirrors the oracle raising Type_error on the first conflict).

-- `insertIfaceRequired`, the retired writer of `universeIfaceRequiredRef` (see that
-- cell's tombstone in `CrossRun`), is superseded by `classEnvRowsOf`, which mints the
-- SAME key from the SAME `ifaceOrigin` and stores the SAME `requiredMethodNames
-- methods` value on `CeRow`; its DAttrib-unwrapping is preserved by
-- `classEnvDeclFact`, which unwraps `DAttrib` on the way into `CE`.

-- ── #1111 Stage A-2 unit A-2.11 (#1319 unit 1): the CONSTRUCTOR-namespace peer ──
--
-- Everything from here to `ctorExportsInto` (in `tc_decls`) is the constructor twin of the METHOD
-- method machinery (`MethodEnv` … `exportsIdentFor`).  It is a
-- deliberate near-copy and it is NAMED SCAFFOLDING, not an oversight:
--
--   * the METHOD index is already, by its own admission, a THIRD implementation of a
--     fact `resolve.mdk` derives, with **#1288** named as the absorbing stage that
--     deletes it.  This makes a FOURTH.  #1288's scope names the constructor peer, so
--     the deleter is real rather than asserted;
--   * B2 (resolve owns the fact and typecheck consumes it) is the target-conformant
--     answer under L2's identity-at-resolve law, and was rejected for now for a stated
--     reason: resolve's only delivery channel to typecheck is the AST, so B2 implies a
--     decl-adjacent carrier whose cost across printer / sexp / fmt / lower / mangle was
--     never costed.  See #1319's adjudication comment.
--
-- Mirror discipline is live between the two families until #1288 lands: a fix to the
-- scope ladder, the witness, or the export index on ONE side is silently absent from the
-- other.  They are kept together on purpose (this file and `tc_decls`).
--
-- ── What this fixes, and what it deliberately does not ──────────────────────
-- The ctor floor (`universeCtorPop`'s `spFloor`) is keyed by the BARE constructor name and is
-- last-write-wins, so when two unrelated modules each declare a `Dup` the whole loaded
-- program has ONE entry for `Dup` and load order picks the winner.  #1111 A-2.6 attacked
-- that with an import-scoped DECL overlay (`importedCtorTypeDecls`), and the boundary
-- moved one hop out twice: a member reached through a RE-EXPORT is not identity-matched
-- (#1283), and a ride-along SIBLING constructor of an overlaid decl out-ranks a ctor the
-- unit imports BY NAME from an earlier clause (#1284).
--
-- This unit keeps the overlay (unit 4 retires it) and adds the A-2.5-shaped answer on
-- top: an identity-keyed COMPANION of the bare map, plus a per-module SCOPE DECISION for
-- the colliding names only.  The overlay's unit is the DECL; a scope decision's unit is
-- the NAME, which is why a ride-along sibling cannot win here.
--
-- It must not re-decide ambiguity.  Constructor-name ambiguity is provenance-based
-- (`ctorAmbiguousSet` → `keepAmbiguous` in `frontend/resolve.mdk`: ≥2 distinct
-- declarations in scope), and that rule is deliberately ONE rule across the value / type /
-- interface / ctor namespaces — resolve's own note says a change there must change all
-- four at once.  This is a different question on the same data: "which declaration does
-- this module mean" is asked only where resolve has already ACCEPTED the program.  Two
-- witnesses decline to the floor here rather than picking one, so a genuinely ambiguous
-- import is still resolve's to reject and nothing here can quietly accept it.
--
-- MODULE-PATH-ONLY, exactly as A-2.5: a flat/single-file declaration carries
-- `OriginUnresolved`, `mkIdent` refuses it, no candidate is recorded, and the override
-- site is on the `Module` arm alone.
--
-- No prelude rung, unlike the method ladder's `methodUndecided` — and that asymmetry is measured, not
-- assumed.  A module that declares OR imports a constructor spelled like a prelude
-- constructor is rejected outright (`Duplicate constructor: Lt`), so `core` can never be
-- one of two candidates for one ctor name and there is no rung for it to be.

-- The two tables `registerRecordInfoKeyed` writes in lockstep — `recordByNameRef`
-- (registry key → RecordInfo) and `fieldOwnersRef` (field name → owning registry keys) —
-- are the constructor namespace's other half; a record literal / record pattern /
-- `.field` access never reads the constructor scheme env, so
-- `applyCtorScopeOverrides` cannot reach them. Only `recordByNameRef` is fixed here: an
-- identity-keyed COMPANION plus a per-module SCOPE DECISION for the collided keys.
-- `fieldOwnersRef` stays a graph-global multimap, open on #1070 / #1319 — its value (the
-- candidate owner set) needs a REACHABILITY predicate (can a value of this record type
-- be obtained here at all, including transitively), not a constructor-witness test; a
-- prior attempt at the witness-predicate shape was reverted for producing accept→reject
-- regressions on legitimate value-only imports, a quadratic memory cost, and
-- non-monotonic ambiguity decisions — do not re-attempt that shape here.
--
-- The witness half is deliberately NOT re-implemented here: `declaresCtorNamed` and
-- `anyUsePathBindsCtor` are SHARED with unit 1, because a record's name IS its
-- constructor and a named-field variant is keyed by its constructor; only the candidate
-- ROW type differs (RecordInfo instead of Scheme), so only the two projections below are
-- new. It must not re-decide ambiguity, for the same reason as unit 1: two witnesses
-- decline to the floor rather than picking one, so a genuinely ambiguous import stays
-- resolve's to reject. Scaffolding with a named deleter, same as unit 1: this is a
-- further implementation of a fact `resolve.mdk` derives; #1288 owns the consolidation.
--
-- A DECL-level scope decision is the right instrument and no node carrier is needed:
-- neither an interface-default specialization's copied body nor another module's impl
-- body re-resolves a record occurrence in the importer's own record scope, so the
-- occurrence is decided once, at the declaration, not per node. This is falsifiable by a
-- future change: a driver that hands `desugar` a concatenation of modules would reopen
-- it.

-- ── WS-5: phantom interface method detection ──────────────────────────────
-- A method whose declared type mentions NONE of the interface's type parameters
-- (neither in argument position nor in the result) cannot be dispatched: there
-- is no argument or return type to select an impl from.  Reject at check time
-- so the silent mis-dispatch at runtime is caught early.
--
-- Examples:
--   interface Phantom a where
--     name : String          -- PHANTOM: `a` appears nowhere in the type
--   interface Show a where
--     show : a -> String     -- OK: `a` appears in argument position
--   interface Pure f where
--     pure : a -> f a        -- OK: `f` appears in result position
--
-- The check uses the full method type (args AND result via tyVarNames), so
-- return-position methods like `pure : a -> f a` or `minBound : a` are NOT
-- flagged.  Multi-param interfaces: phantom iff ALL params absent.

-- ── WS-4a: cyclic superinterface detection ────────────────────────────────
-- A cyclic `interface A requires B; interface B requires A` is ill-formed: no
-- impl can ever be constructed (each demands the other) and the transitive
-- evidence resolver would diverge.  DFS over the `requires` graph; if any node
-- is re-encountered on the current DFS stack, report the cycle.
-- Runs over ALL interfaces in scope (prelude + user) — a prelude cycle would
-- be equally ill-formed.  The `done` ref tracks fully-explored nodes to avoid
-- redundant traversal and re-reporting multi-root cycles.
-- #1557 A-3.5c: relocated onto stage K's `CE`, AND RE-KEYED — this is the
-- unit's #1258-class acceptance delta, the one the owner ruling of 2026-08-12
-- accepts by name (*"re-keying a bare-name table does not create wrongness, it
-- creates VISIBILITY"*).
--
-- WAS: `collectIfaceNames allDecls` for the roots and `ifaceSupersOf allDecls
-- <name>` for every edge — BOTH bare-name scans of a decl list, so a superinterface
-- edge resolved to whichever same-spelled interface appeared FIRST in
-- the concatenation of every module's decls.  Two unrelated modules each declaring `Same` collapsed
-- into one node: `mod_a.Same requires mod_b.Same` could be reported as a self-cycle it
-- is not, and a genuine cycle could be masked by the other module's row winning the
-- scan.  Import order selected the symptom, exactly as it did for `#1257`'s kind rows.
--
-- NOW: nodes are `CeRow`s and an edge is followed by IDENTITY —
-- `ceLookupAt (regKeyOfTab (ifaceTabKey superOrigin superHead))`.  `Super` has carried
-- `superOrigin` since #1110 Stage A-1 and `resolve.mdk` stamps it through the SAME
-- `mapSuperOcc`/`tyOriginScope` walk that stamps a `DImpl`'s `implOrigin`, so the
-- occurrence and the `DInterface`'s `ifaceOrigin` declaration layer meet for the same
-- reason `ifaceTabKey`'s header argues for the impl-head key.  The `done` set and the
-- DFS `path` are keyed on `regKeyRender` rather than the bare name for the identical
-- reason.
--
-- The miss arm is an abstention, and it is the one place this could go
-- LOUD→SILENT.  An edge whose target is not in `CE` at this ordinal yields `None` and
-- the walk stops — the same disposition `ifaceSupersOf`'s `None` arm already had
-- ("unknown interface, reported elsewhere"), and resolve rejects an interface naming
-- an unknown superinterface before typecheck runs.  What is NEW is that a super whose
-- identity failed to resolve would now MISS where a bare name used to hit.  That is
-- why the abstention arm needs a POSITIVE control -- a shape whose edge MUST still be
-- followed -- rather than only a negative one.
--
-- Cite fixtures that exist, and check what actually consumes them.  The gate
-- for this arm is `test/diff_compiler_fmt_test.mdk`'s `typecheckErrorFailures`,
-- which walks its fixture directory RECURSIVELY via `fixtureFiles` -- so a
-- directory-nested fixture IS discovered and consumed.  No gate catches a wrong
-- citation here: `make docs-links` and `make agent-doc-symbols` do not scan
-- compiler comments.  It matters more than a typo because this is the one
-- sentence answering "is the abstention arm covered?", and a citation like
-- that gets relayed into the next unit's design as an established fact.  The
-- real controls, both verified to reject on this binary:
--   * MULTI-MODULE: `test/analyze_project_fixtures/1557_a35c_cycle_masked_by_samespelled/`
--     (consumed by `test/diff_compiler_fmt_test.mdk`'s `analyzeProjectFixtureFailures`)
--     -- its edge must resolve BY IDENTITY for the fixture to pass at all, so
--     an abstention that swallowed a real edge reds it.
--   * FLAT: `test/dict_fixtures/s3-w1-cyclic-superinterface-rejected.mdk` (no imports)
--     and `test/typecheck_error_fixtures/cyclic_superinterface.mdk`.
-- The negative direction -- an edge that must NOT be followed -- is
-- `1557_a35c_samespelled_iface_bystander/`.  The set is the assertion; no member is.
--
-- ROOTS are `ceRowsVisibleAt` — the reading module's whole ordinal prefix: {prelude} ∪
-- {every earlier module in full} ∪ {this module}, members and order.  `accAll` is
-- seeded with the WHOLE `coreDecls` and grown `accAll ++ prog` (not `publicDataDecls
-- prog`; only `accData` is public-filtered) and every Module-arm driver runs
-- `AccumulateBoth`, so the prefix is the full set.  `CE` at this ordinal is that set in
-- ordinal order.
--
-- An earlier cut of this comment claimed a deliberate widening here — *"`accAll`/
-- `accData` carry only `publicDataDecls`, so a PRIVATE interface of an earlier module
-- was never a root"* — and it was simply WRONG about `accAll`, so the widening it
-- conceded does not exist.  Measured on two binaries rather than argued: a module
-- declaring a PRIVATE cyclic interface pair has that cycle reported in a sibling
-- module with NO import path to it, on the pre-relocation arm too, byte-identical.
-- Do not reinstate the hedge; the relocation is root-set-preserving.
checkInterfaceCycles : ModuleCtx -> List Decl -> ClassEnv -> Int -> Unit
checkInterfaceCycles mctx prog ce cur =
  -- #973: `done` is an OrdMap-backed SET (omHasKey/omInsert), NOT a growing `List` scanned
  -- by `contains`.  The membership test ran once per interface over the fully-explored
  -- set, which grows to the interface count → O(interfaces^2), the flat-path
  -- `manyifaces:typecheck` op-count quadratic (independent of use sites — it surfaces on a
  -- file of N cycle-free interfaces).  Keyed into a set it is O(log n): `done` only prunes
  -- already-explored nodes (set membership is order-insensitive), and the DFS order +
  -- cycle messages are driven by `path`/the root list.  A-3.5c keeps the shape and only
  -- changes the KEY from a bare name to `regKeyRender`.
  let done = Ref omEmpty
  each (checkOneIfaceCycle mctx prog ce cur done) (ceRowsVisibleAt cur ce)

-- #152: the whole-program final-checks tail, shared by all five entry points
-- (the `checkOne*` projections and `checkModuleFullDiags`). [cohDecls] feeds coherence and
-- checkSuperImpls's impl-iteration argument.
-- trap #5: coherence must see USER decls only, so
-- this helper has NO access to `prog` — the caller decides [cohDecls] explicitly
-- (never a `prog` fallback).  Call ORDER is load-bearing (all push into typeErrors).
--
-- #1557 A-3.5c: two of the four members now read stage K's `CE` instead of a decl
-- list.  [ownCe]/[cycCe] are the class environments the caller is entitled to read
-- and [cur] is the READING module's ordinal — Module passes the whole-graph
-- `deIfaces` twice with its own ordinal; Flat passes two single-module envs built
-- from the very lists it used to hand `checkPhantomMethods`/`checkInterfaceCycles`,
-- at ordinal 0 in the standalone-list compatibility helper; reading a graph envelope there
-- would switch both checks silently OFF.
--
-- Call order is unchanged and that is deliberate.  A-3.5c moves two members'
-- SOURCE, not their position: coherence still runs first, then cycles, then phantom,
-- then super-impls, so no diagnostic changes rank against another family.  Whichever
-- unit lands A-3.5a/A-3.5b inherits this constraint — pulling a member OUT of this
-- sequence re-orders every accumulated diagnostic after it.
-- #1557 A-3.5b: the super-impl check reads stage K, taking [cycCe] and [cycIe] at the
-- same ordinal.  Both envs must be built over the same population: the whole-graph
-- `deImpls` with the reader's own ordinal on the Module arm, `flatImplEnvOf` of the
-- one list the Flat callers hand `cycCe`'s peer.
-- #1559 A-3.7: coherence reads stage K too, so this helper carries TWO `IE`s for
-- the same reason A-3.5c gave it two `CE`s — [cohIe] is the env over the population
-- [cohDecls] names (the USER boundary, trap #5) and [cycIe] is built over the
-- whole-graph population.  They are the same env only on the paths where those two
-- populations were already the same.  [cohIe] must contain the prelude at
-- ordinal 0, since coherence pairs the module's own rows with the prelude's.
--
-- SA-6 (sprint repair round): the `-1` sentinel is fail-closed here, and it has to be
-- Announced at this reader.  [cur] is `declEnvsOrdOf mid` on the Module arm, whose miss
-- value is `0 - 1` (see that function: a miss is documented fail-CLOSED, and it says in
-- so many words that the remedy for a stray id is at the READER — "abstain loudly / fail
-- the pass" — never a sentinel that means "everything").  All four members below take
-- [cur], and at `-1` they abstain in OPPOSITE directions: `checkCoherence` takes
-- `cohRowsOwnedBy -1` → no rows → **it checks nothing, silently**, where pre-A-3.7 it
-- walked the decl list unconditionally; `checkInterfaceCycles`/`checkPhantomMethods` take
-- `ceRowsVisibleAt -1`/`ceRowsOwnedBy -1` → likewise nothing; and `checkSuperImpls`,
-- whose row read SA-3 just widened to `ieCandidacyVisibleAt`, still finds no SUPERS
-- (`ceLookupAt … -1`) and so also reports nothing.  So the whole tail degrades to a
-- SILENT ACCEPT — a loud→silent transition, which this repo ranks as a severity increase
-- even while unreachable.
-- Unreachable today, and the guard is still right.  The three drivers pass either a
-- literal `0` (both Flat entries) or `declEnvsOrdOf mid` over the very list
-- `buildDeclEnvs` indexed, so no live path can arrive at `-1`; a fourth Module-mode
-- driver, or any path stamping a mid `buildDeclEnvs` did not index, makes it live.  It
-- cannot fire spuriously, because `cur < 0` is exactly and only the miss sentinel and no
-- caller can construct a negative ordinal otherwise.
-- 🎯 What this does *not* cover, stated because it is the near miss: a driver that
-- threads a VALID-but-WRONG ordinal (some other module's) is undetectable here — the
-- sentinel is the only self-identifying failure — and `globalCoherenceConflict` takes no
-- ordinal at all (`cohRowsOf`, whole graph), so it is outside this guard entirely.
runFinalChecks : ModuleCtx ->
  List Decl ->
  ClassEnv ->
  ClassEnv ->
  ImplEnv ->
  ImplEnv ->
  Int ->
  Unit
runFinalChecks mctx cohDecls ownCe cycCe cohIe cycIe cur =
  ordinalSentinelGuard mctx cur
  checkCoherence mctx cohIe cur
  checkInterfaceCycles mctx cohDecls cycCe cur
  checkPhantomMethods mctx ownCe cur
  checkSuperImpls mctx cohDecls cycCe cycIe cur

-- SA-6's guard, split out so the arm is a GUARD and not a `whenL`.  Medaka is strict:
-- `whenL (cur < 0) (pushTypeError …)` would evaluate the push on EVERY call — the
-- documented not-a-stub trap.  A guarded function does not.
ordinalSentinelGuard : ModuleCtx -> Int -> Unit
ordinalSentinelGuard mctx cur
  | ordinalIsSentinel cur =
    pushTypeError mctx "T-INTERNAL-ORDINAL" ordinalSentinelMsg
  | otherwise = ()

-- is [cur] `declEnvsOrdOf`'s miss sentinel?  A real ordinal is an index into the list
-- `buildDeclEnvs` was handed, so it is >= 0 by construction; `0 - 1` is the only negative
-- value any producer in this file yields.
-- > ordinalIsSentinel (0 - 1)
-- True
-- > ordinalIsSentinel 0
-- False
-- > ordinalIsSentinel 3
-- False
ordinalIsSentinel : Int -> Bool
ordinalIsSentinel cur = cur < 0

checkOneIfaceCycle : ModuleCtx ->
  List Decl ->
  ClassEnv ->
  Int ->
  Ref (OrdMap Unit) ->
  CeRow ->
  Unit
checkOneIfaceCycle mctx prog ce cur done row =
  if not (omHasKey (ceRowDoneKey row) !done) then
    each
      (pushTypeError
        (mctxAt
          (cycleReportLoc mctx.mcGraph.declEnvsRef.value.deReach prog cur row)
          mctx)
        "T-CYCLIC-SUPERINTERFACE")
      (ifaceDfsCycle ce cur done [row] row)

-- A cycle is reported at the name of the interface its walk started from when
-- the reading module [cur] declares it.  A module that only sees it reports it
-- at its import of the declaring module, or else at its first import of a
-- module that reaches the declaring one ([reach], each module's import
-- closure): the interface's span is in another file.  A module with no import
-- path to it reports it with no span.
cycleReportLoc : OrdMap (OrdMap Unit) -> List Decl -> Int -> CeRow -> Option Loc
cycleReportLoc reach prog cur row
  | ceRowOrd row == cur = ceRowLoc row
  | otherwise = match (ceRowIface row).irOrigin
    OriginModule owner => importLocFor reach prog owner
    _ => None

-- DFS from [current]; [path] is the stack of ROWS visited so far (including
-- [current] as its last element, used to reconstruct the cycle message).
-- Marks [current] as done after all its supers are explored.
ifaceDfsCycle : ClassEnv ->
  Int ->
  Ref (OrdMap Unit) ->
  List CeRow ->
  CeRow ->
  List String
ifaceDfsCycle ce cur done path current =
  let msgs = ifaceDfsCycleSupers ce cur done path (ceRowSupers current)
  done := omInsert (ceRowDoneKey current) () !done
  msgs

ifaceDfsCycleSupers : ClassEnv ->
  Int ->
  Ref (OrdMap Unit) ->
  List CeRow ->
  List Super ->
  List String
ifaceDfsCycleSupers _ _ _ _ [] = []
ifaceDfsCycleSupers ce cur done path ((Super { superHead = superName, superOrigin = so }) :: rest) =
  let msgs1 = ifaceDfsCycleOneSuper ce cur done path so superName
  msgs1 ++ ifaceDfsCycleSupers ce cur done path rest

-- check one super edge: cycle if the super's IDENTITY is on the DFS path; skip if
-- done; else recurse into the row that identity names.  A super naming no visible
-- interface abstains (see the miss-arm note at `checkInterfaceCycles`).
ifaceDfsCycleOneSuper : ClassEnv ->
  Int ->
  Ref (OrdMap Unit) ->
  List CeRow ->
  TyConOrigin ->
  String ->
  List String
ifaceDfsCycleOneSuper ce cur done path so superName =
  let key = regKeyOfTab (ifaceTabKey so superName)
  let rendered = regKeyRender key
  if containsRowKey rendered path then
    [cyclicSuperIfaceMsg (map ceRowIfaceName path ++ [superName])]
  else if omHasKey rendered !done then
    []
  else match ceLookupAt key cur ce
    None => []
    Some r => ifaceDfsCycle ce cur done (path ++ [r]) r

containsRowKey : String -> List CeRow -> Bool
containsRowKey _ [] = False
containsRowKey k (r :: rest)
  | ceRowDoneKey r == k = True
  | otherwise = containsRowKey k rest

-- This recursion is aware (constraint-dictionary-routed) and bounded by the shared
-- `requires` measure (`reqWalkEnter`): a mono pinned to a concrete head routes RKey,
-- an in-scope constraint var routes RDict, per DICT-SEMANTICS §3 `assum`.

-- #80: the single per-module typecheck body, reached from the graph driver through
-- `checkModuleFullImpl`. DICT-SEMANTICS §7's single-evaluator law means one
-- elaboration decision serves check, run, and emit. `mid` and `implDecls` carry the
-- current module's identity and graph-visible impl universe; single-file callers use
-- the same function through the one-module graph projections.
--
-- The module's `ModuleCtx` is minted here, before anything else runs, and returned
-- beside the bindings: the driver continues from it.  [gctx] is the drive's graph
-- record, which the driver passes (the previous record's `mcGraph`).  [runSeed] is its
-- match oracle and warnings (`seedCheckRun`); a driver that does not seed
-- (`cmCheckWorker`, the core passes) passes the previous record's (`checkRunResidue`).
checkBodyImpl : GraphCtx ->
  CheckRunSeed ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  String ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List Decl, ModuleCtx)
-- multi-line RHS: the indented `:=` form is legal but not seed-parseable yet (#1744)
-- lint-disable-next-line rule-prefer-assign-op
checkBodyImpl gctx runSeed moduleSeed mid implDecls prog0 =
  let seed = moduleSeed.msSchemes
  let mctx = freshModuleCtx gctx runSeed mid
  let moduleScope =
    Scopes.freshScope
      (currentScopeStore gctx ())
      None
      mctx.mcCells.currentLevel.value
      mid
      ModuleOwner
  openScope mctx moduleScope
  recordModuleStart gctx mid
  -- #1675: this module's own `import`/`export import` decls, checked for a name the
  -- named dependency exports under two different definitions.  Here rather than in
  -- `importSeed` because the seed is built BEFORE this `freshModuleCtx` — see
  -- `reportAmbiguousImports`.  Reads only `deAmbigValues`, so a driver with no graph
  -- envelope (Flat, single-file) is a no-op.
  reportAmbiguousImports mctx prog0
  let prog = prog0
  let aliasSpellings = aliasMethodSpellings gctx prog
  -- P1 (#1446) / DICT §8 I7: the four operator-and-literal classes' identities, read
  -- off the PRELUDE's own `DInterface` decls before any body is inferred (the operator
  -- handlers consult them during inference).  See `seedBuiltinClasses`.
  seedBuiltinClasses gctx mid prog
  -- #837: stamp binding ids on the program the bodies are INFERRED from.  `prog`
  -- itself stays unstamped for the pre-inference analyses (shadows/key table/…),
  -- which do not need ids; only the inferred bodies (defs + impl/default/prop/test)
  -- carry EVarId, and schemeDefIdsRef feeds registerSchemeObligations the top-level
  -- members' ids.  Flat: a single walk (top-level frame built over the whole `prog`,
  -- so a user body referencing a prelude fn gets the prelude fn's id), then split
  -- back into core/user slices for the two-phase Flat inference.  Module: the
  -- scope alone is minted here; each binding group is MARKED and then stamped as the
  -- schedule reaches it (`processSCC`), and the tail bodies after the groups, so
  -- `progS` is the module's UNMARKED, unstamped tree and `progT` below its inferred
  -- twin.  The Flat callers marked their whole program before this call.
  let stampRes =
    let (top, ids) = stampTopScope prog
    beginModuleMarking mctx mid prog top moduleSeed
    (prog, ids)
  let progS = fst stampRes
  -- #907: index name→id as an OrdMap (was a List scanned linearly per rendered
  -- scheme in ownContext / per SCC member in registerSchemeObligations → O(decls²)).
  -- Built from the REVERSED pairs so omFromPairs' last-write-wins reproduces the old
  -- `lookupAssoc` first-match-wins on a duplicated binder name (same pattern as sigTyMapRef).
  mctx.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef := omFromPairs (reverseL (snd stampRes)) omEmpty
  -- the full visible universe (shadows / key table /
  -- completeness / obligations): Flat = whole flattened program; Module = imported
  -- data + impl universe + this module.
  -- #154 PR-A: every consumer of `fullUniverse` is a `Flat _ =>` arm (grep confirms:
  -- buildDefinerShadows
  -- / checkImplObligations / checkCallObligations — all Flat).  #1557 A-3.5a removed
  -- `checkImplCompleteness` from that list: impl-completeness no longer takes a decl
  -- list, it reads `classEnvHere`,
  -- which IS this binding on that arm).  Medaka is STRICT, so the
  -- Module arm's `accData ++ implDecls ++ prog0` allocated an O(|accData|) list every module
  -- and discarded it unread — a dead per-module O(N) concat.  Bind `[]` on Module.
  -- registerAllData universe (#154 PR-A): the Module path no longer builds `accData ++ prog0`
  -- here — prior modules' public data lives in the persistent DATA-universe accumulators
  -- (universeCtorPop/universeRecordPop), loaded+overlaid at the `dataEnv` binding below.
  -- (#6, #1457) superDeclsRef: unified body OWNS it — Flat = coreProg ++ userProg, Module =
  -- `prog ++ implDecls`.  [implDecls] is core + every EARLIER module ONLY (foldModules never
  -- folds a module into its own universe), so binding it alone EXCLUDED this module's own
  -- `interface F t requires L t` decl from ifaceSelfAndSupers/survivorObligationEntailed and
  -- false-rejected every legal superclass-default body (#1457).  Folding `prog` in matches the
  -- graph path, whose universe includes this module itself.
  -- Operand order is load-bearing, BOTH ways:
  --   * cost — Medaka is strict, so `xs ++ ys` copies xs and shares ys.  [implDecls] is the
  --     growing accumulator, so it MUST be the right operand: `prog ++ implDecls` is O(|prog|)
  --     per module = O(N) overall, whereas `implDecls ++ prog` would resurrect #154 PR-C's
  --     O(modules²) per-module concat.
  --   * meaning — ifaceSupersOf/ifaceSelfAndSupers is a FIRST-MATCH scan, so `prog` first makes
  --     THIS module's own same-spelled interface decl outrank an earlier module's, which is the
  --     precedence the old single-program path had.
  let superDecls = prog ++ implDecls
  mctx.mcCells.superDeclsRef := superDecls
  -- (BREAK #3) effect domains: FLAT populates inline; MODULE relies on the driver
  -- preamble (checkModulesPreambleK / elaborateModules) populating ONCE over the whole
  -- import graph — a per-module populate would wipe another module's domains.
  checkEffectParams mctx prog
  checkLetRecDecls mctx prog
  -- #154 PR1: on the Module path, grow the persistent universe accumulators by THIS
  -- module's decls BEFORE the shadow/key reads below consult them — so they see the
  -- same universe `fullUniverse` (which appended prog0) did.
  -- untouched and rebuilds over its whole `prog` (already O(N)).
  -- #1112 A-3.4 PR2: `ieShadowCompare mid prog0` stood here, and the hard `panic`
  -- surface it put in the released compiler is GONE with it — that instrument had
  -- exactly one job, measuring the §9.5 equality before the flip committed to it,
  -- and the flip has now been made on a strictly stronger measurement (see
  -- `moduleImplUniv` just below for what was measured and how it was shown
  -- fail-capable).  `appendUniverseAccums` no longer grows an impl universe either,
  -- so there is nothing left here for `IE` to be compared against.
  -- #1499 clause (b) / S1-PRELUDE (a): `mid` is BOUND here now — `appendUniverseAccums`
  -- must know whether this pass is the PRELUDE's, because the prelude contributes to
  -- S1's INTERFACE operand but NOT to its STANDALONE one.  See its header.
  appendUniverseAccums gctx mid prog0
  -- #1112 A-3.4 PR2 — THE `IE` READER FLIP.  A production read of stage K's `IE`
  -- (A-3.5b added a second, `ieRowsVisibleAt`; do NOT re-instate "the ONE place" here).
  -- It replaced the three `obUniv*` `CrossRun` accumulators, which are GONE rather than
  -- merely unread — two sources of truth for "what impls exist" is what design law L1
  -- removes.  The WAS/NOW migration narrative, and the whole-prefix equality MEASUREMENT
  -- that licensed deleting the `ieShadowCompare` instrument, live in
  -- `compiler/TYPECHECK-TARGET-ARCHITECTURE.md` §9.5a; what stays here are the facts a
  -- reader of this line needs.
  --
  -- Since A-3.6 (#1558) this is not filtered to this module's ordinal prefix.
  -- `ieSnapAt` routes through `ieCandidacyVisibleAt`, which is `True`, so this binding is
  -- the WHOLE GRAPH's impl universe regardless of `mid`'s ordinal.  This line is where
  -- A-3's C4/I2 claim is cashed: every obligation check on the multi-module path knows the
  -- same impl population in every module — *"import scoping never decides which instances
  -- exist"* (`docs/spec/DICT-SEMANTICS.md` §8 I5).  It WIDENS #1438's same-spelled-
  -- interface reach (#1482 measured that topological prefix scoping was accidentally
  -- load-bearing for it); expected and licensed, but an acceptance delta.  The pin that
  -- used to grade that delta, `test/must_fail_fixtures/1072-overlap-xmod-bare-head-arm-
  -- order`, is gone from the tree (derive: `ls test/must_fail_fixtures | grep 1072`) — see
  -- §9.5a; do not read its absence as "already drained".
  --
  -- `declEnvsOrdOf mid` is still passed and is now inert on this path.  It is
  -- kept so the call site does not move and so re-scoping candidacy later is a
  -- one-line change; do not "simplify" it away, and do not read its presence as
  -- evidence a filter is still applied.  Its `-1` miss sentinel is likewise vacuous
  -- here — an unknown module id now gains the whole graph rather than losing
  -- everything, the opposite of the fail-CLOSED story `declEnvsOrdOf` documents for
  -- the NAME axis.
  --
  -- Bound once, read twice, and the "once" is load-bearing.  There are TWO Module-arm
  -- consumers — `residualUnivRef` (#1549) just below, and the end-of-body obligation
  -- `obUniv` — and A-3.4's own design was written when there was only ONE.  Do not
  -- re-derive the projection at the second site: it is a whole-prefix rebuild, so a
  -- second call doubles this unit's already-measured per-module cost for a value that
  -- cannot have changed (both `ord` and `IE` are immutable for the whole module; the
  -- accumulator these replaced was likewise grown once, above, and read at both sites).
  -- A third consumer should take this binding too, or say in writing why it needs a
  -- different ordinal.
  let moduleImplUniv =
    let envs = gctx.declEnvsRef.value
    ieUniverseAt (declEnvsOrdOf mid envs) envs.deImpls
  -- #1549: stash the impl universe the RESIDUAL REDUCER entails against
  -- (`residualPredsOf`, called from each generalizing group's close).  This is NOT a
  -- third source: it is the SAME two arm-gated sources the end-of-body obligation
  -- checkers consume — `buildImplUniverse prog` on Flat, and since A-3.4 PR2 the `IE`
  -- projection on Module (which carries this module's own impls, its ordinal being
  -- included by `declEnvVisibleAt`, exactly as the retired accumulator did after the
  -- `appendUniverseAccums` above).  Built ONCE per module rather than per group:
  -- rebuilding it inside the reducer would be O(decls) per SCC, i.e. quadratic.
  setRef mctx.mcCells.residualUnivRef moduleImplUniv
  -- (#4) shadows.  definerShadows uses the SHARED form — buildDefinerShadows over the
  -- full visible universe, scoped to THIS module's decls (prog0).  On Flat, prog0 =
  -- userProg and fullUniverse = prog, so this is exactly the old flat `buildDefinerShadows
  -- prog userProg`.  The method-name test, `isMethod`, reads `deMethods` at this module's
  -- ordinal: every interface method a module at or before it declares, whatever its
  -- export.  importerShadows exist only across a module boundary; the `Module _ _ []` arm
  -- (cmCheckWorker / core pass, implDecls = []) keeps the old empty result — `funDefs []
  -- = []` gave no imported fns there.
  -- #1354 unit A (#1353): that test is not scoped to what this module can name, so the
  -- intersection alone implements only S1's LEFT operand.  `nameableIfaceShadows` supplies
  -- the RIGHT one — the interface must be nameable in THIS module.  Applied to the RESULT,
  -- never inside either filter (the result is single-digit; `fnSet` is every fn name in
  -- the graph), and fail-open when the graph index is absent.  See `nameableIfaceShadows`.
  -- The Flat arms are untouched: a single file is one module and its own interfaces are
  -- nameable in it.
  let isMethod =
    let envs = gctx.declEnvsRef.value
    methodNameDeclaredAt (declEnvsOrdOf mid envs) envs.deMethods
  let definerShadows =
    nameableIfaceShadows gctx prog0 (definerShadowsFromSet gctx isMethod prog0)
  let importerShadows =
    if isEmptyL implDecls then
      []
    else
      nameableIfaceShadows
        gctx
        prog0
        (standaloneShadowsFromSet
          gctx
          isMethod
          (omMapValues (_ => ()) moduleSeed.msImportedValues)
          prog0)
  let sigs = sigsOf prog
  mctx.mcCells.definerShadowNamesRef := namesToSet definerShadows omEmpty
  mctx.mcCells.definerShadowSigsRef := omFromPairs (reverseL sigs) omEmpty
  -- #154 PR1 seeded a SECOND impl-existence table here — `shadowKeyTableRef`, from
  -- `buildKeyTable fullUniverse` on Flat and from the cumulative `universeKeyBucketsRef`
  -- on Module.  ARCH B-2.1-g repointed all three of its boolean existence reads onto the
  -- graph-global `bodyImplEnvRef` seeded just below (`ieImplExistsForHead`), and B-2.1-d
  -- deleted both refs.  Do not re-add a prefix-keyed existence table here.  The Module
  -- arm's source was a cumulative TOPOLOGICAL PREFIX while S2's impl universe is
  -- graph-global (`SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md:183-186`, `:226`), so an importer shadow whose
  -- receiver's impl sat in a topologically later module answered "no impl" and called the
  -- standalone — a located reject became exit 0 printing a `Box` pointer fed to `n + 1`,
  -- discriminated by the order of two `import` lines.  One substrate, one decision.
  -- ARCH B-2.1-a2: the SAME impl population, seated on `IE`'s shape, on BOTH arms —
  -- one ref, so the evidence reader B-2.1-b repoints has one substrate rather than an
  -- arm gate.  It landed additive and behaviour-neutral, so the repoints that followed got
  -- a substrate they could trust on a path where `IE` was empty (see `buildFlatImplEnv`
  -- for the measured hazard, RUN-B-017 probe 3).
  -- "nothing reads `bodyImplEnvRef` yet" was true only at a2 and is now FALSE: B-2.1-b2's
  -- three selection legs, and B-2.1-g's route word plus three shadow existence reads, all
  -- read this ref.  Derive the set: `grep -n bodyImplEnvRef compiler/types/typecheck.mdk`.
  -- The Module arm is a pointer copy of the env `buildDeclEnvs` already built; it
  -- deliberately does NOT re-derive, for the reason `moduleImplUniv` gives above.
  mctx.mcCells.bodyImplEnvRef := gctx.declEnvsRef.value.deImpls
  -- (#4 + #3-restore) mode-specific pre-inference ref setup (all pure/independent of the
  -- register+globalS block below, so order-safe).  Module installs importer-shadow state and restores the cross-module dict
  -- accumulators `freshModuleCtx` just wiped.
  -- multi-line RHS: the indented `:=` form is legal but not seed-parseable yet (#1744)
  -- lint-disable-next-line rule-prefer-assign-op

  -- #1425 site 2: `mid` is BOUND here now — the three lockstep re-keys at the end of this
  -- arm need this module's identity, and the arm can no longer discard it.

  mctx.mcCells.standaloneValuesRef :=
    namesToSet (importerShadows ++ definerShadows) omEmpty
  -- #1351: the per-module copy of the identity-keyed dispatch-index universe, read by
  -- `methodDispatchIdx` through `dispatchIdxScoped`.  Its bare-name peer was retired at L5.
  mctx.mcCells.methodDispatchIdxByIdRef :=
    gctx.crossRun.universeMethodDispatchIdxByIdRef.value
  -- The list alone is seeded here and re-keyed twice below; nothing reads the
  -- name index until the final `setFunPredicateSlots` of this arm rebuilds it.
  mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef :=
    gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsRef.value
  -- B-2.2-f (#1113): the declared-prefix sidecar rides the IDS table — every
  -- whole-table replacement of `funConstraintsRef` owes one of these.
  mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef :=
    gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredRef.value
  -- A module alias (`import m as A`) has no `currentImportDefinersRef` row for
  -- each dotted member, so its call sites resolve through these alias-local rows.
  -- The source is nevertheless exact: `aliasConstraintEntries` selects only the
  -- `(defining module, exported name)` row admitted by that alias. Preserve the
  -- complete predicate vectors here; stripping them reduces distinct predicates
  -- such as `Tweak a Int` and `Tweak a Bool` to the ambiguous goal `Tweak a`.
  -- The graph-wide bare-name snapshot below remains vector-free because it lacks
  -- this source identity.
  let _ =
    setRef
      mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef
      (aliasConstraintEntries
          gctx
          prog
          gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsQualRef.value
        ++ mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef.value)
  let _ =
    setRef
      mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef
      (aliasConstraintEntries
          gctx
          prog
          gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredQualRef.value
        ++ mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef.value)
  -- #1425 site 2: the three tables above were just seeded WHOLESALE from graph-wide
  -- BARE-NAME accumulators, so every name this module declares is currently answered by
  -- whichever module's same-named binding the topological order happened to put first.
  -- Re-key those names — and only those — onto this module's own `(mid, name)` rows.
  -- Three writes, in lockstep, per B-2.2-f (#1113): the declared-prefix sidecar rides the
  -- IDS table, and this is a whole-table replacement of all three.
  let _ =
    setFunPredicateSlots
      mctx
      (selfDeclArities
        mid
        prog
        gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsQualRef.value
        mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef.value)
  let _ =
    setRef
      mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef
      (selfDeclArities
        mid
        prog
        gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredQualRef.value
        mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef.value)
  -- RUN-XMOD-062: ONE derivation, two projections.  The two refs are read together
  -- (`qualConstraintKey` assembles a `(definer, origin)` key out of both), so they
  -- must not be derived by two independent walks that could admit different rows.
  let admitted = importDefinersOf gctx prog
  mctx.mcCells.currentImportDefinersRef :=
    omFromPairs (reverseL (definerRowsOf admitted)) omEmpty
  mctx.mcCells.currentImportOriginsRef :=
    omFromPairs (reverseL (originRowsOf admitted)) omEmpty
  -- #1114 (#845): this module's imported SCHEME CONTEXTS, keyed by local spelling.
  -- `reverseL` before `omFromPairs` so the FIRST entry wins a duplicate key, matching
  -- the first-match policy of the sibling tables set just above
  -- (`omFromPairs` is last-write-wins; see its own note in support/ordmap.mdk).
  let _ =
    setRef
      mctx.mcCells.importedSchemeOblsRef
      (omFromPairs
        (reverseL
          (importedSchemeOblEntries
            prog
            gctx.crossRun.crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef.value))
        omEmpty)
  ()
  -- #2563: install THIS module's method scope — its decls and ordinal, the inputs
  -- `methodScopeHere` answers from on first read of each spelling (stage K's
  -- `deMethods` is the whole graph; nothing is copied per module).

  mctx.mcCells.methodScopeCtxRef :=
    Some (prog, declEnvsOrdOf mid gctx.declEnvsRef.value)
  -- #1386: the alias-qualified spellings (`A.mth`) this module's imports supply.
  -- Built eagerly, here, because a key that names two declarations is reported
  -- (`T-AMBIGUOUS-ALIAS-METHOD`) whether or not the module ever spells it.
  mctx.mcCells.methodAliasEntriesRef :=
    aliasQualifiedMethodEntries mctx prog omEmpty
  -- #1386: the ORIGIN name behind each of those alias-qualified keys, from the
  -- same rows, in the same module scope, on the same line as the supply they
  -- accompany — `originMethodName`'s source.
  mctx.mcCells.aliasMethodOriginsRef := omFromPairs aliasSpellings omEmpty
  -- Module arm: copy the seven persistent DATA-universe accumulators (prior modules'
  -- public data + ctor env, each minted exactly once) into the `freshModuleCtx`-wiped
  -- working refs, then register ONLY this module's `prog0` as a transient FRONT
  -- overlay — O(|prog0|) per module, O(N) total. Ordering must stay [prog0,
  -- pub_{k-1}..pub_1] to preserve lookupAssoc (first-wins) / omInsert (last-write-wins)
  -- semantics.
  let dataEnv =
    -- A-3.2b (#1512): `mid` is BOUND here now — the overlay pool below is read at this
    -- module's own ordinal, so the arm can no longer discard it.
    -- A-3.2b residual (#1512): `envs`/`cur` are bound BEFORE the reload, not
    -- after, because the reload's alias half is now a projection of `envs` at
    -- `cur`.  Both bindings are pure reads of `GraphCtx`, so hoisting them
    -- above `loadDataUniverse` changes nothing else.
    let envs = gctx.declEnvsRef.value
    let cur = declEnvsOrdOf mid envs
    loadDataUniverse mctx cur
    -- #674: OVERLAY the ctors of the types THIS module imports so a bare ctor name
    -- resolves to the module the unit imports it from, not the universe's load-order
    -- winner.  Reads the persistent universe DECLS (not the Module `accData` field —
    -- that is dead `[]` on the elaborate/emit path, #154 PR-C), so the overlay fires
    -- on the run/build gate too, not just `check`.
    --
    --     universe  <  imported-ctor overlay  <  this module's OWN `prog0`.
    -- The overlay registers AFTER the universe (that is #674's requirement — it must
    -- win the `omInsert` tie-break over the load-order winner) and BEFORE `prog0`
    -- (this module's own declarations outrank anything it imports — ordinary
    -- shadowing, and the SHADOW spec's S1 per-module scoping).
    --
    -- one: an imported decl's constructors then beat locally-declared ones, so
    -- `import json.{Json, JNull}` + a local `data Tok = JString String` was rejected
    -- `Type mismatch: Json vs Tok` — clobbered by a SIBLING constructor of `Json`
    -- that the program never imported.  It reaches the stdlib and it is not narrow:
    -- every selective constructor-naming import carries it.  Pinned by
    -- `test/engine_fixtures/ctor_scope_local_ctor_beats_stdlib_import.mdk` (the
    -- stdlib `json` shape, on all three engines) and
    -- `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_local_decl_wins/` (`{Ext, Other}` plus
    -- a local decl, prelude-free).
    --
    -- cause was exactly two of them disagreeing, and a FUTURE edit re-creates that by
    -- moving ONE.  This note used to name a PAIR ("both structures agree"), and the
    -- pair it named was the one that cannot disagree — while omitting the one that can.
    --
    -- Registration order here is  universe < overlay < prog0.  Under it:
    --   * the `TcEnv` ctor scheme map (`addCtor` → `omInsert`, BARE ctor name) —
    --     LAST-write-wins → prog0.  BARE-keyed, so two modules genuinely collide here;
    --     this is #1259's table and the one the overlay exists for.
    --   * `recordByNameRef` (`registerRecordInfoKeyed` → `omInsert`, BARE ctor name) —
    --     LAST-write-wins → prog0.  #1256's table, same keying, same hazard.
    --   * `dataParamKindsRef` (`recordParamKinds`, a consed list read FIRST-match) —
    --     IDENTITY-keyed since A-2.3 (`tyTabKey o name`), so two modules' same-named
    --     types get DIFFERENT keys and registration order cannot decide between them
    --     at all.  It is order-observable only for a decl registered twice, where it
    --     agrees.  It is NOT evidence about the bare-keyed tables above.
    --     (`checkModuleFullDiags`: `seedCheckRun (prog ++ importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins prog …
    --     ++ accData)`), from a LIST, with `oracleMap` = `omFromPairs (reverseL …)`,
    --     i.e. FIRST-wins on that list → prog0 > overlay > universe.  Same verdict,
    --     opposite mechanism, and reached through none of the code on this line.
    --     THIS is the one a future edit can silently flip, so check it too.
    -- All four land on prog0 > overlay > universe.  Getting any two to disagree is
    -- #674's root cause.
    -- #1111 A-2.11 (#1319 unit 1): a FIFTH layer, and it is a per-NAME overlay rather
    -- than a fifth registration — `applyCtorScopeOverrides` re-points the (normally
    -- empty) set of COLLIDED constructor names at the declaration THIS module's import
    -- scope selects.  It sits ABOVE prog0 and declines for any name prog0 declares, so
    -- the ladder above is unchanged: own > import scope > import overlay > universe.
    -- A collision-free program returns this very env unchanged and unallocated.
    -- #1111 A-2.12 (#1319 unit 2): the two RECORD-table overlays are the same fifth
    -- layer one namespace-half over, and they must run HERE — after `registerAllData …
    -- prog0` has written the floor into `recordByNameRef` / `fieldOwnersRef` — for the
    -- same reason `applyCtorScopeOverrides` wraps that call.  They are statements rather
    -- than an env transform only because those two tables are per-run REFS, not `TcEnv`
    -- fields; the ladder they implement is identical.  See applyRecordScopeOverrides.
    -- `CrossRun.universeDataDecls` — a positional `List Decl` accumulated by
    -- `appendDataUniverse`, carrying no key at all — is RETIRED; the pool is the
    -- whole-graph envelope read at THIS module's ordinal through
    -- `declEnvRowVisible`/`declEnvVisibleTo`.
    --
    -- `buildDeclEnvs` indexed the row under, threaded here by the driver
    -- (`elabModuleStamp mid` on the emit path; `cmCheckWorker mid` since #1508).  It
    -- is NOT re-derived from import syntax, which is why the emit path's
    -- `private_mangle` rewriting cannot break it: mangling renames top-level
    -- bindings, never a loader module id, and the decls in the pool are the very
    -- objects the accumulator held before.  (Re-deriving a read key by re-walking
    -- `DUse` syntax is what made #1425's four-site change resolve on eval and MISS on
    -- emit — `DUse` decls are never mangled.  This key does not go through that
    -- channel.)
    -- #1112 A-3.2b (#1512) slices 2+3: the KIND and FIELD-OWNER halves of the seed
    -- `loadDataUniverse` used to copy out of two `GraphCtx.crossRun` accumulators.  Same
    -- position in the ladder (it is the UNIVERSE layer, under the overlay and under
    -- `prog0`), same content, one ordinal-aware source.  See `declEnvSeedChain` for
    -- why the reader's OWN row is excluded here and registered by the two
    -- `registerAllData` calls below instead.
    declEnvSeedDataUniverse mctx mid envs
    -- `omInsert` tables, so the LAST row wins, and the overlay's named-outranks-wildcard
    -- rule therefore needs the `UseGroup` rows LAST.  The oracle call site takes the
    -- opposite order for the opposite reason — see the partition note at
    -- `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins`.  Swapping these two inverts the rule silently.
    let base =
      registerImportOverlay
        (withCtors mctx (spFloor gctx.crossRun.universeCtorPop.value))
        prog0
        (importedCtorTypeDeclsLastWins prog0 cur envs.deModules)
    let env0 = registerAllData base prog0
    applyRecordScopeOverrides mctx prog0
    applyCtorScopeOverrides prog0 env0
  recordInvocationView dataEnv
  -- #784: reject under-determined return-only effect vars on interface methods.
  -- MUST run AFTER `dataEnv` above populated `dataParamKindsRef` — the kind-aware
  -- predicate (rowArgNames) reads that table to distinguish a row-kinded ctor arg
  -- (`Async e a`, pins the effect) from a type-kinded one (`List e`, does not).
  checkUndeterminedRetEffVars mctx prog
  -- EFFECTS-SEMANTICS §6.4/§6.5: declared-kind consistency.  Same placement — the
  -- slot-kind reads consult the table `dataEnv` just populated.
  checkDeclaredKinds mctx prog
  -- One authority, one domain, for a declaration's `Authority` parameters.
  -- Same placement: an index slot's domain is read from that table.
  checkDeclAuthorityUses mctx prog
  -- #822: instance-head kind check for graded interfaces.  Same placement rationale
  -- as the line above for the HEAD-constructor half, which still reads
  -- `dataParamKindsRef` that `dataEnv` above has just populated.
  -- #1557 A-3.5c: the INTERFACE-slot half no longer reads a per-run table at all —
  -- it reads stage K's `CE` at this module's ordinal, mirroring `moduleImplUniv`'s
  -- arm-gated shape exactly. Standalone-list helpers build a single-module `CE` from
  -- `prog`, the very list `registerAllData (envFor mctx) prog` walked to fill the
  -- retired table, so the check does not go silent on a no-import `medaka check`.
  -- #1557 A-3.5a: this pair is now BOUND ONCE and read by TWO consumers — the graded
  -- impl-head check here and impl-completeness at the end of the body — hence the
  -- neutral name.  Binding once is not a style choice: `flatClassEnvOf` folds the whole
  -- decl list into a registry, so a second construction at the completeness site would
  -- pay that fold twice per flat compile, and the two copies could drift.  On Flat
  -- `fullUniverse = prog` (see its binding above), so `flatClassEnvOf prog` IS the
  -- `flatClassEnvOf fullUniverse` the completeness relocation calls for.
  let classEnvHere =
    let envs = gctx.declEnvsRef.value
    (envs.deIfaces, declEnvsOrdOf mid envs)
  checkGradedImplHeads mctx (fst classEnvHere) (snd classEnvHere) prog
  rejectCyclicAliases mctx prog
  -- #1280: `prog` reached here through a driver's stamping walk already, and
  -- `stampTyHead` is first-write-immune, so the builtins-only scope can only fill
  -- a head no driver could attribute — an `extern` written in a FLAT user file,
  -- where there is no module id and the primitive heads are all that is knowable.
  -- #2072: `userExternSchemes`, not `externSchemes` — this is the extern
  -- DECLARATION site (the program under check).  It builds the declared type
  -- verbatim; the two guards below are what hold a user extern to its row.
  let currentMethodRows = ifaceMethodSchemeRows mctx prog
  recordMethodGrants gctx currentMethodRows
  let visibleMethodRows = ifaceMethodSchemeRows mctx implDecls
  let visibleMethodRowIndex =
    methodRowsByIdentity
      (isNonEmptyL importerShadows)
      visibleMethodRows
      regEmpty
  let externS = userExternSchemes mctx (externTyOriginScope []) prog
  let globalS = legacyMethodSchemes currentMethodRows ++ externS
  -- #2074 / F1 (epic #2070): the USER-EXTERN DECLARATION guards, all gated on
  -- the same `ffiStampMode` ownership Bool — a stdlib-owned program's externs ARE
  -- the vocabulary and none of these rules owns them.  They run AFTER
  -- `rejectCyclicAliases` (`tc_decls`) so `expandAliasHeadTy` cannot recurse forever on a
  -- cyclic alias.  Order between them is not significant: diagnostics
  -- accumulate ([T-ERRORS-ACCUM]) and a signature can honestly fail more than one.
  --
  -- Four sibling loops rather than four arms inside one walk: each has its own
  -- exemption discipline (the crossable rule exempts a catalog-name
  -- redeclaration, the label and binding rules hold one to its `bind` clauses
  -- only, the shadow rule IS that exemption's admission test, and the
  -- catalog-row rule fires ONLY on a catalog name), and folding them together
  -- would force one `if` to speak for four notions of "this declaration is not
  -- mine".
  ffiCheckExternsCrossable mctx (ffiStampMode gctx mid) prog
  ffiCheckExternsLabelled mctx (ffiStampMode gctx mid) prog
  ffiCheckExternsShadow mctx (ffiStampMode gctx mid) prog
  ffiCheckExternsCatalogRow mctx (ffiStampMode gctx mid) prog
  -- (#4 cont) post-globalS mode setup.  Module: shadowMethodSchemes env layer (rebinding
  -- imported method names to the interface-method scheme) + the method-constraint cross
  -- seed (AFTER globalS's row registration, per installMethodPredicateSlots).
  let envSeed = extendVars dataEnv seed
  let shadowLayer =
    -- The rows remain local: they carry live scheme cells needed only by setup and
    -- must not become another graph-lived authority.
    let ifaceSchemes = legacyMethodSchemes visibleMethodRows
    -- #259/#2157: seed both halves from the same already-minted row selection.
    seedNumLitFromIntAnchor mctx visibleMethodRows
    -- #1351 L2 MIGRATE.  The importer-shadow layer now binds each shadowed method
    -- name to the scheme of the declaration THIS module's import scope admits
    -- (`methodScopeAt`), not to `lookupAssoc`'s decl-walk first match.
    -- Falls back to the bare-name answer on a miss — see `pickSchemesByDecl`.
    let sm =
      pickSchemesByDecl
        mctx
        importerShadows
        ifaceSchemes
        visibleMethodRowIndex
        moduleSeed.msImportedValues
    mctx.mcCells.methodPredicateSlotsRef :=
      mctx.mcCells.methodPredicateSlotsRef.value
        ++ gctx.crossRun.crossModuleMethodPredicateSlotsRef.value
    attachStandaloneSources moduleSeed.msImportedValues sm
  let envImported =
    annotateImportedMethodRows
      envSeed
      (importedMethodBindingNames gctx prog0 aliasSpellings)
      (nameableIfaceMethodSet gctx prog0)
      (namesToSet importerShadows omEmpty)
      visibleMethodRowIndex
  let envGlobal =
    extendVars (extendMethodRows envImported currentMethodRows) externS
  let env1 = extendBindings envGlobal shadowLayer
  let defs = funDefs progS
  let (topSchemes, groupedOut) =
    processTopGroups
      env1
      sigs
      defs
      (groupNames defs omEmpty)
      (namesToSet (publicValNames prog) omEmpty)
  recordTopBinderTypes gctx (omFromPairs topSchemes omEmpty) progS
  -- The tail bodies (impl, default, prop and test bodies) see the top-level
  -- bindings as the binding groups do (`processTopGroups`' result env): each
  -- binding keeps its standalone descriptor, so an occurrence of a top-level value
  -- spelled like a method resolves to the value in a tail body too.  Marking and
  -- inference read this one env.
  let tailEnv = extendTermBindings env1 topSchemes
  -- Module: the marked tree this call returns (the groups' marked clauses written
  -- back, the tail bodies marked with the module's final dict-name set) and its
  -- stamped twin, which the tail-body inference below reads.  Flat: `progS` is both.
  let progOut = match mctx.mcCells.markingRef.value
    Some mm => finishModuleMarking mm tailEnv groupedOut progS
    None => progS
  let progT = match mctx.mcCells.markingRef.value
    Some mm => stampTailDecls mm.mmTop progOut
    None => progS
  -- The module path runs a single inference pass; core is checked as its own
  -- invocation, which isolates the prelude's method bodies from a user binding
  -- that shadows an interface method (List Int vs Int).

  -- Unified impl-body inference form (Module arm, both drivers): inferUserImplBodies
  -- infers parametric heads and preserves diagnostics. allProg = implDecls
  -- (interfaces + accumulated impls) grounds the field types. See
  -- compiler/TYPECHECK-TARGET-ARCHITECTURE.md § "SA-10a. Landing log" (#2546, ARCH Q3a).

  inferUserImplBodies
    tailEnv
    implDecls
    (fst classEnvHere)
    (snd classEnvHere)
    progT
  inferDefaultBodies tailEnv progT
  inferPropBodies tailEnv progT
  inferTestBodies tailEnv progT
  -- (#3) cross-module dict-threading SNAPSHOT — MODULE ONLY (flat SKIPS: running it would
  -- pollute the cross-module accumulators with a phantom single-file attribution).
  -- multi-line RHS: the indented `:=` form is legal but not seed-parseable yet (#1744)
  -- lint-disable-next-line rule-prefer-assign-op

  let _ = dedupPredicateSlotsTable mctx ()
  -- #1425: restore the rows the per-module view masked (see `selfNameRows`) — the view
  -- must not propagate as a graph-wide deletion into the next module's seed.
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsRef
      (predicateSlotTableWithoutArgs
        (mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef.value
          ++ selfNameRows
            prog
            gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsRef.value))
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsQualRef
      (attributeModuleEntries mid prog mctx.mcCells.funPredicateSlotsRef.value
        ++ gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunPredicateSlotsQualRef.value)
  -- B-2.2-f (#1113): the sidecar's two snapshots, paired with the ids snapshots above.
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredRef
      (mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef.value
        ++ selfNameRows
          prog
          gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredRef.value)
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredQualRef
      (attributeModuleEntries
          mid
          prog
          mctx.mcCells.funConstraintDeclaredRef.value
        ++ gctx.crossRun.crossModuleFunConstraintDeclaredQualRef.value)
  gctx.crossRun.crossModuleMethodPredicateSlotsRef :=
    mctx.mcCells.methodPredicateSlotsRef.value
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleMethodPredicateSlotsQualRef
      (emptyMethodModulePredicateSlots mid prog
        ++ attributeMethodModulePredicateSlots
          mid
          prog
          mctx.mcCells.methodPredicateSlotsRef.value
        ++ gctx.crossRun.crossModuleMethodPredicateSlotsQualRef.value)
  -- #1114 (#845, DICT-SEMANTICS §4.2 D6(a)): snapshot THIS module's scheme contexts
  -- alongside the three arity tables above.  The arity tables carry only what
  -- `funConstraintsRef` holds, whose INFERRED half is gated on `dictEligibleSetRef`
  -- and is therefore EMPTY on the check path (see that ref's comment) — so an
  -- UNSIGNATURED constrained export was invisible to every importer at check time
  -- while `run`/`build` threaded its dict anyway.  `schemeObligationsRef` is the
  -- always-on store of the same fact and is what the SAME-module use site already
  -- reads; this makes the cross-module use site read it too, under the same
  -- source-exact (mid, name) key.
  let _ =
    setRef
      gctx.crossRun.crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef
      (attributeModuleSchemeObls
          mid
          prog
          gctx.crossRun.crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef.value
          mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value
        ++ gctx.crossRun.crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef.value)
  ()
  -- P0-17: impl missing a required method.  #1557 A-3.5a: ONE call, both modes — the
  -- arm split is gone because `classEnvHere` above already carries the mode's `CE` and
  -- its reading ordinal (Flat: a single-module `CE` over `prog`, which IS `fullUniverse`
  -- on that arm, at ordinal 0; Module: stage K's whole-graph `deIfaces` at this module's
  -- ordinal).  It must NOT read `declEnvsRef` unconditionally: that envelope is
  -- `emptyDeclEnvs` in compatibility helpers, so reading it there would make `ceRequiredAt` miss on
  -- every interface, and the miss arm is `[]` — every live rejection here would become a
  -- silent accept.  `classEnvHere`'s `Flat _` arm is what prevents that.
  checkImplCompletenessMap mctx (fst classEnvHere) (snd classEnvHere) prog
  -- (#5 / BREAK #2) obligation channels.  FLAT runs both channels + setNumlitFloats
  -- here so the per-module pass can
  -- stamp the Float-typed int literals, and a Flat consumer that read them unstamped
  -- would get Int cells where the inference said Float.  MODULE runs both channels
  -- here and leaves the Float stamp to the graph-end drain (`SSNumlitFloats`), on
  -- every driver (#2705).

  -- #1112 A-3.4 PR2: the obligation universe is now the `IE` projection bound ONCE
  -- as `moduleImplUniv` far above (see its block for the flip, the measurement, and
  -- why re-deriving it here would be a second whole-prefix rebuild for a value that
  -- cannot have changed).  The three `obUniv*` accumulators this used to read are
  -- deleted from `CrossRun`.

  let obUniv = moduleImplUniv
  -- #838 I4: unified checker, same `obUniv`.  Impl channel = (deferNonGround=True);
  -- call channel = (deferNonGround=False).
  -- #2705: BOTH channels run on EVERY driver.  A driver that infers impl
  -- bodies without checking their obligations lets a program `check` rejects
  -- reach codegen through `build`'s own elaboration.  `setNumlitFloats` is not called
  -- here on any driver any more: the Float literal stamp is `SSNumlitFloats`,
  -- first step of the graph-end drain every Module-arm driver now runs.
  let _ = recordLiveRoots mctx ()
  let _ = judgeQuiescentGoals mctx ()
  let _ = refileDeterminedRows mctx ()
  let _ =
    checkCallObligationsU mctx True obUniv mctx.mcCells.implObls.items.value
  let _ = checkCallObligationsU mctx False obUniv mctx.mcCells.obls.items.value
  let _ = settleDeferredPatLits mctx ()
  let _ =
    checkDeferredMatches mctx (reverseL mctx.mcCells.matchChecksDeferred.value)
  let _ = checkFixedWidthLiterals mctx mctx.mcCells.numlitRanges.value
  let _ = checkWideLiterals mctx mctx.mcCells.wideLitRanges.value
  ()
  mctx.mcCells.localSchemesOut :=
    mapLocalSchemes mctx mctx.mcCells.localBindRefs.value
  mctx.mcCells.seedSchemesOut := seed
  -- #154 PR-A: grow the persistent DATA-universe by THIS module's PUBLIC data, AFTER the
  -- module's inference is complete (so the copy+overlay above saw pub 1..k-1 and NEVER this
  -- module's own pub twice) — the register-time mirror of foldModules' `accData ++
  -- publicDataDecls prog` threading.  appendDataUniverse clobbers the working table refs,
  -- which are DEAD here (the next module's `freshModuleCtx` wipes them).  Flat leaves it untouched.
  -- A-3.2b residual (#1512): `mid` is BOUND here now — `appendDataUniverse`'s
  -- reload reads the alias universe at this module's own ordinal.
  appendDataUniverse mctx mid prog0
  let result = (
    ModuleBindings {
      mbSchemes = globalS ++ topSchemes,
      mbStandaloneSchemes = omFromPairs (reverseL topSchemes) omEmpty,
    },
    progOut,
    mctx,
  )
  closeScope mctx ()
  result
-- D2: check top-level `let rec` bindings for non-function members before
-- type-checking the DFunDef groups (the LetRecNonFunction guard,
-- which fires for
-- is_letrec=true groups — i.e. those coming from DLetGroup).

-- Phase 83/84 single-level: AFTER top-level inference (so the scheme output is
-- unchanged), and ONLY on the elaborate/dict path, infer each parametric impl's
-- return-position method bodies, registering the requires' element dict so the
-- inner ref routes RDict and the call site routes RKey + impl dicts.

-- Phase 69.x-e: likewise infer each interface DEFAULT body that carries a
-- method-level constraint (foldMap's default), registering the constraint var so
-- the in-body return-position ref (`empty`) routes RDict to `$dict_<method>_<slot>`.

-- Phase 4.55: type-check each prop body with
-- its declared params in scope.  This is what RESOLVES the EDictAt/EMethodAt route
-- refs the prepass marked inside a prop body — without it the routes stay empty,
-- and on the dict-passed eval path (DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 2 promotes the file's own
-- `=>`-constrained fns) the prop body would call a now-dict-passed `wf`/`fromList`
-- with no dict argument → under-application → `force` a partial closure → not VBool
-- → every prop "fails" (set's `fromList []` reported ill-formed).  The compiler
-- typechecker previously skipped props entirely (funDefs ignores DProp), so this
-- gap was latent until Phase 2's promotion exposed it.

-- Shape A (gap #44): ground a container literal's free result-element var THROUGH
-- the impl's method type, BEFORE the resolve* passes read the cells.

-- Error-path B4: now every method occurrence's dispatch type is fully resolved;
-- reject any whose concrete head type has no matching impl (the
-- `No impl of … for …` check).

-- Phase 4.55: infer each prop body with its declared params in scope, so the
-- prepass-marked EDictAt/EMethodAt route refs inside resolve.
-- Runs unconditionally (props are eval-only; the
-- emit/golden paths have no props): infer prop_body; unify t_bool.
-- A prop body is eval-only and is NOT dict-passed for operator dispatch: like the
-- OCaml oracle (which evals raw prop bodies against the marked env), its comparison
-- operators must stay PRIMITIVE EBinOps the eval builtin handles structurally, not
-- get stamped and rewritten to eq/lt method apps (whose dict route would then be
-- applied to a non-dict-passed comparison, panic: applied non-function).  So
-- bracket the goal channel around prop inference (`dropGoalsSince`): the routes
-- the prop body NEEDS (EDictAt for the file's promoted constrained fns, via the
-- dictionary-application and return-site goals) are still collected; only the
-- operator-rewrite goals the prop body would otherwise contribute are dropped.
inferPropBodies : TcEnv -> List Decl -> Unit
inferPropBodies env prog =
  let mark = goalsMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ()
  inferPropBodiesGo env prog
  dropGoalsSince env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph mark [
    GKBinopSite, GKUnopSite, GKArithSite
  ]

inferPropBodiesGo : TcEnv -> List Decl -> Unit
inferPropBodiesGo env [] = ()
inferPropBodiesGo outer ((DProp _ name params body) :: rest) =
  -- The body's checks are located at the body (the declaration has no span),
  -- and its type at the value it returns.
  let env = envAtIfSome (exprSpanLoc body) outer
  let local = extendPropParams env params
  enterLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  let parentScope = captureScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  let bodyScope =
    Scopes.freshScope
      (currentScopeStore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ())
      (Some parentScope)
      env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentLevel.value
      (Scopes.scopeFrame
        (currentScopeStore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ())
        parentScope).sfModuleId
      (PropOwner name)
  openScope env.teDyn.dcModule bodyScope
  let oblMark = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls
  let callMark = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls
  let dictMark = goalsMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ()
  unifyIn
    (dynAtIfSome (valueSpanLoc body) env.teDyn)
    (infer local body)
    (tconBuiltin "Bool")
  exitLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  settleClosedBody
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    (tconBuiltin "Bool")
    oblMark
    callMark
    dictMark
  closeScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  inferPropBodiesGo outer rest
inferPropBodiesGo env (_ :: rest) = inferPropBodiesGo env rest

-- Phase 127 (restored 2026-07-11): typecheck each `test "…" = body` body,
-- requiring it to have type `Expectation` (a nullary TCon, so a plain nominal
-- unify).  Unlike props, test bodies ARE dict-passed (their `expectEqual`/… calls
-- come from `import test`), so this runs on the normal inference path with binop
-- stamping enabled (no snapshot/restore).  Without this, `test "x" = 1 + "str"`
-- and `test "x" = 42` slipped through `check` with zero diagnostics.
inferTestBodies : TcEnv -> List Decl -> Unit
inferTestBodies env [] = ()
inferTestBodies outer ((DTest _ name body) :: rest) =
  -- The body's checks are located at the body (the declaration has no span),
  -- and its type at the value it returns.
  let env = envAtIfSome (exprSpanLoc body) outer
  enterLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  let parentScope = captureScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  let bodyScope =
    Scopes.freshScope
      (currentScopeStore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ())
      (Some parentScope)
      env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentLevel.value
      (Scopes.scopeFrame
        (currentScopeStore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ())
        parentScope).sfModuleId
      (TestOwner name)
  openScope env.teDyn.dcModule bodyScope
  let oblMark = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls
  let callMark = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls
  let dictMark = goalsMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ()
  -- #1110: `Expectation` is declared by `stdlib/test.mdk`, a USER-SPACE module —
  -- the checker fabricates this head from a literal and has no decl in view, so
  -- there is no identity to acquire.  `tconUnresolved`, not `tconBuiltin`: the
  -- language does not provide `Expectation`, and claiming §8 I6.2's
  -- program-global identity for a type some module declares would be a lie the
  -- shared carrier type cannot object to.
  unifyIn
    (dynAtIfSome (valueSpanLoc body) env.teDyn)
    (infer env body)
    (tconUnresolved "Expectation")
  exitLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  settleClosedBody
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    (tconUnresolved "Expectation")
    oblMark
    callMark
    dictMark
  closeScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  inferTestBodies outer rest
inferTestBodies env (_ :: rest) = inferTestBodies env rest

-- A test or property body is a numeric boundary that publishes no polymorphic
-- result: nothing after it can determine its variables.  A variable below the
-- body's level (a property parameter, a value-restricted outer binding) belongs
-- to its enclosing binder and is held rigid, judged where it lives.  The body runs
-- the method-body settle sequence with an empty surviving set, then the verdict,
-- so a goal the body leaves open is judged here as a top-level binding's is.
-- [bodyTy] is the closed type the body was unified with.
settleClosedBody : ModuleCtx -> Mono -> Int -> Int -> Int -> Unit
settleClosedBody mctx bodyTy oblMark callMark dictMark =
  let level = mctx.mcCells.currentLevel.value + 1
  let addedObls = wWindow mctx.mcCells.implObls oblMark
  let bodyObls = addedObls ++ callUOblsWindow mctx callMark
  let rigidIds =
    outerRigidIds
      (Some level)
      (flatMap (uOblArgs mctx.mcGraph) bodyObls)
      omEmpty
  improveByUniqueImpl (dynFor mctx) rigidIds bodyObls
  determineByUniqueInstance mctx rigidIds bodyObls
  let withheld = finalizeNumBoundary (dynFor mctx) NumBoundary {
    nbDisposition = NumBoundaryOwnedMethodBody level,
    nbMembers = [bodyTy],
    nbSurvivingIds = omEmpty,
    nbDefaultObls = addedObls ++ calleeNumObls mctx callMark dictMark,
    nbAmbiguityObls = addedObls,
    nbGuard = NumGuard {
      ngGoals = bodyObls,
      ngRigidIds = rigidIds,
      ngOuterBelow = Some level,
    },
  }
  improveByUniqueImpl (dynFor mctx) rigidIds bodyObls
  determineByUniqueInstance mctx rigidIds bodyObls
  rejectUndeterminedVectors
    mctx
    rigidIds
    omEmpty
    addedObls
    (callUOblsWindow mctx callMark
      ++ nestedRequireGoals mctx rigidIds omEmpty bodyObls)
  rejectWithheldAmbiguity mctx rigidIds withheld bodyObls

-- bind each prop param `x : ty` as a monomorphic scheme in the env (fresh tyvars
-- for any free type variable in ty; props are normally concrete, e.g. `List Int`).
extendPropParams : TcEnv -> List PropParam -> TcEnv
extendPropParams env [] = env
extendPropParams env ((PropParam x _ ty) :: rest) =
  let scheme =
    monoScheme
      (fromAstType
        env.teDyn.dcModule
        (freshTvMap env.teDyn.dcModule (dedup (tyVarNames ty)))
        ty)
  extendPropParams (extendLocalVar env x scheme) rest

-- #760 (S1, check-vs-emit soundness): infer the USER program's impl-method bodies on
-- EVERY path, and check their obligations on every path (#2705).  A path that
-- inferred an impl body without checking its obligations, or checked without
-- inferring, lets a `deriving (Eq)`/`(Debug)` (or a hand-written impl) over a field
-- whose type lacks that instance `check` clean and `build` panic ("no impl of method
-- 'eq' for type 'Inner'").  Inferring the body records the field's `Eq Inner`
-- obligation via recordImplObligation, which the impl channel then rejects with the
-- SAME diagnostic a direct `==` already emits.
--
-- Scoped to the user decls (`prog0`), NOT the prelude (`coreProg`): re-inferring CORE's
-- own parametric impl bodies on the check path is heavier and pointless — core is
-- known-good (it builds + self-compiles), so only user impls need check-path inference.
--
-- Scoped to ground-headed impls (no type variable in the impl head).  A ground impl —
-- `impl Eq Outer`, the derived instance for a non-parametric type — only ever calls
-- methods on its fields' CONCRETE types, so every obligation it records is concrete:
-- checkImplObligations/checkCallObligations either finds the impl or emits the genuine
-- `No impl of Eq for Inner`.  A PARAMETRIC impl (`impl Eq (List a) requires Eq a`) instead
-- records obligations on its abstract element tyvar; those never ground on the check path
-- and would reach checkUndeterminedObligations, which reports a spurious `Ambiguous instance`
-- for any iface with >= 2 impls (it fired on EVERY program the moment core's own parametric
-- impls were inferred standalone — the prelude snapshot).  The emit path never exercised
-- that because it SKIPS the obligation gate.  Inferring only ground impls records the
-- concrete missing-instance rejects (the whole point of #760) while structurally avoiding
-- the abstract-element ambiguity.  A parametric impl over a concrete field (a rarer nesting)
-- stays deferred to `build` exactly as it was before #760 — a residual, not a regression.
--
-- DIAGNOSTICS PUSHED *DURING* body inference: #760 originally DROPPED all of them (snapshot +
-- restore typeErrors / typeErrorsSticky), because it only wanted the missing-INSTANCE rejects,
-- which are recorded as pendingImpl/CallObligations (side effects) that
-- checkImplObligations/checkCallObligations gate LATER — AFTER this returns — so dropping the
-- error accumulators did not lose a single missing-instance reject.  #777 (type mismatch) and
-- #782 (effect leak) then established that the type/effect errors the body inference itself
-- raises (e.g. an EFFECT LEAK: `putStr` in a pure-typed method body, or a body type mismatch)
-- are genuine soundness bugs that must reject symmetrically with top-level functions — so those
-- are now KEPT — and since issue 1146 they are kept because nothing removes them: the
-- window that used to rebuild this list is retired (see the retirement note at the end
-- of inferUserImplBodies below).  Per-axis history follows.
-- #777 (S0, silent wrongness): a GENUINE type mismatch inside a ground impl-method
-- body (`sig _ = idInt "str"` where `idInt : Int -> Int` but `sig`'s declared return
-- is `String`) is a `unify`/`typeMismatch` error pushed DURING body inference, not an
-- obligation.  Blanket-restoring `typeErrors` here (as this did before #777) discarded
-- it, and because every driver that decides a build reaches this same body inference —
-- `check`'s directly, `build`'s through the `medaka_emitter` child's `elaborateModules` —
-- discarding it here makes `check` and `build` accept the program together: the built binary
-- then printed a wrong value at exit 0 or SEGFAULTED (an impl method returning a
-- differently-typed field/value).  We now KEEP those body clash errors so both paths
-- reject.
-- #782 (S2, ratified 2026-07-20): the effect axis is now enforced HERE too, symmetric
-- with top-level functions.  An effect leak in an impl-method body (`putStr` in a body
-- whose method signature declares no effect row) is a `T-EFFECT-LEAK`/`T-EFFECT-PARAM`
-- pushed DURING body inference, exactly like a body type mismatch.  Previously these two
-- codes were SUPPRESSED here (isSuppressedImplBodyErr) to spare the intentionally-effectful
-- `impl Speak Dog` in test/engine_fixtures/nullary_memo_with_args — but that made the
-- effect discipline the spec commits to (EFFECTS-SEMANTICS §method: an impl's latent row is
-- bounded by the signature) hold for top-level functions yet silently NOT for impl bodies.
-- The ratified fix is to STOP suppressing: the fixture's method now declares its concrete
-- `<Stdout>` row (`speak : a -> <Stdout> String`), so its body is bounded-by-signature and
-- stays valid, while a pure-signatured method that leaks an effect is now a real error.
-- Missing-instance rejects are unaffected regardless: they are recorded as
-- pendingImpl/CallObligations (side channel) and gated LATER, so they were never in this
-- window.

inferUserImplBodies : TcEnv -> List Decl -> ClassEnv -> Int -> List Decl -> Unit
inferUserImplBodies env allProg ce cur decls =
  let rpNames = returnPosMethodNames allProg
  -- GROUND heads (#760/#777): infer their bodies and KEEP the obligations they record
  -- — a ground impl only calls methods on CONCRETE field types, so every obligation is
  -- concrete and checkImplObligations either finds the impl or emits a genuine
  -- `No impl of <iface> for <field>` reject.
  let impls = locatedImpls None decls
  inferImplBodies
    env
    rpNames
    ce
    cur
    (filterList (i => implHeadGround (snd i)) impls)
  -- PARAMETRIC heads (#781, S0): a parametric impl head (`impl Sig (List a)`) is never
  -- reached by the ground filter, so a genuine non-literal type mismatch inside its
  -- method body (`sig _ = idInt "str"`, `idInt : Int -> Int` in a String-returning
  -- method) went unenforced end-to-end — `check`/`build` exit 0 and the built binary
  -- printed a wrong value.  Infer these bodies too so the `unify`/`typeMismatch` clash
  -- is surfaced (it is pushed to `typeErrors`, and kept because nothing removes it —
  -- see the retirement note at the end of this function), mirroring the
  -- ground-head fix.  BUT a parametric impl also records obligations on its ABSTRACT
  -- element tyvar (`impl Eq (List a) requires Eq a` → an `Eq a` obligation over an
  -- unground var); those never ground on the check path and would reach
  -- checkUndeterminedObligations as a SPURIOUS `Ambiguous instance` for any iface with
  -- >= 2 impls (it fired on every program the moment core's own parametric impls were
  -- inferred — the prelude snapshot).  So WINDOW the obligation channel across this
  -- pass and restore it afterward: the body-clash errors go through `typeErrors`, NOT
  -- the obligation channel, so windowing keeps the #781 reject while dropping the
  -- abstract-var false positives.
  --
  -- #1114 (#792): the rollback is ground-preserving, and it did not used to be.
  -- The two `wRestore`s below used to run bare, which threw away EVERY obligation the
  -- parametric pass recorded — including the GROUND ones, which are exactly the ones
  -- the abstract-var argument above does not cover.  `impl Foo (List a)` whose body
  -- calls `debug Bar` records a fully-ground `Debug Bar`; nothing about it is abstract,
  -- the gate would have rejected it on the spot, and the blanket rollback deleted it —
  -- so `check` exited 0 while `build` PANICKED (`no impl of method 'debug' for type
  -- 'Bar'`).  That is a `run_check_agreement` break, and the sentence this comment
  -- replaced ("stays deferred to `build` exactly as before — a residual, not a
  -- regression") named it as a known residual without naming its cost.
  --
  -- The policy is now written down: DICT-SEMANTICS §4.2 **D1** (a predicate the gate can
  -- decide where it is recorded is discharged there — no window may discard it) and
  -- **D6(b)** (a window that exists to suppress D3 false positives must filter by what is
  -- DECIDABLE, not discard wholesale).  So: window as before, then re-push exactly the
  -- obligations `uOblIsDecidableNow` accepts.  The abstract element tyvar of a parametric
  -- head is decidable by nothing, so every false positive the window was built to stop is
  -- still stopped.
  --
  -- The predicate is "the GATE can decide it now", NOT "it is ground" — those differ,
  -- and this site is where believing otherwise costs a verdict.  `uOblIsDecidableNow`
  -- carries the derivation and the counterexample (`Debug (Int -> Int)`); read it before
  -- touching either side.
  -- Re-pushed in `reverseL` order because `wWindow` yields newest-first (items are
  -- consed): reversing restores the original record order, which the gate reports in.
  let implOblsSnap = wSnapshot env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls
  let callSnap = wSnapshot env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls
  inferImplBodies
    env
    rpNames
    ce
    cur
    (filterList (i => implHeadParametric (snd i)) impls)
  let keptImpl =
    filterList
      (uOblIsDecidableNow env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph)
      (wWindow env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls (snd implOblsSnap))
  let keptCall =
    filterList
      (uOblIsDecidableNow env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph)
      (wWindow env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls (snd callSnap))
  wRestore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls implOblsSnap
  wRestore env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls callSnap
  wPushAll env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls (reverseL keptImpl)
  wPushAll env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls (reverseL keptCall)
  ()

-- ── PR3 keyed lookups (replace implMatches / implMatchesReceiver / findMatchingImplReqs /
-- implCountForIface).  Each first scans the concrete "iface|tag" bucket, then the iface's
-- headless bucket.  implMatchesU / bucketRecvMatch are EXISTENCE tests, so the
-- concrete-then-headless order is byte-identical to the old flat scan.  findMatchingImplReqsU
-- is first-MATCH and DOES depend on that order — a deliberate soundness/conformance fix,
-- documented on it below. ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

-- ── Error-path B4: missing-impl dispatch-obligation check ──────────────────
-- The `No impl of … for …` reject.  For each recorded interface-method occurrence, recover the types the
-- interface params resolved to at this site (dispatchMonosOf), and — ONLY when
-- they are all CONCRETE (a resolved head tycon, no remaining polymorphic var, so
-- a constrained-generic `Ord a =>` site whose receiver is still `a` is correctly
-- deferred to the caller) — verify the program declares a matching impl.  None →
-- `No impl of <iface> for <args>`.
-- #838 I4: the second obligation checker (checkImplObligations/checkImplObligationsU/
-- checkOneImplObligation) is RETIRED — impl obligations now route through the unified
-- checkOneCallObligation (so a rule lands ONCE).
-- #991: the `implOblToU` bridge that used to project a surviving
-- `pendingImplObligations` 5-tuple onto `UObligation` is GONE — both channels store
-- `UObligation` directly.  The projection it performed still happens at CHECK time and
-- in the same place: `uOblArgs`, via the `OpMethodOcc` arm (NOT `oblDispatchMonos`,
-- which special-cases Num → [] and would drop a numeric literal's operand).
-- Improvement, determination and defaulting at each group's close run BEFORE the gate
-- and ground the shared mono cells, so a determined multi-param goal's projected args
-- are already ground here.

-- PLAN.md #11: after HM + defaulting every source integer literal's type var is
-- ground (or a genuine `Num a` survivor).  Decide each literal's runtime rep
-- (3-way):
--   • ground Float ⇒ stamp the float cell + CLEAR the fromInt route (dref RNone) ⇒
--     dictPass emits `ELit (LFloat f)` (eval is value-tagged; eval_arith rejects
--     mixed int/float tags, so a Float literal must carry a real float).
--   • ground Int (incl. via ambiguous→Int defaulting) ⇒ clear both cells ⇒
--     `ELit (LInt n)`, no dict dispatch.
--   • NOT a primitive head (a still-polymorphic `Num a` — the soundness case, `1`
--     in `inc x = x + 1` — or an exotic user numeric type) ⇒ leave [dref]'s
--     resolveSites-stamped route in place ⇒ dictPass emits
--     `EApp (EMethodAt "fromInt" …) (ELit (LInt n))` so the literal dispatches
--     through the runtime Num dict (Int→identity, Float→intToFloat).  A dref left
--     RNone (route never filled, e.g. unreachable position) falls to Int below.
-- Reads numlitRefs (populated by inferNumLit during THIS run/module's inference).
setNumlitFloatsGo : List (Mono, Ref (Option Float), Int, Ref Route) -> Unit
setNumlitFloatsGo [] = ()
setNumlitFloatsGo ((var, fref, n, dref) :: rest) =
  match normalize var
    TCon "Float" _ =>
      fref := Some (intToFloat n)
      dref := RNone
    TCon "Int" _ => dref := RNone
    _ => ()
  setNumlitFloatsGo rest

-- Records, at the module's end, the variables its non-generalized members still
-- leave open, for the graph-end drain (`GraphRun.liveRoots`).
recordLiveRoots : ModuleCtx -> Unit -> Unit
recordLiveRoots mctx _ =
  let members = mctx.mcCells.liveMemberMonosRef.value
  let liveIds = dedupI (flatMap (liveVarIdsOf mctx) members)
  if isNonEmptyL liveIds then
    let goals =
      mctx.mcCells.implObls.items.value ++ mctx.mcCells.obls.items.value
    let numObls =
      mctx.mcCells.implObls.items.value
        ++ numCallObls mctx.mcGraph mctx.mcCells.obls.items.value
    let numIds =
      filterList (i => containsI i liveIds) (numConstrainedIds numObls)
    let numRoots = filterMap (i => liveNumVarGo i numObls) (dedupI numIds)
    let g = mctx.mcGraph.graphRun
    g.liveRoots :=
      filterMap (findTvarInMonos members) liveIds ++ g.liveRoots.value
    g.liveGoals := reverseL (liveGoalsOf mctx members) ++ g.liveGoals.value
    if isNonEmptyL numRoots then
      g.liveNumRoots := numRoots ++ g.liveNumRoots.value
      g.liveNumGoals :=
        filterList
            (o =>
              any
                (i => elem i numIds)
                (flatMap monoUnboundIds (uOblArgs mctx.mcGraph o)))
            goals
          ++ g.liveNumGoals.value

-- The module's held goals that a live root still leaves open, in the order they
-- were held, each under the first of [members] whose type mentions one of its live
-- variables.  A goal a later group grounded is the module's obligation check's.
liveGoalsOf : ModuleCtx -> List Mono -> List LiveGoal
liveGoalsOf mctx members =
  let mid = mctx.mcCells.currentModuleRef.value
  let owners = memberOfIds mctx members
  filterMap
    ((rigidIds, window, o) =>
      let ids = flatMap monoUnboundIds (uOblArgs mctx.mcGraph o)
      map
        (k => LiveGoal {
          lgModule = mid,
          lgBinding = k,
          lgRigidIds =
            fold
              (acc i =>
                if IdMap.has i owners then
                  omDelete (intToString i) acc
                else
                  acc)
              rigidIds
              ids,
          lgWindow = window,
          lgGoal = o,
        })
        (findMap (i => IdMap.get i owners) ids))
    (reverseL mctx.mcCells.heldLiveGoalsRef.value)

-- Each live variable of [members] under the index of the first member whose type
-- mentions it.
memberOfIds : ModuleCtx -> List Mono -> Map Int Int
memberOfIds mctx members =
  fst
    (fold
      ((owners, k) m => (
        fold
          (acc i => if IdMap.has i acc then acc else IdMap.set i k acc)
          owners
          (liveVarIdsOf mctx m),
        k + 1,
      ))
      (Tip, 0)
      members)

-- The verdict on the goals boundaries held for live roots, at the drain, after the
-- live `Num` roots are defaulted: every module has closed, so nothing is left to
-- ground them, and each is judged as its boundary would have judged it had it owned
-- the live variables (DICT-SEMANTICS §6.2 T4, R-SC3), in its own module's context.
-- A binding gets one verdict: the first of its goals that draws one.
judgeLiveGoals : ModuleCtx -> List StampCtx -> List DrainFinding
judgeLiveGoals saved ctxs =
  let g = saved.mcGraph.graphRun
  let goals = reverseL g.liveGoals.value
  g.liveGoals := []
  if isEmptyL goals then
    []
  else
    let byModule = omFromPairs (map (c => (c.scModule, c)) ctxs) omEmpty
    reverseL (fst (fold (judgeLiveGoal saved byModule) ([], omEmpty) goals))

-- [saved] is the record the drain began with, which each entered stamp context takes
-- its drain tables from (`enterStampCtx`).
judgeLiveGoal : ModuleCtx ->
  OrdMap StampCtx ->
  (List DrainFinding, OrdMap Unit) ->
  LiveGoal ->
  (List DrainFinding, OrdMap Unit)
judgeLiveGoal saved byModule (found, judged) lg =
  let binding = "\{lg.lgModule}\t\{intToString lg.lgBinding}"
  match (omHasKey binding judged, omLookup lg.lgModule byModule)
    (False, Some ctx) =>
      let stamp = enterStampCtx saved ctx
      let rigidIds = lg.lgRigidIds
      let diags = captureTypeErrors stamp (_ =>
        fold
          (_ o =>
            undeterminedVectorVerdict stamp rigidIds omEmpty lg.lgWindow o)
          ()
          (lg.lgGoal :: nestedRequireGoals stamp rigidIds omEmpty [lg.lgGoal]))
      match diags
        [] => (found, judged)
        _ => (
          reverseL (map (verdictFinding lg.lgModule True) diags) ++ found,
          omInsert binding () judged,
        )
    _ => (found, judged)

-- The verdict on the goals boundaries left to quiescence, once every group of the
-- module has closed: no later group is left to ground their variables, so each is
-- judged as its boundary would have judged it, and so are the prerequisites its
-- boundary left to this point (`nestedRequireGoals`).
judgeQuiescentGoals : ModuleCtx -> Unit -> Unit
judgeQuiescentGoals mctx _ =
  let goals = reverseL mctx.mcCells.quiescentGoalsRef.value
  mctx.mcCells.quiescentGoalsRef := []
  fold
    (_ (rigidIds, window, o) =>
      fold
        (_ g => undeterminedVectorVerdict mctx rigidIds omEmpty window g)
        ()
        (o :: nestedRequireGoals mctx rigidIds omEmpty [o]))
    ()
    goals

-- The variables a later group can still ground (§6.2 T2): those no scheme
-- quantifies in the types of this group's members [monos] and of every earlier
-- top-level member of the module.  The members that still carry one are kept for
-- the groups after this one; a member grounded since is dropped.
liveMemberIds : ModuleCtx -> List Mono -> OrdMap Unit
liveMemberIds mctx monos =
  let open =
    filterList
      (m => isNonEmptyL (liveVarIdsOf mctx m))
      (monos ++ mctx.mcCells.liveMemberMonosRef.value)
  mctx.mcCells.liveMemberMonosRef := open
  omFromNames
    (map intToString (dedupI (flatMap (liveVarIdsOf mctx) open)))
    omEmpty

liveVarIdsOf : ModuleCtx -> Mono -> List Int
liveVarIdsOf mctx m =
  filterList
    (i => not (IdMap.has i mctx.mcCells.deferrableVarIds.value))
    (monoUnboundIds m)

-- The impls of [decls], each with the span a check in it that names no construct
-- takes: the impl's interface name, or for an impl `deriving` generated (it has
-- none), the name of the type declared before it (`expandDecl` places a type's
-- derived impls right after it).
locatedImpls : Option Loc -> List Decl -> List (Option Loc, Decl)
locatedImpls _ [] = []
locatedImpls derivedFrom (d :: rest) = match implDeclLoc d
  Some here => (orElseLoc here derivedFrom, d) :: locatedImpls derivedFrom rest
  None => locatedImpls (derivingTypeLoc d) rest

derivingTypeLoc : Decl -> Option Loc
derivingTypeLoc (DAttrib _ d) = derivingTypeLoc d
derivingTypeLoc (DData { dataNameLoc, ... }) = dataNameLoc
derivingTypeLoc (DNewtype { newtypeNameLoc, ... }) = newtypeNameLoc
derivingTypeLoc _ = None

-- G4: mark binop sites recorded while inferring this impl body so resolveBinopSite
-- routes their element dict (RDict) — and ONLY theirs, not top-level operators.

-- extern declarations become top-level schemes (like interface methods)
--
-- 🚨 #1280: THE STAMPING WALK IS PART OF THIS FUNCTION, NOT OF ITS CALLERS, AND
-- THAT IS THE FIX.  Every other tree that reaches typecheck has already been
-- through `stampTyOrigins` at a driver (`stampGraphTyOrigins` on the module arm,
-- the retired single-program stamping path).
-- `runtimeDecls` was the one tree that reached a `Scheme` without ever entering
-- that walk on EITHER arm, so every `Mono` `fromAstTypeE` built out of an extern's
-- declared type carried `OriginUnresolved` — the SUPPLY half of the dispatch-key
-- blocker recorded at `dispHeadTab`, and the absent head #1279 bridges two
-- distinct identities through.
--
-- The scope is a parameter and not a `List Decl` prelude on purpose: two
-- `List Decl` arguments are positionally interchangeable and `stampTyHead`'s
-- immunity rule makes a wrong first stamp PERMANENT (`stampFlatTyOrigins`'
-- comment in `frontend/resolve.mdk` records that hazard costing a defect once
-- already).  Build it with `externTyOriginScope`, which owns the derivation of
-- WHY one scope is right for this population on both arms.
--
-- ⚠️ `externTyOriginScope []` at a call site is not a shrug: it is the same
-- historical "prelude boundary unknown" answer from that path when its
-- `coreProg0` is `[]` — those callers have already FLATTENED the prelude into the
-- program they check, so the prelude's own `Option`/`Ordering`/`Result`
-- declarations are unstamped on that path too and the two sides stay symmetric.
-- The builtin layer is still supplied there, so it is strictly more identity than
-- before, never a claim the driver cannot justify.
--
-- 🚨 FILTER BEFORE STAMPING, AND THAT ORDER IS PERFORMANCE, NOT STYLE.  The first
-- cut here was `externSchemesGo (stampTyOrigins scope decls)`, which is a no-op
-- difference for `runtimeDecls` (every element IS a `DExtern`) and wrong-shaped at
-- the `checkBodyImpl` call site, where `decls` is the WHOLE MODULE.  Read the
-- chain rather than take this on trust: `stampTyOrigins` is `map
-- (stampDeclTyOrigins scope)`, and `stampDeclTyOrigins` is `mapOriginsInDecl`,
-- i.e. a full `mapTyInDecl` REBUILD of the decl — so that shape reconstructs every
-- declaration in the program and then discards all but the externs, in a stage
-- `compiler/AGENTS.md` calls GC-bound.  Filtering first makes the walk
-- proportional to the externs, which is all this function reads.
--
-- ⚠️ THE MECHANISM ABOVE IS READ OFF THE SOURCE; THE MAGNITUDE WAS NOT MEASURED.
-- No before/after allocation number was taken, so this is a shape argument, not a
-- perf result — do not cite it as one.  It needs no number to be the right shape:
-- rebuilding what you are about to throw away is never the cheaper branch.
externSchemes : ModuleCtx ->
  OrdMap TyConOrigin ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, Scheme)
externSchemes mctx scope decls =
  let ds = externDecls decls
  -- #2074: THIS function is the stdlib extern CATALOG builder — its eight call
  -- sites all pass `runtimeDecls`, and `userExternSchemes` (below) is the
  -- declaration-site sibling that handles the program under check.  So this is
  -- the one seam where the set of names the emitter's `isAnyExtern` ladder will
  -- answer for is known, and it is recorded here rather than re-derived.  See
  -- `ffiIsBuiltinExternName` for what reads it and why.
  noteBuiltinExternNames mctx.mcGraph ds
  externSchemesGo mctx (stampTyOrigins scope ds)

-- Union, never replace: a driver may build the catalog seed more than once per
-- process (`medaka test` runs several engines), and a later call carrying a
-- shorter list must not shrink the set a guard is reading.
--
-- Each row records the catalog's written signature and the positions of its
-- argument binders, which `ffiCheckExternsShadow` and
-- `ffiCheckExternsCatalogRow` (#2163) both read: one map, so the two rules about
-- the same declaration cannot read two tables that fell out of step.
--
-- The signature's rows stay as written labels, not `List Atom`.  `atomsOfWritten`
-- resolves each bare label against `GraphCtx.effectDomains`, and this seam can
-- run before that registry is populated, so both sides are lifted at the check
-- seam, where the registry is the same for both.
--
-- Union means a re-seed REWRITES an existing row rather than skipping it — the two
-- seeds are the same `runtimeDecls` list, so the value is the same pair either
-- way, and preferring the later write keeps the map's contents a function of the
-- last seed rather than of call order.
noteBuiltinExternNames : GraphCtx -> List Decl -> Unit
noteBuiltinExternNames gctx ds =
  gctx.builtinExternNamesRef :=
    externSigsToMap ds gctx.builtinExternNamesRef.value

-- #2072 (epic #2070, R2): the DECLARATION-site sibling of `externSchemes`.
--
-- The eight `externSchemes … runtimeDecls` call sites above build the STDLIB EXTERN
-- CATALOG's schemes: `stdlib/runtime.mdk`'s 138 `extern` lines (derived: `grep -c
-- '^extern ' stdlib/runtime.mdk`), whose written rows ARE the effect vocabulary.
-- They must stay byte-identical.
--
-- THIS one is the `checkBodyImpl` call, which processes whichever program is under
-- check — normally a USER file, where an `extern f : Int -> <> Int` used to type as
-- a pure `Int -> Int`: a one-line escape hatch through the whole effect system.
--
-- 🚨 THIS FUNCTION NO LONGER REWRITES A DECLARED ROW (F1, epic #2070).  Its
-- predecessor STAMPED `FFI` into the terminal row whenever the label was absent,
-- which meant the declared row was never checked against what is enforced —
-- `<Net "a.com/*">` silently became `<FFI, Net "a.com/*">` exactly as `<>`
-- silently became `<FFI>`.  A signature that says one thing and types as another
-- is the defect, not the missing atom.  The honesty requirement is now a CHECK
-- (`ffiCheckExternsLabelled`, run beside the crossable guard at the same
-- `checkBodyImpl` seam): a user extern's terminal row must ALREADY name `FFI`,
-- whatever else it names, or the declaration is a located type error.  The scheme
-- built here is the declaration verbatim.
--
-- `mode` is still taken because every caller has one and the sibling guards read
-- it; the ownership gate now lives entirely on those guards.
userExternSchemes : ModuleCtx ->
  OrdMap TyConOrigin ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, Scheme)
userExternSchemes mctx scope decls =
  externSchemesGo mctx (stampTyOrigins scope (externDecls decls))

-- ── canonical checked FFI rows for backend input (#2174/#2135) ─────────────
--
-- A typed emitter must not reinterpret the written heads of `DExtern`: the five
-- declaration guards above have already expanded aliases through the current
-- module's alias table, and accepting one shape while lowering another is exactly
-- #2174's check/build split.  These two exported producers reuse the SAME helper
-- body (`expandAliasHeadTyWith`) against the alias carrier that survives the
-- relevant typed path:
--
--   * flat typed probes derive one side-effect-free stage-K `DataEnv` from their
--     already-stamped declaration list;
--   * module emitters read `DeclEnvs.deData.deAliases`, projected at each module's
--     own ordinal by the same visibility machinery the live typecheck used.
--
-- The builtin subtraction is a lookup in the extern catalog `noteBuiltinExternNames`
-- seeds.  These producers run after their drive, so they read the catalog from the
-- interners, which hold the catalog of the graph record most recently minted or restored.  Lowering
-- therefore no longer independently scans `runtimeDecls` to decide whether a checked
-- declaration routes as a builtin.
-- Typed emitters use these canonical producers for checked extern facts.
export
checkedFfiExternTypeNames : List Decl ->
  List (String, (List (String, String), (List String, String)))
checkedFfiExternTypeNames decls =
  let denv = dataEnvFromDeclsGo 0 decls emptyDataEnv
  ffiExternTypeRowsWith
    interners.value.externCatalog.value
    (aliasOwnAt 0 denv.deAliases)
    decls

export
checkedFfiExternTypeNamesModules : DriveOutputs ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, (List (String, String), (List String, String)))
checkedFfiExternTypeNamesModules o modules =
  flatMap
    (checkedFfiExternTypeNamesModule interners.value.externCatalog.value o)
    modules

checkedFfiExternTypeNamesModule : OrdMap (Ty, List (String, Int)) ->
  DriveOutputs ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, (List (String, String), (List String, String)))
checkedFfiExternTypeNamesModule catalog o (mid, decls) =
  let cur = ordOfIndex mid o.doOrdIndex
  let aliases =
    aliasOwnAt cur o.doFfiAliases ++ aliasUniverseAt cur o.doFfiAliases
  ffiExternTypeRowsWith catalog aliases decls

-- The current module's aliases arrive in front of its imported alias universe in
-- `checkBodyImpl`.  `DataEnv.deAliases` is already reverse declaration order, so
-- this filter preserves the exact first-match lookup order without rebuilding a
-- second registry.
aliasOwnAt : Int -> List (TabKey, AliasDecl) -> List (TabKey, (List String, Ty))
aliasOwnAt _ [] = []
aliasOwnAt cur ((key, ad) :: rest)
  | ad.adOrd == cur = (key, (ad.adParams, ad.adRhs)) :: aliasOwnAt cur rest
  | otherwise = aliasOwnAt cur rest

-- The pre-#1280 body, unchanged, over the ORIGIN-STAMPED list.  It is split out
-- rather than folded into `externSchemes` so that the recursion cannot re-enter
-- the origin-stamping walk on its own tail — the shape that turns a one-pass `map`
-- into a quadratic, which is the defect class `compiler/AGENTS.md` opens with.
--
-- It took a `stamp : Bool` until F1 (epic #2070), selecting whether to rewrite the
-- declared type with an `FFI` atom.  Both call sites now pass the declaration
-- through verbatim — the stdlib catalog always did, and the user-declaration site
-- checks the row instead of rewriting it (`ffiCheckExternsLabelled`) — so the
-- parameter is gone rather than left as a constant nobody may vary.
externSchemesGo : ModuleCtx -> List Decl -> List (String, Scheme)
externSchemesGo mctx decls =
  map ((n, ty) => (n, sigToScheme mctx ty)) (directExternSigs decls)

-- DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 2: skip when [mname] is ALREADY registered in THIS pass.
-- The ids must be the SAME quantified ids the call-site instantiation maps through
-- its subst (see the method carrier doc).  The module path's checkModuleFullImpl
-- can construct method rows more than once per pass (env setup + Flat name discovery
-- inference re-resolving schemes), and a SECOND registration prepends DIFFERENT
-- freshly-instantiated carrier → first-match would select metadata from the wrong
-- scheme build.  Keeping the FIRST (env-setup) registration — whose scheme is the one
-- lookupVar returns at call sites — restores the flat path's single-registration
-- invariant.  `freshModuleCtx` clears methodPredicateSlotsRef between modules, so this only
-- de-dups WITHIN a pass.

sigsOf : List Decl -> List (String, Ty)
sigsOf [] = []
sigsOf ((DTypeSig _ n ty _) :: rest) = (n, ty) :: sigsOf rest
sigsOf ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) = sigsOf (d :: rest)
sigsOf (_ :: rest) = sigsOf rest

-- The Module arm's import overlay, registered over [env] without elaborating a
-- second time what the populations already hold.  A public data declaration is
-- elaborated once, by `appendDataUniverse` at the end of its own module's arm, and
-- an importer reads that payload back by declaration identity.  The overlay's other
-- decls (aliases, newtypes, attributed or private data, a declaration with no
-- identity) are registered exactly as `registerAllData` registers them.
--
-- A served declaration skips `recordDeclKinds` and `registerVariants`' kind, atom
-- and variance rows: it is public data of an earlier module, so stage K's seed
-- (`declEnvSeedDataUniverse`) already holds those rows under the same identity keys.
-- Its field-owner keys are still added, as `registerRecordInfoKeyed` adds them.
-- The overlay is folded in list order, because its tables are last-write-wins and
-- its named rows come last (`importedCtorTypeDeclsLastWins`).
-- [prog] is the reading module's own declarations.
registerImportOverlay : TcEnv -> List Decl -> List Decl -> TcEnv
registerImportOverlay env prog overlay =
  seedParamPolarityFixpointScoped env.teDyn.dcModule overlay overlay
  let ctorPop = env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph.crossRun.universeCtorPop.value
  let recordPop = env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph.crossRun.universeRecordPop.value
  let served = map (d => (d, overlayCachedRows ctorPop recordPop d)) overlay
  fold
    (_ p => match snd p
      None => recordDeclKinds env.teDyn.dcModule (fst p)
      Some _ => ())
    ()
    served
  let env1 =
    fold
      (e p => if isAliasDecl (fst p) then registerData e (fst p) else e)
      env
      served
  -- Cycles out before any field elaborates, as in `registerDeclsAliasesFirst`.
  rejectCyclicAliases env.teDyn.dcModule prog
  fold
    (e p => match snd p
      None => if isAliasDecl (fst p) then e else registerData e (fst p)
      Some rows => registerCachedRows e rows)
    env1
    served

-- The cached constructor schemes and record infos of [d], in variant order, when
-- every one of its constructors has a population row under its own identity; any
-- miss declines for the whole declaration.  Only `DData` has rows to serve, and
-- declining is never wrong: a declined decl is registered by `registerData`,
-- which is where a new `Decl` constructor has to be decided, so the final
-- clause covers every other constructor rather than naming each.
overlayCachedRows : SpellingPop Scheme ->
  SpellingPop RecordInfo ->
  Decl ->
  Option (List (String, Scheme), List (String, RecordInfo))
overlayCachedRows ctorPop recordPop (DData { dataCtors, dataOrigin, ... }) =
  cachedVariantRows ctorPop recordPop dataOrigin dataCtors
overlayCachedRows _ _ _ = None

cachedVariantRows : SpellingPop Scheme ->
  SpellingPop RecordInfo ->
  TyConOrigin ->
  List Variant ->
  Option (List (String, Scheme), List (String, RecordInfo))
cachedVariantRows _ _ _ [] = Some ([], [])
cachedVariantRows ctorPop recordPop o ((Variant cn payload) :: rest) = do
  ident <- mkIdent NsCtor o cn
  s <- spLookupIdent ident cn ctorPop
  recs <- cachedRecordRow recordPop ident cn payload
  later <- cachedVariantRows ctorPop recordPop o rest
  Some ((cn, s) :: fst later, recs ++ snd later)

-- A named-field variant's cached `RecordInfo`, as a zero- or one-row list; a
-- positional variant has none to look up.
cachedRecordRow : SpellingPop RecordInfo ->
  Ident ->
  String ->
  ConPayload ->
  Option (List (String, RecordInfo))
cachedRecordRow recordPop ident cn (ConNamed _ _) =
  map (ri => [(cn, ri)]) (spLookupIdent ident cn recordPop)
cachedRecordRow _ _ _ _ = Some []

registerCachedRows : TcEnv ->
  (List (String, Scheme), List (String, RecordInfo)) ->
  TcEnv
registerCachedRows env (ctors, recs) =
  fold (_ r => registerCachedRecord env.teDyn.dcModule (fst r) (snd r)) () recs
  fold (e c => addCtor e (fst c) (snd c)) env ctors

registerCachedRecord : ModuleCtx -> String -> RecordInfo -> Unit
registerCachedRecord mctx key ri =
  mctx.mcCells.recordByNameRef :=
    omInsert key ri mctx.mcCells.recordByNameRef.value
  mctx.mcCells.fieldOwnersRef :=
    addFieldOwners key (recordFieldNames ri) mctx.mcCells.fieldOwnersRef.value

-- ── dependency-ordered, SCC-merged letrec processing ──────────────────────
-- The prelude has forward references and mutual recursion throughout, so groups
-- must be type-checked in dependency order (a callee generalized before its
-- callers instantiate it polymorphically) with mutually-recursive cycles merged
-- into one letrec group (generalized together).  Mirrors the reference's
-- order_groups_by_deps + process_letrec_group (Phase 136).

-- ── Tarjan strongly-connected components (O(V+E)) ─────────────────────────
-- Replaces the former all-pairs-reachability + mutual-reachability SCC + topo
-- sort, which was O(n^3) in a module's top-level binding count and dominated
-- the multi-module typecheck (check_modules).  Emits SCCs in dependency-first
-- order (a "sink" component — no outside dependencies — finishes and is emitted
-- first), exactly what processSCCs needs.  State is held in module-level refs,
-- reset at the start of each run (processTopGroups runs sequentially per module).

-- sig-name presence set (which top-level names carry a type signature), set once
-- per module in `processTopGroups` so `memberPeelSource` does an
-- O(log n) presence check instead of an O(sigs) `lookupAssoc` scan per SCC member
-- (was O(members·sigs) over the whole compiler). Module-level ref, matching the
-- `currentFn`/`curEffect` idiom; processing is sequential per module.

sigNamesToSet : List (String, Ty) -> OrdMap Unit -> OrdMap Unit
sigNamesToSet [] m = m
sigNamesToSet ((n, _) :: rest) m = sigNamesToSet rest (omInsert n () m)

-- PERF: name → declared signature, the same `sigs` assoc list as an index.  Three
-- sites (preunifySigsEx, checkEffectEscape, checkSigConstraintOne) did
-- `lookupAssoc m sigs` — an O(N) scan of EVERY top-level signature, run per member
-- of every group, so O(N²) over a large file.  Companion to `sigNameSetRef`, seeded
-- from the same `sigs` in processTopGroups.
--
-- Built from the REVERSED list: `omFromPairs` is last-write-wins, while the
-- `lookupAssoc` it replaces was first-match-wins, so reversing preserves which
-- signature a duplicated name resolves to.

processTopGroups : TcEnv ->
  List (String, Ty) ->
  List (String, (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List String ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  (List (String, Scheme), OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)))
processTopGroups env sigs defs names exported =
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.sigNameSetRef := sigNamesToSet sigs omEmpty
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.sigTyMapRef := omFromPairs (reverseL sigs) omEmpty
  let grouped = groupClauses defs
  let ordered = tarjanSCCs names (depGraphMap names grouped)
  let result = processSCCs env sigs grouped ordered
  resolvePendingJoins env.teDyn 0
  let failures =
    EffectSolver.closeRootAuthorities
      env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.effectSummaries
      env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidAuthvarsRef.value
      (exportedRootCells exported (fst result))
      (widenOpenedFor env.teDyn.dcModule)
  -- read again after the close: a cell the root solved no longer appears
  let unsolved = rootCellsOf exported (fst result)
  let unsolvedIds = fold (acc (_, ids) => IdMap.union acc ids) Tip unsolved
  reportEffectSummaryFailures
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    (filterList (f => not (failureMentions unsolvedIds f)) failures)
  let _ =
    fold
      (seen u => reportUnsolvedExport env.teDyn.dcModule grouped seen u)
      Tip
      unsolved
  result

-- The authority cells an exported binding's published type holds unquantified:
-- a binding the value restriction keeps monomorphic leaves its type's cells to
-- the module root.
exportedRootCells : OrdMap Unit -> List (String, Scheme) -> Map Int Unit
exportedRootCells exported schemes =
  fold (acc (_, ids) => IdMap.union acc ids) Tip (rootCellsOf exported schemes)

rootCellsOf : OrdMap Unit ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, Map Int Unit)
rootCellsOf exported schemes =
  flatMap
    ((name, scheme) =>
      if omHasKey name exported then match unquantifiedAuthIds scheme
        Tip => []
        ids => [(name, ids)]
      else
        [])
    schemes

failureMentions : Map Int Unit -> SummaryFailure ObligationSite -> Bool
failureMentions Tip _ = False
failureMentions ids failure = match failure.esfAuthority
  Some (lo, hi) =>
    anyList (c => IdMap.has (authvarId c) ids) (authVars lo ++ authVars hi)
  None => False

-- An exported binding whose published type (or residual context) still holds
-- a cell after the root solved: nothing in its module bounds it below, and an
-- importer cannot supply it later.  Each cell is reported once, at the first
-- exported binding that holds it; the obligations over it are this failure,
-- not separate ones.  Located at the binding's first located expression.
reportUnsolvedExport : ModuleCtx ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  Map Int Unit ->
  (String, Map Int Unit) ->
  Map Int Unit
reportUnsolvedExport mctx grouped seen (name, ids) =
  if IdMap.foldlWithKey (acc id _ => acc && IdMap.has id seen) True ids then
    seen
  else
    let loc = match clausesOf name grouped
      (_, body) :: _ => firstExprLoc body
      [] => None
    pushTypeErrorAt
      mctx
      "T-EXPORT-UNSOLVED-AUTHORITY"
      loc
      "exported binding `\{name}` has an authority in its type that nothing in this module determines; give `\{name}`, or the value it uses, a type signature that names the authority"
    IdMap.union seen ids
-- process each SCC as a letrec group in topological order.  The clause map is
-- threaded through and returned: a Module-arm group's clauses come back MARKED
-- (`processSCC`), and `checkBodyImpl` writes them back into the module's tree.
processSCCs : TcEnv ->
  List (String, Ty) ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List (List String) ->
  (List (String, Scheme), OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)))
processSCCs env _ grouped [] = ([], grouped)
processSCCs env sigs grouped (scc :: rest) =
  let (env2, schemes, grouped2) = processSCC env sigs grouped scc
  let (restSchemes, grouped3) = processSCCs env2 sigs grouped2 rest
  (schemes ++ restSchemes, grouped3)

processSCC : TcEnv ->
  List (String, Ty) ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List String ->
  (TcEnv, List (String, Scheme), OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)))
processSCC outer sigs grouped0 members =
  -- The group's own checks (generalization, defaulting, its signatures'
  -- coverage) are located at its members' clause bodies.
  let env = envAtIfSome (membersSpanLoc grouped0 members) outer
  -- Marking on the schedule (ARCH §E): a Module-arm group is marked HERE, after
  -- every group it depends on has generalized, with the dict-name set as it stands;
  -- inference reads the stamped twin of the marked clauses.  Flat (`None`): the
  -- caller marked and stamped the whole program, the map is used as given.
  let marking = env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.markingRef.value
  let grouped = match marking
    Some mm => markGroupClauses mm env members grouped0
    None => grouped0
  let groupedStamped = match marking
    Some mm => stampGroupClauses mm members grouped
    None => grouped
  enterLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  beginEffectSummaryScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  let oblN0 = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls
  let callN0 = wMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.obls
  let dictN0 = goalsMark env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph ()
  let placeholders = map (m => (m, freshVar env.teDyn.dcModule ())) members
  let scopedMembers =
    scopeMembers
      env.teDyn.dcModule
      (captureScope env.teDyn.dcModule ())
      groupedStamped
      placeholders
  let (regs, sigTvMaps) = preunifySigsEx env.teDyn.dcModule sigs scopedMembers
  let savedRigidEffects = env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidEffvarsRef.value
  let savedRigidAuths = env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidAuthvarsRef.value
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidEffvarsRef :=
    fold
      (roots member =>
        IdMap.foldlWithKey
          (acc id _ => IdMap.set id () acc)
          roots
          member.smDeclaredRoots.value)
      savedRigidEffects
      scopedMembers
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidAuthvarsRef :=
    fold
      (roots member =>
        IdMap.foldlWithKey
          (acc id _ => IdMap.set id () acc)
          roots
          member.smDeclaredAuths.value)
      savedRigidAuths
      scopedMembers
  let env2 = extendBindings env (map scopedMemberBinding scopedMembers)
  -- P0-19 (constrained-receiver arm): publish THIS group's `=>`-constraint vars for
  -- the duration of body inference, so a definer-shadow method occurrence on one of
  -- them (`neq x y = not (eq x y)` under `neq : Eq a => …`) is seen as a DISPATCH and
  -- not monomorphised to the shadowing standalone's declared domain.
  -- `gcMonos` answers a SPELLING question (`dictVarSatisfiesIface`
  -- compares interface NAMES), so U1b (#1482) projects back to the name here rather than
  -- widening a second ref for a reader that would only throw the identity away.
  let groupCtx = GroupCtx {
    gcMonos = flatMap (r => flatMap ifaceMonoName (regIfaceMonosOf r)) regs,
    gcPendingEscapes = Ref [],
  }
  let groupEnv = { env2 | teDyn = { env2.teDyn | dcGroup = Some groupCtx } }
  -- #837: the schemeObligationsRef snapshot/restore window is RETIRED.  It existed
  -- because registerLocalScheme entries were NAME-KEYED and body-scoped: a prelude
  -- where-helper named `g` (core's `find`) leaked obligations onto an unrelated
  -- top-level `g` in a later body (a phantom `Num a =>` on types/numlit_bidir's
  -- signed fn).  Registration/lookup now key by (name, binding-id), so a top-level
  -- `g`'s use keys (g, its-id) and can never match a where-helper `g`'s (g, 0) entry.
  inferMembers groupEnv sigs groupedStamped scopedMembers
  fold
    (_ member => checkRecursiveValue env.teDyn member.smOwned)
    ()
    scopedMembers
  resolvePendingJoins env.teDyn env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentLevel.value
  let residuals =
    finishEffectSummaryScopeKeeping
      env.teDyn.dcModule
      (quantifiableAuthorities env2 groupedStamped scopedMembers)
      (fold (acc member => memberEffectRoots member acc) Tip scopedMembers)
      (fold
        (acc member =>
          borrowedEffectCells env.teDyn.dcModule member.smOwned.bsValue acc)
        Tip
        scopedMembers)
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidEffvarsRef := savedRigidEffects
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.rigidAuthvarsRef := savedRigidAuths
  checkSigsTooGeneral env.teDyn.dcModule sigTvMaps
  checkSigsFixedByExpansive env2 groupedStamped scopedMembers sigTvMaps
  registerConstraintRegs env.teDyn.dcModule regs
  flushPendingEscapes env.teDyn.dcModule (Some groupCtx)
  exitLevel env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  -- PLAN.md #11 (locked §0.2): GROUP-LEVEL + per-binding Num defaulting.  Ground
  -- every ambiguous Num-only var to Int before this group generalizes.  A live var
  -- carried by a declared signature stays caller-determined across both passes;
  -- an argument-reachable var also stays polymorphic.  Body-only vars (`n = tag [1,2,3]`) still ground.  Scoped to
  -- THIS group's newly-added obligations (prepended at the front) so an earlier
  -- group's already-generalized var is never touched.
  let protectedSigIds = sccProtectedSignatureIds sigTvMaps
  let addedObls = wWindow env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.implObls oblN0
  let groupObls = addedObls ++ callUOblsWindow env.teDyn.dcModule callN0
  -- Determination holds rigid exactly the variables this group does not own: the
  -- declared ones and those in an argument position of a member's type, which the
  -- caller supplies.  A result-position or body-local variable is the group's.
  let unownedIds =
    omFromNames
      (map intToString (flatMap monoArgUnboundIds (map snd placeholders)))
      protectedSigIds
  improveByUniqueImpl env.teDyn protectedSigIds addedObls
  improveByUniqueImpl
    env.teDyn
    protectedSigIds
    (callUOblsWindow env.teDyn.dcModule callN0)
  determineByUniqueInstance env.teDyn.dcModule unownedIds groupObls
  -- #23: also ground an ambiguous Num var that reaches this group ONLY through a
  -- constrained-callee instantiation (its Num obligation lives on the call/dict deltas,
  -- not pendingImplObligations).  See numCallObls/numDictObls.  The synthetic entries
  -- feed ONLY the Num-defaulting search; addedObls alone still drives the
  -- ambiguous-constraint/scheme-obligation registration below.
  let defaultObls = addedObls ++ calleeNumObls env.teDyn.dcModule callN0 dictN0
  let sccGuard = NumGuard {
    ngGoals = groupObls,
    ngRigidIds = protectedSigIds,
    ngOuterBelow = None,
  }
  let withheld = finalizeNumBoundary
    env.teDyn
    NumBoundary {
      nbDisposition =
        NumBoundaryOwnedScc (env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentLevel.value + 1),
      nbMembers = map snd placeholders,
      nbSurvivingIds =
        tiedSurvivors
          env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph
          (map snd placeholders)
          sccGuard
          protectedSigIds,
      nbDefaultObls = defaultObls,
      nbAmbiguityObls = addedObls,
      nbGuard = sccGuard,
    }  -- RETPOS soundness
  -- Defaulting is a substitution like any other, so the goals it changed are
  -- improved and determined once more.
  improveByUniqueImpl env.teDyn protectedSigIds groupObls
  determineByUniqueInstance env.teDyn.dcModule unownedIds groupObls
  checkSigVarGoals
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    DeclaredBySignature
    protectedSigIds
    sigTvMaps
    groupObls
  let schemes =
    sccSchemes
      env2
      sigs
      groupedStamped
      (isLetrecGroup members)
      residuals
      0
      scopedMembers
  let scopedSchemes = alignScopedSchemes scopedMembers schemes
  checkSigsOverExpansive env2 groupedStamped sigTvMaps
  -- #23 two-hop forward: also record constraints FORWARDED from a constrained
  -- callee (living on the call delta, never in addedObls) into schemeObligationsRef,
  -- so a caller of this group re-instantiates them and its own group can ground the
  -- shared ambiguous var.
  -- The CALL delta is passed RAW (it already carries each predicate's joint `List
  -- Mono`); registerSchemeObligations maps it with forwardedVecObls so a multi-param
  -- predicate survives whole.  #838 I5: a constrained callee's DICT slot is forwarded
  -- through THIS same call delta — inferDictAtFound records it as a 1-ary PCallSlot
  -- obligation (recordCallObligations, beside pushDictApp), so the old `allDictObls`
  -- re-derivation off the dict delta was a redundant duplicate (deduped away) and is
  -- retired; addedObls now carries only the real pendingImplObligations delta.
  let callOblsDelta = callOblsWindow env.teDyn.dcModule callN0
  registerSchemeObligations
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef.value
    sigTvMaps
    callOblsDelta
    addedObls
    schemes
  let liveIds = liveMemberIds env.teDyn.dcModule (map snd placeholders)
  rejectUndeterminedVectors
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    unownedIds
    liveIds
    addedObls
    (callUOblsWindow env.teDyn.dcModule callN0
      ++ nestedRequireGoals env.teDyn.dcModule unownedIds liveIds groupObls)
  rejectWithheldAmbiguity env.teDyn.dcModule unownedIds withheld groupObls
  -- SIGNATURE CONSTRAINT SOUNDNESS: a signed member whose body induces an
  -- interface constraint on one of its own quantified vars that its declared `=>`
  -- context (superclass-closed) does NOT cover severs the constraint for callers
  -- → wrong-dictionary dispatch (a silent wrong answer).  Reject at the def-site.
  -- Runs on every path; the census over compiler+stdlib+sqlite was 0 new failures.
  checkSigConstraintCoverage
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    regs
    sigs
    sigTvMaps
    callOblsDelta
    addedObls
    schemes
  registerInferredConstraints
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    sigs
    callOblsDelta
    addedObls
    scopedSchemes
  -- From here on every instance-head matcher holds this group's declared variables
  -- rigid; the group's own generalization above reads them as free variables, so a
  -- goal `checkSigVarGoals` rejected is not reported a second time.
  declareRigidVars env.teDyn.dcModule (omKeys protectedSigIds)
  -- The group's own occurrences of the members it just promoted (see
  -- `markRecursiveOccurrences`); Flat marks nothing here.
  let groupedOut = match marking
    Some mm =>
      markRecursiveOccurrences
        env.teDyn.dcModule
        mm
        placeholders
        scopedMembers
        grouped
    None => grouped
  match marking
    Some _ =>
      recordTopGrants
        env.teDyn.dcModule.mcGraph
        env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentModuleRef.value
        schemes
        groupedOut
    None => ()
  (extendTermBindings outer schemes, schemes, groupedOut)

scopedMemberBinding : ScopedMember -> (String, EnvBinding)
scopedMemberBinding member =
  let binding =
    optionOr
      (ordinaryEnvBinding
        (Forall
          []
          []
          []
          []
          (memberForce member)
          member.smOwned.bsRecursiveValue))
      member.smRecursive.value
  (
    member.smName,
    bindingWithStandalone binding (LocalStandalone, binding.ebValue),
  )

scopeMembers : ModuleCtx ->
  ScopeId ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List (String, Mono) ->
  List ScopedMember
scopeMembers mctx _ _ [] = []
scopeMembers mctx parent grouped ((name, mono) :: rest) =
  let sid =
    Scopes.freshScope
      (currentScopeStore mctx.mcGraph ())
      (Some parent)
      mctx.mcCells.currentLevel.value
      (Scopes.scopeFrame (currentScopeStore mctx.mcGraph ()) parent).sfModuleId
      (BindingOwner name)
  -- This is the evaluator's topGroupValue distinction: a singleton nullary
  -- clause is a thunk, including a thunk whose result happens to be a function.
  let clauses = clausesOf name grouped
  let arity = match clauses
    (pats, _) :: _ => listLen pats
    [] => 0
  let owned = newBindingSummary arity (freshVar mctx) (freshEffectSummary mctx)
  let force = match clauses
    [([], _)] => Some owned.bsForce
    _ => None
  ScopedMember {
      smName = name,
      smMono = mono,
      smOwned = owned,
      smDeclaredRoots = Ref Tip,
      smDeclaredAuths = Ref Tip,
      smSigned = Ref False,
      smRecursive = Ref None,
      smForce = force,
      smForceBound = Ref None,
      smScope = sid,
    }
    :: scopeMembers mctx parent grouped rest

alignScopedSchemes : List ScopedMember ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List ScopedScheme
alignScopedSchemes [] [] = []
alignScopedSchemes (member :: members) ((name, scheme) :: schemes)
  | member.smName == name =
    ScopedScheme {
        ssName = name,
        ssScheme = scheme,
        ssScope = member.smScope,
      }
      :: alignScopedSchemes members schemes
  | otherwise = panic "scope/member scheme alignment mismatch"
alignScopedSchemes _ _ = panic "scope/member scheme arity mismatch"

-- Pass B: infer each member's clauses, unifying with its placeholder.
-- Phase 73 (bidirectional, signature-driven parameter typing): a member WITH a
-- signature peels its declared arrow types onto the clause parameter patterns
-- BEFORE inferring the body, so the body sees concrete parameter types (a
-- receiver-directed field access `f p = p.x` or a `String`-indexed `s.[0]`
-- resolves from the signature alone).  [peel] is Some(placeholder) for a signed
-- member, None otherwise; the placeholder already holds the sig instantiation
-- (preunifySigsEx unified it), and its shared tyvars accumulate unifications
-- across clauses exactly as the oracle's `sig_t` does.  Purely additive: the
-- per-clause `unify v arrow` below imposes the same equalities anyway.
inferMembers : TcEnv ->
  List (String, Ty) ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List ScopedMember ->
  Unit
inferMembers env _ _ [] = ()
inferMembers outer sigs grouped (member :: rest) =
  let m = member.smName
  -- A member's own checks (its signature against its value, its forcing
  -- bound, its effect escape) are located at its clauses' bodies.
  let env = envAtIfSome (membersSpanLoc grouped [m]) outer
  let v = member.smMono
  openScope env.teDyn.dcModule member.smScope
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentFn := m
  let peel = memberPeelSource env.teDyn.dcModule sigs m v
  let effs = inferMemberClauses env peel member.smOwned (clausesOf m grouped)
  -- The signature is checked against the value the clauses return.
  if member.smSigned.value then
    unifyInto
      (dynAtIfSome (joinLocs (clauseSpans valueSpanLoc grouped [m])) env.teDyn)
      True
      member.smMono
      member.smOwned.bsValue
  match member.smForceBound.value
    Some bound =>
      fold
        (_ cl => recordFileBound env.teDyn.dcModule (snd cl) bound)
        ()
        (clausesOf m grouped)
      rowInto
        env.teDyn
        True
        (effrowNorm bound)
        (effrowNorm (optionOr pureRow member.smForce))
    None => ()
  env.teDyn.dcModule.mcCells.currentFn := ""
  closeScope env.teDyn.dcModule ()
  -- A value signature constrains the returned value, not the cost of forcing
  -- its initializer. That cost travels on the binding and is checked at uses.
  match member.smForce
    None => checkEffectEscape env.teDyn sigs m effs
    Some _ => ()
  inferMembers outer sigs grouped rest

-- The span of the named members' clause bodies (`exprSpanLoc`), joined.
membersSpanLoc : OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) -> List String -> Option Loc
membersSpanLoc grouped names = joinLocs (clauseSpans exprSpanLoc grouped names)

-- [span] of each clause body of the named members.
clauseSpans : (Expr -> Option Loc) ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List String ->
  List Loc
clauseSpans span grouped names =
  flatMap
    (m =>
      flatMap
        (cl => option [] (l => [l]) (span (snd cl)))
        (clausesOf m grouped))
    names

-- the peel source for a member: Some placeholder if it has a signature (peel the
-- declared arrow domains onto the clause params), None otherwise
memberPeelSource : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, Ty) ->
  String ->
  Mono ->
  Option Mono
memberPeelSource mctx sigs m v =
  map (_ => v) (omLookup m mctx.mcCells.sigNameSetRef.value)

-- Runs in a carry of [prev], the caller's record, in its graph record with a fresh
-- graph run.
methodReturnProbeStamp : ModuleCtx ->
  MethodSchemeRow ->
  MethodSchemeRow ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, String, String)
methodReturnProbeStamp prev ownedRow foreignRow impls =
  let gctx = GraphCtx { prev.mcGraph | graphRun = freshGraphRun () }
  let mctx = carryModuleCtx gctx prev
  let scope = methodReturnProbeScope mctx "return-owner-stamper"
  let request = methodReturnProbeRequest mctx ownedRow
  methodReturnProbeSpelling mctx foreignRow
  let env = buildFlatImplEnv impls
  mctx.mcCells.bodyImplEnvRef := env
  let tagRef = Ref RNone
  let implRef = Ref []
  let resultMono = stripArrows request.mrrOccurrence
  resolveExactReturnSite
    (siteFor mctx)
    omEmpty
    "make"
    tagRef
    implRef
    resultMono
    request
    ""
    None
    scope
  let goals = optionOr [] (methodReturnArgs gctx request)
  let ownedKey =
    match ieSelectRowByIface (siteFor mctx) env ownedRow.msrIface goals
      Some selected => rowRouteKey selected
      None => ""
  let foreignKey =
    match ieSelectRowByIface (siteFor mctx) env foreignRow.msrIface goals
      Some selected => rowRouteKey selected
      None => ""
  (methodReturnProbeRouteKey tagRef.value, ownedKey, foreignKey)

-- The request is created before the spelling table is pointed at Foreign.  The checker
-- must still reject a graph containing only Foreign's impl, its positive control must
-- accept Owner's impl, and the stamper must select Owner's distinct route.
methodReturnOwnershipProbe : Unit -> (List String, List String, Bool, Bool)
methodReturnOwnershipProbe _ =
  let ownedGraph = probeGraphCtx ()
  let ownedDecl =
    methodReturnProbeIface
      "return-owner"
      "Owner"
      (TyFun (tyConBuiltin "Unit" None) (TyVar "a"))
  let foreignDecl =
    methodReturnProbeIface
      "return-foreign"
      "Foreign"
      (TyFun (tyConBuiltin "Unit" None) (TyVar "a"))
  let ownedCtx = initialModuleCtx ownedGraph
  let ownedRow = firstMethodRow (ifaceMethodSchemeRows ownedCtx [ownedDecl])
  let foreignGraph = GraphCtx { ownedGraph | graphRun = freshGraphRun () }
  let foreignCtx = carryModuleCtx foreignGraph ownedCtx
  let foreignRow =
    firstMethodRow (ifaceMethodSchemeRows foreignCtx [foreignDecl])
  let ownedImpl = methodReturnProbeImpl "return-owner" "Owner"
  let foreignImpl = methodReturnProbeImpl "return-foreign" "Foreign"
  let (rejected, rejectedCtx) =
    methodReturnProbeCodes foreignCtx ownedRow [foreignImpl]
  let (accepted, acceptedCtx) =
    methodReturnProbeCodes rejectedCtx ownedRow [foreignImpl, ownedImpl]
  let (actualKey, ownedKey, foreignKey) = methodReturnProbeStamp
    acceptedCtx
    ownedRow
    foreignRow
    [foreignImpl, ownedImpl]
  let observed = (
    rejected,
    accepted,
    actualKey == ownedKey,
    actualKey /= foreignKey,
  )
  observed

-- > methodReturnOwnershipProbe ()
-- (["T-NO-IMPL"], [], True, True)

regIfaceMonosOf : ConstraintReg -> List (IfaceRef, List Mono)
regIfaceMonosOf reg = reg.crIfaceMonos

-- U1b (#1482): project a reg's (interface occurrence, mono) pair back to the SPELLING
-- for `gcMonos`, whose only reader (`dictVarSatisfiesIface`) compares
-- interface NAMES.
-- ⚠️ S-dict-arity-contract: a reg entry is now ONE PREDICATE carrying ALL its bare-tyvar
-- monos, so this fans back out to one (name, mono) pair per variable.  Collapsing the
-- SLOT must not collapse the READ-ONLY census this and `declaredPairOne` feed: they
-- answer "is this variable declared at this interface", not "how many dicts", and
-- dropping `b` from `Ix a b =>` would make a body use at `b` UNCOVERED — an
-- over-rejection of a program that type-checks today.
ifaceMonoName : (IfaceRef, List Mono) -> List (String, Mono)
ifaceMonoName (ir, monos) = map (mono => (ir.irName, mono)) monos

-- Render accumulated type errors as `TYPE ERROR: <msg>` lines (in order of
-- occurrence — typeErrors accumulates newest-first, so reverse).
typeErrorLines : List String -> List String
typeErrorLines [] = []
typeErrorLines (e :: rest) = "TYPE ERROR: " ++ e :: typeErrorLines rest

-- #415 item 1: seed-and-run for the three FLAT runtime-seeded entry points
-- (the `checkOne*` wrappers). They
-- used to repeat this body verbatim under a "Mirrors checkToLinesWithRuntime's
-- side-effects EXACTLY" comment — a mirror obligation enforced only by that comment.
-- They now differ ONLY in what they HARVEST from the accumulators afterwards, so the
-- seeding+coherence they share has exactly one body and cannot drift.
-- ⚠️ trap #5, same as runFinalChecks: coherence sees the driver-staged USER decls only,
-- NEVER `prog`.  A user impl deliberately overrides a seeded prelude impl, so feeding
-- the seeded set to checkCoherence false-positives on the stdlib itself.
-- ── type-aware non-exhaustive-match check (the `check_match` driver) ────────
-- Run the full typecheck pass over [prog] (runtime externs seeded, NO prelude —
-- mirrors the reference's check_program_no_prelude oracle) and return one
-- `Warning: non-exhaustive match …` line per `match` that isn't exhaustive.
-- The oracle is built from the program's own data decls + the syntactic builtins
-- (same set check_match's env-backed oracle has in the no-prelude path).  Diffs
-- against `dev/diagdump.exe --check-match` (the harness sorts).
-- ── diagnostics entry point (compiler/driver/diagnostics.mdk bridge) ────────────
-- Run the full typecheck pass (runtime-seeded) over [prog] and return the
-- accumulated errors and non-exhaustive-match warnings as `TcDiag`s — each
-- carrying its own code, span (B.10.2b / S4), message, and optional help/fix —
-- so diagnostics.mdk maps them straight to structured `Diag` values.
-- ── multi-module typecheck (bootstrap front-end) ───────────────────────────
-- Threads per-module exports in dependency order.  The prelude (core) is checked
-- ONCE and ALL its schemes + ctors seed every module (it's implicitly visible);
-- each compiler module then contributes only its PUBLIC value schemes — private
-- helpers stay module-local, so same-named helpers across modules never collide
-- (the per-module-frame property, without a frame) — plus its public ctors.
-- Returns each module's OWN value schemes, in load order.

-- typecheck one program with seeded value schemes + accumulated data decls, as
-- the prelude's core pass.  Re-registers prior modules' public data declarations
-- (`accData ++ prog`) so imported constructors AND named-field-variant record
-- info (e.g. ast's DInterface) resolve.  The program's own impls are its impl
-- universe, as the tree arm's core pass (`elabModuleStamp`) has them: an impl
-- body's occurrence then routes through the impl's `requires` given on both arms.
-- Returns the module's own value schemes.  [prev] is the record current before the
-- pass, whose oracle and warnings the unseeded pass carries.
checkModuleFull : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List Decl, ModuleCtx)
checkModuleFull prev seedVars accData prog =
  checkModuleFullImpl
    prev.mcGraph
    (checkRunResidue prev)
    ""
    (plainModuleSeed seedVars)
    accData
    prog
    prog

-- E5: like checkModuleFull but takes [implDecls] (core + every EARLIER module — NOT this
-- module: foldModules only grows the accumulator for LATER modules, so a module is never in
-- its own [implDecls]; a caller that needs this module's own decls too must fold [prog] in
-- itself, as the `superDecls` binding in checkBodyImpl does for #1457) as the impl-body
-- inference universe.  It ALSO infers THIS module's parametric impl
-- bodies, so their
-- ARG-position ELEMENT dispatch (`eq`/`compare`/… on the impl head tyvar) reaches
-- inferMethodAt and lands in pendingArgStamps, which elabModuleStamp's
-- resolveArgStamps then stamps RDict (via the requires dict registered by
-- registerImplRequires).  The stage-K ClassEnv gives body inference the interface
-- declarations, while [implDecls] lets a body dispatch against an earlier module's impl.
-- [gctx] and [runSeed] are the drive's graph record and the module's check-run seed,
-- passed to its mint (see `checkBodyImpl`), and the module's record is returned beside
-- its bindings.
checkModuleFullImpl : GraphCtx ->
  CheckRunSeed ->
  String ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List Decl, ModuleCtx)
checkModuleFullImpl gctx runSeed mid seedVars _accData implDecls prog =
  let (schemes, marked, mctx) =
    checkBodyImpl gctx runSeed seedVars mid implDecls prog
  -- #2705 (M2 phase 2): solving is not optional.  Every module a Module-arm driver
  -- checks — `check`, `run`, `build`, the LSP alike — cuts its stamping context
  -- here, at the point its stampers used to run, for the one graph-end drain
  -- (`drainStampQueue`, DICT §6.2 T4).  A driver that queues and never drains
  -- leaves the queue for the next driver entry, so every Module-arm driver ends
  -- with a drain (`driveGraphK`'s, or `checkGraphFinish` on a memo path).
  enqueueStampCtx mctx mid implDecls
  (schemes, marked, mctx)
-- C5: compute this module's standalone-shadow set BEFORE inference, so the
-- inferMethodAt arg-position record can gate on it (`freshModuleCtx` cleared it via
-- pendingRLocalSites, but standaloneValuesRef is set fresh here per module).
--
-- Two flavours of standalone-shadow, BOTH needing RLocal recording so a method
-- call on a no-impl receiver routes to the standalone instead of mis-dispatching:
--   • IMPORTER shadow (`importerShadows`): a name imported from a sibling that is
--     also an interface method (box importing map's `toList`).  Its scheme must be
--     REBOUND to the interface-method scheme (`shadowMethodSchemes`) so the call
--     site types polymorphically; RLocal then redirects the no-impl receiver back.
--   • DEFINER shadow (`definerShadows`): a name THIS module defines as a top-level
--     funDef that ALSO names an interface method (map-as-root's own `toList` /
--     `isEmpty`).  The MARK pass (`buildStandaloneShadowsGraph`) rewrites these to
--     `EMethodAt`, so without a matching RLocal record the route falls back to the
--     result-type `RKey` (e.g. `toList`'s `List` result), dispatching the method on
--     the WRONG impl (List's `toList = identity`) → `debug`/`display` recurse on the
--     unchanged receiver forever (Map Debug/Eq/Ord impl bodies call standalone
--     `toList`).  These must be RECORDED for RLocal but must NOT be rebound to the
--     method scheme — their local standalone scheme (`Map k v -> …`) is what the
--     impl body types against.

-- E6: re-install the cross-module constraint accumulator that `freshModuleCtx` just
-- wiped, so this module's `=>`-constrained call sites into EARLIER modules resolve.
-- Post-ARGSTAMP-UNIFY (Phase 4): eval and emit both thread dicts, so this runs
-- UNCONDITIONALLY on every path (single elaboration mode).

-- `methodEntryHere` answers from stage K's whole-graph `deMethods` (core, earlier
-- modules and this prog), so resolveSite's ifaceParamMonos can recover a multi-param
-- interface's full param monos (the element grounding `e` for `FromEntries c e`).
-- An empty table on the module path would make ifaceParamMonos return None → a
-- Set/Map-literal return-position `fromEntries` route drops its `Ord` element dict →
-- null dict → SIGSEGV.

-- C5: a standalone-shadow method name (box's `toList`/`isEmpty`) is bound in
-- seedVars to its imported MONOMORPHIC standalone scheme (`Box k v -> …`), which
-- would shadow the polymorphic interface-method scheme and mis-type a genuine
-- method call (`isEmpty [1,2,3]` unifying List with Box).  Re-bind those names to
-- the graph-wide METHOD scheme so the call site types against the method; RLocal then redirects only the no-impl receiver back to the standalone
-- at eval: the EApp method-head arm types against the
-- method scheme even when a standalone value holds the name.
-- DEFINER shadows are EXCLUDED here: the module's OWN funDef (map-root's `toList`)
-- must type against its local standalone scheme (`Map k v -> List (k, v)`), not the
-- imported method scheme — the impl bodies call it ON a Map.  Only IMPORTER shadows
-- (imported-from-sibling method names) get the method-scheme rebind.

-- E5: infer this module's parametric impl bodies (gated, emit path only), seeing
-- [implDecls] for interface method types + cross-module impl dispatch.  The sites
-- it records stay in pendingArgStamps/pendingSites for elabModuleStamp to resolve.

-- C8(b): also infer THIS module's interface DEFAULT bodies, so a default body's type
-- error is caught on every path.

-- Phase 4.55: infer this
-- module's prop bodies so their prepass-marked EDictAt/EMethodAt routes resolve.
-- The multi-module test path (runPropsMulti) evaluates the ELABORATED prop body
-- against the dict-passed env, so the body's calls into the file's own promoted
-- `=>`-constrained fns (set's `wellFormed`/`fromList`) need their dict-arg routes
-- filled here — otherwise the call under-applies the dict-passed callee → not VBool
-- → every prop "fails".  funDefs ignores DProp, so without this the module path
-- never typed props at all.

-- E6: fold THIS module's freshly-registered fn-constraints into the cross-module
-- accumulator so a LATER module's call site finds them after its own reset.
-- Post-ARGSTAMP-UNIFY (Phase 4): unconditional on every path (single elaboration mode).

-- Shape A (gap #44): ground a container literal's free result-element var THROUGH
-- the unique impl's method type, so this module's pending sites/arg-stamps resolve
-- against the GROUNDED element (Set Int, not Set _a).  [implDecls] is the full impl
-- universe (core ++ earlier modules ++ prog), so the Set/Map impls are visible.

-- public top-level value names (the pub flag may sit on the sig, def, or extern).
-- C8(a): a PUBLIC interface ALSO exports its method names so an importer can see
-- their schemes — methods of an
-- `is_pub = true` interface are appended to the exported value schemes.
publicValNames : List Decl -> List String
publicValNames [] = []
publicValNames ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) =
  publicValNames [d] ++ publicValNames rest
publicValNames ((DFunDef True n _ _ _ _) :: rest) = n :: publicValNames rest
publicValNames ((DTypeSig True n _ _) :: rest) = n :: publicValNames rest
publicValNames ((DExtern (ExternDecl { edPub = True, edName = n, ... })) :: rest) =
  n :: publicValNames rest
publicValNames ((DInterface { pub = True, methods, ... }) :: rest) =
  ifaceMethodNames methods ++ publicValNames rest
publicValNames (_ :: rest) = publicValNames rest

-- ⚠️ #1557 A-3.5a REMOVED THE COMPLETENESS LINE from this fold, with the
-- `universeIfaceRequiredRef` cell and its `insertIfaceRequired` writer.  The ordering
-- guarantee of this fold, which `checkBodyImpl`'s Module arm calls BEFORE the shadow
-- reads so that this module's own decls are visible at the read, is no longer what
-- impl-completeness depends on: it reads stage K's `CE`, whose rows
-- carry their declaring module's ordinal, and `declEnvVisibleAt cur o` (`o <= cur`)
-- admits the reader's OWN row — the same 0..cur-inclusive prefix the accumulator held
-- here.  Do not re-add a completeness accumulator to restore an ordering property that
-- has moved onto the ordinal predicate.
appendUniverseAccums : GraphCtx -> String -> List Decl -> Unit
appendUniverseAccums gctx mid prog =
  -- 🚨 SHADOW-SEMANTICS S1-PRELUDE (a), RULED 2026-08-10 (#1375 item 2, #1499 clause (b)):
  -- The prelude is not S1's left (standalone) operand.  The asymmetry with the shadow
  -- builders' interface-method test, `methodNameDeclaredAt`, which counts the prelude's
  -- interfaces at ordinal 0, is deliberate and is S1-PRELUDE's own
  -- (`SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md:225`): S1's INTERFACE operand is the wider "nameable in M",
  -- which INCLUDES the prelude, while its STANDALONE operand is pinned by the
  -- definer/importer kind partition — and a standalone reaching M only as an implicit
  -- prelude export is NEITHER kind, so it creates no shadow in M.
  -- This ref's ONE reader is the `importerShadows` binding in `checkBodyImpl`
  -- (`standaloneShadowsFromSet`'s `fnSet`), i.e. exactly that left operand; folding the
  -- prelude's own funDefs in admitted every prelude standalone as an IMPORTER shadow —
  -- the reading S1-PRELUDE names and REJECTS by consequence — so a user interface method
  -- colliding with `isEven`/`count` fell back to the prelude function at any receiver head
  -- carrying no impl, ACCEPTING at exit 0 where the ruling requires a located reject
  -- (`test/shadow_fixtures/x1_prelude_standalone_not_left_operand.mdk`, x2, matrix rows
  -- 46-48).  ⚠️ SUBTRACTING the prelude's names at the READ site instead would be WRONG:
  -- a name the prelude AND a real user module both define IS imported into M by the user
  -- module and DOES create a shadow there.  Not folding is what expresses "contributes
  -- nothing"; the union still carries the name via whichever user module defines it.
  if isPreludeMid mid then
    ()
  else
    gctx.crossRun.universeFunNamesRef :=
      namesToSet
        (map fst (funDefs prog))
        gctx.crossRun.universeFunNamesRef.value
  -- #154 PR-B: grow the dispatch-index accumulator by THIS module's interface indices.
  -- APPEND (universe first) so the accumulated list stays in the same core-first order the
  -- old `argDispatchIndices (accAll ++ prog)` flatMap produced.  #1351 L5 (CONTRACT) deleted
  -- the bare-name accumulator that used to be grown alongside this one: its only reader was
  -- `dispatchIdxScoped`'s fall-back arm, and the reader is now identity-keyed only, so a
  -- prior-module entry this module never names cannot be queried by spelling at all.
  gctx.crossRun.universeMethodDispatchIdxByIdRef :=
    gctx.crossRun.universeMethodDispatchIdxByIdRef.value
      ++ argDispatchIndicesById prog
  -- #1112 A-3.4 PR2: the #154 PR3 leg that grew the three `obUniv*` obligation-universe
  -- accumulators here is GONE.  Its consumer — the Module arm's `obUniv` — now projects
  -- stage K's `IE` at the reader's ordinal instead (`ieUniverseAt`), so this fold was
  -- building a second copy of a table nothing reads.  Note what that makes this function:
  -- it no longer answers "what impls exist" at all; every remaining line here is an
  -- interface/method/name-side accumulator.  Do not re-add an impl accumulator — the whole
  -- point of the flip is that `IE` is the one answer, and a second one is design law L1's
  -- divergence.
  ()

-- Takes this module's whole decl list, not a pre-filtered one.  The polarity
-- fixpoint runs over all of it (#2112), and filtering at the CALL SITE is what once let
-- the opaque-kind half go missing.
--
-- `fieldOwnersRef` arrives holding the K seed PLUS everything this module registered
-- during inference, and `registerAllDataScoped … pubDecls` below registers this module's
-- PUBLIC records a SECOND time.  `addFieldOwners` conses without dedupe, so every own
-- public record is DOUBLE-listed in `fieldOwnersRef` from that line until the next
-- `freshModuleCtx`.  Unobservable today only because nothing reads the multimap in between
-- (the two population walkers read `env2` and `recordByNameRef`).  A doubled owner key is
-- the shape `declEnvSeedChain`'s own-row note exists to prevent.  `fieldOwnerNames`
-- `sortUniqS`es on read, so the doubling does not move an answer; a new reader of
-- `fieldOwnersRef` after this point must de-duplicate first or restore a reset.
-- (`dataParamKindsRef` gets the same
-- doubling; it is inert there because `lookupTab` is first-match over identity-bearing
-- keys whose two copies are equal.)
appendDataUniverse : ModuleCtx -> String -> List Decl -> Unit
appendDataUniverse mctx mid prog0 =
  let pubDecls = publicDataDecls prog0
  -- The alias half of the reload is a stage-K projection, so it takes THIS module's
  -- ordinal, strictly BELOW-this-module (`aliasUniverseAt`'s `<`): the registration
  -- below is what puts this module's own aliases, public and private, in front.
  loadDataUniverse mctx (declEnvsOrdOf mid mctx.mcGraph.declEnvsRef.value)
  -- #2112: the polarity fixpoint sees the WHOLE module (`prog0`), the universe
  -- receives only the public keys — see `registerAllDataScoped`.
  let env2 =
    registerAllDataScoped
      (withCtors mctx (spFloor mctx.mcGraph.crossRun.universeCtorPop.value))
      (publicDataScopeDecls prog0)
      prog0
  -- Both walkers read the payloads this registration just made back out of `env2` and
  -- `recordByNameRef`, so each population row holds the very object its floor holds.
  mctx.mcGraph.crossRun.universeCtorPop :=
    insertCtorPop env2 pubDecls mctx.mcGraph.crossRun.universeCtorPop.value
  mctx.mcGraph.crossRun.universeRecordPop :=
    insertRecordPop mctx pubDecls mctx.mcGraph.crossRun.universeRecordPop.value
  mctx.mcCells.ownPopsRef :=
    (insertCtorPop env2 pubDecls spEmpty, insertRecordPop mctx pubDecls spEmpty)

-- What `appendDataUniverse` registers to elaborate a module's public data: the
-- public declarations plus every alias the module declares, private ones
-- included.  A public field may name a private alias, and the scheme it
-- elaborates to here is the one every importer reads back, so it expands under
-- the declaring module's aliases.  The private aliases reach only the working
-- alias table, which the next module's arm reloads; the populations take
-- `pubDecls` alone.
publicDataScopeDecls : List Decl -> List Decl
publicDataScopeDecls prog0 =
  filterList (d => publicDataDecl d || isAliasDecl d) prog0

-- L3 (xmod-identity #1351 spine, RUN-XMOD-022/026): the graph-level fact "this
-- compile's decls carry at least one top-level funDef with its own module's
-- mangle prefix" — the DENOTATION the new GraphCtx flag below actually
-- records (F12).  NOT literally "a mangler ran": a hand-written `m__foo` in
-- module `m` on the un-mangled (check/run) path also satisfies it, harmlessly
-- (see that flag's callers).  Reuses `isMangledFor` (F6) rather than a second
-- prefix test, and `funDefs` rather than a second decl walk.
-- Monomorphic, explicit-recursion, short-circuits on the first hit — do NOT
-- delegate to prelude `any`/`elem` here (AGENTS.md: costed +56% self-compile
-- on this file's hot helpers).
--
-- 🚨 RUN-XMOD-045 CORRECTION (post-landing, slice-breaker finding): on EVERY
-- emit path this flag is CONSTANT TRUE, not merely "true when some module
-- shadows an interface method."  `private_mangle.mdk:14-16` states the
-- mangler's actual scope: "EVERY top-level FUNCTION binding (in core + every
-- module) is renamed to a module-qualified unique symbol," unconditionally,
-- BEFORE `elaborateModules` (and therefore this function) ever runs.  So
-- `core`'s own funDefs already carry the `core__` prefix by the time this
-- walks them, and the very first unit checked (`("core", coreDecls)`, see
-- both call sites below) already satisfies `unitCarriesMangledFunDefs` —
-- MEASURED: a feature-free `main = println 1` build emits ~70 distinct
-- `@mdk_core__*`/`@mdk_<mid>__*` symbols in its own IR with zero user
-- modules, zero shadows, zero interfaces.  The ORIGINAL packet's inertness
-- argument (F8: "a non-empty route symbol implies this flag is True") is
-- still literally correct but was read as "this flag is sometimes False,"
-- which is false — it is NEVER False on the emit path, so F8's implication
-- is vacuously satisfied rather than doing narrowing work.  The precondition
-- F8's phrasing hid: BOTH writers (this flag AND the route-symbol conjunct
-- in `importerShadowOnEmitPath`) are stamped by the SAME compile, and on
-- that compile this flag is always True the moment mangling has run at all —
-- see `importerShadowOnEmitPath`'s own header for the consequence.
--
-- 🚨 THIS FACT CURRENTLY HAS NO DISCRIMINATING CONSUMER — L4's conjunct is
-- constant-True on emit and its partner (`occurrenceCarriesMangledShadowSym`)
-- is always-empty off emit.  It is explicit documentation of an implicit
-- invariant, not a live condition.  Do not migrate a reader onto it
-- expecting it to select.  KEPT (not reverted) by owner ruling because it is
-- provably zero-risk and reads better than the anonymous test it replaced —
-- see `importerShadowOnEmitPath`.
graphCarriesMangledFunDefs : List (String, List Decl) -> Bool
graphCarriesMangledFunDefs [] = False
graphCarriesMangledFunDefs ((mid, decls) :: rest) =
  if unitCarriesMangledFunDefs mid (funDefs decls) then
    True
  else
    graphCarriesMangledFunDefs rest

unitCarriesMangledFunDefs : String -> List (String, (List Pat, Expr)) -> Bool
unitCarriesMangledFunDefs mid [] = False
unitCarriesMangledFunDefs mid ((n, _) :: rest) =
  if isMangledFor mid n then True else unitCarriesMangledFunDefs mid rest

-- WS-1a fix: every impl in scope, EXPORT OR NOT.  Instances are GLOBAL (coherence
-- + dispatch leak across module boundaries regardless of `export`), so the
-- superinterface existence query (checkSuperImpls/superImplExists) must see an
-- imported module's `impl Semigroup T` even when it isn't `export`-marked — else
-- `impl Monoid T` in an importer is falsely rejected for a "missing" super impl.
pickSchemes : List String -> OrdMap Scheme -> List (String, Scheme)
pickSchemes [] _ = []
pickSchemes (n :: ns) table = match omLookup n table
  Some s => (n, s) :: pickSchemes ns table
  None => pickSchemes ns table

-- Candidate spellings come from the same three binding sources the environment sees:
-- nameable bare methods (including the implicit core row), selective-import local
-- names, and module-alias-qualified names.  `lookupBinding` below remains the final
-- no-new-name gate, while this bounded list avoids walking every ordinary seed binding.
importedMethodBindingNames : GraphCtx ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, String) ->
  List String
importedMethodBindingNames gctx prog aliases =
  flatMap snd (nameableIfaceRows gctx prog)
    ++ importedMemberBindingNames prog
    ++ map fst aliases

-- The ordinary winner and standalone alternative travel together through
-- import selection, aliases, re-exports, and graph memoization.
tagModuleExports : OrdMap Scheme ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, ExportBinding)
tagModuleExports standalones schemes =
  map
    ((name, scheme) => (
      name,
      ExportBinding {
        xbScheme = scheme,
        xbStandalone = omLookup name standalones,
        xbExtern = False,
      },
    ))
    schemes

publicModuleExports : List Decl ->
  ModuleBindings ->
  List (String, ExportBinding)
publicModuleExports prog bindings =
  let standalones =
    omFromPairs
      (flatMap
        (name => match omLookup name bindings.mbStandaloneSchemes
          Some scheme => [(name, scheme)]
          None => [])
        (pubTopFnNames prog))
      omEmpty
  let externs =
    omFromNames (map fst (directExternSigs (externDecls prog))) omEmpty
  map
    ((name, binding) => (
      name,
      ExportBinding { binding | xbExtern = omHasKey name externs },
    ))
    (tagModuleExports
      standalones
      (pickSchemes
        (publicValNames prog)
        (omFromPairs (reverseL bindings.mbSchemes) omEmpty)))

-- The first row for a name wins, unless a second import supplies a different
-- declaration of the same name: then the occurrence is the ambiguity the
-- resolver reports as R-AMBIGUOUS-OCCURRENCE (`ambiguousSet`, keyed by
-- declaration through `importValueDecls`, whichever module each import names;
-- a local definition, which it exempts, shadows the seed here anyway).
-- Choosing either supplier would type the use against a definition the program
-- never named, and the cascade would change with the order the imports were
-- written.
importedBindingWinners : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, (Option String, ExportBinding)) ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  List (String, ExportBinding)
importedBindingWinners mctx [] _ _ = []
importedBindingWinners mctx ((name, (_, binding)) :: rest) seen ambiguous =
  if omHasKey name seen then
    importedBindingWinners mctx rest seen ambiguous
  else
    let chosen =
      if omHasKey name ambiguous then
        ambiguousImportBinding mctx ()
      else
        binding
    (name, chosen)
      :: importedBindingWinners mctx rest (omInsert name () seen) ambiguous

ambiguousImports : List (String, (Option String, a)) -> OrdMap Unit
ambiguousImports rows = ambiguousImportsGo rows omEmpty omEmpty

ambiguousImportsGo : List (String, (Option String, a)) ->
  OrdMap String ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  OrdMap Unit
ambiguousImportsGo [] _ acc = acc
ambiguousImportsGo ((_, (None, _)) :: rest) firstMid acc =
  ambiguousImportsGo rest firstMid acc
ambiguousImportsGo ((name, (Some mid, _)) :: rest) firstMid acc =
  match omLookup name firstMid
    None => ambiguousImportsGo rest (omInsert name mid firstMid) acc
    Some seen => ambiguousImportsGo rest firstMid (if seen == mid then
      acc
    else
      omInsert name () acc)

-- An ambiguous name types as a fresh, poisoned variable at every occurrence
-- (`freshSubst` recognises the reserved quantifier), so the resolver's
-- rejection stays the only diagnostic on the drivers that keep typechecking
-- after a resolve error (`check --json`, the LSP).
ambiguousImportBinding : ModuleCtx -> Unit -> ExportBinding
ambiguousImportBinding mctx _ =
  let cell =
    Ref (Unbound ambiguousImportTyvarId mctx.mcCells.currentLevel.value)
  ExportBinding {
    xbScheme = Forall [ambiguousImportTyvarId] [] [] [] pureRow (TVar cell),
    xbStandalone = None,
    xbExtern = False,
  }

moduleImportSeed : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, (Option String, ExportBinding)) ->
  ModuleSeed
moduleImportSeed mctx base imported =
  let selected =
    importedBindingWinners mctx imported omEmpty (ambiguousImports imported)
  ModuleSeed {
    msSchemes =
      base ++ map ((name, binding) => (name, binding.xbScheme)) selected,
    msImportedValues =
      omFromPairs
        (flatMap
          ((name, binding) => match binding.xbStandalone
            Some scheme => [(name, scheme)]
            None => [])
          selected)
        omEmpty,
    msImportedNames = omFromNames (map fst selected) omEmpty,
    msImportedExterns =
      omFromNames
        (flatMap
          ((name, binding) => if binding.xbExtern then [name] else [])
          selected)
        omEmpty,
  }

-- `import m as A` binds EVERY value m exports under `A.name` — so it enumerates the
-- dependency's schemes rather than a fixed member list.
--
-- Same golden/mangled split as above: on the emit path the reference is already
-- `<definer>__name`, so the key must stay mangled; on the golden path the reference is
-- the flat `A.name` that desugar produced, so bind it under that.
--
-- #1337/#1427/#1472: the mangle test asks the LOADED MODULE-ID SET, not just the module
-- the importer spelled.  A key reached through `export import` is mangled for its
-- DEFINER, not for the re-exporter `m` that the importer named — so a spelled-mid-only
-- prefix test answered False for it and re-bound `lib_plain__g` as `A.lib_plain__g`,
-- while types/private_mangle (which chases re-exports to the definer) had already
-- rewritten the body's reference to the bare `lib_plain__g`.  The lookup then missed:
-- for a constrained callee that reaches inferDictAt's panic and `build` fails outright
-- (#1427); for an unconstrained one the enclosing prelude call's dict route resolves to
-- nothing, the emitter writes a null dict operand, and the BINARY segfaults (#1472).
--
-- Conservatism (the property this rests on): a BARE key — one no loaded module exports
-- under that exact name with that module's own mangle prefix — is still re-bound under
-- the alias.  That population is exactly the interface METHODS (publicValNames admits
-- them; private_mangle's rename map, built from unitDefNames/pubFnNames, never renames
-- them), which is the occurrence-carrier question #1276/#1386 own — deliberately NOT
-- moved here.  The only keys whose binding changes are those a DIFFERENT loaded module
-- actually exports under its mangled name, i.e. precisely the re-exported ones, i.e.
-- precisely the defect.
--
-- 🚨 THE TEST IS AN IDENTITY TEST, NOT A STRING-SHAPE TEST (RUN-XMOD-039 F2), and it
-- must stay one.  A first version asked only "does `k` carry SOME loaded module's mangle
-- prefix?" — a pure spelling question — and that regressed a program base accepted: with
-- a module `lib.plain` loaded, an unrelated module's ordinary exported binding literally
-- named `lib_plain__g` was misread as already-mangled and rejected `Unbound variable:
-- O.lib_plain__g` at exit 1.  That surface is UNBOUNDED AND GROWS WITH THE MODULE GRAPH
-- (every module `a.b` makes `a_b__x` a landmine), and a sprint whose thesis is "identity
-- must not be derived from spelling" cannot decide identity by spelling, even in a guard.
-- ⚠️ So do NOT repair a future false positive here by TIGHTENING THE PREFIX PATTERN — a
-- narrower heuristic has a smaller unbounded surface, not a bounded one.  The conjunct
-- below is what bounds it: `k` must be a name the candidate module ACTUALLY EXPORTS, so
-- a false positive now requires two modules to genuinely export the same symbol rather
-- than one identifier to merely look like a mangled one.
aliasSchemes : List (String, List (String, a)) ->
  String ->
  List (String, a) ->
  List (String, a)
aliasSchemes deps alias depSchemes =
  map (aliasSchemeEntry deps alias) depSchemes

aliasSchemeEntry : List (String, List (String, a)) ->
  String ->
  (String, a) ->
  (String, a)
aliasSchemeEntry deps alias (k, s) =
  if isMangledExportOfAny deps k then (k, s) else (qualifiedLocal alias k, s)

-- does `k` carry the `<sanitized mid>__` prefix the mangler would have given it?
isMangledFor : String -> String -> Bool
isMangledFor mid k =
  let pre = mangledName mid ""
  let lp = stringLength pre
  lp <= stringLength k && stringSlice 0 lp k == pre

-- is `k` a symbol some loaded module ACTUALLY EXPORTS under that module's own mangled
-- name?  Both halves are load-bearing and the ORDER is deliberate: the cheap spelling
-- test gates the assoc lookup, so the scan below runs only for the rare key that already
-- looks mangled.  Short-circuiting and monomorphic on purpose (a dict-passed `any` over
-- a closure is the measured anti-pattern here).
--
-- 🚨 THE HONEST RESIDUAL, RECORDED SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT HAVE TO RE-DERIVE IT
-- (RUN-XMOD-042).  This is "SHAPE **AND** ACTUALLY-EXPORTED", **not** pure identity, and
-- the distinction matters if you are auditing it against the sprint's own thesis that
-- identity must not be derived from spelling.  `isMangledFor` is still a string test; it
-- has simply stopped being the *whole* decision.  What the `isSome (lookupAssoc k pubV)`
-- conjunct buys is that the surface becomes BOUNDED BY THE PROGRAM'S REAL EXPORT SET: a
-- false positive now needs a GENUINE SYMBOL COLLISION -- module `m` must actually export
-- something named exactly `k`, where `k` also carries `m`'s mangle prefix -- instead of
-- merely a similar-LOOKING name.  Before the conjunct, the surface grew with the module
-- graph (every module `a.b` made `a_b__x` a landmine, which is what regressed `core__foo`
-- and `lib_plain__g`); now it does not grow with the graph at all.
--
-- ⚠️ WHY NOT DROP THE SHAPE HALF AND MAKE IT PURE IDENTITY?  Because that is measurably
-- WRONG here, not merely purer: a re-exported interface METHOD key is also a key of its
-- declaring module's `pubV`, so "is `k` a key of some other loaded module?" alone answers
-- True for methods and would re-bind them -- moving the bare-key population that
-- #1276/#1386 own and that this slice must not touch.  The shape half is precisely what
-- holds methods out (a bare method name carries no `<mid>__` prefix).  So the two halves
-- are not redundant and neither may be removed on tidiness grounds.
--
-- ⚠️ The residual's true root is one level down and is NOT fixed here: private_mangle's
-- `(module, name) -> symbol` encoding is not injective -- a module `lib.plain` and a
-- module literally named `lib_plain` both yield `lib_plain__g`.  That is the common root
-- of both regressions above and is bigger than this function; do not try to repair it by
-- editing this test.
isMangledExportOfAny : List (String, List (String, a)) -> String -> Bool
isMangledExportOfAny [] _ = False
isMangledExportOfAny ((m, pubV) :: rest) k =
  isMangledFor m k && isSome (lookupAssoc k pubV) || isMangledExportOfAny rest k

-- the (key, scheme) pairs a single `import` form brings into scope, resolving each
-- member to its (possibly mangled) key in the dependency's schemes.  `deps` is the
-- loaded dependency environment — module id PLUS that module's public schemes, because
-- the alias arm's test needs the exported NAMES, not just the ids (see aliasSchemes).
-- The non-alias arms ignore it — their resolution is definer-agnostic by construction
-- (a `__<name>` SUFFIX test, and the wildcard form performs no mangle test at all).
--
-- Passing `depEnv` WHOLE is also what supplies the spelled `depId` itself: both callers
-- reach this only through a successful `lookupAssoc depId depEnv`, so the spelled module
-- is in the list by construction and needs no separate term.
--
-- 🚨 `"core"` IS DELIBERATELY **NOT** IN `mids`, AND MUST NOT BE ADDED BACK
-- (RUN-XMOD-039).  It was, briefly.  The argument for it reads well — `reexportSeed`'s
-- own comment records core as the one source NOT in depEnv, and private_mangle *does*
-- mangle core's exports to `core__<name>` — but it was DERIVED, never witnessed: a
-- `export import core.{…}` reached through an alias is green on every verb with or
-- without the term, because core's names are seeded via `baseSeed` and resolve before
-- the alias binding is ever consulted.  Meanwhile the term has a MEASURED cost: it made
-- `mangledName "core" "" == "core__"` a live prefix, so an ordinary user binding named
-- `core__foo` reached through an alias import was misread as already-mangled and left
-- un-rebound — `Unbound variable: O.core__foo`, exit 1, on a program that compiled and
-- printed correctly before.  Zero measured benefit, measured cost.
importFormSchemes : List (String, List (String, a)) ->
  UsePath ->
  List (String, a) ->
  List (String, a)
importFormSchemes deps (UseAlias _ alias) depSchemes =
  aliasSchemes deps alias depSchemes
importFormSchemes _ path depSchemes = importFormRows path depSchemes

-- ── import aliasing: re-key the dict-arity / dict-iface tables ───────────────────
-- Both tables (funConstraintsRef, funConstraintIfacesRef) are keyed by the name a CALL
-- SITE writes.  An alias makes that name differ from the definition's, so without this
-- an aliased constrained callee gets zero dict routes and under-applies.  Generic over
-- the payload so the arity table (List Int) and the iface table (List String) share it.
--
-- [qual] is the (definerMid, name)-keyed table, which is what makes this PRECISE: an
-- entry is copied only when its DEFINER is the very module the alias names.
-- #1114 (#845): THIS module's imported bindings' SCHEME CONTEXTS, keyed by the local
-- spelling the importer writes.  Structurally the sibling of `aliasConstraintEntries`
-- below — same `DUse` walk, same "copy an entry only when its DEFINER is the very module
-- this import names" precision, and the payload is likewise taken from the
-- `(definerMid, name)`-keyed qual table.
--
-- TWO deliberate differences from `aliasConstraintEntries`, both because that function
-- serves the DICT-ARITY tables on the emit path and this one serves the CHECK-time
-- obligation channel:
--   * it has a `UseWild` arm.  `aliasConstraintEntries` needs none — on the emit path a
--     wildcard-imported name keeps its own spelling and `funConstraintsRef` is re-seeded
--     from the bare `crossModuleFunConstraintsRef` accumulator, which already covers it.
--     The check path has no such bare re-seed (that ref is the emit path's), so without
--     this arm `import m.*` would be the one spelling that still dropped the obligation.
--   * it emits the UNALIASED member of a `UseGroup` too (`useMemberLocal`, not
--     `useMemberAlias`), because plain `import m.{f}` is the common spelling and is
--     exactly what #845 pins.
--
-- ⚠️ Kept SEPARATE from `aliasConstraintEntries` rather than generalizing it. Adding a
-- `UseWild` arm there would change what `funConstraintsRef` holds on the emit path, i.e.
-- dict-passing arity — a different judgment, with a different blast radius, reached from
-- here only by the coincidence that both read the same qual-table shape.
importedSchemeOblEntries : List Decl ->
  List ((String, String), List VecObl) ->
  List (String, List VecObl)
importedSchemeOblEntries [] _ = []
importedSchemeOblEntries ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) qual =
  importedSchemeOblEntries [d] qual ++ importedSchemeOblEntries rest qual
importedSchemeOblEntries ((DUse _ path _) :: rest) qual =
  schemeOblEntriesFor (importTableMid path) path qual
    ++ importedSchemeOblEntries rest qual
importedSchemeOblEntries (_ :: rest) qual = importedSchemeOblEntries rest qual

-- ── import aliasing of an interface/impl METHOD ─────────────────────────────────
-- A method is GLOBAL-BY-NAME.  Impl coalescing, dispatch routing, the emitter's method
-- tables and the METHOD-side dict-arity tables are every one of them keyed by the
-- method's own name, and there is exactly ONE cell per name — a method is deliberately
-- NOT a per-module binding (eval.mdk's globalCells / coalesceImpls).  So an alias of a
-- method is not a new binding at all: it is a second SPELLING of the same method.
--
-- No alias spelling is resolved here, module or member.  The alias spelling is what
-- makes the occurrence decidable.  Erasing `A.area` makes it the same name as an
-- unrelated imported standalone `area`, and the standalone shadow wins (#1386 Repro B);
-- erasing a member alias `import m.{area as computeArea}` makes `computeArea` and a bare
-- `area` one name, so the import's method captures an `area` that resolve bound to
-- another declaration (#3708).  Inference gets the spelling supplied to it
-- (`aliasQualifiedMethodEntries`, `moduleMarkCtx`, `originMethodName`) and
-- `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` puts the occurrence back under its origin name once
-- inference is done, so every stage after elaboration still sees an ordinary method
-- reference and needs no alias awareness.
--
-- That is also precisely why the METHOD-side predicate carrier and its qualified
-- scoped projection need no alias re-keying the
-- way funConstraintsRef did: by the time they are built, no aliased method name survives.
-- The standalone `=>`-constrained functions are the opposite case — they ARE per-module
-- bindings that keep their local name, which is why they needed the re-keying.
--
-- What this pass does rewrite is a module alias's `DUse`: it keeps its own non-method
-- values and an `import m.{…m's methods…}` is appended beside it, so the origin name is
-- in scope for the occurrence `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` resolves it to.  A member
-- alias's `DUse` is rewritten to its origin name by that same restore, after inference.
-- Beside the rewritten modules it returns each module's admissions, which the drive's
-- mint installs (`GraphCtx.graphAdmittedMethodsRef`, read by `admittedIn`).
renameAliasedMethods : List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (OrdMap (List (String, String)), List (String, List Decl))
renameAliasedMethods coreDecls modules =
  let coreEntry = ("core", declMethodNames coreDecls)
  let methodsPerMid = coreEntry :: methodsPerMidGo [coreEntry] modules
  let admitted =
    omFromPairs
      (map
        ((mid, prog) => (mid, synthesizedAdmissions methodsPerMid prog))
        modules)
      omEmpty
  (admitted, map (renameAliasedMethodsIn methodsPerMid) modules)

admittedIn : GraphCtx -> String -> List (String, String)
admittedIn gctx mid =
  optionOr [] (omLookup mid gctx.graphAdmittedMethodsRef.value)

-- The method names `deAliasMethodImports` admits WITHOUT a binding the resolver
-- counted: every method of an alias-imported module.  `importSeed` reads this so
-- such a row never makes a bare name look supplied twice.
synthesizedAdmissions : List (String, List String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, String)
synthesizedAdmissions _ [] = []
synthesizedAdmissions mm ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) =
  synthesizedAdmissions mm (d :: rest)
synthesizedAdmissions mm ((DUse _ path _) :: rest) =
  let mid = useModId path
  let here = match lookupAssoc mid mm
    None => []
    Some methods => match path
      UseAlias _ _ => map (n => (mid, n)) methods
      _ => []
  here ++ synthesizedAdmissions mm rest
synthesizedAdmissions mm (_ :: rest) = synthesizedAdmissions mm rest

-- A module's method set is what it DECLARES *plus what it RE-EXPORTS*, folded in
-- dependency-first (loader) order so a re-export resolves against its source's
-- already-computed set (and a >1-hop chain still resolves).
--
-- The `++ reexportedMethodNames` half closes a run≠build divergence that predates
-- core re-exports and is NOT core-specific: with a module's set limited to its own
-- `declMethodNames`, a module that re-exports a method did not list it, so at an
-- importer's `import reExporter.{method as alias}` neither half of the rewrite fired
-- — the alias was not dropped from the DUse (b) and references were not renamed to the
-- origin (a).  `run` still worked (eval's import frame binds the alias to the origin's
-- cell), but a method is global-by-name with no per-module symbol, so EMIT had nothing
-- to bind `alias` to and died with `unbound variable '<alias>'`.  Observed on a pure
-- user-module chain (b defines `area`; c re-exports it; main imports `area as surface`
-- → run prints 16, build fails), and on `import list.{filter as f}` once
-- `export import core.{filter}` began re-exporting at all.
methodsPerMidGo : List (String, List String) ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, List String)
methodsPerMidGo _ [] = []
methodsPerMidGo acc ((mid, decls) :: rest) =
  let entry =
    (mid, dedup (declMethodNames decls ++ reexportedMethodNames acc decls))
  entry :: methodsPerMidGo (entry :: acc) rest

-- method names a module re-exports via `export import src.{…}` (`DUse True`), taken
-- from `acc` — the dependency's already-folded method set.
reexportedMethodNames : List (String, List String) -> List Decl -> List String
reexportedMethodNames acc decls = flatMap (reexportedMethodsOf acc) decls

reexportedMethodsOf : List (String, List String) -> Decl -> List String
reexportedMethodsOf acc (DAttrib _ d) = reexportedMethodsOf acc d
reexportedMethodsOf acc (DUse True path _) =
  match lookupAssoc (useModId path) acc
    None => []
    Some srcMethods => match importedBindings path
      -- `export import src.*` re-exports every one of src's methods
      None => srcMethods
      Some bs => filterList (n => contains n srcMethods) (map fst bs)
reexportedMethodsOf _ _ = []

-- Only a MODULE alias is rewritten before inference (`deAliasMethodImports`).  A
-- member alias reaches inference as written, so `import m.{mth as loc}` binds `loc`
-- and not `mth`; `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` restores the origin name afterwards.
-- The pass is a no-op on a module whose imports name no method.
renameAliasedMethodsIn : List (String, List String) ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (String, List Decl)
renameAliasedMethodsIn methodsPerMid (mid, prog) =
  (mid, flatMap (deAliasMethodImports methodsPerMid) prog)

-- `import m as A` is kept (its non-method values are still `A.f`) and gains a sibling
-- `import m.{…m's methods…}`, the origin name `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` resolves
-- `A.area` to once inference is done.  The rewrite is recorded in
-- `graphAdmittedMethodsRef` (`synthesizedAdmissions`), because the resolver never saw
-- it.  The added import keeps the alias import's path and location, which is how the
-- method scope ladder tells it from a written one (`writtenImportsOf`).
deAliasMethodImports : List (String, List String) -> Decl -> List Decl
deAliasMethodImports mm (DAttrib attrs d) =
  map (DAttrib attrs) (deAliasMethodImports mm d)
deAliasMethodImports mm (d@(DUse pub path loc)) =
  match lookupAssoc (useModId path) mm
    None => [d]
    Some methods => match path
      UseAlias quals _ => match methodMembersOf methods loc
        [] => [d]
        mems => [d, DUse pub (UseGroup quals mems) loc]
      _ => [d]
deAliasMethodImports _ d = [d]

methodMembersOf : List String -> Loc -> List UseMember
methodMembersOf methods loc = map (n => UseMember n False loc None) methods

-- [qualified] holds the `A.mth` spellings: nothing but the method can be spelled that
-- way, so every occurrence moves.  [members] holds the member-alias locals no own
-- top-level value takes (`memberAliasCands`), which a local binder can still spell;
-- only a MARKED occurrence is the import's, since the marker leaves a locally-bound
-- name unmarked (`rewriteArgScoped`).
renameMethodVar : List (String, String) -> List (String, String) -> Expr -> Expr
renameMethodVar qualified _ (e@(EVar x)) = match lookupAssoc x qualified
  Some origin => EVar origin
  None => e
renameMethodVar qualified _ (e@(EVarId x _)) = match lookupAssoc x qualified
  Some origin => EVar origin
  None => e
-- A marked occurrence: `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` runs after inference, on a tree
-- the schedule has already marked.  Only the NAME moves — the route cell carries the
-- dispatch decision inference reached under the alias spelling.
renameMethodVar qualified members (e@(EMethodAt x sym ev)) =
  match lookupAssoc x qualified
    Some origin => EMethodAt origin sym ev
    None => match lookupAssoc x members
      Some origin => EMethodAt origin sym ev
      None => e
renameMethodVar _ _ e = e

-- ── #1386: the alias-qualified spelling, resolved AFTER inference ───────────────
-- `A.mth`, and a member alias `loc` from `import m.{mth as loc}`, reach inference
-- spelled as written, because that spelling is what makes the occurrence decidable:
-- rewriting either to a bare `mth` up front merges it with whatever else a bare `mth`
-- names in this module (`renameAliasedMethods`' header, #1386 Repro B, #3708).  Every
-- reader inference needs is supplied under that spelling — the obligation table, the
-- mark sets, the dispatch index — so the distinction survives exactly as long as it
-- decides something.
--
-- It must not survive further.  Everything DOWNSTREAM of elaboration keys a method by
-- its origin name and only its origin name: eval's coalesced method cells and its
-- per-(method, tag) `requires` counts (`methodReqCountRef`, whose miss would turn a
-- dispatch into the inherited-default path), the Core-IR interpreter's twin, and the
-- emitter's method tables.  Resolving the spelling here leaves all of them the tree
-- they get today, so the fix is a VERDICT change and nothing else.
resolveAliasMethodSpellings : GraphCtx ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (String, List Decl)
resolveAliasMethodSpellings gctx (mid, prog) =
  let qualified = map aliasRowSpelling (useKeyCands (moduleAliasKeys gctx) prog)
  let members = map aliasRowSpelling (memberAliasCands gctx prog)
  match (qualified, members)
    ([], []) => (mid, prog)
    _ => (
      mid,
      mapProg
        (renameMethodVar qualified members)
        (map (deAliasMethodMembers members) prog),
    )

-- `import m.{mth as loc}` → `import m.{mth}`, once every `loc` occurrence carries `mth`:
-- downstream of elaboration a method is bound by its origin name only.  Only a
-- `DUse` names import members, so every other decl passes through unchanged.
deAliasMethodMembers : List (String, String) -> Decl -> Decl
deAliasMethodMembers rows (DAttrib attrs d) =
  DAttrib attrs (deAliasMethodMembers rows d)
deAliasMethodMembers rows (DUse pub (UseGroup quals ms) loc) =
  DUse pub (UseGroup quals (map (deAliasMethodMember rows) ms)) loc
deAliasMethodMembers _ d = d

deAliasMethodMember : List (String, String) -> UseMember -> UseMember
deAliasMethodMember rows (m@(UseMember n allCtors loc (Some local)))
  | containsPair (local, n) rows = UseMember n allCtors loc None
  | otherwise = m
deAliasMethodMember _ m = m

-- every interface/impl method name a decl list declares (interface methods with or
-- without a default, plus impl method names).
declMethodNames : List Decl -> List String
declMethodNames decls = dedup (flatMap declMethodNamesOf decls)

declMethodNamesOf : Decl -> List String
declMethodNamesOf (DAttrib _ d) = declMethodNamesOf d
declMethodNamesOf (DInterface { methods, ... }) = ifaceMethodNames methods
declMethodNamesOf (DImpl { methods, ... }) = map implMethodName methods
declMethodNamesOf _ = []

-- build THIS module's import-scoped seed: walk its DUse decls, and for each one
-- pick the imported names out of the named dependency's public schemes (held in
-- depEnv, keyed by the dotted module id that useModId yields == the loader's
-- mid).  `core` DUse decls are skipped — core is always seeded via baseSeed.
-- ── #1675: reject an import that admits a name with more than one denotation ──────
-- The hub `export import lib.pa.{g}` + `export import lib.pb.{g}` exports ONE spelling
-- `g` standing for TWO definitions.  Downstream, `resolveMemberRows`' `lookupAssoc`
-- took the first of them and every verb exited 0, so which function ran was decided by
-- the ORDER OF TWO `export import` LINES — a semantically null edit moving the printed
-- value from `2` to `10`.  This is the reject.
--
-- ⚠️ THE MECHANISM IS THE EXACT-KEY ARM, NOT THE `__<name>` SUFFIX FALLBACK, and the
-- distinction decides where the check can live.  #1675's body cites the suffix `filter`
-- ("cited, not instrumented", per its own pin's claim) — but `types/private_mangle`
-- runs ONLY in the emit drivers, so on `check`/`run` the hub's rows are keyed by the
-- BARE name, `lookupAssoc origin` HITS, and the fallback is never reached.  A guard
-- written on "the filter returned more than one candidate" is green on the whole repro.
-- The ambiguity is a property of the DEPENDENCY'S EXPORT TABLE, which is where
-- `deAmbigValues` records it, on both paths at once.
--
-- Reported at the IMPORT DECL (that is where the location points, and where the fix
-- belongs), but keyed on an OCCURRENCE: a name is rejected only if this module actually
-- WRITES it, in the spelling the form binds it under — bare for `import m.*` and for a
-- selective `{g}`, alias-qualified (`A.g`) for `import m as A`.  A hub may re-export a
-- hundred names under two denotations; an importer that never mentions any of them has
-- no ambiguity to resolve and must not be rejected.  This is the USE-SITE model taken
-- literally (#1070's reject-at-declaration stays superseded; the hub itself is still
-- legal).  It also keeps the verdict from depending on which definer came first: the
-- moment the name IS written, the import is rejected rather than silently resolved.
--
-- ⚠️ CORRECTED (sprint-review F2, from #1675's own landed behavior): the first cut
-- rejected at ADMISSION — `import lib.hub.*` / `import lib.hub as H` failed even when
-- the importer never mentioned the colliding name, while the selective form correctly
-- did not.  The precedent cited for that, `T-AMBIGUOUS-ALIAS-METHOD`, is a DIFFERENT
-- namespace: methods are dispatched by argument type, where "admitted into scope" and
-- "used" are hard to pull apart because having the method callable is often the point.
-- Whether the method side wants the same narrowing is a separate question, deliberately
-- not answered here.
--
-- ⚠️ THE CHAIN IS NOT WEAKENED by narrowing to an occurrence.  `reexportDefiners` takes
-- a wildcard `export import`'s rows WHOLESALE, so a module that re-exports an ambiguous
-- hub without mentioning the name carries the ambiguity into its OWN export table, and
-- the first module downstream that does write the name is rejected there.  Nothing is
-- laundered clean by passing through a silent hop.
--
-- Called from `checkBodyImpl`, after its module mint, and not from `importSeed`.
-- `importSeed` is where the member resolution lives and was the first home for this, but
-- the seed is computed in `foldModules` BEFORE the module's worker runs, and
-- `checkBodyImpl` opens with `freshModuleCtx` — which clears `typeErrors`.  MEASURED on
-- that version: `run` exited 1 with the unlocated "detected during elaboration … see
-- issue #1812" fallback (the sticky flag survives the reset, the diagnostic does not)
-- and `check`/`check --json` were both still SILENT at exit 0 on the whole repro.  A
-- diagnostic pushed while a seed is being built cannot be reported.  Everything this
-- needs is on the module's own `DUse` decls plus the graph table, so nothing is lost by
-- moving it inside the window; `checkBodyImpl` is the ONE body all four drivers share
-- (`checkModuleFullImpl` and `elabModuleStamp` both route through it), which is what
-- keeps `check` and the run/build elaboration pass answering alike.
reportAmbiguousImports : ModuleCtx -> List Decl -> Unit
reportAmbiguousImports mctx prog0 =
  reportAmbiguousImportsIn mctx (moduleRefNameSet prog0) prog0

-- every name this module's own decl bodies REFERENCE, as a set.  Parallels `groupRefs`'
-- collector (`freeEVars`, which skips locally bound names; `allEVars` here keeps every
-- occurrence) one level up, at the decl layer: the top-level clause bodies plus
-- the impl/default/prop/test bodies, which is every place a user program can spell
-- an imported value.  Alias-qualified references arrive here already flattened into a
-- single dotted `EVar "A.g"` by desugar's `rewriteAliasQual` (via `qualifiedLocal`), so
-- the alias spelling is a plain string lookup and needs no separate walk.
--
-- `allEVars` has no `EMethodRef` arm and does not need one: no production verb ever
-- constructs that node (it does carry `EMethodAt`/`EDictAt`, the shapes the elaborate
-- driver's dict pre-pass actually mints).  Independently of shape, the rows being filtered
-- come from `ambiguousExportRows` → `modulePubDefiners` → `pubTopFnNames`, i.e.
-- `declTopFnNames` ∩ exports — so an interface method name can never be in `amb` at all.
moduleRefNameSet : List Decl -> OrdMap Unit
moduleRefNameSet decls = namesToSet (flatMap declRefNames decls) omEmpty

-- `core` is never in `depEnv` (it is the implicit prelude — `reexportSeed`'s comment
-- carries the full reason), so it is passed separately as `coreV`, and it contributes
-- ONE thing: the keys an ALIAS introduces.  A plain `import core.{f}` still seeds
-- nothing, because `f` is already in `baseSeed` under that very spelling and seeding it
-- twice only adds a duplicate assoc entry — but `import core as C` and
-- `import core.{f as g}` ask for `C.f` / `g`, which `baseSeed` does not hold under any
-- spelling.  `resolve.mdk`'s `coreAliasNames` binds exactly the same two forms, and the
-- two must agree: a key bound in scope with no scheme here is a `T-UNBOUND` at the use
-- site, which is what #95 measured for six years.
-- Each row a use-path BINDS carries the declaration it denotes
-- (`importSupplierKey`), the same identity the resolver's value ambiguity check
-- keys on (`importValueDecls`), so `moduleImportSeed` can refuse to pick a winner
-- by import order for a name the resolver has already rejected, and still
-- accept one declaration reached through two imports. A row the form admits
-- without binding its name (a bare method row admitted for dispatch, a mangled
-- twin) carries no supplier, and core is exempt there as it is in the resolver.
-- The key is looked up by local name, so one member list binding a local twice
-- (`import m.{a as b, b}`) gives both rows one key and the first row wins here;
-- the resolver rejects that occurrence by declaration all the same.
importSeed : GraphCtx ->
  List (String, a) ->
  List (String, String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List (String, a)) ->
  List (String, (Option String, a))
importSeed gctx _ _ [] _ = []
importSeed gctx coreV admitted ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) depEnv =
  importSeed gctx coreV admitted (d :: rest) depEnv
importSeed gctx coreV admitted ((DUse _ path _) :: rest) depEnv =
  let depId = useModId path
  if depId == "core" then
    map (suppliedBy None) (coreAliasSchemes path coreV)
      ++ importSeed gctx coreV admitted rest depEnv
  else match lookupAssoc depId depEnv
    None => importSeed gctx coreV admitted rest depEnv
    Some depSchemes =>
      let bound = boundNames path
      let decls =
        omFromPairs
          (reverseL (importFormRows path (graphPubDefiners gctx depId)))
          omEmpty
      let methods =
        identKeysByName
          omEmpty
          (importFormRows
            path
            (match omLookup depId (methodExportsIndex gctx ())
              Some rows => rows
              None => []))
      map
          (row =>
            suppliedBy
              (if bindsName bound (fst row)
                && not (containsPair (depId, fst row) admitted) then
                Some (importSupplierKey depId decls methods (fst row))
              else
                None)
              row)
          (importFormSchemes depEnv path depSchemes)
        ++ importSeed gctx coreV admitted rest depEnv
importSeed gctx coreV admitted (_ :: rest) depEnv =
  importSeed gctx coreV admitted rest depEnv

-- The identity one import's binding of `name` is compared by: the declaration
-- it denotes.  A standalone value is `(defining module, name there)` from
-- `graphPubDefiners`, first row winning as it does there; an interface method
-- is the set of method declarations the import carries under that name
-- (`meExports`).  A driver without an import graph knows neither, so the
-- binding falls back to the importing module's id.  The three key shapes are
-- two, a multiple of four, and one netstring long, so no two can coincide.
importSupplierKey : String ->
  OrdMap (String, String) ->
  OrdMap (List String) ->
  String ->
  String
importSupplierKey depId decls methods name = match omLookup name decls
  Some (defMid, origin) => "\{lenKey defMid}\{lenKey origin}"
  None => match omLookup name methods
    Some keys => joinWith "" (sortUniqS keys)
    None => lenKey depId

identKeysByName : OrdMap (List String) ->
  List (String, Ident) ->
  OrdMap (List String)
identKeysByName acc [] = acc
identKeysByName acc ((n, ident) :: rest) =
  let seen = match omLookup n acc
    Some keys => keys
    None => []
  identKeysByName (omInsert n (identKey ident :: seen) acc) rest

suppliedBy : Option String -> (String, a) -> (String, (Option String, a))
suppliedBy mid (name, row) = (name, (mid, row))

containsPair : (String, String) -> List (String, String) -> Bool
containsPair _ [] = False
containsPair (a, b) ((x, y) :: rest) =
  a == x && b == y || containsPair (a, b) rest

-- The names an import form binds, as the resolver counts them
-- (`importedNamesMM`): a member list binds its locals, a single dotted name its
-- last component, an alias nothing bare, and a wildcard (`None`) every name it
-- reaches.
boundNames : UsePath -> Option (OrdMap Unit)
boundNames (UseGroup _ ms) = Some (omFromNames (map useMemberLocal ms) omEmpty)
boundNames (UseName ns) =
  Some (omFromNames (if listLen ns > 1 then [lastComponent ns] else []) omEmpty)
boundNames (UseWild _) = None
boundNames (UseAlias _ _) = Some omEmpty

bindsName : Option (OrdMap Unit) -> String -> Bool
bindsName None _ = True
bindsName (Some names) name = omHasKey name names

-- the schemes a `core` use-path binds under a spelling the prelude did not supply.
coreAliasSchemes : UsePath -> List (String, a) -> List (String, a)
coreAliasSchemes (UseAlias _ a) coreV = map (coreQualScheme a) coreV
coreAliasSchemes (UseGroup _ ms) coreV = flatMap (coreRenameScheme coreV) ms
coreAliasSchemes _ _ = []

coreQualScheme : String -> (String, a) -> (String, a)
coreQualScheme a (k, s) = (qualifiedLocal a k, s)

coreRenameScheme : List (String, a) -> UseMember -> List (String, a)
coreRenameScheme coreV m = match useMemberAlias m
  None => []
  Some local => match lookupAssoc (useMemberOrigin m) coreV
    None => []
    Some s => [(local, s)]

-- WS-2/D2: THIS module's imported value name → import-source module id, from its DUse
-- decls.  Mirrors importSeed's walk but records (localName, depId) for EXPLICIT imports
-- only (importedBindings = Some bindings); a wildcard `import mod.*` (None) is skipped —
-- it can't enumerate names here, so those names fall back to the bare arity table at the
-- call site.  `core` is skipped (core fns route via the prelude dict machinery, never the
-- cross-module qual tables).  depId == useModId == the loader mid == the qual
-- tables' definer key for a DIRECT import; a re-exported fn's depId is the intermediate
-- (≠ definer), so its qual lookup misses and it too falls back to bare (correct).
--
-- Keyed by the LOCAL name, because that is the name the CALL SITE writes: under a member
-- alias (`import m.{a as b}`) the call site says `b`, so `b` is what must map to m.
--
-- A whole-module alias (`import m as A`) contributes nothing, for the same reason a
-- wildcard doesn't: its locals are one-per-export and this walk has no depEnv to
-- enumerate them.  Those call sites take the same documented bare-table fallback as
-- every wildcard import does today.  The dict ARGS themselves are unaffected — they are
-- stamped from the callee's SCHEME, which importSeed binds under the alias local — so
-- this costs only the cross-module arity DISAMBIGUATION, and only when two modules
-- export same-named constrained fns of differing dict arity.
-- ── #674 / #1111 A-2.6: import-scoped constructor overlay ──────────────────
-- The `data`/`newtype` decls (from the surrounding universe `pool`) whose
-- CONSTRUCTORS this module binds by name, read from its own DUse decls.  They
-- OVERLAY the whole-universe ctor tables (the typecheck scheme table, the
-- record-info table AND the exhaust oracle) so a bare ctor / record name resolves
-- to the SPECIFIC module this unit imports it from — instead of the universe's
-- arbitrary load-order winner, the #674 root cause where the check side
-- (last-loaded-wins) and the native mangler (first-import-wins) DISAGREED.
--
-- A `UseGroup` member contributes its owning decl when EITHER
--   (a) it carries the `(..)` flag and names the decl's TYPE (`import m.{T(..)}`) —
--       the original #674 rule; or
--   (b) it NAMES ONE OF THE DECL'S CONSTRUCTORS (`import m.{T, C}` — and, because a
--       record's sole variant carries the type's own name, `import m.{Cfg}` for a
--       `data Cfg = { … }`).  #1111 A-2.6: (b) is the half that drains #1259 (the
--       ctor-scheme env) and #1256 (the record-info table).
--
-- ⚠️ Rule (b) is a CONSTRUCTOR-name test, not a "not a type name" test, and the
-- difference is observable.  A member spelled like a TYPE contributes nothing only when
-- no decl of `mid` happens to have a constructor of that spelling; if one does, the
-- member matches by rule (b) and that SIBLING decl is overlaid.  Measured: `pmod` with
-- `data Wrap = T Int`, `import pmod.{T, pf}`, and a local `data Loc = T String` —
-- pinned as `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_type_only_sibling_decl/`.
-- (An earlier revision of this note claimed such a member "contributes NOTHING"; it was
-- measured false and the paragraph is rewritten rather than re-pointed.)
--
-- That contribution is harmless BECAUSE of the layering at the registration site
-- (`dataEnv`, `Module` arm): the overlay sits BELOW this module's own `prog0`, so a
-- sibling decl riding along can never clobber a locally-declared constructor.  What it
-- can still do is out-rank a ctor the unit names from a DIFFERENT module in an EARLIER
-- import clause, decided by source order — the residual this note used to claim was
-- prevented outright.  It is the pre-existing `(..)` hazard (#674's remainder) now also
-- reachable via (b), and it is order-dependent on `main` too.
--
-- 🚨 IT IS **#1284**, and that number is the point of this paragraph.  The note used to
-- end "it is tracked, not fixed here" while NOTHING tracked it — #1283 is the RE-EXPORT
-- spelling, #733 item 1a is marked FIXED and item 1c is wildcards only.  A declared-
-- but-unfiled residual is indistinguishable from an unnoticed one.  Pinned self-
-- drainingly by `test/must_fail_fixtures/1284-a26-overlay-sibling-ctor-beats-named-
-- import/`, whose CONTROL is the discriminator that dates it: the NON-sibling case is
-- rejected on `main` and accepted here, so A-2.6 repairs that one and leaves exactly
-- the sibling one.
--
-- 🚨 The pool lookup is IDENTITY-scoped, not bare-name.  The pool is the WHOLE
-- universe, so two modules declaring the same TYPE name (#1256's two `Cfg`s) made the
-- old bare-name first-match a LOAD-ORDER coin flip inside the very overlay that exists
-- to defeat load order.  The pool is appended in LOAD order and searched FIRST-match
-- while the tables it overlays are `omInsert` LAST-write-wins, so the two orders are
-- OPPOSITE: an unscoped lookup picks right only when the wanted module is loaded
-- FIRST, and `test/eval_typed_modules_fixtures/ctor_scope_load_order/` is the measured
-- counterexample (stubbing `declOwnedBy` to `True` regresses it to `7.0`, an answer
-- pristine `main` gets right).  `declOwnedBy` compares the decl's own identity against
-- `OriginModule <this import's module id>`; `useModId` yields exactly the loader
-- mid that `stampDeclOrigins` stamped with (see `aliasDictNamesOfPath`'s note, which
-- states the same equality for the qual tables).
--
-- ⚠️ PERF (issue #154) — THE REAL BOUND, stated for the WORK and not just the RESULT.
-- The RESULT is bounded by IMPORT COUNT x PER-MODULE PUBLIC DECL COUNT: at most one
-- overlay decl per UseGroup member, and — since `wildOverlayDecls` — the wildcard-named
-- module's whole published row per `UseWild`.  It is still a per-module VIEW (the row is
-- the envelope's own memoized `demPubDecls`, not a copy), never a per-module rebuild of
-- the global universe.  A module importing no constructors AND no wildcard overlays
-- NOTHING and stays byte-identical.  A bare `import mod` (UseName) still contributes no
-- overlay: the bare form binds no ctors at all, so no ctor occurrence in the reader can
-- mean that module's declaration by that import.  A wildcard `import mod.*` DOES
-- contribute, as of `wildOverlayDecls` below — it cannot name its owning type, but it
-- does not need to: every decl the module publishes is in scope by construction, and
-- leaving it to the universe-fallback made import-clause ORDER decide which declaration
-- a ctor occurrence meant, in both the spurious-warning and the lost-warning direction.
--
-- The WORK is NOT import-count bounded, and saying it is has already shipped a
-- regression.  `pool` is the WHOLE accumulated universe and it is SCANNED — so the cost
-- is O(members x |pool|) per module, i.e. O(modules x universe) across a run, the same
-- shape `main` has paid since #674 for its `(..)` members.  What keeps that acceptable
-- is entirely the CONSTANT, and the constant has exactly two load-bearing parts, both
-- enforced downstream and both measured:
--   * ONE traversal per member, not three (see `findOverlayDecl`/`overlayScan`); and
--   * ZERO allocation per visited decl (see `overlayNote` — name tests before the
--     identity mint).
-- Break either and the perf gate's `modules` shape goes superlinear: this comment
-- previously asserted the universe-independence outright, the first cut of A-2.6 broke
-- both parts, and the gate read typecheck TIME r2=3.24 (hard threshold 3.0) with net
-- allocation 306/744/2013 MB against `main`'s 242/491/1010.  A cheaper bound is
-- available if it is ever needed — index `pool` by owning module id, which would make
-- the owned passes O(log n) — but it does not exist today, so do not cite one.
--
-- 🚨 A-3.2b (#1512, absorbing #1319 unit 4): THE POOL IS NO LONGER A MATERIALISED
-- ACCUMULATOR.  It used to be `CrossRun.universeDataDecls` — a bare `List Decl`
-- grown by `appendDataUniverse` one module at a time, with NO record of which module
-- contributed what and no key at all beyond list position.  It is now the whole-graph
-- envelope (`DeclEnvs.deModules`, stage K) read at the READER'S OWN ORDINAL through
-- `declEnvRowVisible`, i.e. through `declEnvVisibleTo`'s two conjuncts.  `cur` is
-- `declEnvsOrdOf mid` for the module being checked — a LOADER-derived identity, not a
-- spelling re-derived from import syntax, which is what makes it survive the emit path
-- (see the call site in `checkBodyImpl`).
--
-- ⚠️ THE POPULATION IS NOT BYTE-IDENTICAL TO THE OLD ACCUMULATOR, and the difference
-- is exactly one row: the READER'S OWN module.  The accumulator held only STRICTLY
-- EARLIER modules' public decls (`appendDataUniverse` runs at the END of a module's
-- own pass); `declEnvVisibleTo` admits the reader's own row too, privately, because
-- `entryOrd == cur` short-circuits the publicity conjunct.  That is the ruled
-- predicate and it is deliberately not special-cased away here.  Its reach is
-- bounded: `ownedType`/`ownedCtor` are scoped by `declOwnedBy mid` where `mid` comes
-- from a `DUse` path, and no module imports itself, so an own-module decl can only
-- ever fill the `bareType` fallback — LAST in a FIRST-wins walk, so it can only add a
-- candidate where every earlier row supplied none, never displace one.
-- 🚨 THE RESULT IS PARTITIONED: NAMED (`UseGroup`) ROWS AND WILDCARD (`UseWild`) ROWS
-- ARE TWO SEPARATE HALVES, and which half a consumer must see FIRST depends on that
-- consumer's own tie-break direction.  This is not a style choice; a single flat
-- source-ordered list shipped an S1.
--
-- WHY.  Before `wildOverlayDecls`, only `UseGroup` contributed, and every row was ONE
-- precisely-named decl.  A wildcard row is the module's WHOLE published set, so once
-- `UseWild` was enrolled the flat `flatMap` over the unit's decls in SOURCE ORDER made
-- import-clause order decide same-named-type collisions BETWEEN the two spellings: with
-- `import zzcol.*` written above `import aacol.{Red, Blue}`, a non-exhaustive match over
-- `aacol.Col` was reported as `Missing case: 'Green'` — a constructor of `zzcol.Col`,
-- the wrong type entirely — and the real missing case went unreported, so the program
-- then died at runtime on the case nobody warned about.  Swapping the two clauses fixed
-- it.  Measured on af6e0414 (broken) vs c7c879b7 (correct in BOTH orders).
--
-- THE RULE, and it is the ordinary scoping one: AN EXPLICITLY NAMED MEMBER OUTRANKS A
-- WILDCARD.  "You named this type" beats "you imported everything and it happened to be
-- in there", whichever clause is written first.
--
-- 🚨 THE TWO CONSUMERS TIE-BREAK IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, so they take opposite orders.
-- Enumerated, because a future edit that routes one of them through the other's wrapper
-- silently inverts the rule for that consumer with no diagnostic anywhere:
--   * `registerAllData` (the `dataEnv` overlay) folds the list LEFT and its bare-keyed
--     tables are `omInsert`, i.e. LAST-WRITE-WINS  -> named rows must come LAST
--     -> `importedCtorTypeDeclsLastWins`.
--   * `seedCheckRun` (the exhaustiveness ctor ORACLE) builds `oracleMap` =
--     `omFromPairs (reverseL …)`, i.e. FIRST-WINS   -> named rows must come FIRST
--     -> `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins`.
-- Same rule, both sites; only the list order differs.  The big note at the `dataEnv`
-- call site enumerates the full set of order-observable structures — this pair is the
-- overlay's own contribution to it.
--
-- ⚠️ WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES **NOT** DECIDE: named-vs-named.  Two `UseGroup` clauses
-- each naming a same-spelled type from a different module are still resolved by
-- import-clause order, exactly as before this partition — within each half the walk is
-- the same `flatMap` over `unitDecls` in source order, and neither consumer's tie-break
-- direction moved.  That case is a SEPARATE, PRE-EXISTING defect (it reproduces on
-- c7c879b7, before the wildcard arm existed) and fixing it needs the overlay keyed by
-- RESOLVED DECLARATION IDENTITY rather than by bare type name — #1319/#1185 territory,
-- not an ordering change.  Do not "while you are here" it: an ordering answer for
-- named-vs-named would freeze a guess into the oracle.
--
-- ⚠️ PERF: the unit's decl list is walked TWICE (once per half) instead of once, but the
-- POOL — the expensive part, see `THE REAL BOUND` above — is scanned exactly as many
-- times as before: each clause yields its rows in whichever half claims it and `[]` in
-- the other, so the total member/wildcard lookups are unchanged.  The extra cost is one
-- linear pass over `unitDecls`, which is import-clause bounded, not universe bounded.
importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins : List Decl ->
  Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  List Decl
importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins unitDecls cur rows =
  overlayHalf False unitDecls cur rows ++ overlayHalf True unitDecls cur rows

importedCtorTypeDeclsLastWins : List Decl ->
  Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  List Decl
importedCtorTypeDeclsLastWins unitDecls cur rows =
  overlayHalf True unitDecls cur rows ++ overlayHalf False unitDecls cur rows

-- One half of the partition, in the unit's own source order.  `wildHalf` selects which:
-- True = `UseWild` rows only, False = `UseGroup` rows only.
overlayHalf : Bool -> List Decl -> Int -> List DeclEnvModule -> List Decl
overlayHalf wildHalf unitDecls cur rows =
  flatMap (importOverlayDecls wildHalf cur rows) unitDecls

importOverlayDecls : Bool -> Int -> List DeclEnvModule -> Decl -> List Decl
importOverlayDecls wildHalf cur rows (DAttrib _ d) =
  importOverlayDecls wildHalf cur rows d
importOverlayDecls wildHalf cur rows (DUse _ (p@(UseGroup _ ms)) _) =
  if wildHalf then [] else flatMap (memberOverlayDecl cur rows (useModId p)) ms
importOverlayDecls wildHalf cur rows (DUse _ (p@(UseWild _)) _) =
  if wildHalf then wildOverlayDecls cur rows (useModId p) else []
importOverlayDecls _ _ _ _ = []

-- The exhaustiveness oracle's import rows beyond `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins`:
-- every decl a module ALIAS import's module publishes, and the declarations of the
-- constructors a wildcard or alias import reaches through that module's own
-- `export import`.  The oracle reads constructors by bare name (resolve strips `A.`
-- before inference), so without these rows a same-spelled constructor of a module
-- loaded earlier answers for them (#1185).  They follow both halves of
-- `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins` because a bare occurrence means an unaliased
-- import's constructor.  Kept out of `importOverlayDecls`, whose other reader is the
-- `dataEnv` overlay.  A selective `import m.{…}` is already one of those rows, and no
-- decl other than a `DUse` imports anything, so `oracleImportDeclsOf` adds nothing for
-- either.
oracleImportDecls : GraphCtx ->
  List Decl ->
  Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  List Decl
oracleImportDecls gctx unitDecls cur rows =
  flatMap (oracleImportDeclsOf gctx cur rows) unitDecls

oracleImportDeclsOf : GraphCtx -> Int -> List DeclEnvModule -> Decl -> List Decl
oracleImportDeclsOf gctx cur rows (DAttrib _ d) =
  oracleImportDeclsOf gctx cur rows d
oracleImportDeclsOf gctx cur rows (DUse _ (p@(UseAlias _ _)) _) =
  let dep = useModId p
  wildOverlayDecls cur rows dep ++ reexportedCtorDecls gctx cur rows dep
oracleImportDeclsOf gctx cur rows (DUse _ (p@(UseWild _)) _) =
  reexportedCtorDecls gctx cur rows (useModId p)
oracleImportDeclsOf gctx _ _ _ = []

-- The decls declaring the constructors `dep` re-exports from other modules, found
-- through the graph's constructor export index, whose rows carry each constructor's
-- declaring identity across any chain of `export import`.  One decl per (declaring
-- module, type), however many of its constructors are re-exported.
reexportedCtorDecls : GraphCtx ->
  Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  String ->
  List Decl
reexportedCtorDecls gctx cur rows dep =
  match omLookup dep (ctorExportsIndex gctx ())
    None => []
    Some es =>
      flatMap
        (k => reexportedTypeDecl cur rows (fst k) (snd k))
        (dedupBy (k => "\{fst k}.\{snd k}") (flatMap (reexportedOwner dep) es))

-- (declaring module, owning type) of an export row declared outside `dep`.
reexportedOwner : String -> (String, String, Ident) -> List (String, String)
reexportedOwner dep (_, owner, Ident _ io _) =
  identOriginFold [] (m => if m == dep then [] else [(m, owner)]) io

reexportedTypeDecl : Int -> List DeclEnvModule -> String -> String -> List Decl
reexportedTypeDecl _ [] _ _ = []
reexportedTypeDecl cur (m :: rest) declarer tyName =
  if m.demId == declarer then
    filterList (declIsOwnedType declarer tyName) (declEnvRowVisible cur m)
  else
    reexportedTypeDecl cur rest declarer tyName

-- Only a `data` or `newtype` declaration declares constructors, so no other decl owns
-- a type whose constructors a re-export reaches.
declIsOwnedType : String -> String -> Decl -> Bool
declIsOwnedType mid tyName (DAttrib _ d) = declIsOwnedType mid tyName d
declIsOwnedType mid tyName (DData { dataName = tn, dataOrigin = o }) =
  tn == tyName && declOwnedBy mid tn o
declIsOwnedType mid tyName (DNewtype { newtypeName = tn, newtypeOrigin = o }) =
  tn == tyName && declOwnedBy mid tn o
declIsOwnedType _ _ _ = False

-- A wildcard import's overlay: EVERY decl the named module publishes, as the reader at
-- ordinal `cur` may see it.  A wildcard names no member, so there is nothing to look up
-- by name — the whole published row IS the answer, and one row is all a module id can
-- have (`deOrdIndex` keys rows by `demId`), hence the stop at the first match.
--
-- 🚨 `declEnvRowVisible`, NEVER `m.demDecls`.  The visible projection is what confines
-- this to `publicDataDecl`s — public `DData`/`DInterface`/`DImpl`/`DTypeAlias` — and
-- `DNewtype`'s ABSENCE from that predicate is deliberate (see `publicDataDecl`): reading
-- `demDecls` here would leak a wildcard-imported module's PRIVATE and NEWTYPE
-- constructors across the module boundary, draining #1311/#1305 in the loud→silent
-- direction, i.e. a severity INCREASE.  A wildcard-imported `newtype`'s ctors are
-- consequently NOT overlaid; that is the ruled behaviour, not an oversight.
--
-- The private branch of `declEnvRowVisible` (`entryOrd == cur`, the reader's own row) is
-- UNREACHABLE from here for the reason the pool note above already gives: `mid` comes
-- from a `DUse` path, and no module imports itself.
--
-- ⚠️ PERF: this returns the row's OWN memoized `demPubDecls` list — no filtered copy is
-- materialised, per `declEnvRowVisible`'s zero-allocation contract.  It does move the
-- RESULT bound: see the `THE REAL BOUND` note above.
wildOverlayDecls : Int -> List DeclEnvModule -> String -> List Decl
wildOverlayDecls _ [] _ = []
wildOverlayDecls cur (m :: rest) mid =
  if m.demId == mid then
    declEnvRowVisible cur m
  else
    wildOverlayDecls cur rest mid

-- One member's overlay decl.  The bare-name fallback is kept for the `(..)` spelling
-- ALONE, so no overlay that fired before #1111 A-2.6 is lost where the identity cannot
-- match — a RE-EXPORTED type's decl names the ORIGINAL module, not the re-exporter
-- this import path names.  The (b) spellings are new, so they get no fallback: an
-- unscoped guess there would be the load-order coin flip this unit is removing.
-- (The fallback is not a second pass over `pool` — `overlayScan` collects it in the
-- same walk as the two identity-scoped candidates; see `findOverlayDecl`.)
memberOverlayDecl : Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  String ->
  UseMember ->
  List Decl
memberOverlayDecl cur rows mid (UseMember n ctors _ _) =
  match findOverlayDecl mid n ctors cur rows
    Some d => [d]
    None => []

-- The pool decl DECLARED BY `mid` that this member names.
--
-- 🚨 TWO PASSES, not one first-match, and the order between them is the point.  A
-- module may declare BOTH a type `T` and, in an earlier decl, a constructor spelled
-- `T` (`data Wrap = T Int` above `data T = A | B`).  For `import m.{T(..)}` the member
-- means the TYPE — `(..)` is what makes it a type spelling — so the type-name match
-- must be tried over the WHOLE pool before any constructor-name match is considered.
--
-- ⚠️ A single first-match pass that accepted `variantNamed n vs || ctors && tn == n`
-- shipped and was wrong: `import m.{T(..)}` selected `Wrap` (whose ctor is `T`), so
-- `T`'s own `A`/`B` were never overlaid and a universe collision on `A` won instead —
-- `Type mismatch: T vs Other`.  `declOwnedBy` had removed the CROSS-module first-match
-- hazard; this WITHIN-module one is what `variantNamed` newly made reachable.  Pinned
-- by `test/llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_ctors_prefer_type_name/`.
--
-- The constructor pass is still the fallback for `(..)`, not dead: `import m.{C(..)}`
-- where `C` is a constructor and not a type name of `m` keeps its pre-A-2.6 meaning.
--
-- 🚨 THE THREE PASSES SHARE ONE TRAVERSAL, and that is a MEASURED PERF CONTRACT.
-- `pool` is the WHOLE accumulated universe and this runs once per UseGroup member of
-- every module, so each independent pass over it is an O(modules x universe) term.
-- The first cut of A-2.6 ran three (type-owned, then ctor-owned, then the bare
-- fallback) where `main` ran ONE, and that alone put the perf gate's `modules` shape
-- at typecheck TIME r2=3.24 — over the hard 3.0 threshold.  `overlayScan` collects all
-- three candidates in a SINGLE walk (their predicates are independent, so first-wins
-- per candidate is unchanged) and stops the instant the DECIDING candidate is found,
-- which is the early exit the three-pass form had.  See `overlayScan`.
findOverlayDecl : String ->
  String ->
  Bool ->
  Int ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  Option Decl
findOverlayDecl mid n ctors cur rows =
  match overlayScanRows mid n ctors cur (None, None, None) rows
    (ownedType, ownedCtor, bareType) =>
      if ctors then
        orElseOpt (orElseOpt ownedType ownedCtor) bareType
      else
        ownedCtor

-- ONE walk of the pool, THREE first-wins candidates, in the precedence order
-- `findOverlayDecl` applies:
--
--   ownedType — first decl DECLARED BY `mid` whose TYPE name is `n`   (the `(..)` rule)
--   ownedCtor — first decl DECLARED BY `mid` with a CONSTRUCTOR named `n`  (rule (b))
--   bareType  — first decl of ANY owner whose TYPE name is `n`  (the `(..)` fallback,
--               `dataDeclForType`'s old job: a RE-EXPORTED type's decl names the
--               ORIGINAL module, so no identity-scoped pass can ever match it)
--
-- Each is recorded independently and FIRST-wins, which is exactly what three separate
-- first-match passes computed; a decl can legitimately fill two of them at once (a
-- record's sole variant carries the type's own name), so they are collected with
-- INDEPENDENT tests and never with an `else`.
--
-- ⚠️ THE EARLY EXIT IS `ctors`-DEPENDENT AND MUST STAY THAT WAY.  With `(..)` the
-- deciding candidate is `ownedType` (it outranks both others), so finding it ends the
-- walk; WITHOUT `(..)` only `ownedCtor` is ever read, so exiting on `ownedType` would
-- abandon the walk before the answer was found.  Exiting on "all three filled" is
-- likewise wrong for the second case.
-- A-3.2b (#1512): the OUTER half of the same one-traversal walk, over the envelope's
-- per-module rows in ORDINAL order — which is the identical decl order the old
-- accumulator was appended in (`appendDataUniverse` ran once per module, dependency
-- first), so every FIRST-wins candidate below sees the same sequence it saw before.
--
-- 🚨 THE EARLY EXIT MUST BE CHECKED AT BOTH LEVELS.  `overlayScan` stops mid-row on
-- the deciding candidate; without the identical test here the walk would continue into
-- every LATER row having already decided, which is the O(modules x universe) term this
-- whole neighbourhood is written to avoid.  `declEnvRowVisible` allocates nothing, so
-- a skipped row costs one comparison.
overlayScanRows : String ->
  String ->
  Bool ->
  Int ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl) ->
  List DeclEnvModule ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl)
overlayScanRows _ _ _ _ acc [] = acc
overlayScanRows mid n ctors cur acc (m :: rest) =
  let acc2 = overlayScan mid n ctors acc (declEnvRowVisible cur m)
  if overlayDecided ctors acc2 then
    acc2
  else
    overlayScanRows mid n ctors cur acc2 rest

overlayScan : String ->
  String ->
  Bool ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl) ->
  List Decl ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl)
overlayScan _ _ _ acc [] = acc
overlayScan mid n ctors acc (d :: rest) =
  let acc2 = overlayScanOne mid n acc d
  if overlayDecided ctors acc2 then acc2 else overlayScan mid n ctors acc2 rest

overlayDecided : Bool -> (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl) -> Bool
overlayDecided ctors (ownedType, ownedCtor, _) =
  if ctors then isSome ownedType else isSome ownedCtor

overlayScanOne : String ->
  String ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl) ->
  Decl ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl)
overlayScanOne mid n acc (DAttrib _ d) = overlayScanOne mid n acc d
overlayScanOne mid n acc (d@(DData { dataName = tn, dataOrigin = o })) =
  overlayNote mid n tn o d acc
overlayScanOne mid n acc (d@(DNewtype { newtypeName = tn, newtypeOrigin = o })) =
  overlayNote mid n tn o d acc
overlayScanOne _ _ acc _ = acc

-- 🚨 NAME TESTS FIRST, IDENTITY SECOND — a MEASURED perf contract, not style.
-- `tn == n` / `declCtorNamed` are string compares over fields already in hand and
-- ALLOCATE NOTHING; `declOwnedBy` MINTS an identity on both sides (`OriginModule`,
-- `IdentModule`, the `map` closure, the `Ident`, the `Some` — five objects per call).
-- This runs ONCE PER POOL DECL, i.e. over the whole universe per member per module, so
-- minting unconditionally is O(modules x universe) ALLOCATION — `compiler/AGENTS.md`'s
-- named exception ("when the KEY PROJECTION allocates"), and it re-created the #154
-- quadratic that `modules` was promoted out of `KNOWN_SUPERLINEAR` for.  Measured on
-- the gate's own `modules` shape (N=100/200/400, K=8, one worktree, one emitter,
-- interleaved): mint-first 306/744/2013 MB net (r2=2.71) vs name-first 242/491/1010 MB
-- (r2=2.06), which is `main`'s figure to 0.1 MB.  A name test matches at most the
-- handful of pool decls sharing that spelling, so the mint is O(1) per member.
--
-- The unchanged `acc` is returned BY IDENTITY when neither name matches — the common
-- case — so the walk allocates nothing at all until it actually finds something.
overlayNote : String ->
  String ->
  String ->
  TyConOrigin ->
  Decl ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl) ->
  (Option Decl, Option Decl, Option Decl)
overlayNote mid n tn o d acc = match acc
  (ownedType, ownedCtor, bareType) =>
    let hitType = tn == n
    let hitCtor = declCtorNamed n d
    if hitType || hitCtor then
      let owned = declOwnedBy mid tn o
      (
        if hitType && owned then orElseOpt ownedType (Some d) else ownedType,
        if hitCtor && owned then orElseOpt ownedCtor (Some d) else ownedCtor,
        if hitType then orElseOpt bareType (Some d) else bareType,
      )
    else
      acc

-- Does this decl's DECLARATION identity name module `mid`?  Both sides go through
-- `mkIdent`, so an ABSENT identity matches NOTHING — not even another absence.  That
-- polarity is deliberate and is A-2.3's, not A-2.10's: this answers a LOOKUP, where a
-- miss is recoverable (the universe fallback stands, i.e. today's behaviour) and a
-- false hit would silently overlay the wrong module's constructors.  It is why `mid`
-- being `""` (the I6.3 "origin not recorded" sentinel, which `identOriginOf` refuses)
-- cannot pair with an `OriginUnresolved` decl.
declOwnedBy : String -> String -> TyConOrigin -> Bool
declOwnedBy mid tyName o = match mkIdent NsType (OriginModule mid) tyName
  Some want => mkIdent NsType o tyName == Some want
  None => False

-- ── the local-spelling → DEFINER map, for EVERY import form ─────────────────────
-- This is the ONE representation the dict-arity identity seam resolves through:
-- `qualConstraintKey` turns a name into a `(definer, origin)` key with it, and
-- `hasImportDefiner` reads the same rows to decide that a qual MISS means "genuinely
-- unconstrained in its source module" rather than "look in the source-blind bare
-- table".  Every reader of that decision — `declaredConstraintFor`,
-- `declaredConstraintIds`, the pinned-var guard — takes its answer from here, and
-- none of them knows which import FORM produced a row.  So the forms are normalized
-- here, once, and a form whose rows are missing is not a gap in one arm: it is a
-- silent fall-through to bare-name first-match for that whole spelling.
--
-- 🚨 `import m.*` USED TO CONTRIBUTE NOTHING, and that hole is #1425's F1.
-- `importedBindings` answers `None` for `UseWild` because a wildcard's member list
-- cannot be read off the `DUse` decl alone — it is every public top-level binding the
-- DEPENDENCY declares — so this walk simply skipped it and every wildcard-imported
-- name fell to the bare table.  While the bare table happened to be wrong on BOTH
-- sides of a same-name collision (a foreign constrained row sized the define AND the
-- call), the two errors cancelled and the spelling looked correct.  `selfDeclArities`
-- then repaired the DEFINE side by identity; the call side, still on the bare table,
-- began applying a dict its callee no longer abstracts —
-- `E-NOT-A-FUNCTION: applied non-function: <dict:>` on nine ordinary 3-line stdlib
-- programs, in BOTH import orders, behind a `check` that exits 0.  The defect did not
-- move because the repair was wrong; it moved because the repair was applied at one
-- arm of a seam whose other arm had no rows at all.
--
-- The graph is what supplies the missing member list, and it is already built and
-- already keyed by module id: `declEnvsRef.deModules`.  On the Flat/loader-less paths
-- that envelope is EMPTY, so the wildcard rows are empty and this is byte-identically
-- today's behaviour — the same fail-closed-on-absent-identity discipline
-- `selfDeclArities` takes for `mid == ""`.
--
-- ORDER AND SHADOWING.  Named-form rows come FIRST (every reader is first-match), and a
-- wildcard row is dropped when the name is already spoken for — by a named import of
-- this module, or by this module's OWN top-level declaration, which shadows the
-- wildcard binding and whose arity `selfDeclArities` answers from its own qual row.
--
-- 🚨 THE SUPPLY SIDE DOES NOT ENUMERATE (RUN-XMOD-062).  It used to, twice over — one
-- walk for the named forms, one for the wildcard form, each deciding for itself what a
-- dependency "makes available".  Both walks answered that with the names a dependency
-- DECLARES, so a name it RE-EXPORTS (`export import u.{pick}` / `export import u.*`,
-- at any depth) got no row and fell to the source-blind bare table — the same
-- `E-NOT-A-FUNCTION: applied non-function: <dict:>` on a well-typed program, one hop
-- further out.  That was the THIRD enumeration in this seam to miss a member.  The rows
-- are now DERIVED: `graphPubDefiners` answers "what does module m export, and who
-- DEFINED each of those names" (declared publics, plus re-exports resolved through the
-- same predicate, transitively), and `importFormRows` — the one admission predicate,
-- shared with `importFormSchemes` — answers "which of those does this import form
-- admit, under which local key".  A form or a re-export shape this seam has never seen
-- is covered by construction, not by a case.
--
-- Also, the payload is now a PAIR: `(definer module, origin name at the definer)`,
-- because an IMPORTER can rename a re-exported member (`import r1.{pick as pk}` — an
-- ordinary import with an as-alias on a name that happens to be re-exported).  A
-- RE-EXPORT hop itself cannot rename: `export import u.{pick as pk}` is a hard parse
-- error (`parser.mdk:2379`), so that is not the motivating shape.  The qual tables are
-- keyed `(definer, ORIGINAL exported name)`, so carrying only the definer would key them
-- with the local spelling, MISS, and — via `hasImportDefiner` — read that miss as
-- "genuinely unconstrained", silently dropping the callee's dict.  Both halves of the
-- key come out of one derivation; they cannot disagree.
importDefinersOf : GraphCtx -> List Decl -> List (String, (String, String))
importDefinersOf gctx prog =
  let named = useAdmittedRows gctx False prog
  let taken = namesToSet (map fst named ++ declTopFnNames prog) omEmpty
  named
    ++ filterList
      (e => not (omHasKey (fst e) taken))
      (useAdmittedRows gctx True prog)

-- one precedence TIER of the walk above — [wild] selects the wildcard-form rows or the
-- named-form rows.  This is a precedence split, not a re-enumeration of the forms:
-- WHICH names a form admits is `importFormRows`' answer in both tiers.
useAdmittedRows : GraphCtx ->
  Bool ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, (String, String))
useAdmittedRows gctx _ [] = []
useAdmittedRows gctx wild ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) =
  useAdmittedRows gctx wild [d] ++ useAdmittedRows gctx wild rest
useAdmittedRows gctx wild ((DUse _ path _) :: rest) =
  useDefinerRows gctx wild path ++ useAdmittedRows gctx wild rest
useAdmittedRows gctx wild (_ :: rest) = useAdmittedRows gctx wild rest

-- `core` is skipped: the implicit prelude is never an explicit provenance (every reader
-- of these rows relies on that — see `declaredConstraintFor`).
useDefinerRows : GraphCtx -> Bool -> UsePath -> List (String, (String, String))
useDefinerRows gctx wild path =
  let depId = useModId path
  if depId == "core" then
    []
  else match importedBindings path
    None => if wild then graphPubDefiners gctx depId else []
    Some bindings =>
      if wild then
        []
      else
        flatMap (memberDefinerRow depId (graphPubDefiners gctx depId)) bindings

-- resolve ONE named member against the dependency's export table.  The fallback is the
-- load-bearing part: on the Flat / loader-less drivers `deModules` is EMPTY, so the
-- table is silent about every dependency, and a named import must keep contributing its
-- row (attributed to the SPELLED module, which is what it contributed before this
-- derivation existed) rather than vanish.  The wildcard form has no such fallback — it
-- cannot enumerate without the graph — which is the same fail-closed-on-absent-identity
-- discipline `selfDeclArities` takes for `mid == ""`.
memberDefinerRow : String ->
  List (String, (String, String)) ->
  (String, String) ->
  List (String, (String, String))
memberDefinerRow depId pubs (origin, local) =
  match resolveMemberRows (origin, local) pubs
    [] => [(local, (depId, origin))]
    rows => rows

-- #739 fix follow-up: local-import-name → ORIGINAL exported name.  `importedBindings`
-- yields (origin, local) pairs, so an aliased member `{x as y}` gives (y ↦ x) and a plain
-- `{f}` gives (f ↦ f).  qualConstraintFor keys the (source, name) qual tables by the ORIGIN,
-- not the surface alias — the tables are attributed under the DEFINING name — so a constrained
-- aliased import (`hash_map.{set as hmSet}`) resolves its `Hash`/`Eq` dict instead of being
-- misclassified as unconstrained and short-circuited (which DROPPED the dict → dispatch panic).
--
-- RUN-XMOD-062: this is now a PROJECTION of `importDefinersOf`'s rows rather than its own
-- `DUse` walk.  Two walks over the import forms could disagree about which rows exist —
-- and a `(definer, origin)` key assembled from two disagreeing walks is exactly the
-- silent misroute this seam pays for.  `definerRowsOf` is its sibling projection; both
-- take the SAME rows, computed once at the single write site.
originRowsOf : List (String, (String, String)) -> List (String, String)
originRowsOf rows = map (e => (fst e, snd (snd e))) rows

definerRowsOf : List (String, (String, String)) -> List (String, String)
definerRowsOf rows = map (e => (fst e, fst (snd e))) rows

-- the (key, scheme) pairs a module RE-EXPORTS via `export import` (`DUse True`).
-- Mirrors resolve's re-export set (resolve.pubUsePaths filters the same `DUse True`)
-- into the typecheck seed: walk the export-flagged DUse decls and, for each, pick the
-- re-exported member names out of the SOURCE dependency's public schemes already in
-- depEnv — the exact member resolution importSeed uses (importFormSchemes /
-- resolveMemberRows), so it works on both golden and mangled emit paths.  Schemes
-- come from depEnv by IDENTITY (the definer's original schemes, never re-generalized),
-- which preserves the original-definer constraint ids (D2 diamond-safety).  ONLY
-- `DUse True` is threaded, so a plain `import` never leaks the importer's names
-- downstream.  Transitivity is automatic: pubV accumulates these and is carried
-- forward as depEnv, so a second `export import` hop re-resolves against the enriched
-- pubV.  Methods need no special case — the source module's pubV already holds the
-- method scheme (publicValNames includes DInterface method names).
--
-- `core` is the one source that is NOT in depEnv: it is the implicit prelude, so its
-- schemes go into `baseSeed`, not into the per-module dep chain.  It is therefore
-- passed separately as `coreV` — without it, `lookupAssoc "core" depEnv` missed and
-- `export import core.{filter}` re-exported nothing (in silence, and forever).
-- `coreV` is consulted ONLY here, on `DUse True`: `importSeed` keeps taking the plain
-- depEnv, so a plain `import core.{…}` still seeds nothing (its names are already in
-- baseSeed — seeding them twice would only add duplicate assoc entries).
reexportSeed : List (String, a) ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List (String, a)) ->
  List (String, a)
reexportSeed _ [] _ = []
reexportSeed coreV ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) depEnv =
  reexportSeed coreV [d] depEnv ++ reexportSeed coreV rest depEnv
reexportSeed coreV ((DUse True path _) :: rest) depEnv =
  let depId = useModId path
  let src = if depId == "core" then Some coreV else lookupAssoc depId depEnv
  match src
    None => reexportSeed coreV rest depEnv
    Some depSchemes =>
      importFormSchemes depEnv path depSchemes ++ reexportSeed coreV rest depEnv
reexportSeed coreV (_ :: rest) depEnv = reexportSeed coreV rest depEnv

foldModules : Accumulate ->
  (ModuleCtx -> String -> ModuleSeed -> List Decl -> List Decl -> List Decl -> (ModuleBindings, e, ModuleCtx)) ->
  Option (ModuleServe e) ->
  (Bool -> String -> e -> acc -> acc) ->
  acc ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, ExportBinding) ->
  List (String, List (String, ExportBinding)) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (acc, Option (ModuleServe e), ModuleCtx)
foldModules acc worker serve collect base baseSeed coreV depEnv accData accAll prev modules =
  let (done, served, last) =
    workModules
      acc
      worker
      serve
      baseSeed
      coreV
      depEnv
      accData
      accAll
      []
      prev
      modules
  (collectModules collect base done, served, last)

-- `foldModules`' loop: runs the worker over [modules] head-first, threading the
-- accumulators, the record and the serve policy, and returns each module's payload,
-- newest first.  The recursive call is a tail call: a frame waiting on the rest would
-- keep its module's record reachable until the graph end.
workModules : Accumulate ->
  (ModuleCtx -> String -> ModuleSeed -> List Decl -> List Decl -> List Decl -> (ModuleBindings, e, ModuleCtx)) ->
  Option (ModuleServe e) ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, ExportBinding) ->
  List (String, List (String, ExportBinding)) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, e) ->
  ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, e), Option (ModuleServe e), ModuleCtx)
workModules _ _ serve _ _ _ _ _ done prev [] = (done, serve, prev)
workModules _ worker serve baseSeed coreV depEnv accData accAll done prev [(mid, prog)] =
  let seed = moduleSeedFor prev baseSeed coreV mid prog depEnv
  let (_, extra, next) = worker prev mid seed accData accAll prog
  ((mid, extra) :: done, serve, next)
workModules acc worker serve baseSeed coreV depEnv accData accAll done prev ((mid, prog) :: rest) =
  let step = map (policy => policy.msStep mid prog prev) serve
  let (own, done2, serve2, next) = match step
    Some (ServeHit own extra policy) =>
      (own, (mid, extra) :: done, Some policy, prev)
    _ =>
      let seed = moduleSeedFor prev baseSeed coreV mid prog depEnv
      let (schemes, extra, next) = worker prev mid seed accData accAll prog
      let own = publicModuleExports prog schemes
      match step
        Some (ServeCheck after) =>
          let (done3, policy) = after next own ((mid, extra) :: done)
          (own, done3, Some policy, next)
        _ => (own, (mid, extra) :: done, serve, next)
  workModules
    acc
    worker
    serve2
    baseSeed
    coreV
    ((mid, own ++ reexportSeed coreV prog depEnv) :: depEnv)
    (match acc
      AccumulateBoth => accData ++ publicDataDecls prog
      AccumulateNone => accData)
    (match acc
      AccumulateBoth => accAll ++ prog
      AccumulateNone => accAll)
    done2
    next
    rest

-- The import-scoped seed [mid] is checked from: [baseSeed] and what [prog]'s imports
-- select from [depEnv].
moduleSeedFor : ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List (String, ExportBinding) ->
  String ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List (String, ExportBinding)) ->
  ModuleSeed
moduleSeedFor prev baseSeed coreV mid prog depEnv =
  moduleImportSeed
    prev
    baseSeed
    (importSeed prev.mcGraph coreV (admittedIn prev.mcGraph mid) prog depEnv)

-- The right fold of `collect` over the payloads [done] (newest first): the last module
-- first, the only one `collect` sees as last, onto [base].
collectModules : (Bool -> String -> e -> acc -> acc) ->
  acc ->
  List (String, e) ->
  acc
collectModules _ base [] = base
collectModules collect base ((mid, extra) :: older) =
  fold (acc (m, x) => collect False m x acc) (collect True mid extra base) older

-- The core pass, through the prelude interface (`types/prelude_interface.mdk`).
-- Under the caller's prelude key a hit is the cached interface whose section lists
-- this drive's IO join.  A miss checks core, drains it, freezes the result, caches
-- it when keyed, and continues from that frozen value as a hit does, so the record
-- past core is one shape however the drive came by it: core's evidence, binder rows,
-- scope frames and goals stay in the run core was checked in, and its schemes reach
-- inference as fresh cells.
-- The interface's extern catalog is the one [runtimeDecls] alone build, not the
-- process's union, which holds whatever runtimes earlier drives declared.
-- Returns the bindings inference reads, the prelude's published rows and the module
-- record to continue from, whose `mcGraph` is the graph record.
checkCoreMemoized : ModuleCtx ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  (ModuleBindings, List (String, PubScheme), ModuleCtx)
checkCoreMemoized prev key runtimeDecls coreDecls runtimeSeed =
  let gctx = prev.mcGraph
  let join = gctx.config.value.tcIoJoin
  let cached = match key
    Some k => preludeCacheHit k join
    None => None
  match cached
    Some pi => continueFromInterface gctx gctx.graphRun pi
    None =>
      -- #2705: core is marked inside the pass, with the unsplit set
      -- (`moduleMarkCtx`'s core arm), in the run the continuation below leaves.
      let (coreSchemes, _, coreCtx) =
        checkModuleFull prev runtimeSeed [] coreDecls
      -- #2705: drain core's stamping context before the freeze, so core is solved
      -- once per process rather than once per analyze.  This treats core as closed
      -- at its own end.  The prelude has no diagnostics list for a hole to land in.
      match checkGraphFinish coreCtx
        [] => ()
        f :: _ => panic "evidence hole in the prelude: \{tcMsg f.dfDiag}"
      -- #673/#837: the published rows carry core's own obligations, keyed by bare
      -- name; the thaw derives `coreSchemeObligationsRef` from them.
      let pi =
        freezeInterface
          gctx.crossRun
          gctx.graphRun
          (externSigsToMap runtimeDecls omEmpty)
          (publishCore (coreOwnContexts coreCtx) coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
          (omMapValues (_ => ()) coreSchemes.mbStandaloneSchemes)
          coreCtx.mcCells.invocationViewRef.value
      match key
        Some k => seedPreludeInterface k [([join], pi)]
        None => ()
      continueFromInterface gctx (graphLineageRun (internersOf gctx).lineage) pi

-- The record a drive continues from past the core pass: [gctx] with [pi] thawed
-- into it.  [run] is a graph run core was not checked in; it takes the interface's
-- id counters and existential cells and nothing else of core's.  Core's module
-- record is not carried: the diagnostics worker seeds each module from its own
-- decls (`seedCheckRun`), and `cmCheckWorker` seeds its first module from this
-- blank record, an empty oracle (the one core was checked from) and no warning.
-- The interface's externs join the drive's catalog, whose own rows lose to them on
-- a collision, so a hit leaves the catalog a miss would and never shrinks it.
continueFromInterface : GraphCtx ->
  GraphRun ->
  PreludeInterface ->
  (ModuleBindings, List (String, PubScheme), ModuleCtx)
continueFromInterface gctx run pi =
  let (bindings, corePub, cross, seed) = thawInterface gctx pi
  run.tyvarCounter := seed.grsTyvarNext
  run.effvarCounter := seed.grsEffvarNext
  run.ctorExistentialsRef := seed.grsCtorExistentials
  run.anyExistentialsRef := seed.grsAnyExistentials
  let restored = GraphCtx { gctx |
    crossRun = cross,
    graphRun = run,
    builtinExternNamesRef =
      Ref (IdMap.union pi.piExternNames gctx.builtinExternNamesRef.value),
  }
  let mctx = initialModuleCtx restored
  mctx.mcCells.invocationViewRef := readInvocationView pi.piInvocationView
  (bindings, corePub, mctx)

preludePreambleMemoRef : Ref (Option (((Int, Int), String), PreludePreamble))
preludePreambleMemoRef = Ref None

preludePreambleOf : GraphCtx -> List Decl -> DeclEnvModule -> PreludePreamble
preludePreambleOf gctx coreDecls coreRow = PreludePreamble {
  ppEnvAcc = declEnvAccOf gctx [coreRow] emptyDeclEnvAcc,
  ppMangledFunDefs = unitCarriesMangledFunDefs "core" (funDefs coreDecls),
  ppMarkFacts = preludeMarkFactsOf coreDecls,
}

preludePreambleFor : GraphCtx ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  DeclEnvModule ->
  PreludePreamble
preludePreambleFor gctx None coreDecls coreRow =
  preludePreambleOf gctx coreDecls coreRow
preludePreambleFor gctx (Some k) coreDecls coreRow =
  match !preludePreambleMemoRef
    Some entry =>
      if fst entry == (k, gctx.config.value.tcIoJoinProfile) then
        snd entry
      else
        preludePreambleStore gctx k coreDecls coreRow
    None => preludePreambleStore gctx k coreDecls coreRow

-- One entry only (the prelude does not change under a process); a different
-- prelude simply misses and replaces it.
preludePreambleStore : GraphCtx ->
  (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  DeclEnvModule ->
  PreludePreamble
preludePreambleStore gctx k coreDecls coreRow =
  let p = preludePreambleOf gctx coreDecls coreRow
  preludePreambleMemoRef := Some ((k, gctx.config.value.tcIoJoinProfile), p)
  p

-- The drive's configuration: the IO join of the selected profile's grants (after the
-- effect domains it reads), the profile's name, and the stdlib ownership.
export
tcConfigOf : List (String, Param) -> DriveInputs -> TcConfig
tcConfigOf domains inputs = TcConfig {
  tcIoJoin = ioJoinAtoms domains inputs.diIoJoinGrants,
  tcIoJoinProfile = inputs.diProfileName,
  tcStdlibOwned = inputs.diStdlibOwned,
  tcFlatEntryIsStdlib = inputs.diFlatEntryIsStdlib,
  tcKey =
    "\{inputs.diProfileName}\n\{inputs.diFlatEntryIsStdlib}\n\{joinWith "," (omKeys inputs.diStdlibOwned)}",
}

-- [admitted]: the per-module method admissions `renameAliasedMethods` computed for
-- the drive's graph, or `omEmpty` on a drive that does not rename.  The last
-- component is the record the core pass leaves current, which the drive's first
-- module starts from.
graphPreamble : DriveInputs ->
  OrdMap (List (String, String)) ->
  GraphOut ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, Scheme), ModuleBindings, Option (List (String, PubScheme)), OrdMap (List VecObl), List Decl, MarkSets, ModuleCtx)
graphPreamble inputs admitted sel preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  -- S-emit-interleave-leak (#2049), TREES only: THE EMIT DRIVER OWNS ITS OWN
  -- (empty) ctor oracle.  A module checked without a `CheckRunSeed` carries the
  -- previous module record's `matchOracle`, and the tree arm's core pass is one
  -- (the seed is `seedCheckRun`'s, reached from `checkModuleFullDiags`
  -- only).  So every `oGetCtors`
  -- reader the tree arm reaches — `ctorSiblingWithholdingName`, `matchCol0Type` —
  -- would read whatever the LAST CHECK RUN IN THIS PROCESS left behind.
  --
  -- That residue used to be unreachable by accident rather than absent: `typeCtors`
  -- is keyed by DECLARATION IDENTITY (`tabKeyOf NsType <dataOrigin> <tyname>`), the
  -- flat entries build it from UNSTAMPED decls (`TkBare` rows), and this arm's
  -- readers mint `TkIdent` for a module-stamped head — so every lookup MISSED and
  -- every reader fell through its fail-open `None` arm.  A `Module`-arm re-check
  -- interleaved between two elaborations (the emit path's async-wrap re-check)
  -- writes rows under the entry module's OWN identity, i.e. under exactly the key
  -- these readers mint — so the lookups start HITTING, against rows built from the
  -- UN-MANGLED tree while the drive walks the MANGLED one.  MEASURED:
  -- `ctorSiblingWithholdingName` then reads the un-mangled ctor `A` as a sibling of
  -- the mangled survivor and false-rejects `a.k` with `Field 'k' is not declared by
  -- every constructor of 'A'`; `Display` cannot ground, the `println` route stays
  -- RNone, and the emitter writes a NULL dict word (`i64 0`) — SILENTLY on
  -- `runEmitWith`'s own path (`compiler/entries/entry_support.mdk`), which never
  -- reads `hadTypeErrors` after the call.  Clean exit 0, SIGSEGV at runtime.
  --
  -- The module record starts from `initialModuleCtx` of the minted graph record,
  -- whose oracle is empty, so no reader can see a previous run's residue, no matter
  -- which arm (or none) ran before.  It is what the core pass below runs against; each
  -- user module then overwrites it from its own decls (`checkModuleFullDiags` →
  -- `seedCheckRun`).
  --
  -- A process holding the prelude's elaborated trees (`types/prelude_elab.mdk`)
  -- starts every lineage past the evidence ordinals those trees carry, so no drive
  -- of it mints one again, whichever arm it takes.  Local binder names need no such
  -- raise: they carry their module's id.
  match heldPreludeEvCounter ()
    Some k =>
      let lineage = interners.value.lineage
      lineage.lnEvCounter := max lineage.lnEvCounter.value k
    None => ()
  let gctx = mintGraphCtx inputs admitted
  let initial = initialModuleCtx gctx
  -- #80/#201: populate effect domains ONCE over the WHOLE import graph (builtins +
  -- every `effect …` decl in core or any module), BEFORE the per-module foldModules
  -- loop, so a module using an effect declared in ANOTHER module sees its domain.
  -- `freshModuleCtx` no longer re-seeds, so this survives every per-module reset below.
  -- Must precede the core checkModuleFull (it runs checkEffectParams, reading these).
  --
  -- It must also precede the declaration ENVELOPE below, and that ordering is the
  -- newer half of the constraint: the envelope's atom-guarantee seed
  -- (`declEnvRowAtomsEntries` → `dtopFor`) READS this table, so an envelope built
  -- first degrades every parameterized label to its ⊤ and over-rejects an honest
  -- relay into an abstractly-exported wrapper.
  --
  -- #1112 A-3.1: the units here are what `deAllDecls` flattens (`declEnvDeclsOf`),
  -- in the same order; `populateEffectDomainsGraph` walks them without building
  -- that concatenation a second time.
  populateEffectDomainsGraph gctx coreDecls modules
  -- What `IO` joins, from the grants of the profile the verb selected, after the
  -- domains the atoms take their tops from.  `run` checks against `runProfile` of
  -- that profile, whose host-label grants are the selected profile's own.
  gctx.config := tcConfigOf gctx.effectDomains.value inputs
  -- the prelude's own contribution to every derivation below, from the memo when
  -- the caller supplied a prelude key -- see `PreludePreamble`.  The row is built
  -- HERE, not inside the memo, because it carries THIS analyze's decls.
  let coreRow = declEnvModule gctx 0 "core" coreDecls
  let modRows = declEnvModulesFrom gctx 1 modules
  let prelude = preludePreambleFor gctx preludeKey coreDecls coreRow
  -- L3 (xmod-identity #1351 spine): the graph-level mangled-funDefs fact, under the
  -- two-part safety property stated on `meExports` (F3: unconditional
  -- whole-value overwrite at the Module-mode driver entry + no reset point after it
  -- is what keeps compile N's flag from leaking into compile N+1).  The two terms
  -- are the `("core", coreDecls) :: modules` unit list split on its head, which
  -- `graphCarriesMangledFunDefs` folds with `||`.
  gctx.mangledFunDefsPresentRef :=
    prelude.ppMangledFunDefs || graphCarriesMangledFunDefs modules
  -- #1112 A-3.1: the whole-graph declaration envelope, under the same two-part
  -- property as the line above (nothing resets it within the drive, and the
  -- Module-mode driver entry writes it unconditionally).  See `buildDeclEnvs`.  It
  -- carries the method namespace (`deMethods`) and the constructor export index
  -- (`deData.deCtorExports`), so it is written before any module — the core pass
  -- included — reads a method's scope or a constructor's import witness.
  let declEnvs = buildDeclEnvs gctx prelude.ppEnvAcc coreRow modRows
  gctx.declEnvsRef := declEnvs
  -- #1280: `coreDecls` arrives here ALREADY STAMPED (the caller's
  -- `stampGraphTyOrigins` line), but the scope is derived from its type
  -- NAMES, not its origins, so it is the same map either way.
  -- An extern's signature may index a prelude head by an authority
  -- (`netSend : Socket h -> … <Net h> …`), and a name in an index slot is an
  -- authority binder only by the head's declared kind.
  let runtimeSeed = withDeclParamKinds initial coreDecls (u =>
    externSchemes initial (runtimeExternTyOriginScope coreDecls) runtimeDecls)
  seedAbstractRecordTypes gctx coreDecls modules
  -- #2705: BOTH arms MARK (solving is not optional), with the same sets, and each
  -- module is marked on the schedule inside `checkBodyImpl` — after any memo
  -- restore of `GraphCtx.graphRun`, never before, which is what keeps a minted cell indexing
  -- the table it was minted into.  `markSetsOf` publishes the sets to
  -- `checkBodyImpl` and writes `mangledShadowMapRef`/`argDispatchIdxByIdRef`.
  -- The split of the mark set (`msMarkRpNames` vs `msMarkSharedNames`) and every
  -- input that can become an `EMethodAt` are enumerated in `moduleMarkCtx`; the
  -- return-position/non-nameable axis it scopes is expressed by
  -- `test/shadow_fixtures/d24_definer_return_pos_not_nameable/`.
  let ms =
    markSetsOf gctx prelude.ppMarkFacts coreDecls modules declEnvs.deAllDecls
  -- Both arms PROMOTE: an unsignatured user fn whose body dispatches a method gets
  -- an inferred constraint and joins the dict-name set, so its occurrences route
  -- through its own dictionary parameter on every verb.  The completeness judgment
  -- reads those routes, and a check driver that left such a body unpromoted would
  -- see `RNone` where the tree arm sees `RDict`.  The eligible set is written and
  -- the harvest cleared at the entry of every drive, which `maybeInferConstraint`
  -- reads.
  setDictEligible gctx (moduleUserFnNames modules)
  gctx.promotionHarvestRef := []
  -- The core pass.  The diags arm continues from the prelude interface, cached
  -- under the caller's prelude key (see `checkCoreMemoized`, which drains core's
  -- stamping context before it freezes).  The tree arm needs core's dict-passed
  -- decls as well, which an interface does not hold: it continues from the
  -- interface only when the process also holds the prelude's shipped trees for
  -- the drive's key (`treeArmHit`), and otherwise runs the pass outright and
  -- drains only at graph end.
  -- The prelude's published rows come back here only when this pass closed core
  -- (the diags arm, and the tree arm's hit); otherwise the tree arm publishes them
  -- after its graph-end drain.  Both arms return core's own contexts as a value.
  -- The tree arm's snapshot is never written to
  -- `CrossRun.coreSchemeObligationsRef`, which inference reads.
  let (coreSchemes, corePub, coreContexts, coreTree, coreCtx) = match sel
    GOutDiags =>
      let (bindings, published, coreCtx) =
        checkCoreMemoized initial preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls runtimeSeed
      -- The graph record the drive continues from, with the interface thawed into
      -- it.  Nothing holds `initial` past this point.
      noteInterners coreCtx.mcGraph
      (
        bindings,
        Some published,
        coreCtx.mcGraph.crossRun.coreSchemeObligationsRef.value,
        [],
        coreCtx,
      )
    GOutTrees => match treeArmHit inputs coreDecls gctx
      Some (pe, pi) =>
        let (bindings, published, coreCtx) =
          continueFromInterface gctx gctx.graphRun pi
        noteInterners coreCtx.mcGraph
        (
          bindings,
          Some published,
          coreCtx.mcGraph.crossRun.coreSchemeObligationsRef.value,
          pe.peDecls,
          coreCtx,
        )
      None =>
        let (bindings, tree, coreCtx) =
          elabModuleStamp initial "" runtimeSeed [] coreDecls coreDecls
        (bindings, None, coreOwnContexts coreCtx, tree, coreCtx)
  (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, corePub, coreContexts, coreTree, ms, coreCtx)

-- The prelude's shipped trees for a tree-arm drive (`types/prelude_elab.mdk`), moved
-- onto the binders of [coreDecls], the drive's own desugar of core: held for the
-- drive's prelude key.
treeArmElab : DriveInputs -> List Decl -> Option PreludeElab
treeArmElab inputs coreDecls = match inputs.diPreludeKey
  Some k => preludeElabFor k coreDecls
  None => None

-- Those trees with the interface of the same key cached for the drive's IO join:
-- a drive takes both or neither.
treeArmHit : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  GraphCtx ->
  Option (PreludeElab, PreludeInterface)
treeArmHit inputs coreDecls gctx = do
  k <- inputs.diPreludeKey
  pi <- preludeCacheHit k gctx.config.value.tcIoJoin
  pe <- treeArmElab inputs coreDecls
  Some (pe, pi)

-- `graphPreamble`'s `(runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, record)` projection, for the
-- schemes-only driver (`checkModulesK`), which neither marks trees nor reads the mark
-- sets back.
checkModulesPreambleK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, Scheme), ModuleBindings, ModuleCtx)
checkModulesPreambleK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  let (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, _, _, _, _, coreCtx) =
    graphPreamble
      inputs
      omEmpty
      GOutDiags
      preludeKey
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls
      modules
  (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, coreCtx)

-- worker: plain per-module typecheck (no diagnostics harvest, no accAll).  Surfaces
-- `schemes` twice — once as the pubV source foldModules needs, once as the payload
-- checkModules collects.
--
-- #1508 (U2a): it calls `checkModuleFullImpl` with the module id `foldModules` hands
-- it, so every user module reaches `checkBodyImpl`'s Module arm under its own id
-- (`declEnvsOrdOf`), as `graphModuleWorker` does through `checkModuleFullDiags`; only
-- the prelude is `""`, and a user module keyed `""` loses the qualified cross-module
-- tables (below).  It passes `accData` and `[]` implDecls (the perf-gate
-- path reads neither accumulator).
--
-- 🚨 THIS IS NOT ATTRIBUTION-ONLY, AND THE FIRST DRAFT OF THIS COMMENT SAID IT WAS.
-- The `""` key made `crossModuleSchemeOblsQualRef` and its three sibling qual tables
-- unreadable on this driver — their readers derive `src` from a real import path
-- (`useModId`), so they missed universally — which means an INFERRED
-- cross-module obligation was SILENTLY DROPPED from the importer's scheme.  MEASURED,
-- two arms, same fixture and `MEDAKA_ROOT`, only the probe differing:
--
--   p.mdk      export f x = "\{x}!"        -- Display a, INFERRED (no signature)
--   entry.mdk  import p.{f} / g v = f v
--
--   before:  g : a -> String              ← the `Display a =>` context is GONE
--   after:   g : Display a => a -> String  (and `medaka check`, an untouched driver
--                                           routing through `analyzeProject`, agrees)
--
-- That reached users through `projectEntrySchemes` — LSP project hover reported the
-- unconstrained type.  Pinned at
-- `test/check_module_fixtures/inferred_cross_module_obligation/`, whose header records
-- why the other eight fixtures were blind to the shape (all signatured or
-- interface-method-typed, so their contexts are DECLARED and ride the module seed).
-- An under-claimed fix is one nobody writes a test for, which is exactly what happened
-- between this comment's first draft and adversarial review.
cmCheckWorker : ModuleCtx ->
  String ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List (String, PubScheme), ModuleCtx)
cmCheckWorker prev mid seed _accData _accAll prog =
  -- #2705: this is the SCHEMES-ONLY query driver — LSP hover/completion
  -- (`projectEntrySchemes`) and the linear-time perf gate — and it does NOT solve:
  -- it types the marked tree (`checkBodyImpl` directly, so no stamping context is
  -- queued) and reads schemes off inference.  A solve here would need the impl
  -- universe (`accAll ++ prog`, the concat #154 PR-C removed from this path; MEASURED
  -- to red `diff_compiler_perf_scaling`'s `modules` row at 4.48 against a 4.2
  -- ceiling) and would re-solve the whole graph per hover.  The memoized solve that
  -- makes this path one driver with the others is #2719.
  let (schemes, _, mctx) =
    checkBodyImpl prev.mcGraph (checkRunResidue prev) seed mid [] prog
  (schemes, publishOwn mctx schemes.mbSchemes, mctx)

-- The graph every Module-mode driver typechecks: type-constructor identity
-- acquired over the whole graph (`stampGraphTyOrigins`), then every impl head in
-- alias normal form (`tyAliasNormalForm`).  An impl head enters the instance
-- environment here, once, as the type it denotes, so instance selection,
-- coherence, the super-context checks and both engines read the same head, and
-- none of them expands an alias on it.  The alias table is the whole graph's,
-- keyed by declaration identity, so a head expands with the aliases of the
-- module that wrote it.
stampGraphDecls : List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List Decl, List (String, List Decl))
stampGraphDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  let (coreDecls, modules) = stampGraphTyOrigins coreDecls0 modules0
  let aliases = flatMap graphAliasRow (coreDecls ++ flatMap snd modules)
  if isEmptyL aliases then
    (coreDecls, modules)
  else
    (
      map (implHeadNormalDecl aliases) coreDecls,
      map (m => (fst m, map (implHeadNormalDecl aliases) (snd m))) modules,
    )

-- check every module in dependency order against a shared prelude + runtime.
-- modules = (moduleId, desugared program) in dependency-first order.
export
checkModules : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, List (String, PubScheme))
checkModules inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  checkModulesK inputs None runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0

-- Keyed form of `checkModules`.  `preludeKey` is the desugar-cache generation pair
-- identifying `runtimeDecls`/`coreDecls0`; passing it lets `checkCoreMemoized` hit
-- the process-wide prelude interface instead of re-typechecking AND re-solving the
-- whole prelude on every call.  `None` reproduces the unkeyed behavior exactly.
--
-- #2719 (a1): the LSP hover/completion driver (`projectEntrySchemes`) used the
-- unkeyed `checkModules`, so an unkeyed lookup never hit and every hover paid
-- a cold prelude check-and-solve.  Caching the interface from HERE cannot poison a
-- later analyze: the miss arm computes the identical thing either way
-- (`checkModuleFull` over core, then `checkGraphFinish`), and this driver's own
-- worker (`cmCheckWorker`) queues no stamping context, so no drain interacts.
export
checkModulesK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, List (String, PubScheme))
checkModulesK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  -- #1110: acquire type-constructor identity over the whole graph before anything
  -- typechecks; the rebind shadows the raw params so no line below can read the
  -- unstamped tree.  See `checkModulesPreambleK` for why it is here and not there.
  let (coreDecls, modules) = stampGraphDecls coreDecls0 modules0
  let (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, coreCtx) =
    checkModulesPreambleK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls modules
  fst (cmFold runtimeSeed coreSchemes coreCtx modules)

-- `checkModulesK` also handing back what its drive left for the last module it
-- checked (its locals and import seed), taken at the drive's end.  It returns no
-- `TcReport`: the drive harvests no diagnostics, so it judges nothing.
export
checkModulesPartialK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  PartialAnalysis
checkModulesPartialK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  fst
    (checkModulesPartialLast inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0)

-- `checkModulesPartialK` beside the record its drive ends in, for the callers that
-- read what the drive left (`matchWarningsNow`).
export
checkModulesPartialLast : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (PartialAnalysis, ModuleCtx)
checkModulesPartialLast inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  let (coreDecls, modules) = stampGraphDecls coreDecls0 modules0
  let (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, coreClosed, coreContexts, _, _, coreCtx) =
    graphPreamble
      inputs
      omEmpty
      GOutDiags
      preludeKey
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls
      modules
  let (rows, lastCtx) = cmFold runtimeSeed coreSchemes coreCtx modules
  (
    partialAnalysis
      rows
      (publishedCore coreClosed coreContexts coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
      (captureEntryTables lastCtx coreContexts modules),
    lastCtx,
  )

-- [coreCtx] is the record the core pass left current; the last module's record comes
-- back beside the rows.
cmFold : List (String, Scheme) ->
  ModuleBindings ->
  ModuleCtx ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, List (String, PubScheme)), ModuleCtx)
cmFold runtimeSeed coreSchemes coreCtx modules =
  -- cmCheckWorker reads NEITHER accData (checkModuleFull → Module path, where
  -- checkBodyImpl binds it `_` and never reads it) NOR accAll (its `_accAll` param),
  -- so AccumulateNone threads them unchanged (no copy) and the `[] []` initials keep
  -- the dead values empty → the whole perf-gate `checkModules` path is O(N).  #2705: this
  -- driver marks but does not solve — see `cmCheckWorker` — so it queues nothing and
  -- drains nothing.
  let (rows, _, lastCtx) =
    foldModules
      AccumulateNone
      cmCheckWorker
      None
      (_last mid schemes rest => (mid, schemes) :: rest)
      []
      (runtimeSeed ++ coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
      (tagModuleExports coreSchemes.mbStandaloneSchemes coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
      []
      []
      []
      coreCtx
      modules
  (map (m => (fst m, freezeRows (snd m))) rows, lastCtx)

-- ── per-module ISOLATED diagnostics (the analyzeProject typecheck stage) ────
-- Like checkModuleFull but ALSO harvests THIS module's type diagnostics: it shares the
-- flat paths' seeding via `seedCheckRun` (#415 item 1 — matchOracle/matchWarnings)
-- and runs coherence over the module's OWN decls, while keeping the
-- module ISOLATED — only
-- PUBLIC schemes/data of earlier modules are seeded (via seedVars/accData), so a
-- private same-named helper in another module never collides (the bug the old
-- flat prefix-concat diagnostics path had).  checkModuleFull calls `freshModuleCtx`,
-- which clears typeErrors at module start, so the post-call typeErrors snapshot is
-- exactly this module's contribution; matchWarnings starts from the seed's empty list
-- (`seedCheckRun`).  Returns (own value schemes, errs, warns) so the
-- driver can thread accV/accData exactly as foldModules does.
--
-- Precondition (#154 PR1/PR2): this is per-module, and the Module path consults the
-- persistent universe accumulators (`universeFunNamesRef` / …), which survive the
-- per-module `freshModuleCtx`.  They are grown by `appendUniverseAccums prog0` per module but
-- must be cleared once per run by `mintGraphCtx` (and seeded by the core pass)
-- before the first module.  Every existing caller goes through a driver that does exactly
-- that (`driveGraphK`, via `graphPreamble`'s `mintGraphCtx`); a future standalone
-- caller that skips it would leak the previous run's universe into this one.
--
-- The stage-K envelope (`GraphCtx.declEnvsRef`, which carries the method namespace
-- `deMethods`) runs the other way (#1111 A-2.5b): it is not a `CrossRun` accumulator and
-- `mintGraphCtx` does not clear it, deliberately — it is derived once from the
-- whole graph with no per-module re-seed, so clearing it mid-run is the defect that put
-- the method index on `GraphCtx` (F1: `check` clean while `run`/`build` reported a
-- type error).  For it the precondition is "overwrite with this run's graph before the
-- first module":
--     gctx.declEnvsRef := declEnvs
-- which `graphPreamble` — the one Module-mode driver entry — does.  The failure modes
-- differ in kind: skipping `mintGraphCtx` leaks the previous run's universe;
-- skipping this write leaves the previous run's envelope in place, and a stale method
-- namespace can pick a wrong interface where an empty one finds nothing.  A standalone
-- caller must do both.
checkModuleFullDiags : GraphCtx ->
  String ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List Decl, List TcDiag, List TcDiag, ModuleCtx)
checkModuleFullDiags gctx mid seedVars accData accAll prog =
  -- #415 item 1: same seeding preamble as the flat entry points (one body), but the
  -- oracle decls are THIS module's own first.
  -- P0-9: THIS module's own decls (`prog`) come FIRST so its constructors win
  -- the oracle's first-wins bare-name tables.  Another loaded module (in accData)
  -- can define a same-named ctor at a different arity (`map`'s arity-5 `Bin` vs
  -- `set`'s arity-4 `Bin`); with accData first, the type-aware `check_match` reads
  -- the WRONG arity/type for this module's own `Bin` in a match and false-warns
  -- W-NONEXHAUSTIVE.  buildOracle is duplicate-tolerant, and accData still
  -- supplies prelude Option/Result ctors (this module never redefines them).
  -- #674 / #1111 A-2.6: the decls whose CONSTRUCTORS this module binds by name are
  -- prepended BEFORE accData so their ctors win the oracle's first-wins bare-name
  -- tables over any same-named ctor a non-imported module contributes by load order —
  -- the exhaustiveness peer of the typecheck-scheme overlay.  Own-module `prog` still
  -- wins first.  The overlay decls come from the persistent universe (same source the
  -- dataEnv overlay uses), which equals accData here but keeps the two overlays in
  -- lockstep.
  --
  -- ⚠️ TWO CLAIMS THAT USED TO STAND HERE ARE FALSE SINCE A-2.6's RULE (b), which is
  -- why they are written out rather than re-pointed:
  --   * NOT "the types this module imports with `(..)`" — EVERY selective
  --     constructor-naming member contributes now (`import m.{T, C}`, and a record's
  --     `import m.{Cfg}`), not only the `(..)` spelling.
  --   * NOT "a module with no `T(..)` import is byte-identical" — only a module that
  --     names no constructor at all AND carries no wildcard import is.  A wildcard
  --     (`import m.*`) contributes m's whole published row without naming any
  --     constructor, since `wildOverlayDecls`; before that arm existed it fell to the
  --     universe fallback and import-clause ORDER decided the oracle's answer.
  -- The PERF claim is corrected at `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins` itself (the result is bounded
  -- by import count x per-module public decl count; the WORK is O(members x |universe|),
  -- held down by one zero-allocation traversal per member).  Do not restate it as "no
  -- O(N²)" here: this
  -- call site is the SECOND per-module scan of the same pool, so it doubles whatever
  -- that constant is.
  -- A-3.2b (#1512): the SECOND consumer of the retired `universeDataDecls`, re-pointed
  -- at stage K in lockstep with the `dataEnv` overlay — the two must stay on the same
  -- pool ("keeps the two overlays in lockstep", above), and `mid` is this function's
  -- own parameter, so the same loader-derived key reaches both.
  let declEnvsHere = gctx.declEnvsRef.value
  -- 🚨 `…FirstWins`: `oracleMap` is `omFromPairs (reverseL …)`, so the FIRST row wins and
  -- the `UseGroup` rows must come FIRST for an explicitly named member to outrank a
  -- wildcard.  The `dataEnv` overlay takes `…LastWins` — opposite order, same rule,
  -- because that consumer is last-write-wins.  See `importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins`.
  let runSeed =
    seedCheckRun
      (prog
        ++ importedCtorTypeDeclsFirstWins
          prog
          (declEnvsOrdOf mid declEnvsHere)
          declEnvsHere.deModules
        ++ oracleImportDecls
          gctx
          prog
          (declEnvsOrdOf mid declEnvsHere)
          declEnvsHere.deModules
        ++ accData)
  -- Bug C: pass the cumulative full impl universe (core + earlier modules) as
  -- implDecls so buildStandaloneShadows sees
  -- an imported standalone funDef that shadows an interface method (`map`'s `isEmpty`
  -- vs Foldable's): without it the importer-shadow set is EMPTY on the CHECK path and
  -- `isEmpty m` routes to method dispatch rather than to the standalone the importer
  -- named.
  -- It feeds the shadow/dispatch-index/key tables and the impl-body inference
  -- universe alike, matching the eval path's elabModuleStamp (which passes
  -- accAll ++ prog too).
  -- #749: thread the REAL module id (not "") so this module's constrained fns are
  -- attributed into crossModuleFunConstraintsQualRef under their OWN source module —
  -- SOURCE-EXACT, exactly as the emit path (elabModuleStamp mid) does.  With mid=""
  -- every module attributed under "", so qualConstraintFor (which keys by the import's
  -- real source module via currentImportDefinersRef) always MISSED for a user-module
  -- constrained callee → declaredCrossModuleObls dropped its obligation → `check`
  -- false-greened on `p.f Z` (Display Z missing) while run/build rejected (the #673
  -- residual #738 fixed only for the prelude/core coreSchemeObligationsRef flow).  The
  -- source-exact keying makes it MISS for a genuinely-unconstrained same-name import
  -- (#739's `q.f`, attributed nowhere), so no over-rejection is reintroduced.
  let (schemes, marked, mctx) =
    checkModuleFullImpl gctx runSeed mid seedVars accData accAll prog
  -- #1557 A-3.5c: the whole-graph `CE`, read at THIS module's ordinal — the same
  -- `declEnvsOrdOf mid` key `seedCheckRun`'s overlay pool above and `moduleImplUniv`
  -- (`checkBodyImpl`) already read at.  ONE env for both members; they differ only in
  -- which population they take from it (`ceRowsOwnedBy` vs `ceRowsVisibleAt`).
  let ceHere = declEnvsHere.deIfaces
  let ordHere = declEnvsOrdOf mid declEnvsHere
  -- #1557 A-3.5b: the whole-graph `IE`, read at the SAME `ordHere` — the same key
  -- `ceHere` and `moduleImplUniv` (`checkBodyImpl`) already read at.
  -- #1559 A-3.7: coherence reads the SAME whole-graph `IE` at the SAME `ordHere`, and
  -- takes this module's OWN rows from it (`cohRowsOwnedBy`) — the population `prog`
  -- named, derived in `checkCoherence`'s header.  This env came
  -- from `buildDeclEnvs`, which seats the prelude at ordinal 0, so the module's rows
  -- are also paired with the prelude's.
  runFinalChecks
    mctx
    prog
    ceHere
    ceHere
    declEnvsHere.deImpls
    declEnvsHere.deImpls
    ordHere
  (
    schemes,
    marked,
    reverseL mctx.mcCells.typeErrors.items.value,
    reverseL mctx.mcCells.matchWarnings.value,
    mctx,
  )
-- Seed the ctor oracle over ALL data decls in scope (prelude + every imported
-- module via accData, plus this module's own prog), NOT just `prog`.  The
-- one-module path sees the same module-scoped dump, so
-- its `buildOracle prog` already includes Option/Result; the multi-module path
-- keeps modules separate, so prelude ADTs live in accData.  Seeding with only
-- `prog` left the oracle missing Some/None|Ok/Err → exhaustive matches on
-- Option/Result false-warned "non-exhaustive".  buildOracle reads only DData
-- and is duplicate-tolerant, so accData ++ prog is safe.

-- checkModuleFull → checkModuleFullImpl runs `freshModuleCtx` first (clears
-- typeErrors + currentLevel), so the snapshot below is this module's own errors.

-- coherence over this module's own impls runs in `runFinalChecks` above: its
-- `checkCoherence` sweeps this module's rows of the whole-graph `IE` (`cohRowsOwnedBy`),
-- and a hard conflict is pushed into typeErrors AFTER the `freshModuleCtx` above.

-- multi-module diagnostics driver: threads the runtime+core seed and per-module
-- PUBLIC accumulators exactly as foldModules threads, but at each module harvests its
-- ISOLATED diagnostics via checkModuleFullDiags.  Returns one
-- (mid, (errs, warns)) per module in load order.  Core is checked ONCE (its
-- diagnostics are not surfaced — it is the trusted prelude, as in checkModules).
-- IMPORT-SCOPED seed (baseSeed ++ importSeed prog depEnv), exactly like
-- foldModules — NOT a flat union of every earlier module's publics, which let
-- a same-named function from a non-imported module win by load order (e.g.
-- string.reverse shadowing the list.reverse a module imports → spurious errors).
-- worker: ISOLATED per-module diagnostics harvest (checkModuleFullDiags — see its
-- doc above).  Surfaces this module's own `schemes` (for pubV) plus the
-- (marked tree, schemes, errs, warns) payload.  IMPORT-SCOPED seed exactly like
-- cmCheckWorker; NOT a flat union.  ONE worker serves EVERY graph consumer — the
-- per-module diagnostics fold behind `analyzeProject` and the LSP, the entry report (`checkModulesEntryFullSplitK`), and the elaboration behind
-- `run`/`build`/`test`/the emit entries.  They differ in what they PROJECT from the
-- payload, never in what a module is checked against.  Keeping it one function is
-- what makes that a property of the code rather than of a comment: the consumers
-- CANNOT drift on the universe or the seed.
--
-- The `GOutTrees` writes are the only per-module divergence, and each is a
-- consequence of dict-passing rather than of checking: `mainSchemeRef` is written
-- Some-only so the last module binding
-- `main` wins (the diags arm's `collect` writes it SET-OR-CLEAR on the entry module
-- instead), and the promotion harvest unions THIS module's inferred-constraint
-- promotions before the next module's `freshModuleCtx` wipes `promotedRef` —
-- `foldModules` calls the worker HEAD-first, which is what makes that read safe.
--
-- The impl universe is `accAll ++ prog`, NOT bare `accAll` — it includes THIS module.
-- `foldModules` never folds a module into its own
-- `accAll` (see `checkModuleFullImpl`'s E5 note: "a caller that needs this module's own
-- decls too must fold [prog] in itself"), so the readers of `checkBodyImpl`'s
-- `implDecls` on this arm — `visibleMethodRows`, `inferUserImplBodies`' field-type
-- universe, `superDecls` and the `importerShadows` emptiness test — see this
-- module's own declarations only through this fold.  Return-only dispatch on an
-- own-module impl (`test/engine_fixtures/return_only_param_dispatch.mdk`, and
-- `test/run_check_agreement_fixtures/accept_own_module_impl_return_only_dispatch.mdk`
-- for the entry module of a graph) does not depend on it: determination reads the
-- whole-graph `IE` through `improvementPool`.
-- Cost: `accAll ++ prog` copies the accumulator, so O(|accAll|) per module and
-- quadratic over the graph; this is not the perf-gated `checkModules` path, which is
-- `cmCheckWorker`'s.  Each copy is short-lived only while nothing holds it until graph
-- end, which is why a module's stamping context keeps the interface decls alone.
--
-- #2246: the payload carries `schemes` a SECOND time.  `foldModules` hands the worker's
-- FIRST component to the NEXT module (it is the fold's seed, never `collect`'s), so the
-- only way the terminal module's own bindings reach `graphCollect` — which owes
-- `mainSchemeRef` its SET-OR-CLEAR write — is inside the payload, and the entry-report
-- projection needs the same triple to keep the terminal module's OWN schemes/warns.
-- Costs nothing: `checkModuleFullDiags` already computed them, and the marked tree is
-- the same list value the module was marked in, not a copy. The diagnostics
-- payload omits trees because the fold retains payloads until graph completion.
graphModuleWorker : OrdMap Unit ->
  GraphOut ->
  ModuleCtx ->
  String ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, (List Decl, List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag, Option InvocationView), ModuleCtx)
graphModuleWorker capture sel prev mid seed accData accAll prog =
  let implDecls = accAll ++ prog
  let (schemes, marked, errs, warns, mctx) =
    checkModuleFullDiags prev.mcGraph mid seed accData implDecls prog
  let published = publishOwn mctx schemes.mbSchemes
  let tree = match sel
    GOutTrees =>
      match lookupAssoc "main" schemes.mbSchemes
        Some sc => prev.mcGraph.mainSchemeRef := Some sc
        None => ()
      prev.mcGraph.promotionHarvestRef :=
        dedup
          (prev.mcGraph.promotionHarvestRef.value
            ++ mctx.mcCells.promotedRef.value)
      marked
    GOutDiags => []
  let view =
    if omHasKey mid capture then
      Some mctx.mcCells.invocationViewRef.value
    else
      None
  (schemes, (tree, published, errs, warns, view), mctx)

-- collect: keeps the worker's marked tree and its `(schemes, errs, warns)` payload
-- per module in load order, and on the DIAGS selection's TERMINAL module records
-- that module's `main` scheme.  Two accumulators rather than two folds — the worker
-- computes both in one per-module pass, and the tree arm's `dictPassModulesIfEnabled`
-- consumes the first while every arm's caller consumes the second.  The tree arm
-- does NOT write `mainSchemeRef` here: its worker already wrote it per module,
-- Some-only.  The `schemes` half is what lets a caller derive the ENTRY REPORT — the
-- terminal module's own schemes and warnings, plus every module's errors — from this
-- one pass instead of from a second, entry-only driver over the same graph
-- (`checkModulesEntryFromDiags` below is that projection, and it is the old
-- entry-only `collect` written as a fold over this list).  `checkModulesDiagsChain`'s
-- exported signature (a selfproc LEG A row) keeps the narrower `(errs, warns)` shape:
-- it projects.
--
-- #2246: this is the multi-module `analyzeProject` path's ONLY producer of
-- `mainSchemeRef`.  S-3 (#2234) made `mainShapeWarnings` pure — it now READS the ref
-- rather than re-elaborating — which was correct for every caller that had a
-- producer, but `checkJsonFile`'s multi-module arm (driver/diagnostics.mdk) had NONE:
-- `analyzeProject` → `typecheckPassFull` → here, and nothing on that chain wrote it.
-- Both directions of that hole are pinned:
--   * fresh process — the ref is `None`, `mainIsUnitNow` answers False on `None`,
--     so W-MAIN-SHAPE FIRED on a clean Unit main (false positive on every
--     `check --json` / MCP `medaka_check` of a multi-module project);
--   * long-lived process (`medaka mcp`, `medaka lsp`) — the ref held whatever an
--     EARLIER request left, so a genuinely non-Unit `main` had its real warning
--     SILENTLY VANISH behind a previous request's Unit scheme.  [W-QUIETER]: that
--     direction is the more dangerous one.
-- SET-OR-CLEAR in ONE write, never a conditional/partial one: `lookupAssoc` yields
-- `None` when the terminal module has no `main`, and writing that `None` is what
-- clears a previous run's leftover.  Mirrors `checkModulesEntryFullSplit`'s write
-- (same last-wins entry-module rule; dependency-first order puts the entry last).
graphCollect : GraphCtx ->
  GraphOut ->
  Bool ->
  String ->
  (List Decl, List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag, Option InvocationView) ->
  (List (String, List Decl), List ModDiags, List (String, InvocationView)) ->
  (List (String, List Decl), List ModDiags, List (String, InvocationView))
graphCollect gctx sel isLast mid (marked, schemes, errs, warns, view) (markedRest, diagsRest, views) =
  match sel
    GOutDiags => setMainSchemeIfEntry gctx isLast schemes
    GOutTrees => ()
  -- The DIAGS selection keeps no tree: its `driveGraphK` arm returns
  -- `gdModules = []`, and its worker puts none in the payload (`graphModuleWorker`).
  let markedOut = match sel
    GOutDiags => markedRest
    GOutTrees => (mid, marked) :: markedRest
  (
    markedOut,
    (mid, (schemes, errs, warns)) :: diagsRest,
    option views (v => (mid, v) :: views) view,
  )

-- the `(errs, warns)` projection of one full per-module entry, for the exported
-- drivers whose contract is the narrower pair.
export
dropModSchemes : ModDiags -> (String, (List TcDiag, List TcDiag))
dropModSchemes (mid, (_, errs, warns)) = (mid, (errs, warns))

-- The ENTRY REPORT as a projection over the full per-module list: the TERMINAL
-- module's own schemes and warnings, and every module's errors accumulated
-- dependency-first — a type error in an IMPORTED module is still a hard error of
-- `check`ing the entry graph and must surface (else it silently reaches codegen, the
-- G1-class soundness hole).  This is what makes the entry report and the diagnostics
-- fold ONE typecheck rather than two.
--
-- The whole-graph coherence verdict is already attached by the driver
-- (`attachCoherenceConflict`): the soft half sits at the front of the terminal
-- module's warnings, exactly where `checkModulesEntryFullSplitK` prepends it.  The
-- hard half sits at the front of the terminal module's ERRORS rather than at the
-- front of the accumulated list, which differs only on a graph that has both a
-- conflict and an imported-module type error — a graph whose caller has already
-- reported errors and exited.
export
checkModulesEntryFromDiags : List ModDiags ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkModulesEntryFromDiags [] = ([], [], [])
checkModulesEntryFromDiags [(_, (s, e, w))] = (s, e, w)
checkModulesEntryFromDiags ((_, (_, e, _)) :: rest) =
  let (eS, eE, eW) = checkModulesEntryFromDiags rest
  (eS, e ++ eE, eW)

-- `checkModulesEntryReport`'s renderer, over per-module diagnostics a driver already
-- produced.  Both must render identically or `check`'s multi-module report changes
-- shape, which is why the match below is `checkModulesEntryReport`'s verbatim.
export
entryReportFromDiags : List ModDiags -> String
entryReportFromDiags perMod = match checkModulesEntryFromDiags perMod
  (schemes, [], warns) => joinNl (schemeLines schemes ++ map tcMsg warns)
  (_, errs, _) => joinNl (typeErrorLines (map tcMsg errs))

-- The SET-OR-CLEAR write itself, on the terminal (entry) module only.  Split out of
-- `graphCollect` so the "never a partial write" property is one two-clause
-- function rather than an inline conditional.
setMainSchemeIfEntry : GraphCtx -> Bool -> List (String, PubScheme) -> Unit
setMainSchemeIfEntry gctx False _ = ()
setMainSchemeIfEntry gctx True schemes =
  gctx.mainSchemeRef := map (p => p.psScheme) (lookupAssoc "main" schemes)

-- The prelude generation pair plus the chain key: the driver's pass caches
-- (`driver/diagnostics.mdk`) carry it whole, and the module interfaces' graph key
-- (`serveStateOf`) only its first line, the generation, never the entry or roots.
export
chainFullKey : Int -> Int -> String -> String
chainFullKey rk ck chainKey = "\{intToString rk},\{intToString ck}\n\{chainKey}"

-- `driveGraphK GOutDiags` over [modules0].  Under a prelude key the drive serves the
-- modules before the entry from their interfaces (`diagsServe`), with [stepKeys]
-- pairing one source key with each module positionally.  The per-module payload is
-- the worker's whole `(schemes, errs, warns)` triple: a caller that also needs the
-- graph's ENTRY REPORT derives it from this list (`entryReportFromDiags`) instead of
-- running a second, entry-only driver over the same graph.  `dropModSchemes` is the
-- narrower `(errs, warns)` projection the bucketing fold consumes.
export
checkModulesDiagsChain : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  String ->
  List (Option String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List ModDiags
checkModulesDiagsChain inputs preludeKey chainKey stepKeys runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  checkModulesDiagsChainFor
    inputs
    False
    preludeKey
    chainKey
    stepKeys
    runtimeDecls
    coreDecls0
    modules0

-- `checkModulesDiagsChain` for a caller that passes its value-main candidate
-- (`autoPrintWanted`).
export
checkModulesDiagsChainFor : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  String ->
  List (Option String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  List ModDiags
checkModulesDiagsChainFor inputs valueMain preludeKey chainKey stepKeys runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  (fst
    (checkModulesChainReportFor
      inputs
      valueMain
      preludeKey
      chainKey
      stepKeys
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls0
      modules0)).trDiagnostics

-- `checkModulesDiagsChainFor` returning the drive's whole report beside its
-- outputs.
export
checkModulesChainReportFor : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  String ->
  List (Option String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (TcReport, DriveOutputs)
checkModulesChainReportFor inputs valueMain preludeKey chainKey stepKeys runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  fst
    (checkModulesChainReportForGraph
      (chainArgs
        inputs
        valueMain
        preludeKey
        chainKey
        stepKeys
        runtimeDecls
        coreDecls0
        modules0))

-- The arguments of a drive keyed under the prelude generation pair [preludeKey] and
-- [chainKey], with one source key per module in [stepKeys]; unkeyed under `None`.
export
chainArgs : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  String ->
  List (Option String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  DriveArgs
chainArgs inputs valueMain preludeKey chainKey stepKeys runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  let base =
    driveArgs inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0
  { base |
    daKeyed =
      map ((rk, ck) => (chainFullKey rk ck chainKey, stepKeys)) preludeKey,
  }

-- The arguments of an unkeyed drive that executes nothing.
export
driveArgs : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  DriveArgs
driveArgs inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  DriveArgs
    inputs
    valueMain
    None
    preludeKey
    None
    runtimeDecls
    coreDecls0
    modules0

-- `checkModulesChainReportFor` beside the graph record its drive ends in, for a test
-- that observes that record after the drive.
export
checkModulesChainReportForGraph : DriveArgs ->
  ((TcReport, DriveOutputs), GraphCtx)
checkModulesChainReportForGraph args =
  let g = driveGraphK GOutDiags args
  ((g.gdReport, g.gdOutputs), g.gdGraph)

-- ── serving modules from their interfaces ───────────────────────────────────

-- The drain and open-variable test the serve policy (`types/serve_policy.mdk`) calls.
serveOps : ServeOps
serveOps = ServeOps {
  soDrain =
    mctx rows => match checkGraphFinish mctx
      [] => rows
      findings => attachDrainFindings findings rows,
  soUnboundIds = monoUnboundIds,
}

-- [lo, hi) less the ranges a scoped drain judged, as sub-ranges in order.
unjudgedRanges : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int) -> List (Int, Int)
unjudgedRanges lo hi skips =
  cutRanges lo hi (sortOn fst (filterList ((a, b) => b > lo && a < hi) skips))

cutRanges : Int -> Int -> List (Int, Int) -> List (Int, Int)
cutRanges lo hi [] = if lo < hi then [(lo, hi)] else []
cutRanges lo hi ((a, b) :: rest) =
  (if a > lo then [(lo, a)] else []) ++ cutRanges (max lo b) hi rest

-- [rows] with every row past the oldest [n] frozen: the oldest [n] were frozen when
-- they were recorded or served.
freezeFrom : Int -> List ModDiags -> List ModDiags
freezeFrom n rows = take n rows ++ map freezeModDiags (drop n rows)

-- #1112 A-3: the disposition producer.  For every accepted instance
-- (`ieRowsAll` — every impl row in the whole-graph `IE`, prelude included) and
-- every method of its interface's `CeRow`, exactly one row: `Supplied` when the
-- impl names the method, `InheritedDefault` when it does not and the method is
-- not in `ceRowRequired` (a required method with no default and no override is
-- rejected upstream, M1 — never reaches this walk at all).
buildDispositions : GraphCtx -> DeclEnvs -> DispositionTable
buildDispositions gctx envs =
  let impls = ieRowsAll envs.deImpls
  let table =
    buildDispositionTableWithShapes
      (dispositionRowsOfImpls envs.deImpls impls envs.deIfaces)
      (map (ifaceSuperCountOfRow gctx) impls)
  validateDispositionTable (dispositionSlotsOfImpls impls envs.deIfaces) table
  table

-- Every slot an accepted instance must have exactly one disposition for: each
-- method of its interface that it names, or that has a default to inherit.
dispositionSlotsOfImpls : List ImplRow ->
  ClassEnv ->
  List (InstId, IfaceRef, String)
dispositionSlotsOfImpls [] _ = []
dispositionSlotsOfImpls (r :: rest) ce =
  let (iface, _, _) = ieRowTriple r
  let inst = InstId (instRefMid (ieRowInst r)) (instRefSeq (ieRowInst r))
  let supplied = ieRowMethodNames r
  let here =
    match (ceLookupAt
      (regKeyOfTab (ifaceTabKey iface.irOrigin iface.irName))
      (ieRowOrd r)
      ce)
      None => []
      Some ceRow =>
        map
          (m => (inst, iface, m))
          (filter
            (m => contains m supplied || not (contains m (ceRowRequired ceRow)))
            (ceRowMethodNames ceRow))
  here ++ dispositionSlotsOfImpls rest ce

dispositionRowsOfImpls : ImplEnv ->
  List ImplRow ->
  ClassEnv ->
  List (InstanceShape, String, MethodDisposition)
dispositionRowsOfImpls _ [] _ = []
dispositionRowsOfImpls ie (r :: rest) ce =
  dispositionRowsOfImpl ie r ce ++ dispositionRowsOfImpls ie rest ce

-- Each row carries its instance's shape and its interface's word.  The shape's
-- route word is minted by the same call the lowering and eval install passes make
-- on the impl's declaration (`implRouteKeyWord` over the impl's own origin and type
-- arguments), so an inherited slot's entry is named and reached exactly as the
-- instance's supplied methods are.
dispositionRowsOfImpl : ImplEnv ->
  ImplRow ->
  ClassEnv ->
  List (InstanceShape, String, MethodDisposition)
dispositionRowsOfImpl _ r ce =
  let (iface, tys, _) = ieRowTriple r
  let inst = InstId (instRefMid (ieRowInst r)) (instRefSeq (ieRowInst r))
  let ifaceWord = ifaceWordOf iface.irOrigin iface.irName
  let supplied = ieRowMethodNames r
  let shape = InstanceShape {
    isWord = implRouteKeyWord iface.irOrigin iface.irName tys None,
    isTys = tys,
  }
  match (ceLookupAt
    (regKeyOfTab (ifaceTabKey iface.irOrigin iface.irName))
    (ieRowOrd r)
    ce)
    -- An impl whose interface is not visible has already been reported, and
    -- `dispositionSlotsOfImpls` expects no slot for it either.
    None => []
    Some ceRow =>
      map
        (d => (shape, ifaceWord, d))
        (dispositionRowsOfMethods
          inst
          iface
          supplied
          (ceRowRequired ceRow)
          (ceRowMethodNames ceRow))

-- The interface word of [r]'s interface with its direct superinterface count: the
-- super segment every dictionary of that interface stores.
ifaceSuperCountOfRow : GraphCtx -> ImplRow -> (String, Int)
ifaceSuperCountOfRow gctx r =
  let (iface, _, _) = ieRowTriple r
  (
    ifaceWordOf iface.irOrigin iface.irName,
    listLen (directSuperGoals gctx iface []),
  )

dispositionRowsOfMethods : InstId ->
  IfaceRef ->
  List String ->
  List String ->
  List String ->
  List MethodDisposition
dispositionRowsOfMethods _ _ _ _ [] = []
dispositionRowsOfMethods inst iface supplied required (m :: rest) =
  let restRows = dispositionRowsOfMethods inst iface supplied required rest
  if contains m supplied then
    Supplied { instance = inst, iface = iface, method = m } :: restRows
  else if not (contains m required) then
    InheritedDefault { instance = inst, iface = iface, method = m } :: restRows
  else
    -- A required method with no default and no override: M1 pushes a
    -- diagnostic (accumulate-errors, never an abort — T-ERRORS-ACCUM), so
    -- elaboration still reaches this walk on an already-ill-typed program.
    -- No disposition is correct for a slot the program will not compile
    -- with, so it is skipped rather than fabricated — panicking here turned
    -- a clean REJECT into a crash on
    -- test/run_check_agreement_fixtures/p0_17_missing_impl_method.mdk.
    restRows

-- Drive the whole module graph ONCE: stamp type-constructor identity, run the
-- shared preamble, check every module dependency-first through one worker, drain
-- at graph end, and attach the whole-graph coherence verdict.  `sel` chooses what
-- comes back (see `GraphOut`).  Every whole-graph entry point below is a projection
-- of this function.
--
-- [keyed] is a keyed drive's chain key (`chainFullKey`) and one source key per
-- module, `None` for every other drive.  Under it the fold serves each unchanged
-- module before the entry from its recorded interface and records the modules it
-- checks (`diagsServe`); a tree drive also serves and records each module's marked
-- trees, which `dictPass` then passes with the rest of the graph (`treeServe`).
-- Whether a drive is keyed is its caller's choice: a one-shot process passes no
-- source keys, so it neither serves nor records, and a session (the LSP, the
-- playground, `medaka test`) keeps the interfaces between drives.
--
-- `preludeKey` is the desugar-cache generation pair for the prelude interface (see
-- `checkCoreMemoized`); the tree arm ignores it and reads the drive's own
-- `diPreludeKey` (`treeArmHit`), since it also needs core's dict-passed decls,
-- which an interface does not hold.  `valueMain` is the caller's
-- value-main candidate and `executes` names the `async` driver of a caller that
-- runs the trees, `None` for one that does not (`autoPrintWanted`).
driveGraphK : GraphOut -> DriveArgs -> GraphDrive
driveGraphK sel (DriveArgs inputs valueMain executes preludeKey keyed runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn) =
  -- IMPORT ALIASING of an interface/impl METHOD — a method is global-by-name, so an
  -- alias of one is a second SPELLING, not a second binding.  A member alias and a
  -- module alias both keep their spelling through inference and are resolved at the
  -- end of the tree arm (`resolveAliasMethodSpellings`).  See
  -- `renameAliasedMethods`.  BOTH selections rename: that rewrite makes
  -- `import m as A` bind `m`'s methods under their ORIGIN name as well, which decides
  -- WHICH declaration a bare occurrence of that name resolves to — a binding change,
  -- not a spelling one, so a selection that skips it typechecks a different program.
  -- Only the RESTORE (`resolveAliasMethodSpellings`) is tree-arm-only: it rewrites
  -- occurrences in trees the diags arm does not emit.
  let (admitted, modulesAliased) = renameAliasedMethods coreDecls0 modulesIn
  -- #1110: acquire type-constructor identity over the whole graph before anything
  -- typechecks; the rebind shadows the raw params so no line below can read the
  -- unstamped tree.  It is HERE, not in the preamble, because this is the seam the
  -- RUN path and the separate `medaka_emitter` BUILD process share — the emitter
  -- never runs resolve, so acquiring identity anywhere upstream would give `check`
  -- and `build` different trees.  Method-alias renaming comes first: it is a name
  -- rewrite on VALUES and cannot move a type head, so the graph reaches identity
  -- minting in its final shape and no later pass has to re-mint.
  let (coreDecls, modules) = stampGraphDecls coreDecls0 modulesAliased
  let (runtimeSeed, coreSchemes, coreClosed, coreContexts, coreTree, ms, coreCtx) =
    graphPreamble inputs admitted sel preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls modules
  let capture =
    requestedWrapperViewModules
      inputs.diManifestRequests
      (manifestDriverFor inputs executes)
  let serve =
    map
      (k =>
        let st = serveStateOf serveOps capture coreCtx.mcGraph modules k
        match sel
          GOutDiags => diagsServe st
          GOutTrees => treeServe st)
      keyed
  if isSome serve then raiseToHighWater coreCtx.mcGraph
  -- The worker reads BOTH accData (buildOracle `prog ++ accData`) and
  -- accAll (checkModuleFullImpl implDecls), and
  -- both are order-observable (oracle first-wins, implDecls scan order), so both
  -- concats are RETAINED — this is not the perf-gate path, which is `cmCheckWorker`'s.
  let coreView = coreCtx.mcCells.invocationViewRef.value
  let ((modules2, perModRaw, views), served, lastCtx) =
    foldModules
      AccumulateBoth
      (graphModuleWorker capture sel)
      serve
      (graphCollect coreCtx.mcGraph sel)
      ([], [], [])
      (runtimeSeed ++ coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
      (tagModuleExports coreSchemes.mbStandaloneSchemes coreSchemes.mbSchemes)
      []
      (publicDataDecls coreDecls)
      coreDecls
      coreCtx
      modules
  -- #2548: quiescence.  Every module (core first) has been inferred and has queued
  -- its stamping context; drain them all now, in module order, before any tree goes
  -- to `dictPassModulesIfEnabled`.  The drive ends in the last module's record.  A
  -- keyed drive's drains, its recording modules' and this one, restore the sticky
  -- type-error cells (`checkGraphFinish`), so a served module's recorded sticky rows
  -- are the ones a check of it leaves.
  let findings =
    if isSome serve then checkGraphFinish lastCtx else drainStampQueue lastCtx
  if isSome serve then noteHighWater lastCtx.mcGraph
  let servedCount = optionOr 0 (map (policy => policy.msServed) served)
  -- D3 / WS-2 part-2: attach any cross-module coherence conflict to the ENTRY
  -- module's diagnostics (last in dependency-first order), so LSP/analyzeProject
  -- surfaces it too.  User modules only (prelude excluded).
  -- #414: no span — see globalCoherenceConflict (the entry module it attaches to is
  -- not the module the span would name, and the renderer keys the file by group).
  -- F-3d: the ⊑-incomparable half rides the WARNING list of the same module.
  -- #1559 A-3.7: the whole-graph `IE` the preamble built above; `cohRowsOf` is
  -- what excludes the prelude, replacing "walk the user `modules` list".
  -- It reads `deImpls` only, which nothing between here and the tree arm's return
  -- writes, so attaching once here serves both selections.
  let (wanted, autoPrint) =
    autoPrintWanted
      lastCtx
      valueMain
      executes
      findings
      perModRaw
      coreDecls
      modules
  let outcome = entryOutcomeNow lastCtx (mainHasObligations lastCtx) autoPrint
  -- The graph is closed: the drain, its defaulting and the auto-print verdict
  -- have run, so the published rows are frozen now, once.
  let perMod =
    attachCoherenceConflict
      lastCtx
      (attachDrainFindings
        (findings ++ wanted)
        (freezeFrom
          (optionOr 0 (map (policy => policy.msFrozen) served))
          perModRaw))
  -- Before the tree arm's `dictPass`, which writes the record's slot tables.
  let entryTables = captureEntryTables lastCtx coreContexts modules
  let corePub = publishedCore coreClosed coreContexts coreSchemes.mbSchemes
  let (requests, manifests, manifestDiags) =
    publishSelected
      lastCtx.mcGraph.effectDomains.value
      (invocationResolver
        (frozenInvocationOps
          lastInvocationOps
          (driveOutputsNow lastCtx outcome))
        (completionInvocationViews lastCtx coreView views))
      inputs.diManifestRequests
      perMod
      corePub
      outcome
      (manifestDriverFor inputs executes)
      valueMain
  -- #1112 A-3: the whole-graph IE/CE `buildDeclEnvs` filled in the preamble
  -- above is already complete at this point, on both arms alike.
  let dispositions =
    buildDispositions lastCtx.mcGraph lastCtx.mcGraph.declEnvsRef.value
  match sel
    -- A served module's evidence cells are not carried (`types/module_interface.mdk`),
    -- so a keyed drive's `cpEvidence` holds the checked modules' cells only.  No
    -- diagnostics-arm consumer reads it.
    GOutDiags => GraphDrive {
      gdPerMod = perMod,
      gdCoreSchemes = corePub,
      gdCoreContexts = coreContexts,
      gdCore = [],
      gdModules = [],
      gdResidual = [],
      gdEvTable = lastCtx.mcGraph.graphRun.evTable.value,
      gdDispositions = dispositions,
      gdBinders = lastCtx.mcGraph.graphRun.binderRows.value,
      gdReport =
        graphReportWithOutcome
          outcome
          manifests
          manifestDiags
          perMod
          corePub
          []
          (drivenEvidence lastCtx.mcGraph)
          dispositions
          lastCtx.mcGraph.graphRun.binderRows.value
          None
          entryTables,
      gdGraph = lastCtx.mcGraph,
      gdOutputs = withPublishedManifests
        requests
        manifests
        { driveOutputsNow lastCtx outcome | doServedModules = servedCount },
    }
    GOutTrees =>
      -- The set `dictPass` prepends dict params for: the bare set plus every
      -- promotion the sweep made, with the import aliases of both — the union of
      -- what every module was marked with (`beginModuleMarking`,
      -- `markRecursiveOccurrences`).
      let dictNames =
        markDictNames
          lastCtx.mcGraph
          (dedup
            (ms.msBareDictNames ++ lastCtx.mcGraph.promotionHarvestRef.value))
          coreDecls
          modules
      -- On a hit (`treeArmHit`: the core pass closed core) core's trees arrive
      -- dict-passed, and core's evidence and binder rows join the drive's.
      let shipped = match coreClosed
        Some _ => treeArmElab inputs coreDecls
        None => None
      let (coreD, modules3) =
        dictPassModulesIfEnabled
          lastCtx
          dictNames
          (isSome shipped)
          coreTree
          modules2
      -- `resolveAliasMethodSpellings` restores the origin name behind every
      -- alias-qualified method occurrence (`A.mth` -> `mth`) that inference kept.
      -- Only the user modules go through it: core imports nothing, so it holds no
      -- alias-qualified spelling to restore.
      let modulesOut =
        map (resolveAliasMethodSpellings lastCtx.mcGraph) modules3
      let residual =
        stickyDiagsInPushOrder lastCtx.mcGraph.typeErrorsStickyDiags.value
      -- read after `dictPass`, so it carries the `EMethodAt` nodes `dictPass` minted
      let (evidence, binders) =
        withShippedRows
          shipped
          (drivenEvidence lastCtx.mcGraph ())
          lastCtx.mcGraph.graphRun.binderRows.value
      GraphDrive {
        gdPerMod = perMod,
        gdCoreSchemes = corePub,
        gdCoreContexts = coreContexts,
        gdCore = coreD,
        gdModules = modulesOut,
        gdResidual = residual,
        gdEvTable = evidence,
        gdDispositions = dispositions,
        gdBinders = binders,
        gdReport =
          graphReportWithOutcome
            outcome
            manifests
            manifestDiags
            perMod
            corePub
            residual
            (_ => evidence)
            dispositions
            binders
            (Some (coreD, modulesOut))
            entryTables,
        gdGraph = lastCtx.mcGraph,
        gdOutputs = withPublishedManifests
          requests
          manifests
          { driveOutputsNow lastCtx outcome | doServedModules = servedCount },
      }

-- The whole-graph coherence verdict, attached to the entry module's diagnostics —
-- the tail `driveGraphK` ends with.  The narrower
-- `(errs, warns)` view is `map dropModSchemes` OVER this result: `cohOntoFull`
-- passes `schemes` through untouched and `attachEntryPayload` rewrites one entry,
-- so dropping the schemes commutes with attaching.
attachCoherenceConflict : ModuleCtx -> List ModDiags -> List ModDiags
attachCoherenceConflict mctx perMod =
  match globalCoherenceConflict mctx mctx.mcGraph.declEnvsRef.value.deImpls
    (None, None) => perMod
    (hard, soft) => attachEntryPayload (cohOntoFull hard soft) perMod

cohOntoFull : Option String ->
  Option String ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag) ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
cohOntoFull hard soft (schemes, errs, warns) =
  (schemes, cohHardOnto hard errs, cohSoftOnto soft warns)

cohHardOnto : Option String -> List TcDiag -> List TcDiag
cohHardOnto hard errs = prependDiagOpt "T-CONFLICTING-IMPL" 1 None hard errs

cohSoftOnto : Option String -> List TcDiag -> List TcDiag
cohSoftOnto soft warns =
  prependDiagOpt "W-INCOMPARABLE-IMPLS" 2 (Some cohIncomparableHelp) soft warns

-- rewrite the LAST module's payload in a per-module diag list (the entry module is
-- last in dependency-first order).
attachEntryPayload : (p -> p) -> List (String, p) -> List (String, p)
attachEntryPayload _ [] = []
attachEntryPayload f [(mid, p)] = [(mid, f p)]
attachEntryPayload f (m :: rest) = m :: attachEntryPayload f rest

-- The auto-print obligation of a value `main` (driver/main_autoprint.mdk), posed
-- at graph end by the drive that checks the entry module.  [valueMain] is the
-- caller's syntactic candidate (a parameterless `main` with `println` in scope);
-- whether `main` is printed at all is decided here from its own scheme, so a
-- Unit-shaped or `Async` main poses nothing.  The obligation is the `println`
-- signature's own context at τ_main, `main`'s scheme as the entry module left it:
-- the declared type under a signature, else the inferred one.  It joins the entry
-- module's errors whether or not the drive already has some, except that an
-- ambiguity verdict is dropped when one of [reported] lies in `main`'s binding:
-- that error already explains the undetermined type.  It runs after the drain, which can still default a numeric `main`, and before anything later
-- reads `main`'s variables, since the verdict can ground them.  An [executes]
-- drive, whose trees the driver wraps and runs, also returns the evidence of a
-- use judged clean (`autoPrintEvidence`); a check-only drive mints none.  Such a
-- drive judges an `Async` main's application to the driver it names instead
-- (`asyncDriverFindings`).  [mctx] is the record current after the drain, whose
-- type-error channel the verdict is taken from.
autoPrintWanted : ModuleCtx ->
  Bool ->
  Option String ->
  List DrainFinding ->
  List ModDiags ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List DrainFinding, Option EvId)
autoPrintWanted mctx False _ _ _ _ _ = ([], None)
autoPrintWanted mctx True executes findings perMod coreDecls modules =
  let decls = coreDecls ++ flatMap snd modules
  match (
    mctx.mcGraph.mainSchemeRef.value,
    last modules,
    printlnIface decls,
  )
    (Some scheme, Some (mid, entry), Some iface) =>
      if mainIsUnitNow mctx.mcGraph (mainHasObligations mctx) () then
        ([], None)
      else if mainIsAsyncNow mctx.mcGraph () then
        (asyncDriverFindings mctx executes mid entry scheme perMod, None)
      else
        -- A top-level `main` has no givens: the use is judged in a module scope
        -- of its own.
        let scope =
          Scopes.freshScope
            (currentScopeStore mctx.mcGraph ())
            None
            0
            mid
            ModuleOwner
        openScope mctx scope
        let loc = mainBodySpan entry
        let atMain = mctxAt loc mctx
        let posed = Ref None
        let diags = captureTypeErrors atMain (_ =>
          posed := Some (judgeAutoPrintUse atMain iface scheme loc))
        let ev = match (executes, diags, posed.value)
          (Some _, [], Some tau) =>
            Some (autoPrintEvidence atMain mid scope iface tau)
          _ => None
        closeScope mctx ()
        let reported = driveErrs findings perMod
        let kept =
          if anyList (d => inMainBinding entry (tcLoc d)) reported then
            filterList (d => tcCode d /= "T-AMBIGUOUS-INSTANCE") diags
          else
            diags
        (map (verdictFinding mid False) (mainUseDiags loc entry ++ kept), ev)
    _ => ([], None)

-- Every error a drive has reported so far, the drain's included.
driveErrs : List DrainFinding -> List ModDiags -> List TcDiag
driveErrs findings perMod =
  map (f => f.dfDiag) findings ++ flatMap modDiagErrs perMod

-- The printing use of `main` is a closed boundary, as a test body is (see
-- `settleClosedBody`): it instantiates `main`'s scheme with its context, poses
-- `iface` at the instance, and publishes no polymorphic result.  So a numeric
-- variable defaults as at any such boundary, a variable nothing determines is
-- ambiguous, and a closed goal gets the call channel's verdict, `requires`
-- included.  The instance the goal was posed at is returned.
judgeAutoPrintUse : ModuleCtx -> IfaceRef -> Scheme -> Option Loc -> Mono
judgeAutoPrintUse mctx iface scheme loc =
  judgeMainUse
    mctx
    (tau =>
      recordObl mctx Predicate { iface = iface, args = [tau] } 0 PCallSlot loc)
    scheme

-- The `async` driver [executes] names applied to an `Async` main, judged as the
-- printing use is (`judgeMainUse`) against the driver's scheme as the `async`
-- module published it: the drivers wrap exactly this application
-- (driver/main_autoprint.mdk's `shouldAsyncWrapMain`), after the drive.
asyncDriverFindings : ModuleCtx ->
  Option String ->
  String ->
  List Decl ->
  Scheme ->
  List ModDiags ->
  List DrainFinding
asyncDriverFindings mctx executes mid entry scheme perMod =
  let published = match lookupAssoc "async" perMod
    Some (rows, _, _) => rows
    None => []
  match option None (d => lookupAssoc d published) executes
    Some driver if mainAsyncPayloadIsUnitNow mctx.mcGraph () =>
      let scope =
        Scopes.freshScope
          (currentScopeStore mctx.mcGraph ())
          None
          0
          mid
          ModuleOwner
      openScope mctx scope
      let atMain = mctxAt (mainBodySpan entry) mctx
      let diags = captureTypeErrors atMain (_ =>
        let _ = judgeMainUse atMain (asyncDriverUse atMain driver) scheme
        ())
      closeScope mctx ()
      map (verdictFinding mid False) diags
    _ => []

-- The driver's parameter unified with `main`'s instance, the driver's context
-- posed at its own.
asyncDriverUse : ModuleCtx -> PubScheme -> Mono -> Unit
asyncDriverUse mctx driver tau =
  let (driverTy, subst) = instantiateTracked mctx driver.psScheme
  recordSchemeCallObligations mctx 0 driver.psContext subst
  match normalize driverTy
    TFun param _ _ => unify mctx param tau
    _ => ()

-- A use of `main` judged as a closed boundary: [pose] states the use's goal at
-- `main`'s instance before `main`'s own context is posed there.
judgeMainUse : ModuleCtx -> (Mono -> Unit) -> Scheme -> Mono
judgeMainUse mctx pose scheme =
  enterLevel mctx ()
  let oblMark = wMark mctx.mcCells.implObls
  let callMark = wMark mctx.mcCells.obls
  let dictMark = goalsMark mctx.mcGraph ()
  let (tau0, subst) = instantiateTracked mctx scheme
  -- A variable the value restriction left in `main`'s type is a live root, and
  -- the drain that could bind it has run: at this use it is the boundary's own,
  -- as a quantified one is.
  let fresh = flatMap (p => monoUnboundIds (snd p)) subst
  let held =
    filterList (i => not (anyList (== i) fresh)) (dedupI (monoUnboundIds tau0))
  let tau =
    substMono mctx.mcGraph (map (i => (i, freshVar mctx ())) held) [] [] tau0
  let id = optionOr 0 (omLookup "main" mctx.mcCells.schemeDefIdsRef.value)
  let context =
    lookupSchemeObls "main" id mctx.mcCells.schemeObligationsRef.value
  pose tau
  recordSchemeCallObligations mctx id (optionOr [] context) subst
  exitLevel mctx ()
  settleClosedBody mctx (tconBuiltin "Unit") oblMark callMark dictMark
  checkCallObligationsU
    mctx
    False
    mctx.mcCells.residualUnivRef.value
    (callUOblsWindow mctx callMark)
  tau

-- The evidence of the auto-print's one `Display τ_main` goal, published beside the
-- goals the drive solved so the driver's printing wrap can name it.
autoPrintEvidence : ModuleCtx -> String -> ScopeId -> IfaceRef -> Mono -> EvId
autoPrintEvidence mctx mid scope iface tau =
  let routes = routesOfMonosTopV (siteFor mctx) "" scope [tau] [iface] [[tau]]
  let g = mctx.mcGraph.graphRun
  let n = g.evCounter.value
  g.evCounter := n + 1
  let ev = EvId mid n
  g.evTable := EvEntry ev (EvMany routes) :: g.evTable.value
  ev

-- the entry module is last in dependency-first order
export
entryOwnSchemes : List (String, List (String, a)) -> List (String, a)
entryOwnSchemes = publishedEntrySchemes

-- ── multi-module `check` CLI report (DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 4, OPTION A) ─────
-- The unified `medaka check` path for IMPORT-BEARING files: the caller loads the
-- entry + its transitive imports (loadProgram), then this typechecks the whole
-- graph in dependency order (checkModuleFullDiags per module: per-module-frame,
-- prelude seeded once) and harvests the ENTRY module's OWN (schemes, errs, warns).
-- Imports are RESOLVED (their public schemes seed the entry), so a valid cross-
-- module reference no longer falls through to `UnknownModule` — the known "native
-- check is single-file" limitation, fixed.  The entry is last in dependency-first
-- order.
--
-- ⚠️ ROUTING, corrected 2026-08-26: this comment used to say "no-import (1-module)
-- files still route through runCheck … this path is reached ONLY when the load
-- returned more than one module."  FALSE since S-migrate-check-route (E-1 #1115).
-- A no-import file now reaches HERE too, via the 1-module wrappers
-- `checkOneScheme` / `checkOneDiags` / `checkOneToLinesWithRuntime` /
-- `checkOneErrorsWithRuntime`, each of which calls this function with a SINGLETON
-- module list.  So every clause below must be correct on a 1-module graph, not
-- merely defensible on one: `foldModules`' singleton clause is a genuinely
-- user-reachable path (it is where #2024's `accAll` = initial-value defect lives),
-- and so is the whole-graph coherence prepend at the bottom.
checkModulesEntryFull : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkModulesEntryFull inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  checkModulesEntryFullK inputs None runtimeDecls coreDecls modules

checkModulesEntryFullK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkModulesEntryFullK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  let (_, schemes, errs, warns) =
    checkModulesEntryFullSplitK
      inputs
      False
      preludeKey
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls
      modules
  (schemes, errs, warns)

-- S-full-env-scheme-entry (#1116): the SAME driver, but ALSO returning the
-- prelude's own schemes — the `coreSchemes` `checkModulesPreambleK` already
-- computes and this function has always had in scope but dropped on the floor.
-- Nothing else changes: `checkModulesEntryFull` above is this minus the first
-- component, so every existing caller is byte-identical.
--
-- 🚨 The two lists are returned SEPARATE, and in this order — `(coreSchemes,
-- entryOwnSchemes, …)` — precisely so a caller CANNOT accidentally get #2054's
-- shape.  A consumer that wants one flat env for a first-match lookup
-- (`lookupSchemeL`, `lookupAssoc`) must concat OWN FIRST: `own ++ core`.  With
-- the prelude first, a user top-level binding that shadows a prelude name
-- (`isEven x = "s"`) would lose the linear-scan race to the prelude's
-- `Int -> Bool`, which is exactly the class of defect #2054 pins on the Flat
-- arm.  The Module arm's own fold is NOT affected — `checkModulesEntryFromDiags`'
-- terminal clause still returns only the terminal module's own schemes, and no
-- re-checking or re-unification against `coreSchemes` happens here: the
-- prelude list is carried out for LOOKUP only, alongside (never merged with)
-- the module's own entry for the same bare name.
-- `preludeKey`: the prelude key for the prelude interface (see `checkCoreMemoized`);
-- `None` = unkeyed, no cache.
export
checkModulesEntryFullSplitK : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkModulesEntryFullSplitK inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  entrySplit
    (fst
      (checkModulesEntryFullReportK
        inputs
        valueMain
        preludeKey
        runtimeDecls
        coreDecls0
        modules0))

-- `checkModulesEntryFullSplitK`'s four parts, read off its drive's report.
export
entrySplit : TcReport ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List (String, PubScheme), List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
entrySplit r =
  let (schemes, errs, warns) = checkModulesEntryFromDiags r.trDiagnostics
  (r.trPartial.paCore, schemes, errs, warns)

-- `checkModulesEntryFullSplitK` returning its drive's whole report beside its
-- outputs.
export
checkModulesEntryFullReportK : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (TcReport, DriveOutputs)
checkModulesEntryFullReportK inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0 =
  fst
    (checkModulesEntryFullReportKGraph
      (driveArgs inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0))

-- `checkModulesEntryFullReportK` beside the graph record its drive ends in.
checkModulesEntryFullReportKGraph : DriveArgs ->
  ((TcReport, DriveOutputs), GraphCtx)
checkModulesEntryFullReportKGraph (DriveArgs inputs valueMain _ preludeKey _ runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modules0) =
  -- The single-file front door loads stdlib/core.mdk twice: once as its prelude and
  -- once as the singleton `__user__` module.  Resolve already owns the syntactic
  -- self-test that prevents double-seeding core.  Apply that same authority before
  -- identity stamping, so one declaration graph is not stamped once as `core` and
  -- again as `__user__`.  `None` also prevents the empty prelude pass from sharing a
  -- memo key with the separately loaded declarations we deliberately dropped.
  -- Partial lookalikes remain ordinary because `programIsCore` requires BOTH halves.
  let (effectivePreludeKey, effectiveCoreDecls0, effectiveModules0) =
    match modules0
      [(_, prog)] =>
        if programIsCore prog then
          (None, [], [("core", prog)])
        else
          (preludeKey, coreDecls0, modules0)
      _ => (preludeKey, coreDecls0, modules0)
  let g =
    driveGraphK
      GOutDiags
      (driveArgs
        inputs
        valueMain
        effectivePreludeKey
        runtimeDecls
        effectiveCoreDecls0
        effectiveModules0)
  ((g.gdReport, g.gdOutputs), g.gdGraph)

-- render the entry report: type errors win (each `TYPE ERROR: …`), else the
-- entry's `name : scheme` lines followed by its non-exhaustive-match warnings.
-- The harness sorts, so scheme/warn relative order is not load-bearing.  Mirrors
-- runCheck's single-file shape, but over the entry module of a resolved graph.
export
checkModulesEntryReport : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  String
checkModulesEntryReport inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  fst (checkModulesEntryReportOut inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules)

-- `checkModulesEntryReport` beside the outputs of its drive.
checkModulesEntryReportOut : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (String, DriveOutputs)
checkModulesEntryReportOut inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  let (report, outputs) =
    checkModulesEntryFullReportK
      inputs
      False
      None
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls
      modules
  let (_, schemes, errs, warns) = entrySplit report
  match errs
    [] => (joinNl (schemeLines schemes ++ map tcMsg warns), outputs)
    _ => (joinNl (typeErrorLines (map tcMsg errs)), outputs)

-- exit-code predicate analog of checkHasErrors: any type error in ANY module of
-- the resolved graph (entry or imported — see checkModulesEntryFromDiags).
export
checkModulesEntryHasErrors : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  Bool
checkModulesEntryHasErrors inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  let (_, errs, _) = checkModulesEntryFull inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules
  match errs
    [] => False
    _ => True

-- ── ONE-MODULE Module-arm wrapper (S-check-one-wrapper, E-1 #1115) ─────────
-- The scheme/diagnostic-tree analogue of `elaborateOne` (above): runs a SINGLE
-- program through the `Module` arm as a degenerate one-entry module list, the
-- same way `elaborateOne` runs it through `elaborateModules`.  `checkModulesEntryFull`
-- is the entry-report projection of the one whole-graph driver (see the file header
-- and `driveGraphK`) — it therefore performs BOTH halves of the
-- two-part safety property a standalone `Module`-arm caller must uphold
-- (`mintGraphCtx` and the unconditional `mangledFunDefsPresentRef`/
-- `declEnvsRef` overwrites, the last carrying the method and constructor export
-- indices — see `checkModuleFullDiags`'s header comment just above its `export`).
-- Delegating to it (rather than hand-assembling a bare `checkModuleFullDiags` call
-- with an invented module id / degenerate accData/implDecls) gets that property for
-- free and needs no "empty module id" convention of its own — `foldModules`'s own
-- singleton-list clause (`[(mid, prog)]`) already IS the degenerate one-entry case,
-- run through the identical preamble every N-module call gets.  `rootId` names the
-- program's own module id (analogous to `elaborateOne`'s `rootId`); `checkModulesEntryFull`
-- has previously only been reached with 2+-module graphs (`checkModulesEntryReport`/
-- `checkModulesEntryHasErrors`, both import-bearing-only per its own header comment) —
-- this is its first 1-module caller.
export
checkOneScheme : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  List (String, Scheme)
checkOneScheme inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls (rootId, prog) =
  let (schemes, _, _) =
    checkModulesEntryFull inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls [(rootId, prog)]
  pubSchemes schemes

-- FULL-ENVIRONMENT sibling of `checkOneScheme` (S-full-env-scheme-entry, #1116),
-- `checkOneSchemeFullK`:
-- `(preludeSchemes, ownSchemes)` for a ONE-MODULE program, the Module-arm
-- replacement for the retired single-program environment that the
-- introspection consumers (LSP hover/completion/inlayHint, the playground's
-- hover/complete query, `check-policy --fn <name>`) need: they look names up by
-- BARE NAME with no idea whether the name is the user's or the prelude's, so
-- `checkOneScheme`'s terminal-module-own-only answer is not enough for them.
--
-- The two halves are handed back SEPARATE, not pre-concatenated, so the caller
-- states the precedence it wants explicitly.  Every current consumer wants
-- `own ++ prelude` (first match wins ⇒ a user redefinition shadows the prelude's
-- binding of the same name) — see `checkModulesEntryFullSplit`'s 🚨 note.  This
-- is NOT a widening of the `medaka check` front door, whose narrowed
-- own-schemes-only report is deliberate and unchanged.
--
-- The prelude key is for the prelude interface (see
-- `checkCoreMemoized`): pass `Some (desugaredPreludeKey runtimeSrc,
-- desugaredPreludeKey coreSrc)` when `runtimeDecls`/`coreDecls` are exactly what
-- `desugaredPrelude` returned for those sources; `None` otherwise.
export
checkOneSchemeFullK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (List (String, PubScheme), List (String, PubScheme))
checkOneSchemeFullK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls prog =
  fst
    (checkOneSchemeFullKGraph
      (driveArgs inputs False preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls [prog]))

-- `checkOneSchemeFullK` beside the graph record its drive ends in, for a test that
-- observes that record after the drive.
export
checkOneSchemeFullKGraph : DriveArgs ->
  ((List (String, PubScheme), List (String, PubScheme)), GraphCtx)
checkOneSchemeFullKGraph args =
  let (report, gctx) = checkOneReportKGraph args
  let (coreSchemes, schemes, _, _) = entrySplit report
  ((coreSchemes, schemes), gctx)

-- diagnostics-returning sibling of `checkOneScheme`, matching the former wrapper's
-- return shape (`(errs, warns)`) exactly, so a call site can swap targets with no
-- shape change (this slice's own acceptance bar — see the packet).
export
checkOneDiags : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkOneDiags inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls prog =
  checkOneDiagsK inputs False None runtimeDecls coreDecls prog

-- `checkOneDiags` beside the graph record its drive ends in, for a test that observes
-- that record after the drive.
export
checkOneDiagsGraph : DriveArgs -> ((List TcDiag, List TcDiag), GraphCtx)
checkOneDiagsGraph args =
  let (report, gctx) = checkOneReportKGraph args
  let (_, _, errs, warns) = entrySplit report
  ((errs, warns), gctx)

-- `checkOneDiags` with the prelude key for the prelude interface — same contract as
-- `checkOneSchemeFullK` — and the caller's value-main candidate (`autoPrintWanted`).
export
checkOneDiagsK : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  (List TcDiag, List TcDiag)
checkOneDiagsK inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls prog =
  let (_, _, errs, warns) =
    entrySplit
      (checkOneReportK inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls prog)
  (errs, warns)

-- The report-returning sibling of both `checkOneSchemeFullK` and `checkOneDiagsK`:
-- the one-module drive's whole report, which each of them projects.
export
checkOneReportK : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, List Decl) ->
  TcReport
checkOneReportK inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls prog =
  fst
    (checkOneReportKGraph
      (driveArgs inputs valueMain preludeKey runtimeDecls coreDecls [prog]))

-- `checkOneReportK` beside the graph record its drive ends in.
checkOneReportKGraph : DriveArgs -> (TcReport, GraphCtx)
checkOneReportKGraph args =
  let ((report, _), gctx) = checkModulesEntryFullReportKGraph args
  (report, gctx)

-- S-migrate-check-route (E-1 #1115): thin ONE-MODULE String/Bool wrappers, matching
-- `checkToLinesWithRuntime`'s / `checkErrorsWithRuntime`'s (runtimeDecls, coreProg,
-- userProg) shape exactly, so `compiler/tools/check.mdk`'s two rendering/bool call
-- sites can swap targets with no shape change.  Delegate to the EXISTING entry-report/
-- -hasErrors functions above (already byte-identical in render logic to
-- checkToLinesWithRuntime/checkErrorsWithRuntime — `joinNl (schemeLines ++ warns)` on
-- success, `typeErrorLines` on error) over a singleton module list, rather than
-- duplicating that rendering logic here.  `"__user__"` mirrors `elaborateOne`'s own
-- rootId convention for a bare/no-import file (`entry_support.mdk`).
export
checkOneToLinesWithRuntime : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (String, DriveOutputs)
checkOneToLinesWithRuntime inputs runtimeDecls coreProg userProg = checkModulesEntryReportOut
  inputs
  runtimeDecls
  coreProg
  [("__user__", userProg)]

-- `checkOneToLinesWithRuntime` with the prelude key for the prelude interface (see
-- `checkCoreMemoized`): same contract as `checkOneSchemeFullK`.  Same render as
-- `checkModulesEntryReport`, over the keyed driver.
export
checkOneToLinesWithRuntimeK : DriveInputs ->
  Option (Int, Int) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  String
checkOneToLinesWithRuntimeK inputs preludeKey runtimeDecls coreProg userProg =
  let (schemes, errs, warns) = checkModulesEntryFullK
    inputs
    preludeKey
    runtimeDecls
    coreProg
    [("__user__", userProg)]
  match errs
    [] => joinNl (schemeLines schemes ++ map tcMsg warns)
    _ => joinNl (typeErrorLines (map tcMsg errs))

export
checkOneToLines : DriveInputs -> List Decl -> String
checkOneToLines inputs userProg =
  fst (checkOneToLinesWithRuntime inputs [] [] userProg)

export
checkOneErrorsWithRuntime : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  Bool
checkOneErrorsWithRuntime inputs runtimeDecls coreProg userProg = checkModulesEntryHasErrors
  inputs
  runtimeDecls
  coreProg
  [("__user__", userProg)]

-- One-module match-report wrapper: the type-aware
-- non-exhaustive-match report for ONE module, runtime externs seeded and NO
-- prelude, rendered as one `Warning: non-exhaustive match …` line per warning.
--
-- WARNINGS ONLY — type errors are dropped on the floor rather than rendered, which
-- is this report's contract, not an oversight: the caller
-- (`compiler/entries/check_match_main.mdk`) diffs against
-- `dev/diagdump.exe --check-match`, which reports match warnings and nothing else.
-- `checkOneDiags` is the wrapper this delegates to precisely because it hands the
-- two halves back SEPARATE, so the split needs no new projection.
--
-- The empty `coreDecls` is what makes the seed identical to the `Flat` wrapper's
-- hand-built `externSchemes (runtimeExternTyOriginScope []) runtimeDecls`: `graphPreamble`
-- computes `externSchemes (runtimeExternTyOriginScope coreDecls) runtimeDecls`, which
-- collapses to the same expression at `coreDecls = []`.  `"__user__"` mirrors
-- `checkOneToLinesWithRuntime`'s rootId convention just above.
export
checkOneMatchToLines : DriveInputs -> List Decl -> List Decl -> String
checkOneMatchToLines inputs runtimeDecls prog =
  let (_, warns) = checkOneDiags inputs runtimeDecls [] ("__user__", prog)
  joinNl (map tcMsg warns)

-- run the multi-module front-end and render the ENTRY module's own bindings as
-- `name : scheme` per line (the harness sorts) — diffs vs dev/tc_module_probe.
export
checkModulesEntryLines : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  String
checkModulesEntryLines inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  joinNl
    (schemeLines
      (entryOwnSchemes (checkModules inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules)))

-- Emit EVERY module's bindings in one pass, each section preceded by a
-- `## MODULE <mid>` marker line.  A single full-closure run can then cover many
-- modules at once (the batch harness splits on the marker and diffs each
-- section vs its per-module oracle).  Scheme of a module depends only on its
-- dependency-closure (which precedes it in topo order), so a module's section
-- is identical whether checked alone or inside a larger union closure.
export
checkModulesAllLines : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  String
checkModulesAllLines inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules =
  joinNl (allModuleLines (checkModules inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls modules))

allModuleLines : List (String, List (String, PubScheme)) -> List String
allModuleLines [] = []
allModuleLines ((mid, ss) :: rest) =
  "## MODULE " ++ mid :: schemeLines ss ++ allModuleLines rest

-- The multi-module analog of `elaborate`: the composition of `checkModules`
-- (per-module-frame typecheck, dependency-first) and the EMethodAt route-
-- stamping `elaborate` does single-module.  prePasses the prelude + every module
-- (rewriting return-position method occurrences to EMethodAt over the WHOLE
-- program's RP method set), typechecks each module in dependency order (seeded
-- like checkModules), and STAMPS each module's route refs from its now-resolved
-- result types.  Returns the SAME trees (refs filled) so evalModulesWith can route
-- return-position dispatch (`pure`/`andThen` — the Parser monad) by RKey instead
-- of the untyped arg-tag fallback.  RKey-only (dictNames = []): the bootstrap
-- source uses no `=>`-constrained user polymorphism, so no EDictAt / dict params
-- and no pendingDictApps / pendingRecDictApps to resolve.  Return shape: `ElabResult`.
export
elaborateModules : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  ElabResult
-- E4: D3b's ARG-position dict-passing per module runs inside the drive
-- (`dictPassModulesIfEnabled`), on every path (single elaboration mode, #157).
--
-- Impl-body inference runs on every driver, so a standalone-shadow method call
-- INSIDE an impl body (map's `Debug`/`Eq`/`Ord`/`Display (Map k v)` bodies all call
-- standalone `toList m`) RECORDS its RLocal site (recordRLocalSite -> the goal
-- channel) for resolveRLocalSites to stamp.  Without it the impl-body `toList` route
-- stays at its default RNone -> eval arg-tag-dispatches a Map through the
-- no-matching-tag `keepOrAll` fallback to Foldable List's `toList = identity` ->
-- returns the Map unchanged -> `debug`/`display` recurse forever (SIGBUS on
-- `medaka test stdlib/map.mdk`).
--
-- #2705: what distinguishes this projection from the diagnostics one is no longer
-- WHAT is solved — the same driver, the same preamble, the same worker, the same
-- graph-end drain — but that `GOutTrees` sets the promotion-eligible set (so its
-- groups PROMOTE and their call sites are marked as dict applications), publishes
-- the evidence table and dict-passes the marked trees, and that it returns the
-- diagnostics the drive raised as its residual.
elaborateModules inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn =
  match (elaborateModulesWithSchemes
    inputs
    False
    runtimeDecls
    coreDecls0
    modulesIn)
    (result, _, _) => result

-- `elaborateModules` beside the graph record its drive ends in, for a test that
-- observes that record after the drive.
export
elaborateModulesGraph : DriveArgs -> (ElabResult, GraphCtx)
elaborateModulesGraph args =
  let ((result, _, _), gctx) = elabTreesDrive args
  (result, gctx)

-- `elaborateModules` together with the prelude's schemes and the entry module's own
-- schemes, from the SAME drive: a verb that both reads types and evaluates the
-- elaborated program (`check-policy`) typechecks once.  `valueMain` is the caller's
-- value-main candidate (`autoPrintWanted`).
export
elaborateModulesWithSchemes : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (ElabResult, List (String, PubScheme), List (String, PubScheme))
elaborateModulesWithSchemes inputs valueMain runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn =
  let (result, r, _) =
    elaborateModulesWithReport
      inputs
      valueMain
      None
      runtimeDecls
      coreDecls0
      modulesIn
  let (core, entrySchemes, _, _) = entrySplit r
  (result, core, entrySchemes)

-- `elaborateModulesWithSchemes` returning its drive's whole report and outputs
-- beside the elaboration.  `executes` names the `async` driver of a caller that runs or
-- emits the trees: its checked program then carries the auto-print evidence.
export
elaborateModulesWithReport : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  Option String ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (ElabResult, TcReport, DriveOutputs)
elaborateModulesWithReport inputs valueMain executes runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn =
  fst
    (elabTreesDrive
      (DriveArgs
        inputs
        valueMain
        executes
        None
        None
        runtimeDecls
        coreDecls0
        modulesIn))

-- `elaborateModulesWithReport` keyed as `checkModulesChainReportFor` is: under the
-- prelude generation pair and [chainKey], with one source key per module in
-- [stepKeys], each module before the entry that is unchanged since it was recorded
-- is served from its recorded trees, and each module the drive checks records them.
export
elaborateModulesChainReport : DriveInputs ->
  Bool ->
  (Int, Int) ->
  String ->
  List (Option String) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  (ElabResult, TcReport, DriveOutputs)
elaborateModulesChainReport inputs valueMain (rk, ck) chainKey stepKeys runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn =
  fst
    (elabTreesDrive
      (DriveArgs
        inputs
        valueMain
        None
        None
        (Some (chainFullKey rk ck chainKey, stepKeys))
        runtimeDecls
        coreDecls0
        modulesIn))

-- The tree-producing drive of [args], as `elaborateModules` projects it.
elabTreesDrive : DriveArgs -> ((ElabResult, TcReport, DriveOutputs), GraphCtx)
elabTreesDrive args = elabDriveResult (driveGraphK GOutTrees args)

elabDriveResult : GraphDrive -> ((ElabResult, TcReport, DriveOutputs), GraphCtx)
elabDriveResult g = (
  (
    (
      g.gdCore,
      g.gdModules,
      map dropModSchemes g.gdPerMod,
      g.gdResidual,
      g.gdEvTable,
      g.gdDispositions,
      ElabFacts {
        efManifests = g.gdOutputs.doManifests,
        efBinders = g.gdBinders,
        efEntryOutcome = g.gdOutputs.doEntryOutcome,
      },
    ),
    g.gdReport,
    g.gdOutputs,
  ),
  g.gdGraph,
)

-- The report of `elaborateModules`' drive, for a test that reads it.
export
elaborateModulesReport : DriveInputs ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List Decl) ->
  TcReport
elaborateModulesReport inputs runtimeDecls coreDecls0 modulesIn =
  match (elaborateModulesWithReport
    inputs
    False
    None
    runtimeDecls
    coreDecls0
    modulesIn)
    (_, report, _) => report
-- arg-position interface-method names whose route resolveArgStamps may stamp
-- (populated on every path; an empty set would degenerate prePassDictArg to prePassDict)

-- DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 2 (global): seed the promotion-eligible set with the USER
-- modules' top-level fn names (NOT core/prelude — those keep arg-tag / RKey
-- dispatch, which type-checks the goldens).  Seeded on every path (single
-- elaboration mode) so
-- elaborateModules promotes INFERRED-constraint user fns exactly as flat
-- the retired promotion fixpoint did (it reran discovery whenever the eligible set was
-- non-empty).  The TYPECHECK golden module drivers
-- (checkModulesEntryLines/checkModulesDiagsChain) never reach elaborateModules, so their
-- goldens are unaffected; the eval goldens are captured from the OCaml oracle's
-- run, which dict-passes prelude+modules unconditionally — so promotion-on-eval
-- matches them. Mirrors the graph driver's dictionary-eligibility seed.

-- Cause A / Phase 2: promotion discovery — an unsignatured fn whose body dispatches
-- a method in ARG position (`f s = println s` → inferred `Display a => …`) OR uses a
-- return-position method (`doubleEmpty x = append (append x empty) empty` → inferred
-- `Monoid a => …`) is discovered + registered in promotedRef/funConstraintsRef/
-- activeDictVars, and the promoted names join the dict-name set so their call sites
-- are marked EDictAt (caller supplies the dict ARGUMENT) and their defs get the
-- leading dict PARAM.  The discovery is `registerInferredConstraints` at each
-- group's close, and the schedule marks with its result (`markRecursiveOccurrences`
-- for the group's own occurrences, `beginModuleMarking` for every later group and
-- module); the joint-flattened Flat-arm scratch pass survives only in
-- the retired single-program promotion discovery.

-- E6: dict-name set for the `=>`-constrained-function layer, mirroring the
-- single-file emit driver's assembly (llvm_emit_typed_main.runEmit): prelude
-- return- AND arg-position `=>`-constrained fns (clamp/debugListItems/…) plus the
-- modules' own constrained signatures.  Computed on every path (single elaboration
-- mode).
-- Used to MARK constrained-fn call sites EDictAt in the prePass (so the caller
-- supplies the dict ARGUMENT) AND to add the leading dict PARAM in the final
-- dictPass (so the callee body's witness binds).  BOTH halves are required: marking
-- alone under-defines the param; param alone under-applies the call (ABI mismatch).
-- Cause A: promoted (inferred-constraint) user fns join the set.

-- ARGSTAMP-UNIFY Phase 4: the eval-dict layer is now PERMANENTLY active on both
-- paths (flag retired). The former elaborator ran its resolve chain and dict pass
-- UNCONDITIONALLY; the 1-module / multi-module eval path matches, so method-level
-- constraints (`build : Num e =>`) and parametric-impl `requires` (`impl Default
-- (List a) requires Default a`) — which leave dictNames EMPTY (no top-level
-- constrained FUNCTION) but still need resolveMethodDicts / the DImpl dict-pass —
-- get serviced.  The dictPass/resolve passes are no-ops when nothing was marked, so
-- this leaves the golden eval trees byte-identical where there is nothing to
-- dict-pass.
-- C5: ALSO mark cross-module standalone-shadow method occurrences (box's
-- `toList`/`isEmpty`) as EMethodAt, so they carry a route ref for elabModuleStamp's
-- resolveRLocalSites to stamp RLocal.  Without this they stay EVar (arg-position)
-- → no route ref → arg-tag panic on a no-impl
-- receiver.  Only added to the MARK set (not rpNames proper, which discoverPromoted
-- etc. consume) so other passes are unchanged.
-- DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 2: ALSO include methodConstraintNames (methods with their OWN
-- method-level `=>` constraint, e.g. `build : Num e => e -> t`), mirroring flat
-- the former pre-pass mark set (`returnPosMethodNames prog ++ methodConstraintNames
-- prog`).  Without it the method-constraint occurrence stays unmarked → its impl
-- clause's leading method-dict param is dropped/mis-bound → `non-exhaustive match`
-- (method_constraint_impl_offset).

-- impl table accumulates across modules (dependency order): a module's
-- arg-position dispatch can thread an EARLIER module's / core's parametric
-- `requires` element dict.  core2 seeds it.

-- E5: dict-pass each module's now-stamped
-- trees, preserving the former final `dictPass`. A parametric `requires`
-- impl whose body element-dispatch E5 routed RDict needs its leading
-- `$dict_<m>_<slot>` PARAM (dictPassDecl's DImpl arm) so the emitter can bind the
-- witness the route references; the call site's impl-dict route (filled by
-- resolveArgStamp) supplies the matching ARGUMENT.  E6: dictNames now carries the
-- prelude/module `=>`-constrained fns (clamp/debugListItems/displayListItems/…),
-- so dictPassDecl's DFunDef arm ALSO prepends their `$dict_<fn>_<slot>` params —
-- closing the "unbound dict witness" residual.  Runs on every path (single
-- elaboration mode); when nothing is marked the pass is a no-op returning
-- core2/modules2 verbatim.

-- Post-ARGSTAMP-UNIFY (Phase 4): ALWAYS run on every path (single elaboration mode).
-- Emit:
-- the DImpl arm prepends parametric impl-body element-dict params even when no
-- top-level `=>`-constrained FUNCTION is in [dictNames] (e.g. the empty-prelude
-- nested_dict_dispatch fixture: a parametric `Cmp (Box a) requires Cmp a` with no
-- constrained fn).  Eval: same — a parametric-impl `requires` whose DImpl body needs
-- its leading element-dict param (nested_instance_dicts: `impl Default (List a)
-- requires Default a`) gets dict-passed even with no top-level constrained FUNCTION.
-- Runs the unconditional final dict pass. No-op when nothing was
-- marked → byte-identical golden trees.  scopePredicateSlots sizes each define's leading
-- dict params to its surviving-constraint count.

-- ── the other half of `selfDeclArities`, and it is NOT optional ──────────────
-- `selfDeclArities` is a per-module VIEW; the ref it is written to is ALSO the ref the
-- cross-module accumulator is snapshotted FROM at the end of the same body
-- (`crossModuleFunConstraintsRef` ← `ModuleCtx.funConstraintsRef`).  Without this restore the
-- view becomes a graph-wide LOSS: the rows masked while checking module `mid` never reach
-- the modules processed after it.
--
-- 🚨 MEASURED, not hypothetical — this is what the first cut of #1425's fix did.  Masking
-- `count` while checking `vector` (which declares an unconstrained `count`) deleted the
-- PRELUDE's constrained `count` row from the accumulator, so every later module lost it and
-- an ordinary `count (x => x > 1) [1, 2, 3]` in the entry panicked at `run`.  Prelude names
-- are the ones that expose it, because `importDefinersOf` skips `core` — a prelude name is
-- never an "explicit import", so it can only ever be answered by this bare table.
--
-- Restores by NAME from the accumulator's own previous value (still unwritten at the
-- snapshot, which is this ref's only writer), appended AFTER the module's rows so
-- first-match still prefers the identity-resolved answer.  Content-preserving: the view
-- removed exactly these rows, so appending them back is not duplication.
selfNameRows : List Decl -> List (String, a) -> List (String, a)
selfNameRows prog old =
  let own = namesToSet (declTopFnNames prog) omEmpty
  filterList (e => omHasKey (fst e) own) old

-- attribute each fn DEFINED in [prog] that carries a per-fn table entry to [mid] —
-- the source-exact `(mid, name)` key every cross-module fn table is read under.
--
-- Used for all three of the fn-side qual snapshots: the constraint ARITIES
-- (`funConstraintsRef`), the slot-parallel IFACES (`funConstraintIfacesRef`) and, since
-- B-2.2-f (#1113), the per-entry DECLARED-PREFIX scalar.  It was two byte-identical
-- monomorphic copies (`attributeModuleArities` / `attributeModuleArrIfaces`), each
-- carrying a `rule-duplicate-body` disable deferred to #1201; the sidecar's SCALAR
-- payload does not fit a `List X` signature at all, so the payload is now `a` and both
-- disables are gone rather than joined by a third.
attributeModuleEntries : String ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, a) ->
  List ((String, String), a)
attributeModuleEntries mid prog entries =
  let progNames = namesToSet (declTopFnNames prog) omEmpty
  map
    (e => ((mid, fst e), snd e))
    (filterList (e => omHasKey (fst e) progNames) entries)

-- #1114 (#845): attribute each TOP-LEVEL binding of [prog] that carries a
-- `schemeObligationsRef` entry to [mid], source-exactly keyed `(mid, name)` — the same
-- key discipline as `attributeModuleEntries` above, and for the same #739 reason (a
-- bare-name cross-module table misattributes a same-named export of another module).
--
-- ⚠️ Unlike the arity tables, `schemeObligationsRef` is keyed `(name, binding-id)` and
-- holds LOCAL/`where` binders too (registerSchemeObligations is called from the local
-- sites with `omEmpty` member ids, so those key under id 0).  Two filters make the
-- projection to a bare exported name safe:
--   (1) [progNames] keeps only names this module defines at TOP LEVEL, so a `where`
--       helper whose name matches nothing at top level never enters the table at all;
--   (2) FIRST-occurrence-wins within a name, which resolves the one residual case — a
--       `where` helper that happens to SHARE a top-level name.  `schemeObligationsRef`
--       is consed, and a group's top-level members are registered AFTER the locals
--       inferred inside their bodies, so the head-most entry for a name is the
--       top-level one.  That is byte-identically the policy the SAME-module use site
--       already applies (`lookupSchemeOblsByName`'s first match), which is the point:
--       an importer must see what a same-module caller sees.
--
-- 🚨 A MODULE'S CONTRIBUTION IS WHAT IT DECLARES *PLUS WHAT IT RE-EXPORTS*, and the second
-- half is not optional — it is the recurrence `.claude/workstreams/TYPECHECK.md` records for
-- #1283 and `renameAliasedMethods` (below) already learned for the METHOD tables: "the
-- decl's origin names the DECLARING module and the import path names the RE-EXPORTER, so
-- the identity test misses."  Attributing only `declTopFnNames prog` means a pure
-- re-exporter (`export import lib.def.{f}`, whose `prog` has no function definitions at
-- all) contributes NOTHING, so `import lib.reexp.{f}` found no entry and #845's whole
-- failure came back one hop out — `check` 0, `build` 0, and the built binary SEGFAULTED.
-- Adversarial review produced exactly that program; this arm is the answer to it.
--
-- [qual] is the ALREADY-ACCUMULATED cross-module table, and dependency-first module order
-- is what makes reading it correct here: every module this one re-exports FROM has been
-- processed already, so its entries are present. That also makes a multi-hop chain resolve
-- without special-casing — each hop re-attributes under its own mid as it is processed,
-- exactly as `reexportedMethodNames` folds the method sets.
--
-- Re-export entries come SECOND so the module's own top-level binding wins the first-match
-- dedup below if a module somehow both defines and re-exports one name.
attributeModuleSchemeObls : String ->
  List Decl ->
  List ((String, String), List VecObl) ->
  List ((String, Int), List VecObl) ->
  List ((String, String), List VecObl)
attributeModuleSchemeObls mid prog qual entries =
  attributeSchemeOblsGo
      mid
      (namesToSet (publicValNames prog) omEmpty)
      omEmpty
      entries
    ++ map (e => ((mid, fst e), snd e)) (reexportSchemeOblEntries prog qual)

-- ⚠️ The export test is `publicValNames`, the module's own canonical one — NOT a
-- hand-rolled `DFunDef True` scan.  A first cut used the latter and REGRESSED two of this
-- PR's own fixtures: `export g : Num a => a -> a` on one line with `g x = x + 1` on the
-- next parses as a PUBLIC `DTypeSig` plus a PRIVATE `DFunDef`, so a scan that only reads
-- the `DFunDef` flag calls an exported binding private and drops it from the store.
-- `publicValNames` already answers this (its `DTypeSig True` arm), and reusing it is what
-- keeps this table's notion of "exported" from drifting from the module seed's.

-- the scheme contexts a module RE-EXPORTS (`export import src.{…}` / `export import src.*`),
-- each under the name it re-exports it as.  Reuses `schemeOblEntriesFor` — the same
-- spelling table the IMPORT side uses — so re-export and import can never disagree about
-- what a spelling binds; that is one table, not two kept in step by hand.
-- ⚠️ `export import src as A` contributes entries keyed `A.<name>`, which no importer of
-- THIS module can name (an alias is local to the file that writes it).  They are inert
-- rather than wrong, and excluding them would mean a second spelling table.
reexportSchemeOblEntries : List Decl ->
  List ((String, String), List VecObl) ->
  List (String, List VecObl)
reexportSchemeOblEntries [] _ = []
reexportSchemeOblEntries ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) qual =
  reexportSchemeOblEntries [d] qual ++ reexportSchemeOblEntries rest qual
reexportSchemeOblEntries ((DUse True path _) :: rest) qual =
  schemeOblEntriesFor (useModId path) path qual
    ++ reexportSchemeOblEntries rest qual
reexportSchemeOblEntries (_ :: rest) qual = reexportSchemeOblEntries rest qual

attributeSchemeOblsGo : String ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  OrdMap Unit ->
  List ((String, Int), List VecObl) ->
  List ((String, String), List VecObl)
attributeSchemeOblsGo _ _ _ [] = []
attributeSchemeOblsGo mid progNames seen (e :: rest)
  | not (omHasKey (fst (fst e)) progNames) =
    attributeSchemeOblsGo mid progNames seen rest
  | omHasKey (fst (fst e)) seen = attributeSchemeOblsGo mid progNames seen rest
  | otherwise =
    ((mid, fst (fst e)), snd e)
      :: attributeSchemeOblsGo
        mid
        progNames
        (omInsert (fst (fst e)) () seen)
        rest

-- WS-2/D2 method analog: attribute each method whose INTERFACE is declared in [prog]
-- (carrying a methodPredicateSlotsRef entry) to [mid].  Keyed by the interface's module —
-- the arity is a property of the method signature, registered during that module's
-- method-row construction pass.  The per-run table also holds the rows imported
-- modules contributed; only keys of [prog]'s own interfaces are attributed here.
attributeMethodModulePredicateSlots : String ->
  List Decl ->
  List (String, List MethodPredicateSlot) ->
  List ((String, String), List MethodPredicateSlot)
attributeMethodModulePredicateSlots mid prog entries =
  let progMethods = namesToSet (ifaceMethodSlotKeys prog) omEmpty
  map
    (e => ((mid, fst e), snd e))
    (filterList (e => omHasKey (fst e) progMethods) entries)

-- The `methodSlotKey` of every method of every interface [prog] declares.
ifaceMethodSlotKeys : List Decl -> List String
ifaceMethodSlotKeys [] = []
ifaceMethodSlotKeys ((DAttrib _ d) :: rest) =
  ifaceMethodSlotKeys [d] ++ ifaceMethodSlotKeys rest
ifaceMethodSlotKeys ((DInterface { name, methods, ifaceOrigin, ... }) :: rest) =
  map
      (methodSlotKey IfaceRef { irName = name, irOrigin = ifaceOrigin })
      (ifaceMethodNames methods)
    ++ ifaceMethodSlotKeys rest
ifaceMethodSlotKeys (_ :: rest) = ifaceMethodSlotKeys rest

-- ⚠️ THE IMPLICIT-PRELUDE ARM ABOVE IS LOAD-BEARING, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS MEASURED.
-- The prelude is in scope in EVERY module under BARE names and contributes NO `DUse`
-- decl, so a walk over the module's imports cannot see it: a
-- prelude standalone shadowed by a USER interface method (S1-PRELUDE (a), the
-- `x4_prelude_standalone_live_impl_receiver` / `x5_prelude_constrained_standalone_live_impl`
-- cells) mangles to `core__isEven` with no import form naming `isEven`, so without this
-- arm the entry was dropped and both cells went `DIFF`/FAIL — the prelude standalone's
-- `False` where the ruling requires the interface method's `True`. The prelude is also
-- the one module that CANNOT be alias-imported (`import core as C` → `Unbound variable:
-- C.eq`, SHADOW-SEMANTICS.md §1.1's table), so bare is its only spelling and this arm
-- admits nothing the alias case could have written differently.

-- typecheck one module (seeded like checkModuleFull) AND immediately stamp the
-- EMethodAt route refs it just recorded.  `recordModuleStart` marks the goal channel
-- before inference, so on return the module's window holds exactly THIS module's
-- sites; the tyvar cells are still bound (the next module's `freshModuleCtx` hasn't run
-- yet), so resolveSites reads each site's resolved result type.
-- E4: ALSO stamp this module's ARG-position sites
-- (the `GKArgStamp` goals in the same window) from their now-
-- resolved discriminating-argument monos — the multi-module analogue of
-- the shared `resolveArgStamps`. [implDecls] is core + every earlier module +
-- this module, so a cross-module parametric `requires` element dict resolves; a
-- still-unresolvable case stays RNone (arg-tag fallback).  activeDictVars (the
-- constraint-var → dict-param map registerConstraintRegs built in checkModuleFull)
-- is also still bound, so a tyvar receiver routes RDict.
elabModuleStamp : ModuleCtx ->
  String ->
  List (String, Scheme) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl ->
  (ModuleBindings, List Decl, ModuleCtx)
-- multi-line RHS: the indented `:=` form is legal but not seed-parseable yet (#1744)
-- lint-disable-next-line rule-prefer-assign-op
elabModuleStamp prev mid seedVars accData implDecls prog =
  -- Stampers execute after graph inference via `drainStampQueue`, not here.
  -- See `StampCtx` and `moduleStampOrder` for the replay mechanism.
  checkModuleFullImpl
    prev.mcGraph
    (checkRunResidue prev)
    mid
    (plainModuleSeed seedVars)
    accData
    implDecls
    prog

-- USER modules do NOT come through `elabModuleStamp`: `graphModuleWorker` runs
-- `checkModuleFullDiags` for them on both selections, so a user module's
-- constructor/match ORACLE is seeded from its own decls and `runFinalChecks`
-- (coherence, interface cycles, phantom methods, super impls) runs, identically for
-- `check` and for `build`.  The CORE pass stays here: neither selection runs
-- `checkModuleFullDiags` over the prelude, so seeding an oracle from core or running
-- coherence over it would be a new population, not a shared one.
-- DRIVER-COLLAPSE Phase 2: pass the return-position method names (was `[]`) so
-- resolveSite's RDict-case routes a RETURN-position method site RDictFwd (forward the
-- enclosing dict's nested reqs) rather than RDict (arg-position). The former path
-- passes `returnPosMethodNames prog2`; the empty list mis-stamped a parametric-impl
-- body's inner return-position method (`impl Default (List a) requires Default a where
-- def = [def]` — the inner `def` must be RDictFwd:$dict_def_0, not RDict) → eval
-- `no matching impl for dispatch` (nested_instance_dicts).  `implDecls` is this
-- module's full impl universe (core/earlier-modules ++ prog), the analogue of flat's
-- whole-program `prog2`.

-- Phase 151 / Gap G: stamp this module's comparison-operator binop sites.
-- ungated (preserving the former path's call to `resolveBinopSites`
-- unconditionally) so the EVAL path also dispatches `==`/`<`/… to user/derived
-- Eq/Ord impls (adt_deriving_ord / record_deriving_ord).  No-op when
-- pendingBinopSites is empty (no comparison-operator site) → golden type-only
-- path leaves them RNone → dictPass-skipped → byte-identical.  Gap #21: binop route
-- stamping emits `RKey key []` (empty element reqs), which only narrows the method
-- VMulti and applies ZERO dicts,
-- so even a RECURSIVE user Eq impl (json's `impl Eq Json`) dispatches inner
-- operands by arg-tag without over-applying — no suppression needed.

-- C5: LAST among the route-stampers (after resolveSites AND resolveArgStamps) so the
-- receiver-grounded RLocal/RNone overrides whatever those stamped on the SAME ref —
-- resolveSites mis-stamps RKey<resultType> (eval path) and resolveArgStamps stamps
-- RKey<receiverHead> (emit path), both wrong for a standalone-shadow site.

-- E6: fill THIS module's `=>`-constrained-fn EDictAt routes (the call sites
-- prePassDictArg marked) so the emitter supplies the matching dict ARGUMENT —
-- realizeRecDictApps for self/mutually-recursive calls, resolveDictApps for the
-- normal ones.  ARGSTAMP-UNIFY Phase 4: unconditional on BOTH paths — the eval path
-- also marks the RETURN-position constrained fns (sum/product/when/unless) EDictAt
-- via moduleDictNames, so their call-site routes must resolve here too.  No-op when
-- nothing was marked (pendingDictApps/pendingRecDictApps empty → golden drivers
-- byte-identical).

-- G7: fill each foldMap-style method occurrence's methRoutes from the monos its
-- `Monoid m` constraint resolved to at the call site (List vs String), so the
-- default's entry receives the right per-call method dictionary.
-- Independent of the body-side dict routing (the emitter restamps the body's
-- cross-interface `empty` to its `$dict_<method>_<slot>` param directly).
-- ARGSTAMP-UNIFY Phase 4: run unconditionally on every path (single elaboration
-- mode), preserving the former path (which
-- calls resolveMethodDicts unconditionally) — a method with its OWN method-level
-- constraint (`build : Num e => e -> t`) needs its method-dict route filled so its
-- impl clause binds the leading dict param instead of mis-binding it into the first
-- value param (method_constraint_impl_offset: was `non-exhaustive match`).  No-op
-- when pendingMethodDicts is empty → golden drivers byte-identical.

-- THE stamper order, run once per module by `drainStampQueue`.
moduleStampOrder : List StampStep
moduleStampOrder = [
  SSNumlitFloats,
  SSReturnSites,
  SSBinopSites,
  SSUnopSites,
  SSArithSites,
  SSArgStamps,
  SSRLocalSites,
  SSRecDictApps,
  SSDictApps,
  SSMethodDicts,
]

-- Whether a finding at [loc] is live: it is located, at one of [live]'s spans.
export
isLiveSpan : OrdMap Unit -> Option Loc -> Bool
isLiveSpan _ None = False
isLiveSpan live loc = omHasKey (locKey loc) live

-- [stamp] is the record the drain entered for [ctx].
runStampSteps : ModuleCtx -> StampCtx -> List StampStep -> Unit
runStampSteps stamp ctx steps = each (runStampStep stamp ctx) steps

-- the numeric-literal channel's window, the counterpart of `goalsSince`
numlitSince : GraphCtx -> Int -> List (Mono, Ref (Option Float), Int, Ref Route)
numlitSince gctx mark =
  moduleWindow gctx.graphRun.numlitN.value mark gctx.graphRun.numlitRefs.value

-- The stamp context of the module [mid] whose record is [mctx]: the module's run
-- fields, and its tables dropped (`moduleCtxFrom`).
stampCtxOf : ModuleCtx ->
  String ->
  List Decl ->
  List Obligation ->
  List (Mono, Ref (Option Float), Int, Ref Route) ->
  StampCtx
stampCtxOf mctx mid implDecls goals numlits =
  let dropped = blankModuleCtx mctx.mcGraph mid
  dropped.mcCells.superDeclsRef := filterList isInterfaceDecl implDecls
  StampCtx {
    scModule = mid,
    scModuleCtx = moduleCtxFrom False mctx dropped dropped dropped,
    scRpNames = returnPosMethodNames implDecls,
    scGoals = goals,
    scNumlits = numlits,
    scLiveLocs = liveLocsOf mctx goals,
  }

-- The spans of [goals] and of the module's admitted-set occurrences whose types
-- mention a variable of a live root (`GraphRun.liveRoots`): their evidence and
-- verdicts rest on what quiescence leaves that variable.
liveLocsOf : ModuleCtx -> List Obligation -> List String
liveLocsOf mctx goals =
  let ids = flatMap monoUnboundIds mctx.mcGraph.graphRun.liveRoots.value
  if isEmptyL ids then
    []
  else
    let live = fold (acc i => IdMap.set i () acc) Tip ids
    let mentions =
      ms => anyList (m => anyList (i => IdMap.has i live) (monoUnboundIds m)) ms
    spanKeys
      (map (o => o.oLoc) (filterList (o => mentions (goalMonos o)) goals)
        ++ filterMap
          ((_, m, loc) => if mentions [m] then Some loc else None)
          mctx.mcCells.admittedOccObls.value)

-- The span keys (`locKey`) of the located [locs].  An unlocated goal or occurrence
-- names no span, so no unlocated finding is joined to it (`isLiveSpan`).
export
spanKeys : List (Option Loc) -> List String
spanKeys [] = []
spanKeys (None :: rest) = spanKeys rest
spanKeys (loc :: rest) = locKey loc :: spanKeys rest

isInterfaceDecl : Decl -> Bool
isInterfaceDecl (DAttrib _ d) = isInterfaceDecl d
isInterfaceDecl (DInterface {  }) = True
isInterfaceDecl _ = False

-- This module's windows, cut now — see `goalsOfKindIn`.  [mctx] is the record the
-- module was checked in.
enqueueStampCtx : ModuleCtx -> String -> List Decl -> Unit
enqueueStampCtx mctx mid implDecls =
  let mk = currentModuleMarks mctx.mcGraph ()
  let ctx =
    stampCtxOf
      mctx
      mid
      implDecls
      (goalsSince mctx.mcGraph mk.mGoals)
      (numlitSince mctx.mcGraph mk.mNumlit)
  mctx.mcGraph.graphRun.stampCtxs :=
    ctx :: mctx.mcGraph.graphRun.stampCtxs.value

stampNoting : ModuleCtx -> (SiteCtx -> Obligation -> Unit) -> Obligation -> Unit
stampNoting stamp resolve o =
  let acc = Ref innerNone
  resolve { siteFor stamp | sxInner = Some acc } o
  o.oInner := innerOr o.oInner.value acc.value

runStampStep : ModuleCtx -> StampCtx -> StampStep -> Unit
runStampStep mctx ctx SSNumlitFloats = setNumlitFloatsGo ctx.scNumlits
runStampStep stamp ctx SSReturnSites =
  let rpNames = omFromNames ctx.scRpNames omEmpty
  stampEach
    stamp
    (sx o => resolveSites sx rpNames (siteEntry o))
    GKReturnSite
    ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSBinopSites =
  stampEach stamp (sx o => resolveOpSites sx True (siteEntry o)) GKBinopSite ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSUnopSites =
  stampEach stamp (sx o => resolveOpSites sx False (siteEntry o)) GKUnopSite ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSArithSites =
  stampEach stamp (_ o => resolveArithSites (arithEntry o)) GKArithSite ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSArgStamps =
  stampEach stamp (sx o => resolveArgStamps sx (siteEntry o)) GKArgStamp ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSRLocalSites =
  stampEach stamp (sx o => resolveRLocalSites sx (siteEntry o)) GKRLocalSite ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSRecDictApps =
  stampEach
    stamp
    (sx o => realizeRecDictApps sx (recDictAppEntry o))
    GKRecDictApp
    ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSDictApps =
  stampEach stamp (sx o => resolveDictApps sx (dictAppEntry o)) GKDictApp ctx
runStampStep stamp ctx SSMethodDicts =
  stampEach
    stamp
    (sx o => resolveMethodDicts sx (methodDictEntry o))
    GKMethodDict
    ctx

-- Stamps [ctx]'s goals of kind [k] one at a time, in channel order, and ORs what
-- each stamp noted below its destination's top level into that goal's `oInner`.
stampEach : ModuleCtx ->
  (SiteCtx -> Obligation -> Unit) ->
  GoalKind ->
  StampCtx ->
  Unit
stampEach stamp resolve k ctx =
  fold (_ o => stampNoting stamp resolve o) () (goalsOfKindIn k ctx.scGoals)

-- #2548 (ARCH M3/Q2, DICT §6.2 T4): ONE ordered resolve pass over the whole graph,
-- at quiescence.  Every module's goals are stamped after every module has been
-- inferred, so a goal in an early module is decided against the final solution
-- rather than against the solution as it stood when that module closed.
--
-- The per-module context is REPLAYED, not re-derived (see `StampCtx`), and restored
-- afterwards so `dictPassModulesIfEnabled` — which runs next — sees the state the
-- last module left, exactly as it does today.
-- #2705: the drain at the core pass's freeze (`checkCoreMemoized`), at a recording
-- module's end and at a keyed drive's graph end (`diagsServe`).  Nothing is
-- published: a check driver has no reader of the evidence table.
--
-- The sticky type-error cells are saved before the drain and restored after it, so
-- a recorded interface never carries a drain's diagnostics.  An unkeyed drive
-- (`driveGraphK`) keeps them instead; the two agree because the drain raises none on
-- an accepted program.  The drain's evidence holes are returned, not recorded: a
-- per-module diagnostics list is closed before any drain, so the caller attaches
-- them to it (`attachEvidenceHoles`).
checkGraphFinish : ModuleCtx -> List DrainFinding
checkGraphFinish mctx =
  let savedSticky = mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsSticky.value
  let savedStickyDiags = mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsStickyDiags.value
  let holes = drainStampQueue mctx
  mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsSticky := savedSticky
  mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsStickyDiags := savedStickyDiags
  holes

verdictFinding : String -> Bool -> TcDiag -> DrainFinding
verdictFinding mid live d = DrainFinding {
  dfModule = mid,
  dfDiag = d,
  dfVerdict = True,
  dfLive = live,
}

-- What [act] records in the type-error channel of [mctx], the current record, taken
-- back out of it and out of the sticky one: a drain runs after every module's
-- diagnostics list closed, so its caller attaches them (`attachDrainFindings`).  The
-- dedup state [act] reads and writes stays, so a verdict the module already reported is
-- not repeated.
captureTypeErrors : ModuleCtx -> (Unit -> Unit) -> List TcDiag
captureTypeErrors mctx act =
  let w = mctx.mcCells.typeErrors
  let items = w.items.value
  let mark = wMark w
  let sticky = mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsSticky.value
  let stickyDiags = mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsStickyDiags.value
  act ()
  let added = wWindow w mark
  w.items := items
  w.n := mark
  mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsSticky := sticky
  mctx.mcGraph.typeErrorsStickyDiags := stickyDiags
  reverseL added

-- Runs every queued stamping context, then judges the evidence they finished.
-- Returns the verdicts the drain decides (held live-root goals, admitted-set
-- occurrences) and one `T-UNROUTED-DISPATCH` per hole, each under its module.
-- [saved] is the record the drain begins with; each stamp context's walk runs in the
-- record the drain enters for it (`enterStampCtx`).
drainStampQueue : ModuleCtx -> List DrainFinding
drainStampQueue saved =
  let ctxs = reverseL saved.mcGraph.graphRun.stampCtxs.value
  defaultLiveNumRoots saved
  let held = judgeLiveGoals saved ctxs
  let verdicts = runStampCtxs saved ctxs
  let liveLocs = omFromNames (flatMap (c => c.scLiveLocs) ctxs) omEmpty
  let holes =
    map
      ((mid, d) => DrainFinding {
        dfModule = mid,
        dfDiag = d,
        dfVerdict = False,
        dfLive = isLiveSpan liveLocs (tcLoc d),
      })
      (judgeEvidence saved.mcGraph ctxs)
  materializeBinderTypes saved.mcGraph ()
  saved.mcGraph.graphRun.stampCtxs := []
  held ++ verdicts ++ holes

-- The completeness judgment (`types/evidence_complete.mdk`) over the evidence this
-- drain finished: every method cell minted since the last drain, and the goals of
-- [ctxs] whose destination is not a method cell.  A return-site, arg-stamp or
-- RLocal goal writes its cell's dispatch route and a method-dict goal its method
-- dictionaries, so those are judged once, as the cell.
judgeEvidence : GraphCtx -> List StampCtx -> List (String, TcDiag)
judgeEvidence gctx ctxs =
  let g = gctx.graphRun
  let lo = g.evJudged.value
  g.evJudged := g.evCounter.value
  let base = g.evBase
  let cellHoles =
    flatMap
      ((a, b) => judgeCells g.evCells.value (a - base) (b - base) [])
      (unjudgedRanges lo g.evCounter.value g.evScopedJudged.value)
  let goalHoles = flatMap (c => flatMap (judgeGoal c.scModule) c.scGoals) ctxs
  dedupBy holeKey (cellHoles ++ goalHoles)

holeKey : (String, TcDiag) -> String
holeKey (m, TcDiag _ _ loc msg _ _) =
  "\{lenKey m}\{lenKey (locGoalKey loc)}\{msg}"

-- The cells in slots [i, hi) (ordinals less `evBase`), last first onto [acc].  A
-- cell no inference reached (no arity) carries no answer to judge.
judgeCells : Array (Option EvCell) ->
  Int ->
  Int ->
  List (String, TcDiag) ->
  List (String, TcDiag)
judgeCells arr i hi acc
  | i >= hi || i >= arrayLength arr = acc
  | otherwise = match arrayGetUnsafe i arr
    Some c => match c.ecArity.value
      Some _ =>
        let row =
          OccurrenceRow
            c.ecName.value
            c.ecIface.value
            c.ecTag.value
            c.ecImpl.value
            c.ecMeth.value
        judgeCells
          arr
          (i + 1)
          hi
          (holeDiags (evIdModule c.ecId) c.ecLoc.value c.ecInner.value row
            ++ acc)
      None => judgeCells arr (i + 1) hi acc
    None => judgeCells arr (i + 1) hi acc

judgeGoal : String -> Obligation -> List (String, TcDiag)
judgeGoal mid o = match (o.oPayload, evValueOf o.oDest)
  (GPDictApp app, EvMany rs) =>
    holeDiags
      mid
      o.oLoc
      o.oInner.value
      (DictAppRow app.pdaCallee (map (r => r.irName) app.pdaIfaces) rs)
  (GPRecDictApp (RecDictApp _ callee _ _ _ _), EvMany rs) =>
    holeDiags mid o.oLoc o.oInner.value (DictAppRow callee [] rs)
  (GPArith _, EvOne r) => holeDiags mid o.oLoc o.oInner.value (SiteRow r)
  (GPSite _ _ _, EvOne r) =>
    if goalKindTag o.oKind == goalKindTag GKBinopSite
      || goalKindTag o.oKind == goalKindTag GKUnopSite then
      holeDiags mid o.oLoc o.oInner.value (SiteRow r)
    else
      []
  _ => []

holeDiags : String ->
  Option Loc ->
  Inner ->
  EvidenceRow ->
  List (String, TcDiag)
holeDiags mid loc inner row = match rowHoleWith inner row
  None => []
  Some (_, msg) => [(mid, TcDiag "T-UNROUTED-DISPATCH" 1 loc msg None None)]

-- Each drain verdict joins the errors of the module it belongs to, and each hole
-- does unless that module then has errors: a hole beside a type error is usually its
-- consequence.  A live finding is dropped when any module of [perMod] has errors,
-- since the live root it rests on may be one an erroneous importer grounded or left
-- open.  A finding in no module of [perMod] is a module the drive has no list for,
-- which only the prelude is.
attachDrainFindings : List DrainFinding -> List ModDiags -> List ModDiags
attachDrainFindings [] perMod = perMod
attachDrainFindings findings perMod =
  let orphans =
    filterList (f => not (anyList (pm => fst pm == f.dfModule) perMod)) findings
  match orphans
    [] => ()
    f :: _ =>
      panic
        "evidence hole in module `\{f.dfModule}`, which has no diagnostics list: \{tcMsg f.dfDiag}"
  let erred = anyList (pm => isNonEmptyL (modDiagErrs pm)) perMod
  let kept = if erred then filterList (f => not f.dfLive) findings else findings
  map (pm => findingsOnto kept pm) perMod

modDiagErrs : ModDiags -> List TcDiag
modDiagErrs (_, (_, errs, _)) = errs

findingsOnto : List DrainFinding -> ModDiags -> ModDiags
findingsOnto findings (mid, (schemes, errs, warns)) =
  let mine = filterList (f => f.dfModule == mid) findings
  let verdicts = map (f => f.dfDiag) (filterList (f => f.dfVerdict) mine)
  match errs ++ verdicts
    [] => (mid, (schemes, map (f => f.dfDiag) mine, warns))
    all => (mid, (schemes, all, warns))

-- Read every queued binder's type cell into the graph's runtime-type table, now
-- that the stampers have run and nothing left in the graph can refine a drained
-- module's types.  Runs at every drain, including the one before a memo snapshot,
-- so a snapshot carries only materialized rows.
materializeBinderTypes : GraphCtx -> Unit -> Unit
materializeBinderTypes gctx _ =
  let g = gctx.graphRun
  match g.binderPending.value
    [] => ()
    pending =>
      let cells = cellTypeHeads gctx.declEnvsRef.value.deAllDecls
      let isCell = n o => omHasKey (cellHeadKey n o) cells
      g.binderRows :=
        fold
          (acc p => addBinderRow (materializeBinder isCell p) acc)
          g.binderRows.value
          (reverseL pending)
      g.binderPending := []

materializeBinder : (String -> TyConOrigin -> Bool) ->
  PendingBinder ->
  BinderRow
materializeBinder isCell (PendingBinder bid name t arity signed) = BinderRow {
  brId = bid,
  brName = name,
  brType = runtimeTypeOf isCell t,
  -- A solved type with fewer arrows than the declared parameters fixes nothing.
  brReturns =
    map
      (n =>
        optionOr RTValue (map (runtimeTypeOf isCell) (resultAfterArrows n t)))
      arity,
  brSigned = signed,
}

-- The type heads whose values are constructor cells: every `data` and `newtype`
-- declaration in the graph except an `extern data` one, keyed by identity.
cellTypeHeads : List Decl -> OrdMap Unit
cellTypeHeads decls = fold (acc d => addCellHead d acc) omEmpty decls

addCellHead : Decl -> OrdMap Unit -> OrdMap Unit
addCellHead (DData { dataName = n, dataOrigin = o, dataExtern = ext, ... }) acc =
  if ext then acc else omInsert (cellHeadKey n o) () acc
addCellHead (DNewtype { newtypeName = n, newtypeOrigin = o, ... }) acc =
  omInsert (cellHeadKey n o) () acc
addCellHead (DAttrib _ d) acc = addCellHead d acc
addCellHead _ acc = acc

cellHeadKey : String -> TyConOrigin -> String
cellHeadKey n OriginBuiltin = "builtin|\{n}"
cellHeadKey n OriginUnresolved = "unresolved|\{n}"
cellHeadKey n (OriginModule m) = "module \{m}|\{n}"

-- Stamps each context in turn and decides its admitted-set occurrences
-- (`checkAdmittedOccObls`), with the selector their routes commit through, against
-- the same final solution.
runStampCtxs : ModuleCtx -> List StampCtx -> List DrainFinding
runStampCtxs saved ctxs =
  reverseL (fold (acc ctx => reverseL (runStampCtx saved ctx) ++ acc) [] ctxs)

runStampCtx : ModuleCtx -> StampCtx -> List DrainFinding
runStampCtx saved ctx =
  let stamp = enterStampCtx saved ctx
  refileDeterminedRows stamp ()
  runStampSteps stamp ctx moduleStampOrder
  recordEvidence saved.mcGraph ctx.scGoals
  let liveSpans = omFromNames ctx.scLiveLocs omEmpty
  map
    (d => verdictFinding ctx.scModule (isLiveSpan liveSpans (tcLoc d)) d)
    (captureTypeErrors stamp (_ => checkAdmittedOccObls stamp ()))

-- #2549 M2: publish this module's solved evidence, AFTER its stampers have run and
-- READ BACK OUT OF THE CELLS THEY WROTE.  Deriving the table any other way would make
-- it a second answer that can disagree with the cells; derived this way it cannot.
recordEvidence : GraphCtx -> List Obligation -> Unit
recordEvidence gctx gs =
  let g = gctx.graphRun
  let _ = map (noteEvidenceObservation gctx) gs
  g.evTable := evEntriesOf gs ++ g.evTable.value

evEntriesOf : List Obligation -> EvTable
evEntriesOf gs = map (o => EvEntry o.oEv (evValueOf o.oDest)) gs

evValueOf : EvDest -> EvVal
evValueOf (EvRoute r) = EvOne !r
evValueOf (EvRoutes rs) = EvMany !rs

-- Writes `mangledShadowMapRef`, `argDispatchIdxByIdRef` and `markSetsRef` as a side
-- effect: the first two are read by the inference of a marked tree (P0-18 shadow
-- recovery, `argDispatchOf`), the third by `checkBodyImpl`, which marks each module's
-- groups on the schedule — so the driver that marks must be the driver that sets
-- them.  Empty / index-only on the un-mangled check path, exactly as the emit path
-- derives them.  `markSetsOf` below is the function; unkeyed, its prelude half is
-- `preludeMarkFactsOf coreDecls`.

preludeMarkFactsOf : List Decl -> PreludeMarkFacts
preludeMarkFactsOf coreDecls = PreludeMarkFacts {
  pmfReturnPos = returnPosMethodNames coreDecls,
  pmfMethodConstraints = methodConstraintNames coreDecls,
  pmfArgDispatch = argDispatchIndices coreDecls,
  pmfArgDispatchById = argDispatchIndicesById coreDecls,
  pmfConstrainedNames = constrainedSigNames coreDecls,
}

-- Install the module's marking state at module start.  Reads the graph's mark sets
-- off the driver preamble; a Module arm reached without one is a driver bug, and
-- fails loud rather than marking nothing.
beginModuleMarking : ModuleCtx ->
  String ->
  List Decl ->
  OrdMap Int ->
  ModuleSeed ->
  Unit
beginModuleMarking mctx mid prog top seed =
  let ms = match mctx.mcGraph.markSetsRef.value
    Some s => s
    None =>
      panic
        "checkBodyImpl: Module arm entered with no mark sets (no driver preamble ran)"
  -- Core imports nothing, so the dict names nameable in it are its own `=>`
  -- signatures, the set its `dictPass` gives parameters to.  A user definition that
  -- shares a bare name with a core one is a different definition.
  let known = match mid
    "" => constrainedSigNames prog
    _ => dedup (ms.msBareDictNames ++ mctx.mcGraph.promotionHarvestRef.value)
  let aliases = aliasDictNames mctx.mcGraph known ms.msUnits [(mid, prog)]
  mctx.mcGraph.graphRun.localAbs.lasNext := (mid, 0)
  mctx.mcGraph.graphRun.localAbs.lasMinted := []
  mctx.mcCells.markingRef := Some ModuleMarking {
    mmCtx = moduleMarkCtx mctx.mcGraph ms mid prog seed.msImportedValues,
    mmTop = top,
    mmDictSet = Ref (omFromNames (known ++ aliases) omEmpty),
    mmUserNames = omFromNames (declTopFnNames prog) seed.msImportedNames,
    mmExterns = omFromNames (declExternNames prog) seed.msImportedExterns,
  }

-- The stamped twin of the group's marked clauses — what inference reads.  Binding
-- ids are minted over the module's declaration heads (`stampTopScope`), so stamping
-- one group at a time yields the ids `stampBindingIds` would have.
stampGroupClauses : ModuleMarking ->
  List String ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr))
stampGroupClauses mm members grouped =
  mapGroupClauses (stampClauseWith mm.mmTop) members grouped

-- The module's marked tree: its binding groups' marked clauses written back into the
-- declarations in source order, then the remaining bodies (impl, default, prop,
-- test) marked with the module's final dict-name set — they are inferred
-- after every group, so every promotion the module made is in the set by then.
finishModuleMarking : ModuleMarking ->
  TcEnv ->
  OrdMap (List (List Pat, Expr)) ->
  List Decl ->
  List Decl
finishModuleMarking mm env grouped prog =
  markTailDecls
    env.teDyn.dcModule
    mm.mmCtx
    mm.mmDictSet.value
    (grantNameIn env)
    (rebuildGroupDecls grouped prog)

-- The stamped twin of the tail declarations, for their inference; binding groups
-- were stamped as the schedule reached them and are not walked again.
stampTailDecls : OrdMap Int -> List Decl -> List Decl
stampTailDecls top decls = map (stampTailDecl top) decls

stampTailDecl : OrdMap Int -> Decl -> Decl
stampTailDecl _ (d@(DFunDef _ _ _ _ _ _)) = d
stampTailDecl _ (d@(DLetGroup _ _)) = d
stampTailDecl top (DAttrib attrs d) = DAttrib attrs (stampTailDecl top d)
stampTailDecl top d = stampDeclWith top d

export
schemeLines : List (String, PubScheme) -> List String
schemeLines [] = []
schemeLines ((n, p) :: rest) = "\{n} : \{ppPubScheme p}" :: schemeLines rest
